WI-415" data-label="○ WI-415" data-summary="Process-tab polish:…" data-wi="WI-415" data-status="queued" style="--ring:#0f172a"><title>WI-415 — Process-tab polish: the ff-wording one-string fix and the 390px legibility observation (WI-389 REVIEW-A findings 1-2, minted trunk-side at intake per the R3 invariant). FINDING 1, the one-string fix: the Trunk advance card&#x27;s note reads &#x27;ff trunk to the barred tree&#x27;, which misstates the shipped act - trunk advances via the --no-ff merge commit itself (integrate.py&#x27;s slot; a true ff is what the RULING-6 audit reds); the reviewer&#x27;s replacement &#x27;advance trunk to the barred tree&#x27; fits the 34-char note budget. One string in traj_panels&#x27; station panel + regen; the drawn picture must not contradict the code it derives from. FINDING 2, per the render-critique discipline (observations filed as their own WIs): at 390px the station&#x27;s note lines render ~3.3 CSS px - illegible without pinch-zoom; no overflow or truncation, titles marginal, the esc list and svg title tooltips carry the content. JUDGE the fix honestly at the panel&#x27;s design constraints (bigger notes at narrow widths, a two-line wrap, or dropping notes below a width threshold in favor of the tooltips) - verify by pixels per the skill matrix, and take the smallest change that makes the 390px render honest; recording a measured accept-with-tooltips is a legitimate outcome if every alternative damages the 1280/1680 reads. Scope: traj_panels station panel + its tests + pixel evidence. (queued) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="7566.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#94a3b8" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="7589.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="7589.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="7589.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0f172a"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">○ WI-415</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">Process-tab polish:…</tspan></text></g><g class="block cancelled" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-417" data-label="⊗ WI-417" data-summary="The handback reason…" data-wi="WI-417" data-status="cancelled" style="--ring:#ffffff"><title>WI-417 — The handback reason string does two unrelated jobs and one of them is a layering violation (found 2026-08-03 while reading WI-413/WI-416; filed as its own row per the working agreement, NOT folded into either). JOB 1 - THE LAYERING VIOLATION: `NEEDS-HUMAN` in a handback reason is the LANE prescribing its own disposition, which R3 gives to the disposition row (docs/rulings-context-2026-08-01.md §R3 point 2 - the dispatcher surfaces an open item &#x27;where a human ruling is genuinely needed&#x27;, and that &#x27;genuinely&#x27; is the adjudicator&#x27;s judgement, not the returning lane&#x27;s). It also ANCHORS the judge it triggers: intake._handback_drafts clips the reason at _LINE_CLIP=140 into the minted row&#x27;s ## Context, so the adjudication brief OPENS with the defendant&#x27;s verdict - observed live on WI-416, truncated mid-word. The dispatcher&#x27;s AUTOMATIC reason (&#x27;worker exit 7 (NEEDS-HUMAN)&#x27;, dispatch.py:464-466) is an exit code plus its label, a fact and probably defensible; a hand-written prose reason that prescribes an outcome is not. JOB 2 - THE COUPLING: tier_signal (intake.py:151-152) is the reason string&#x27;s ONLY consumer - a case-insensitive substring test for &#x27;NEEDS-HUMAN&#x27; selecting strong vs medium buildtier. So the review TIER and the disposition VERDICT ride one magic substring, with no shared constant, no validation and no refusal on a miss: &#x27;NEEDS_HUMAN&#x27; or &#x27;needs human&#x27; silently downgrades the disposition row. THE ASK, three judgements: (1) key the tier off something FACTUAL the dispatcher already has (the worker exit-code class, or review-rounds-exhausted) rather than off a word that also reads as a verdict; (2) rule whether a handback reason is constrained at all - a named constant plus a loud refusal, or explicitly free prose with the prescription convention retired in the guidance; (3) judge whether the automatic &#x27;worker exit N (NEEDS-HUMAN)&#x27; label survives - it may, an exit code being a fact. Consider also whether the ## Context clip should quote the reason at all, given it anchors. (4) RENAME THE LABEL - `NEEDS-HUMAN` names an ACTOR where the signal is policy-independent, and the code already knows better: plan_round._PAGE_ACTIONS (plan_round.py:70-74) maps the SAME situation to stop-needs-human / surface-block-continue-others / design-check-session BY GATE LEVEL, and agent_loop.py:2851 records the invariant that under autonomous/single-ratify an attention-only outcome lands on EXIT_STALL, never a NEEDS-HUMAN gate. So exit 7&#x27;s name hard-codes the ATTENDED answer into a signal every gate level reads - and this repo&#x27;s declared level is autonomous, where it is wrong on its face. Owner&#x27;s proposed replacement (2026-08-03): NEEDS-JUDGEMENT, which names the owed ACT and leaves WHO supplies it to the adjudicator&#x27;s combination of gate level + issue + high-level view. The exit NUMBER (7) is the machine contract and does not move, so this is a label/doc migration for adopters, not a behaviour change; sequence it AFTER judgement (1), because once the tier keys off cause class the label stops being load-bearing. Surfaces to change: agent_common END_STATES + EXIT_NEEDS_HUMAN, agent_loop&#x27;s --help exit table and its two stop_banner headlines, dispatch._lane_close&#x27;s reason string, PROCESS_OPTIONS.md and ADOPTING.md. DO NOT rename bootstrap.STACK_NEEDS_HUMAN or check_docs._NEEDS_HUMAN_RE - those name the &#x27;Needs-&lt;human&gt;&#x27; open-items bucket, where an owner call genuinely IS the point. SCOPE-NARROWING EVIDENCE (traced 2026-08-03, so the builder need not re-hunt): of the five layers carrying this label, only two reach a later agent&#x27;s context - dispatch&#x27;s reason string and intake&#x27;s context clip. stop_banner (agent_common.py:1811) PRINTS ONLY; it reads status.md via current_state_excerpt and writes nothing, its old durable home docs/run-state having retired at concurrency-restructure Phase 5. END_STATES feeds classify_outcome into the session log. Neither anchors anything. Scope: intake.py tier_signal + the _handback_drafts context clip, dispatch.py _lane_close, agent_common&#x27;s constants, and tests; the shipped guidance (AGENTS.template.md / PROCESS.md / PROCESS_OPTIONS.md / ADOPTING.md) as judgements 2 and 4 require. (cancelled) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="7634.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#78716c" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="7657.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="7657.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="7657.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">⊗ WI-417</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">The handback reason…</tspan></text></g><g class="block cancelled" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-418" data-label="⊗ WI-418" data-summary="Derived prose injec…" data-wi="WI-418" data-status="cancelled" style="--ring:#ffffff"><title>WI-418 — Derived prose injected at claim can ANCHOR the agent it briefs - sweep intake.context_block&#x27;s imperative sections (found 2026-08-03 alongside WI-416/WI-417). This is a DESIGN QUESTION, not a proven defect: judge it before changing anything, and read the counterweight below FIRST. THE SHAPE, from the observed WI-416 case: intake._handback_drafts clipped a returned spec&#x27;s reason into the minted adjudication row&#x27;s ## Context, so the judge&#x27;s brief opened with the defendant&#x27;s verdict, truncated mid-word. context_block (intake.py:182) is the same shape at far higher frequency - three consumers, one of them agent_loop.worker_prompt computing it FRESH AT EVERY CLAIM, for every WI. THE SPECIFIC OBSERVATION: its first section header is an IMPERATIVE - &#x27;Cancelled precedent on the same SRs (do not re-propose the refuted)&#x27; (intake.py:238) - and it carries the cancelled row&#x27;s Deliverable as &#x27;reason&#x27;, clipped at 160. The docstring says the block is &#x27;advisory, NEVER gating&#x27;, and MECHANICALLY that is true (any failure answers the empty string, nothing can block on it); but the emitted PROSE says &#x27;do not&#x27;, so to the model reading it the block is rhetorically mandatory. Advisory-in-machinery plus imperative-in-prose is a contradiction, and the prose is the half an LLM obeys. &#x27;Review records of precedent rows&#x27; (intake.py:259) carries predecessor and cancelled-row VERDICTS with the same property. THE COUNTERWEIGHT, WHICH MUST NOT BE DISCARDED: this section exists for a MEASURED reason - it prevents re-proposing the refuted, which is the WI-391 failure mode, and WI-391 is this repo&#x27;s only park and it WAS a mis-assumption (docs/rulings-context-2026-08-01.md §R3). Softening the tone into a regression would be worse than the anchoring. THE ASK, three judgements: (1) can a joined fact be stated as a FACT (&#x27;WI-391 was cancelled; reason: ...&#x27;) leaving the inference to the reader, WITHOUT losing the WI-391 protection - i.e. is the imperative doing work the fact alone would not do, and is that demonstrable rather than assumed; (2) should a clipped free-prose Deliverable be quoted as &#x27;reason&#x27; at all, given the 160-char mid-word truncation reproduces the exact WI-416 anchoring shape; (3) does any OTHER derived-prose surface reaching a claim state a CONCLUSION where it could state an OBSERVATION - worker_prompt&#x27;s pred_block deliverables (clipped 200), the REWORK FINDING block (clipped 80), the minted row&#x27;s ## Context. Scope: intake.context_block, its section headers and formatters, and tests; agent_loop.worker_prompt only if judgement (3) finds a second site. NOT a licence to weaken the joins: the joins are good and their content is load-bearing - the question is only whether their PROSE prescribes where it could inform. (cancelled) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="7702.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#78716c" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="7725.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="7725.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="7725.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">⊗ WI-418</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">Derived prose injec…</tspan></text></g><g class="block queued" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-422" data-label="○ WI-422" data-summary="Measured dead-symbo…" data-wi="WI-422" data-status="queued" style="--ring:#0f172a"><title>WI-422 — Measured dead-symbol sweep across project-trajectory/scripts: nothing in the kit catches an ORPHANED symbol. The module-size ratchet catches GROWTH (a module that gets bigger must argue for it), test_complexity_ratchet catches a function getting harder, and check_dupes catches a block getting copied - but a helper whose last caller left is invisible to all three, because deleting the caller made the module SMALLER, which every ratchet reads as an improvement. The 2026-08-08 mechanized-loop program retired several call paths (the gate-policy enum&#x27;s four re-interpretation tables, the handback-into-queued shape, the three prompt string constants), so this sweep runs at a moment there is likely real residue. METHOD, and it must be MEASURED rather than eyeballed: take gen_arch_map.py&#x27;s symbol inventory as the candidate set, grep every candidate across scripts/ + tests/ + the templates, and classify each zero-hit symbol as (a) genuinely dead - delete it, (b) a PUBLIC seam a downstream repo may call, in which case it is not dead and its docstring should say who calls it, or (c) reached dynamically (a getattr, a CLI subcommand table, a re-export). Class (b) is the reason this is a judgement and not a script: the kit&#x27;s scripts are copied INTO other repos, so an unused-here symbol may be someone else&#x27;s entry point. Deliver the classified inventory in the spec&#x27;s Deliverable, not just the deletions. EXPLICITLY NOT a broad refactor: WI-390 forbids being built as a dead-code sweep and this row does not license one either - delete what is provably unreachable, record what is not, and stop. (queued) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="7770.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#94a3b8" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="7793.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="7793.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="7793.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0f172a"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">○ WI-422</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">Measured dead-symbo…</tspan></text></g><g class="block queued" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-423" data-label="○ WI-423" data-summary="Fold the six remain…" data-wi="WI-423" data-status="queued" style="--ring:#0f172a"><title>WI-423 — Fold the six remaining check-enablement toggles into docs/process.toml, or rule that they stay files. SN-028 consolidated ~10 one-word policy files into one TOML home and left SIX behind, naming them in the shipped template rather than omitting them quietly: docs/trajectory-check, docs/interfaces-check, docs/components-check (check_trajectory.py), docs/subagent-gate (subagent_gate.py), docs/live-status (agent_loop.py) and docs/okf-export (gen_okf.py). The stated reason each was left is real and is the whole question this row has to answer: every one is read by an INDEPENDENTLY COPYABLE checker that imports nothing of the config layer, so folding it in means each of those scripts grows its own local tomllib reader - a genuine cost paid five times over, against a benefit (one home) that is real but smaller for a toggle than for a policy. DECIDE IT, do not drift: either (a) fold them, accepting five small local readers and stating the F5 duplication in docs/dupes-allow like every other sanctioned copy, or (b) RULE that check-enablement is a different KIND of declaration from process policy - it answers &#x27;is this check on&#x27;, not &#x27;how is work processed&#x27; - and record that line in process.toml&#x27;s header as a decision rather than as a to-do. Whichever way it goes, the shipped template&#x27;s paragraph 4 must stop describing an open question. (queued) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="7838.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#94a3b8" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="7861.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="7861.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="7861.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0f172a"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">○ WI-423</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">Fold the six remain…</tspan></text></g><g class="block queued" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-424" data-label="○ WI-424" data-summary="Route each ADJUDICA…" data-wi="WI-424" data-status="queued" style="--ring:#0f172a"><title>WI-424 — Route each ADJUDICATE session to its own adjudicator brief - the four templates are authored, shipped, catalogued, and consumed by NOTHING. SN-032 moved the loop&#x27;s prompts into files and authored four adjudicator briefs (adjudicate-amendment, adjudicate-disposition, adjudicate-conflict, adjudicate-red-tc); SN-026 gave adjudication rows their own routed phase, tier and cross-family rule. The seam between them was never built: agent_loop.route_session composes EVERY non-review session from the generic worker prompt, so an adjudication row routes to a strong cross-family model and then receives an implementer&#x27;s instructions. The judge is briefed as a builder. TWO THINGS THIS ROW MUST DECIDE, and the second is why it was not done inside the program that found it. (1) THE DISCRIMINATOR: which of the four briefs a row wants is a typed fact, and the row does not currently carry it - the options are a new frontmatter key (honest, but a schema change across three F5-synced loaders), or derivation from the SpecRef cell (a spine CSV means amendment, a terminal spec means disposition, test-cases.csv means red TC), which costs nothing but infers rather than declares. Prefer the declared field unless the schema cost is real. (2) THE SLOTS: each brief demands assembled EVIDENCE - adjudicate-amendment wants {baseline} and {rows}, adjudicate-conflict wants {mechanical}, {open_rows}, {spine} and {digests}, adjudicate-disposition wants {report} and {evidence}. Those are real derivations (a baseline diff via trace._attested_baseline, the queue-conflict findings, a spine excerpt), and a half-filled brief is WORSE than the generic prompt: a judge&#x27;s brief with hollow sections reads as though the evidence was looked for and found wanting. Fill every slot faithfully or leave the template unrouted. Done when: an adjudication row&#x27;s session provably receives its own brief (fake-CLI prompts.txt capture, one test per template), the typed verdict line each brief demands is written to the declared path, and no slot is filled with a placeholder. (queued) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="7906.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#94a3b8" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="7929.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="7929.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="7929.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#0f172a"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">○ WI-424</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">Route each ADJUDICA…</tspan></text></g><g class="block queued" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-425" data-label="○ WI-425" data-summary="Repoint the ~71 liv…" data-wi="WI-425" data-status="queued" style="--ring:#0f172a"><title>WI-425 — Repoint the ~71 live source citations of the three SN ids the 2026-08-10 sitting retired (SN-030, SN-031, SN-032) at the SR rows that now carry their obligation. The sitting ruled all three MIS