ate returns G0 for a draft, dropping the gate off G3. When the last mechanizable anchor under an SR is bound, retire the coarse LLR/TC and flip that SR to Verification=Test - tests first, flip second, never the reverse. (done) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="1174.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#047857" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="1197.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="1197.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="1197.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">✓ WI-295</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">Dashboard Process-t…</tspan></text></g><g class="block done" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-296" data-label="✓ WI-296" data-summary="When-tab explainer …" data-wi="WI-296" data-status="done" style="--ring:#ffffff"><title>WI-296 — When-tab explainer over-promises hover-to-highlight in TIERED mode (narrowed from a WRONG premise - read the whole row before acting) - HTML_TEMPLATE emits &#x27;Hover a work item to highlight its neighbourhood&#x27; UNCONDITIONALLY, but the neighbourhood controller reads `.wi`/`.edge` nodes that only the FLAT-DAG emitter (dag_svg) produces. Any project above the SR-089 &gt;3 rule (&gt;3 phases OR &gt;3 workstreams - this repo has 12 workstreams) renders the TIERED drill view, where wiNodes/wiEdges are empty and hover does nothing of the kind; drill blocks have their own `.hl` ring but no neighbourhood logic. THE ORIGINAL &#x27;DEAD CODE&#x27; PREMISE WAS WRONG AND MUST NOT BE ACTED ON: 117-CRITIQUE&#x27;s note observed class=&quot;wi&quot; = 0 occurrences in THIS repo&#x27;s artifact and concluded the `.wi` emitter/CSS/JS was retired dead code to delete. Verified 2026-07-24 that it is NOT - rendering a minimal scaffold (&lt;=3 phases and &lt;=3 workstreams) emits 8 `class=&quot;wi&quot;` nodes, and test_when_view_below_thresholds_returns_none_flat_dag + test_a3_flat_dag_fallback_also_prefixes_the_status_glyph both assert that path. Because the kit SHIPS downstream and a freshly-scaffolded adopter has few phases and workstreams, the flat DAG is the DEFAULT render for exactly the copy-ready starting state the kit exists to provide - deleting it would break the When tab for every new downstream project. The JS is also already guarded (`dag ? [...] : []`), so nothing errors in tiered mode. REAL FIX (small): make the explainer sentence conditional on which emitter ran, or implement neighbourhood highlighting for the drill blocks so the promise holds in both modes. Open question for the owner: an explainer promising absent behaviour is arguably an SR-054 usability defect; SR-Refs left empty so re-gating it as critique-verified stays a deliberate choice, not a side effect. (done) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="1242.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#047857" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="1265.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="1265.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="1265.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">✓ WI-296</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">When-tab explainer …</tspan></text></g><g class="block done" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-297" data-label="✓ WI-297" data-summary="Dashboard SVGs decl…" data-wi="WI-297" data-status="done" style="--ring:#ffffff"><title>WI-297 — Dashboard SVGs declare role=img over 1,146 focusable descendants - an ARIA children-presentational conflict (found while adjudicating the 004/118-CRITIQUE A2 split) - 42 of the 43 `&lt;svg role=&quot;img&quot;&gt;` containers in the generated PROJECT_STATE.html hold interactive nodes, and 1,146 of the document&#x27;s 1,150 `tabindex=&quot;0&quot;` elements sit INSIDE a role=img subtree. ARIA specifies role=img as children-presentational, so that subtree is expected to be pruned from the accessibility tree - which means the per-node `&lt;title&gt;`s that A2 compliance was resting on may never reach a screen reader, and the interactive nodes may be unreachable. THIS IS THE CAUSE BOTH CRITIQUE DISAGREEMENTS WERE CIRCLING WITHOUT NAMING: 116-CRITIQUE (OPENCODE-GROK) PASSED A2 arguing the per-node titles cover it (they may be pruned); 004-CRITIQUE (OPENAI-SOL) FAILED A2 asking for container names (naming an image whose interactive children are pruned does not fix it); 118-CRITIQUE (OPENAI-SOL again) PASSED A2 on byte-identical evidence. The symptom they argued over was naming; the cause is the role. Fix: drop role=img from any SVG with focusable descendants, or replace it with role=group - the pattern the `.view` wrappers already use correctly (`role=&quot;group&quot;` + aria-label); keep role=img only for genuinely non-interactive graphics, which then do each need their own accessible name. CAVEAT: AT implementations vary in how strictly they prune, so treat this as an authoring-conformance defect to correct rather than a proven end-user failure; confirm against a real screen reader if that is cheap. MECHANIZE (OI-9): ship a structural check on the EMITTED html - no element carrying role=&quot;img&quot; may contain a focusable descendant, and any genuinely non-interactive role=&quot;img&quot; must carry an accessible name. That check owns A2 and would have caught this without a critic. | WI-300 (f) OBLIGATION (2026-07-25 ruling): this row now CLOSES BY BINDING its anchor - land the fix, the test that owns the anchor, AND the child LLR+TC rows naming that test in Evidence, in the SAME commit. A child row cannot land ahead of its test: Draft exempts it from --require-verified but derive_gate returns G0 for a draft, dropping the gate off G3. When the last mechanizable anchor under an SR is bound, retire the coarse LLR/TC and flip that SR to Verification=Test - tests first, flip second, never the reverse. (done) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="1310.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#047857" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="1333.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="1333.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="1333.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">✓ WI-297</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">Dashboard SVGs decl…</tspan></text></g><g class="block cancelled" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-298" data-label="⊗ WI-298" data-summary="Dashboard --border …" data-wi="WI-298" data-status="cancelled" style="--ring:#ffffff"><title>WI-298 — Dashboard --border misses the 3:1 graphical-boundary floor in both themes (004-CRITIQUE A4, OPENAI-SOL) - the --border token supplies the boundary for focusable .view regions, detail cards and linked Process-stage rectangles, but measures light #e2e8f0 = 1.23:1 on --surface #ffffff and 1.18:1 on --bg #f8fafc, dark #1e293b = 1.22:1 on --surface #0f172a and 1.29:1 on --bg #0b1120, against the 3:1 floor the accessibility rubric&#x27;s A4 states for graphical/UI boundaries. All four ratios re-verified independently. NOTE THE CRITIC DISAGREEMENT: 116-CRITIQUE (OPENCODE-GROK) dismissed this under WCAG 1.4.11&#x27;s identifiable-by-other-means exception (a card with a text label needs no boundary contrast); 004-CRITIQUE (OPENAI-SOL) held that these particular elements ARE controls - focusable regions and links - so the exception does not apply. Owner ruling needed, same reason as WI-297. Fix if upheld: theme-specific boundary colours reaching 3:1 against every adjacent surface, or another persistent boundary cue meeting the same threshold. Distinct from WI-293, which fixed TEXT-on-fill contrast, not boundaries. (cancelled) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="1378.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#78716c" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="1401.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="1401.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="1401.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">⊗ WI-298</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">Dashboard --border …</tspan></text></g><g class="block done" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-299" data-label="✓ WI-299" data-summary="Dashboard focus ind…" data-wi="WI-299" data-status="done" style="--ring:#ffffff"><title>WI-299 — Dashboard focus indicator misses the 3:1 boundary floor, and VANISHES on the phase-3 block (118-CRITIQUE A4, OPENAI-SOL) - `.drill .block:focus rect` strokes in var(--accent), but --accent #4f46e5 IS the phase-3 block fill, so a keyboard user focusing that block gets a ring at 1.00:1 - literally invisible, no focus indication at all. Dark theme is 2.11:1 (#818cf8 on #4f46e5), and the icicle&#x27;s amber #f59e0b ring misses 3:1 on the SR #0e7490 (2.49:1), LLR #64748b (2.22:1) and TC #047857 (2.55:1) fills. All five ratios re-verified independently. FOUND THREE TIMES INDEPENDENTLY at three severities - OPENCODE-KIMI filed it MINOR under uniformity U5 (117-CRITIQUE), OPENCODE-GROK saw it and declined to file it (116-CRITIQUE, &#x27;focus still updates the detail pane&#x27;), OPENAI-SOL files it MAJOR under accessibility A4 - which makes it the best-corroborated finding of the 2026-07-24 critique rounds. Fix: a dedicated focus treatment independent of the palette (e.g. a two-layer ring: a light inner + dark outer stroke, which clears 3:1 against ANY fill), verified against every node fill in both themes. RELATED BUT DISTINCT: WI-292 owns the --accent/phase-3 hue COLLISION (a uniformity defect); WI-294 owns unifying the amber-vs-accent ring TOKEN across emitters; this row owns the ring&#x27;s CONTRAST against what it is drawn on. Doing WI-292/294 without this one can still leave a sub-3:1 ring. MECHANIZE (OI-9): ship a focus-indicator contrast check - the focus ring must clear 3:1 against EVERY node fill it can be drawn on, in BOTH themes (the 1.00:1 case is ring-colour == fill-colour, which a simple equality check also catches). That check owns the focus half of A4, beside the theme-token test A4-hub already has. | WI-300 (f) OBLIGATION (2026-07-25 ruling): this row now CLOSES BY BINDING its anchor - land the fix, the test that owns the anchor, AND the child LLR+TC rows naming that test in Evidence, in the SAME commit. A child row cannot land ahead of its test: Draft exempts it from --require-verified but derive_gate returns G0 for a draft, dropping the gate off G3. When the last mechanizable anchor under an SR is bound, retire the coarse LLR/TC and flip that SR to Verification=Test - tests first, flip second, never the reverse. (done) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="1446.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#047857" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="1469.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="1469.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="1469.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">✓ WI-299</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">Dashboard focus ind…</tspan></text></g><g class="block done" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-305" data-label="✓ WI-305" data-summary="Dashboard has no ne…" data-wi="WI-305" data-status="done" style="--ring:#ffffff"><title>WI-305 — Dashboard has no next-work surface (119-CRITIQUE T1) - dashboard-usability.md&#x27;s core reading task 2 (find the next work) has no path: with 0 active items nothing is marked you-are-here, the Process tab&#x27;s resume-loop panel is a static method diagram carrying no data, and the only route to a queued item is When -&gt; double-click a phase -&gt; double-click a workstream -&gt; scan for a queued node - the anchor&#x27;s own bad case, expanding nested blocks to locate something. Fix: surface the ready/queued work items derived from the DAG (schedule.py&#x27;s frontier, already computed for IF-071) on the landing view or within one tab switch, named, with their blocking predecessor. Re-affirmed 2026-07-26 against the amended SR-054 (the T1/T3 ruling retires T3 and rewords T1; T1 stays a LIVE critique anchor, so this defect is unaffected). WI-315 - the T1 binding - is gated behind this row: binding the anchor while its defect is open would be the lax classification the option-(f) ruling forbids. (done) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="290.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#047857" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="313.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="313.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="313.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">✓ WI-305</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">Dashboard has no ne…</tspan></text></g><g class="block done" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-306" data-label="✓ WI-306" data-summary="Dashboard landing (…" data-wi="WI-306" data-status="done" style="--ring:#ffffff"><title>WI-306 — Dashboard landing (What) tab does not start-collapsed (119-CRITIQUE T2) - unlike the three wired tabs (When/How-SW/Knowledge, which correctly open at a summary layer per the SR-089 &#x27;&gt;3&#x27; rule), the What tab&#x27;s icicle renders all four lanes fully exploded (~340 blocks in this repo). Two visible consequences: the TC lane compresses to unlabelled 1-unit bars fading into a right-edge gradient, and the card clips the last row mid-block with only a sideways-scroll hint that says nothing about the vertical cut. Fix: apply the same start-collapsed threshold to the icicle (open at the SN lane, descend on click), or cap the rendered depth so the landing view is a summary; add a vertical affordance if it stays scrollable. Re-affirmed 2026-07-26 against the amended SR-054 (the T1/T3 ruling touches T1 and T3 only; T2 stays a live critique anchor and this defect is unaffected). (done) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="1514.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#047857" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="1537.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="1537.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="1537.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">✓ WI-306</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">Dashboard landing (…</tspan></text></g><g class="block done" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-307" data-label="✓ WI-307" data-summary="Dashboard SVG emitt…" data-wi="WI-307" data-status="done" style="--ring:#ffffff"><title>WI-307 — Dashboard SVG emitters do not reflow at their declared viewport widths (119-CRITIQUE T7) - two independent instances: at 390px the How-SW module graph is sliced mid-label at the viewport edge (CMP-002 reads &#x27;CMP...&#x27;) forcing a sideways scroll; at the desktop 1280px width the What icicle&#x27;s TC lane is clipped at the card&#x27;s right edge and fades out, a fit failure at a DESKTOP width, not just mobile. The 390px above-the-fold landing is clean (cards reflow, nothing clipped), so the fault is specific to the SVG emitters, not the page layout. Fix: make the SVG viewBox responsive (scale-to-fit or re-layout under a width threshold) so narrow/wide content reflows; keep the sideways-scroll hint only as a fallback for content that genuinely cannot fit. Re-affirmed 2026-07-26 against the amended SR-054 (the T1/T3 ruling touches T1 and T3 only; T7 stays a live critique anchor and this defect is unaffected). (done) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="1582.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#047857" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="1605.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="1605.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="1605.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">✓ WI-307</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">Dashboard SVG emitt…</tspan></text></g><g class="block done" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-314" data-label="✓ WI-314" data-summary="SR-054 T6 theme-loc…" data-wi="WI-314" data-status="done" style="--ring:#ffffff"><title>WI-314 — SR-054 T6 theme-lock is mechanizable-but-unbound (adversarial review F12 of the SR-052 flip) - the WI-300 ruling&#x27;s SR-054 per-anchor table marks T6 (&#x27;every surface&#x27;s background/text token resolves from one theme set; no mid-page inversion&#x27;) as mechanized with residue NONE, needing only a static token check and no browser harness - yet unlike every other mechanizable SR-054 core it has neither a binding WI nor an open defect row, making it the residue most likely to be forgotten. The close-narrative correction the review asked for, RESTATED 2026-07-26 after the T1/T3 ruling (this row re-affirmed against the amended SR-054): SR-054&#x27;s residue is now T4-affordance/T8-crossings perceptual PLUS T6/T7 mechanizable-but-unbound - T3 is RETIRED as a critique anchor (bound wholly to LLR-100/TC-103), T1 is reworded onto its operational gloss and filed for binding as WI-315 behind WI-305, T7 is carried by open defect WI-307, and T4&#x27;s core needs the browser harness, which is cheaper than assumed since Playwright is already vendored and driven by scripts/dashboard-shots/shoot.mjs. Close by binding, per the (f) pattern: the T6 check + child LLR/TC under SR-054 with Automated=Yes evidence in the same commit. (done) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="1650.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#047857" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="1673.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="1673.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="1673.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">✓ WI-314</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">SR-054 T6 theme-loc…</tspan></text></g><g class="block done" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-315" data-label="✓ WI-315" data-summary="Bind T1 task findab…" data-wi="WI-315" data-status="done" style="--ring:#ffffff"><title>WI-315 — Bind T1 task findability - the last SR-054 anchor whose mechanizable half has no owner (only T2/T3/T5 have child rows; the WI-300 ruling called T1&#x27;s core assertable against the nav graph and then bound everything except it, the same mechanizable-but-unbound hole F12 found for T6/WI-314). The 2026-07-26 owner ruling reworded the anchor off the word &#x27;obvious&#x27; onto its own operational gloss, so the bar is now checkable: each of the three core reading tasks reachable within one tab switch from the landing view, its entry point a LABELLED nav control or a named landing surface. Bind it as a child LLR + Automated=Yes TC under SR-054 per the WI-300 (f) pattern. Design constraint that makes the test worth writing: assert against the RENDERED artifact carrying real registry data, not the nav skeleton - 119-CRITIQUE&#x27;s MAJOR was a next-work surface that exists in the emitter (the hero &#x27;sub nowat&#x27; line, already covered by test_t1_hero_names_the_active_work_item) but rendered nothing because it fires only on status=active and the registry had zero active rows, a state test_t1_hero_active_line_absent_when_nothing_is_active explicitly blesses; a structural nav check passes on exactly the artifact the critic failed. Gated behind WI-305 (the open T1 defect): binding an anchor while its defect is open is the lax classification the WI-300 ruling forbids. (done) · unphased</title><rect x="202.0" y="1208.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#047857" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="202.0" cy="1231.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="326.0" cy="1231.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="264.0" y="1231.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff"><tspan x="264.0" dy="-2" class="blab">✓ WI-315</tspan><tspan x="264.0" dy="13" class="bsub">Bind T1 task findab…</tspan></text></g><g class="block done" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-317" data-label="✓ WI-317" data-summary="Dashboard containme…" data-wi="WI-317" data-status="done" style="--ring:#ffffff"><title>WI-317 — Dashboard containment/descend arrow misses the 3:1 UI-boundary floor in both themes (T5, found by the WI-305 train critique 2026-07-26) - the `.cedge` parent-&gt;child arrow _drill_layer_svg emits on every descendable block, plus its `cedgearrow` marker, is measured at 1.06:1 in light (#4f46e5 on #0369a1) and 1.99:1 in dark (#818cf8 on #0369a1) against the node fill it is drawn over. It is an EXPAND/DESCEND AFFORDANCE - the control a reader must find to descend a tier - so T5&#x27;s floor applies to it exactly as it applied to the focus ring WI-299 fixed. Same defect class, same fix shape: derive the arrow and marker stroke from the host fill&#x27;s contrast-safe control token (the computed `--ring` / `_ring_ink` machinery WI-299 already built and LLR-105 owns) instead of a fixed hue that vanishes on whichever fill happens to match it - WI-299&#x27;s own measured collision was the phase-3 accent at 1.00:1, the identical failure mode one control over. Bind by widening LLR-105/TC-108 (T5&#x27;s core, already &#x27;the descend/hover/focus ring painted over a node&#x27;s own fill&#x27;) to cover the containment arrow + marker, floor-checked against EVERY declared fill in both themes. Guard must be proven against the current 1.06:1 value before the fix. NOTE: this WI is the one piece of value the otherwise-deadlocked train dispatch bought - no other WI owned it. (done) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="1718.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#047857" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="1741.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="1741.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="1741.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">✓ WI-317</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">Dashboard containme…</tspan></text></g><g class="block done" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-318" data-label="✓ WI-318" data-summary="Architecture node d…" data-wi="WI-318" data-status="done" style="--ring:#ffffff"><title>WI-318 — Architecture node descriptions clip past their box on every width (T4, 121-CRITIQUE MAJOR) - in the What/Architecture decomposition each visible SN block draws its description as a single unwrapped line that begins outside the left edge of the node and runs past the right edge, so the text is unreadable AS a label and also breaks the box it belongs to. Reported at 390px (390px-light-arch-full.png, SN-001..SN-009) and still present at desktop width (1680px-light-arch-full.png, 1680px-dark-arch-full.png), so this is not a narrow-viewport reflow issue that WI-307&#x27;s scale-to-fit covers - the string is simply longer than any box it is drawn in. Fix by WRAPPING within the node, sizing the node to its content, or emitting a deliberately budgeted summary that fits (the _drill_block_label wrap idiom WI-246 built for CMP blocks is the existing shape). Bind by widening the T4 label-legibility owner rather than filing a new anchor: no character of a node label may render outside its own rect, checked against the emitted geometry, not by eye. RE-AFFIRMED against the amended SR-054 (2026-07-26, WI-321&#x27;s prompt): the amendment removed the row&#x27;s restatement of which critique anchors are live, moving that to the rubric alone. The requirement text this finding is judged against is unchanged, and the rubric still carries the anchor, so the finding stands as scoped. (done) · unphased</title><rect x="18.0" y="1786.0" width="124" height="46" rx="8" fill="#047857" stroke="rgba(15,23,42,.15)"></rect><circle class="port in" cx="18.0" cy="1809.0" r="4.5"></circle><circle class="port out" cx="142.0" cy="1809.0" r="4.5"></circle><text x="80.0" y="1809.0" text-anchor="middle" fill="#fff"><tspan x="80.0" dy="-2" class="blab">✓ WI-318</tspan><tspan x="80.0" dy="13" class="bsub">Architecture node d…</tspan></text></g><g class="block done" data-tier="work-item" tabindex="0" data-node="WI-319" data-label="✓ WI-319" data-summary="Next-work card trun…" d