40.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-040)", "title": "IF-040", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-041": {"description": "invokes the configured agent CLI headless (build_argv substitutes {model}/{prompt}; on Windows it refuses prompt-in-argv through a .cmd/.bat shim because the OS may reparse it via cmd.exe even with shell=False) per the in-process phase and captures the typed session outcome", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-041.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-041)", "title": "IF-041", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-042": {"description": "the pre-commit floor runs trace.py --strict-integrity on staged content and blocks the commit on any duplicate/malformed id or mis-columned row", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-042.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-042)", "title": "IF-042", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-043": {"description": "the pre-push hook runs check_privacy.py --range over the outgoing commit range and blocks a push that would publish a secret or a gated identity", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-043.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-043)", "title": "IF-043", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-044": {"description": "agent_route.py module/CLI: load_registry/load_enabled + resolve_enabled (a version-less enable-list token -> the newest pair in its Family-Model line) + parse_env (a pair row's Env cell) + select(pool,tier)->routed id honoring per-pair cooldown, the Family-keyed reviewer heterogeneity preference and tier-up-never-down; escalate()/failure_action() the fixed win-stay/lose-shift policy keyed to docs/gate-policy; planner_pair()/planner_fallback() the dual-plan two-hat selection (WI-196: different families where the pool allows, else two fresh same-family sessions with a recorded PAIR_* reason; the fallback hard-excludes a launch-nonresponsive family) - pure selection returning the reason as data, the coordinator owning launch; --select prints the routed id + reason, --list the enabled pool", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-044.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-044)", "title": "IF-044", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-045": {"description": "reads the docs/agents.csv pair-row model registry (Id/Family/Model/Version/Tier/CmdTemplate/Env; one row = one model x route pair; legacy Provider read as Family) and the ordered docs/agents-enabled enable-list to compose the selectable pool; absent enable-list = routing off", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-045.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-045)", "title": "IF-045", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-046": {"description": "score_reviews.py module/CLI: parse_verdict + substance (anchored precision, actionability, cross-reviewer corroboration, optional confirmed rate; length never positive) + fired_tripwires (non-scored gates) + merge_verdict + record_round; --verdict scores a block and exits 1 when a tripwire fired", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-046.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-046)", "title": "IF-046", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-047": {"description": "reads the docs/reviews/*.md verdict files (log.md block + the VERDICT machine line) and maintains docs/reviews/scoreboard.txt - advisory decayed per-provider tallies + the round history the escalation policy reads", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-047.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-047)", "title": "IF-047", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-048": {"description": "run_menu.py CLI: reads docs/stack.ini [run] and presents a numbered menu (no args) / direct launch by name with exit-code passthrough (run_menu.py <name>) / --list (name<TAB>desc, the agent surface); an absent or empty [run] section prints guidance and exits 1", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-048.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-048)", "title": "IF-048", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-049": {"description": "reads the [run] section (one <name> = <command> line per capability plus an optional <name>.desc) declaring the launch capabilities the run.* launchers present", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-049.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-049)", "title": "IF-049", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-050": {"description": "docs/gate derived marker: derive_gate writes the computed gate as the first non-comment line (with the # basis derivation above it); check.py resolve_gate reads it; --check is the freshness gate", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-050.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-050)", "title": "IF-050", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-051": {"description": "reads SR/LLR/TC Status + the SN section-state to compute per-artifact maturity (also low-level-requirements.csv, test-cases.csv, stakeholder-needs.md)", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-051.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-051)", "title": "IF-051", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-052": {"description": "reads the cached derived gate (first non-comment line, the derive_gate.py cache) as the Process tab's render condition and current-gate highlight; an absent docs/gate omits the tab so a gate-less repo renders byte-identically (WI-280: the read is traj_parse._gate_value and the tab traj_panels.process_panel, both re-exported by the facade that declares this seam)", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-052.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-052)", "title": "IF-052", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-053": {"description": "the ready-frontier + deterministic-order + safety-classification library/CLI: `ready [--explain|--format json]` and `simulate --jobs N` over the WI registry (docs/work/; legacy work-items.csv via dual-read) return the ordered frontier and reason-coded dispositions; pure and side-effect-free (never mutates the registry, spawns a worker, or touches git). Shared by validation, the dashboard, and the dispatcher (Slice D).", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-053.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-053)", "title": "IF-053", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-054": {"description": "reads the WI registry - Status + hard/soft Predecessors + the optional Priority/Exclusive/BlockRef/EstTokens/SafetyClass columns (frontmatter keys in the folder home) - to derive readiness; an absent optional column reads as its documented default (SafetyClass absent => unclassified, fail-closed; Priority => 0; Exclusive => empty).", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-054.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-054)", "title": "IF-054", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-055": {"description": "the integrator's claim derives the ready frontier + class eligibility through the pure library (load_wis/evaluate + the SCHED_* classification constants) and claims only positively-classified ordinary work - unclassified or non-ordinary refuses per WI; the integrator owns the claim commit and the branch cut, the library stays side-effect-free (Phase 5: the retired dispatcher was this seam's prior consumer).", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-055.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-055)", "title": "IF-055", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-056": {"description": "gen_trajectory's DERIVATION-loader sibling: the dashboard derives THROUGH the validator's loaders/joins (read_rows/load_wis/_split_refs/_norm_module/load_ifs/component_top_view/_cycles + the registry path constants) so ranking/derivation rules live in one home and the render can never disagree with validation; the two scripts always ship + re-sync together (bootstrap MAPPING). This is the DERIVATION seam (validate); the dashboard's ready-frontier DECISION is read SEPARATELY from schedule.py via IF-071 (decide) - the two are distinct concerns. WI-280 split the consumer: the facade still imports the validator (this row) and the siblings that now carry their own imports declare the same seam as IF-082 (traj_parse) / IF-083 (traj_views) / IF-084 (traj_status) — one contract stated once here. Declared at WI-064 when the cross-CMP rule surfaced the CMP-002 -> CMP-001 edge as the check's first live finding; reworded at WI-290 to name the second (frontier) seam.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-056.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-056)", "title": "IF-056", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-057": {"description": "reads interfaces.csv (IF-ID column) and system-requirements.csv (SR-ID column) to resolve a proposed plan's per-WI Interfaces/Covers cites - the dual-plan coverage pre-pass (process-options.md \"Dual-plan decomposition\"); registries absent = refs unvalidated with an honest note, never findings", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-057.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-057)", "title": "IF-057", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-058": {"description": "pure dual-plan round-lifecycle library (WI-194): new_round/ready_steps/record/disposition/page_action - typed steps (PLAN/COVERAGE/REPAIR/CRITIQUE/REVISE/ARBITER), hard caps raised as RoundCapError (n=1 critique, one revision each, one coverage repair per plan per stage), a round-budget ledger, the position-swap x2 agreement rule, and JSON-able state for crash-resumable rounds", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-058.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-058)", "title": "IF-058", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-059": {"description": "reads system-requirements.csv (SR-ID/Title/Requirement) and interfaces.csv (the IF-ID/Direction/ThisProject/Counterpart/Status/Contract excerpt) to build the allowlist-only SR surface + IF registry the dual-plan planner/critic/arbiter briefs embed, and registers the three hat prompt-map keys (DUALPLAN-PLANNER/CRITIC/ARBITER) on --prompt-map/AGENT_PROMPT_MAP with a kit-template fallback; the two-file read IS the redaction boundary - status.md/log.md/self-assessments are unreachable by construction", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-059.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-059)", "title": "IF-059", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-060": {"description": "run_coverage invokes the scripts/plan_coverage CLI headless (sys.executable, --goal/--root/--out) and consumes its report/exit contract - 0 clean, 1 findings (FAIL - <planfile>: lines parsed to implicated true plan keys via a caller-supplied plan_key_of), 2 malformed (its own flag; both bounce, empty implicated = both plans re-run, fail-closed); returns the typed {exit, findings, malformed, implicated, report} projected onto plan_round's STEP_COVERAGE via to_record_kwargs; the captured report is the payload the critic/arbiter briefs embed", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-060.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-060)", "title": "IF-060", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-061": {"description": "round artifact write-side (WI-198): allocate_round_dir (next docs/plans/DP-NNN-<slug>/), write_stage (stable-named UTF-8 stage artifacts), append_log_summary (verdict block -> docs/log.md), and file_selected_wis (the selected Plan-WI rows -> queued registry entries (docs/work/queued/ spec files in the folder home; legacy work-items.csv rows via dual-write; ids allocated over BOTH homes): fresh ids, mapped predecessors + the round's parent, empty Deliverable, SpecRef, BuildTier) such that check_trajectory.py passes; csv-module append preserves the file's line-ending convention", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-061.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-061)", "title": "IF-061", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-064": {"description": "agent_session.py module: the session-launch layer split out of agent_loop (WI-218) - build_argv(template, model, prompt) -> (argv, stdin_input) deciding prompt delivery (a {prompt} placeholder rides argv except a Windows .cmd/.bat shim is refused; omitted = the prompt is piped to the child's STDIN, immune to command-line caps and batch-shell re-parsing, WI-216); run_session(argv, root, timeout, env, on_line, stdin_input) -> (exit_code, output, timed_out) - one fresh headless session, stdin closed or fed-then-closed (never an interactive wait, SN-016), reader-thread pumped, per-session timeout, codex --output-last-message read back as the result (WI-217); plus parse_json_result and the console echo/LiveStatus renderers. agent_loop re-exports these names, so its public surface is unchanged.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-064.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-064)", "title": "IF-064", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-065": {"description": "agent_common.py module: the shared coordinator primitives split out of agent_loop (WI-218) - typed exit codes + END_STATES; git/head_sha[_full] wrappers; the declared-surface reads (read_declared/pause_reason/the WI-148 blackout window) + stop_banner; the per-worktree kernel advisory lock (acquire_lock/release_lock - the held descriptor lives HERE, one lock namespace per process); worker-assignment primitives (WI token/train slug/registry/trailer-evidence readers); parse_map; preflight (SR-027 launchability refusal); and the session-log family (size-bounded logs, regenerated iteration index, telemetry commit) + the generated run-state write. agent_loop re-exports the historical names, so its public surface is unchanged.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-065.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-065)", "title": "IF-065", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-066": {"description": "plan_runner.py module: the dual-plan round runner split out of agent_loop (WI-218) - wi_plan_mode(row) reads the registry PlanMode signal; run_dual_plan_round(root, wi, row, template, model, timeout, prompt_map) drives the WI-194..198 modules through the headless session layer and returns (SELECTED|PAGE, detail), with routing-ON planner pairs (tag-rank-aware resolve), the one runtime-nonresponse fallback, and the position-swapped arbiter pair. agent_loop re-exports wi_plan_mode/PLAN_MODE_DUAL/run_dual_plan_round (+ _dp_* helpers), so its public surface is unchanged.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-066.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-066)", "title": "IF-066", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-068": {"description": "the session dial read (WI-274 part B): agent_loop reads the docs/stack.ini [agent-loop] section (fallback model / model-map) at boot via agent_common.read_agent_loop_config, resolving each dial with precedence CLI flag > AGENT_* env > declared file > built-in default, so the agent-resume launchers need no dial copies. (The dispatcher-era jobs dial retired at Phase 5; a declared value is ignored.)", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-068.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-068)", "title": "IF-068", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-069": {"description": "check_coverage.py CLI: exits 1 when a module in the docs/coverage-floors census is below its declared per-module line-coverage in coverage.json or is absent from it; a missing report SKIPs and an empty census is a no-op", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-069.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-069)", "title": "IF-069", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-070": {"description": "reads the pytest-cov JSON report (per-file summary.percent_covered, path-normalized) and the docs/coverage-floors census (module + min percent; blank/# lines ignored)", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-070.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-070)", "title": "IF-070", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-071": {"description": "the gen_trajectory FAMILY reads the scheduler's ready-frontier for the generated STATUS block + the landing Next-work card (schedule.load_registry_rows/load_wis/frontier/evaluate). WI-280: the guarded `import schedule` has its ONE home in traj_parse.py (IF-085 is that module-level row) and traj_status/traj_panels read it as traj_parse.schedule; this row remains the seam as the facade declares it so the dashboard shows the SAME dependency-ready ordering the dispatcher acts on - derived once in schedule.py (IF-053 already names it 'shared by ... the dashboard') and never recomputed in a second ranker. The frontier DECISION seam - distinct from the derivation-loader seam to check_trajectory (IF-056); degrades to an empty frontier block when schedule.py is absent (the WI-284 try/except).", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-071.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-071)", "title": "IF-071", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-072": {"description": "the declared-absence read (WI-308): check_doc_refs reads an optional `<path> — <reason>` file (--declared-absences, default docs/declared-absences) and reclassifies a reference to any listed path as UNTRACED with the DECLARED reason quoted back, rather than dangling. The seam exists because a second reader needs the same facts: tests/test_dogfood_sync.py loads the file as its SCAFFOLD_OMISSIONS declaration, so the file — not an import — is the shared home, keeping the stdlib-only kit script free of any dependency on a test module. Fail-soft in the safe direction: an absent file, or a line carrying no separator, declares nothing and leaves the path dangling, so a malformed entry can never silence real rot.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-072.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-072)", "title": "IF-072", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-073": {"description": "the owner decision registry read (WI-322, OI-10 ruled option (b)): gen_open_items loads one row per decision (OI-ID/Title/Status/OneLine/Decision/BlastRadius/Options/Recommendation/WI-Refs) and renders every Status=pending row as a brief card. A ruled row is history, not a decision, and is deliberately NOT rendered - the Decisions log in docs/log.md is its record. -000 example rows are dropped, the copy-ready placeholder convention every other registry uses; an absent registry renders an empty queue rather than failing, so a repo carrying no decisions still has a surface.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-073.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-073)", "title": "IF-073", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-074": {"description": "the generated owner surface (WI-322): the ONLY document a human reads for pending decisions plus every Draft/Modified spine row's per-cell before/after. Contract of the emitted file: it carries an attestation-baseline stamp that --check re-reads (so a --since render is reproducible by the freshness gate), and a machine-local advisory region between explicit markers that --check MASKS before comparing (refs/llm/* facts do not transport with clone/push - the M-10/WI-266 rule inherited from the markdown block it replaces). The depth itself is computed by trace.reattest_model and the pending pointers by gen_trajectory.pending_block; this module renders, and if it ever disagrees with `trace.py --ratify` the brief is authoritative.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-074.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-074)", "title": "IF-074", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-075": {"description": "the attestation-model seam (WI-322): gen_open_items imports trace.reattest_model — the SINGLE computation of what a Draft/Modified spine row's chain looks like against its attested baseline (git archaeology + per-cell comparison) — and renders it as HTML, while trace.reattest_lines renders the same model as the markdown brief. Split out of reattest_lines rather than reimplemented, because a second copy of the baseline derivation in a second module is the paraphrase-not-decompose failure this kit exists to prevent; the markdown output was proven byte-identical across the extraction. A cross-component import (Generators -> Traceability core) declared here rather than retagged: the model belongs with the registries it reads, and the renderer belongs with the generators.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-075.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-075)", "title": "IF-075", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-076": {"description": "trace_text.py module: the spine-row TEXT layer split out of trace.py (WI-329) - four PURE predicates (rows in, findings out, no I/O) plus the row primitives they share. provenance_findings(srs,llrs,tcs) and form_findings(srs,llrs,tcs) return gating findings that join trace's --strict exit_code; ac_advisories(srs) and paraphrase_advisories(srs,llrs) return warn-only advisories that never join a failure set; refs/is_example/is_draft are the shared row primitives, homed here as the lower layer so the dependency runs one way. Finding STRINGS are part of the contract: the three golden files assert them byte-for-byte, which is what makes the split provably behaviour-preserving.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-076.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-076)", "title": "IF-076", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-077": {"description": "check_trajectory resolves the ANCHOR half of an open WI's `doc#anchor` SpecRef THROUGH check_docs' markdown parser (parse_doc -> the doc's anchor set, which applies check_docs' slugifier), so ONE slugifier defines what an anchor is in both homes and the same reference can never pass as a SpecRef while failing as a link — the split that let WI-326 cite a truncated heading for two days. Lazy import that degrades to path-only when check_docs is absent, because this module runs in the SHIPPED pre-commit hook and a hard requirement there would block an adopter's every commit; the two scripts always ship + re-sync together (bootstrap MAPPING), which is the same ground IF-056 stands on. Declared at WI-354 when the cross-CMP rule surfaced the new CMP-001 -> CMP-003 edge.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-077.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-077)", "title": "IF-077", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-078": {"description": "sibling import for the registry write side: the filing-home resolution and write_spec_file(row) when file_selected_wis files selected Plan-WIs as docs/work/queued/ spec files - the folder half of IF-061's write seam; CSV mode byte-unchanged.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-078.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-078)", "title": "IF-078", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-079": {"description": "reads either registry FORM - the live docs/work/ spec folder and/or a legacy docs/requirements/work-items.csv being migrated - to convert between them (the CSV is interchange format only since Phase 5); --verify round-trips in a temp dir and never writes either live home.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-079.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-079)", "title": "IF-079", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-080": {"description": "integrate.py CLI (the local integration seam, concurrency-restructure SS1.2/SS2.3): `claim` performs the serial trunk claim (queued spec -> docs/work/active/<branch>/ in one bookkeeping commit, branch cut from it; refusal ladder: paused / dirty / branch-exists / unsafe-name / non-ordinary / off-frontier), `integrate` runs the serial fail-closed merge queue (--no-ff onto a candidate worktree, trunk step folded in, the DECLARED bar on the composed tree - missing/empty declaration or any SKIP refuses - and the RULING-7 verdict gate with git-derived freshness before the ff-only trunk advance), `audit` is the RULING-6 non-merge-product-commit window check.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-080.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-080)", "title": "IF-080", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-081": {"description": "trunk_step.py CLI (the serial trunk lane's shared-surface steps, concurrency-restructure SS5.1/SS5.2): --compile-log validates and appends docs/log.d/ fragments to docs/log.md in git add-time order (all-or-nothing, links rebased, fragments deleted); --regen re-derives the generated document families (arch-map, okf, derived-gate BEFORE the dashboards whose input it is, trajectory, status, open-items). Fail-loud: a red trunk lane halts claiming.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-081.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-081)", "title": "IF-081", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-082": {"description": "IF-056's derivation-loader seam, as HELD by the split sibling that now carries the import (WI-280): traj_parse.py is gen_trajectory's registry/doc/git source layer and reads the validator's loaders + path constants (read_rows/_split_refs/_norm_module/load_ifs/SR_CSV) directly. The CONTRACT is IF-056's, unchanged and stated once there; this row exists because a seam is declared per importing module and the import moved.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-082.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-082)", "title": "IF-082", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-083": {"description": "IF-056's derivation-loader seam, as HELD by the split sibling that now carries the import (WI-280): traj_views.py renders the What/When/How-SW views through the validator's joins (component_top_view/load_ifs/_norm_module/_split_refs/read_rows), so the picture and the right-sizing rule can never disagree. Contract: IF-056's.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-083.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-083)", "title": "IF-083", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-084": {"description": "IF-056's derivation-loader seam, as HELD by the split sibling that now carries the import (WI-280): traj_status.py derives the status snapshot + the pending-owner-actions projection through the validator's loaders (read_rows/read_registry_rows/load_wis/load_ifs). Contract: IF-056's.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-084.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-084)", "title": "IF-084", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-085": {"description": "IF-071's frontier DECISION seam, as HELD by the split sibling that now carries the import (WI-280): traj_parse.py is the guarded `import schedule` block's ONE home in this module family (traj_status's Ready-frontier block and traj_panels' Next-work card both read it as traj_parse.schedule), so the dashboard and status.md project the SAME dependency-ready ordering. Contract: IF-071's; OPTIONAL by construction — a scaffold without schedule.py degrades to an empty block.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-085.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-085)", "title": "IF-085", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-086": {"description": "check_figures.py CLI: flags a declared fig: figure marker that carries neither a non-empty cmd= plus rev= pair nor a non-empty derived= derivation, over root+docs markdown and docs/stack.ini; presence only, warn-first (exit 0), --strict exits 1 - wired as the opt-in [step:figures] G3 product step", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-086.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-086)", "title": "IF-086", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-087": {"description": "reuses doc_files (the root *.md + docs/**/*.md walk with the linguist-generated prefix skip) and authored_lines (the GENERATED-marker-block skip) - so the two doc checks agree on the scanned surface and the authored-lines rule instead of each declaring its own", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-087.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-087)", "title": "IF-087", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-088": {"description": "the dispatcher's exit-banner pending read (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A4, the 2026-08-01 amendment): _pending_cards imports the gen_trajectory FACADE (the gen_open_items shape) and reuses _blocked_pending + _spine_pending — the SAME pending_block(root) derivation the dashboard and open-items.html render — so the queue-drained / ratifications-waiting banners can never disagree with the owner surfaces about what is blocking. Deferred import, paid only at an exit banner; the tracked-pause card is excluded (the pause has its own earlier exit) and the coordinator's git-trailer reads stay for in-flight lanes only.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-088.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-088)", "title": "IF-088", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-089": {"description": "the empty-frontier gap census (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A4 ladder rung 1 / §A5.2): gap_census calls trace.load_registries + trace.analyze (phase unfiltered, require_verified on) and reports findings.orphans + findings.status_findings + the draft SN ids — the registry gaps trace already names, reused not re-derived. The list is the intake mint helper's handoff (it mints concrete gap-closure rows from it; the dispatcher itself never mints — no silent planner mints).", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-089.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-089)", "title": "IF-089", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-090": {"description": "the unified trunk-side intake mint (WI-388, R1/R3): intake_after_merge(root, before, after, outcomes, branch) mints the merge's forced rows (amendment adjudication, handback disposition, ## Dispositions drafts) as one bookkeeping commit inside the held slot; mint_gap_rows(root, census) is the dispatcher's rung-1 arm; flip_verified/adjudication_action enact ruled decision 2; context_block(root, wi_row) renders the advisory joins agent_loop's worker prompt consumes; all-or-nothing, idempotent by exact-title dedup (the sweep/census/adjudicate CLI is the recovery path)", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-090.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-090)", "title": "IF-090", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-091": {"description": "reads the WI-380 amendment seam as-is - staged_spine_amendments(root, before, after) per-row {ratified, traced} cell dicts with before/after over a rev pair - plus SPINE_CSVS for the flip's registry walk; only the routed traced subset (SN-Refs, Verifies, LLR SR-Refs per the WI-388 ruling) mints, the rest stays silent by ruling", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-091.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-091)", "title": "IF-091", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-092": {"description": "sibling import for the registry write side (the IF-078 shape): COLUMNS (the 18-column schema the minted row dict is keyed by) and write_spec_file(row) - the format's single writer, self-verifying at write - materialize every minted spec under docs/work/queued/; ConvertError is the mint's all-or-nothing refusal input", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-092.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-092)", "title": "IF-092", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-093": {"description": "sibling import for the station-cycle render's derived vocabulary (WI-389, the IF-092/IF-078 constants shape): OUTCOME_DIRS - the A3 terminal outcomes keyed by the spec directories that declare them, the same one-home read the merge slot classifies a finished lane by - draws the Process tab's outcome cards and their notes, and BAR_GREEN (the refresh attestation trailer label) the refresh note; constants only, no call and no write, so the dashboard's outcome vocabulary moves with the integrator instead of drifting as folklore", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-093.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-093)", "title": "IF-093", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-094": {"description": "sibling import for the station-cycle render's derived vocabulary (WI-389): _KIND_CONCURRENCY/_KIND_RANK/CONCURRENCY_EXCLUSIVE derive the exclusive-kind list the spine-barrier glyph and the escalation notes name, in rank order - the ruled A1/A8 tables read as constants; no call, no write", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-094.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-094)", "title": "IF-094", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-097": {"description": "The prompt LOADER seam (SN-032): `load(key, override)` returns one shipped template's text or raises a named `PromptError`; `fill(key, text, values)` refuses an unknown slot and a declared-but-unfilled slot rather than launching a silently empty instruction; `preflight(keys, overrides)` turns a missing template into a launch-time refusal instead of a first-session crash; `digest(text)` fingerprints a template over NORMALIZED line endings, so a CRLF checkout is not a change. `KIT_PROMPTS` is the declared key->file map and the only vocabulary a caller may use.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-097.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-097)", "title": "IF-097", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-098": {"description": "The generated catalogue reads `catalog_rows()` — `(key, file, slots, digest)` per shipped prompt — and nothing else, so the catalogue cannot disagree with the loader about what ships. `render()` is pure, which is what lets `--check` and the write path share one answer to 'what is current'.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-098.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-098)", "title": "IF-098", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-099": {"description": "The coordinator side of the prompt loader (SN-032, the IF-097 seam consumed): `agent_loop._kit_prompt` resolves WORKER/REVIEWER/CRITIQUE lazily and caches per key for the process lifetime — never at import, because a missing template must be a named PREFLIGHT refusal rather than an ImportError for every consumer of the module; `plan_briefs` reads the dual-plan hats the same way. Both fill with the single-brace `{slot}` vocabulary, which is also every operator override file's contract.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-099.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-099)", "title": "IF-099", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-100": {"description": "The dual-plan hats' side of the prompt loader (SN-032, the IF-097 seam consumed): `plan_briefs` loads the planner, critic and arbiter templates through `prompts.load`/`fill` with the same strict slot rules the session prompts take — an unknown slot and a declared slot left unfilled are both refusals, never a silently empty instruction to a planner.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-100.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-100)", "title": "IF-100", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-101": {"description": "reads the id watermark so a mint counts from the RECORD, not from the live tree - read_watermark(root) -> {space: int}, raising on an absent or malformed mark rather than degrading to zero, and bump_watermark(root) to raise the marks in the same commit that files the specs. next_wi_id mints max(watermark, live filenames) + 1. Shared rather than duplicated because the mint and the checker must agree on what an id is: a second copy of that rule is the defect the check exists to catch.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-101.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-101)", "title": "IF-101", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-102": {"description": "spine_carrier.py module: the requirement spine's registry CARRIER (OI-12 / repo-lock D-5). SPINE_TABLE maps an id column to its TOML tier table (SR-ID->requirement, LLR-ID->design, TC-ID->test); SPINE_COLUMN maps a carrier key to today's COLUMN NAME and is the exact inverse of migrate_carrier.KEY. rows_from_text(text, id_col, carrier) -> {id: row} under today's column names, or None when a TOML text does not parse (absent, never empty); rows_from_toml / rows_from_csv are the two arms. carriers(rel) -> both suffixed paths, for an applicability test that must span the cutover. resolve(path) -> the live carrier file, RAISING when both exist rather than resolving by precedence; load(path, id_col) -> rows in file order, the shape csv.DictReader returns, raising on a carrier that exists and will not parse. The `-000` example filter is NOT applied here - that is trace_text's rule.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-102.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-102)", "title": "IF-102", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-103": {"description": "migrate_carrier.py: the ONE-SHOT converter that moves the spine's four tiers from their two legacy carriers (stakeholder-needs.md prose tables, SR/LLR/TC CSV) to the TOML carrier spine_carrier reads. convert(root, write) -> (findings, written_paths): findings is EMPTY only when every cell survived a re-read of what was emitted, so the caller decides whether a lossy conversion is allowed to write. CLI: --check converts in memory and verifies the round-trip, writing nothing, exit 1 with a finding naming the registry, row id and field for each cell that did not survive; without --check it writes each .toml beside its source and STILL refuses on a lossy result. KEY is the column -> carrier-key map and the definition of record; spine_carrier.SPINE_COLUMN is its inverse, pinned by test_rule_sync.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-103.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-103)", "title": "IF-103", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-104": {"description": "reads the requirement spine through the CARRIER (IF-102) instead of csv.DictReader, so the Evidence-class pointer scan over TC.Evidence and LLR.Module/CodeSymbol/TestRefs answers whichever of TOML/CSV is live and the rows arrive under today's column names either way. Consumes spine_carrier.resolve/spine_carrier.load. resolve() is called first so an absent registry still SKIPS the tier cleanly rather than reporting every pointer in it as dangling - the pre-spine rung this checker has always tolerated.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-104.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-104)", "title": "IF-104", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-105": {"description": "reads the requirement spine through the CARRIER (IF-102) instead of csv.DictReader, so the known-id sets the runtime-flow citation check validates against answers whichever of TOML/CSV is live and the rows arrive under today's column names either way. Consumes spine_carrier.load. The one consumer that needs no column vocabulary at all: under TOML an id is the TABLE KEY, so this seam reads keys and nothing else.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-105.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-105)", "title": "IF-105", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-106": {"description": "reads the requirement spine through the CARRIER (IF-102) instead of csv.DictReader, so the SR/LLR/TC rows the OKF knowledge bundle exports answers whichever of TOML/CSV is live and the rows arrive under today's column names either way. Consumes spine_carrier.load. The bundle is generated and --check-gated, so a cell this seam failed to hand over would surface as bundle drift rather than silently - which is why the export reads through the carrier rather than keeping a private CSV reader.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-106.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-106)", "title": "IF-106", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-107": {"description": "reads the requirement spine through the CARRIER (IF-102) instead of csv.DictReader, so the SR/TC/LLR rows the release checklist groups by phase answers whichever of TOML/CSV is live and the rows arrive under today's column names either way. Consumes spine_carrier.load. Phase arrives as an INT from the TOML carrier and as a string from CSV; the carrier renders both to the same cell text, which is what keeps the checklist's grouping stable across the migration.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-107.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-107)", "title": "IF-107", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-108": {"description": "reads the requirement spine through the CARRIER (IF-102) instead of csv.DictReader, so the SR surface handed verbatim to an LLM planning brief answers whichever of TOML/CSV is live and the rows arrive under today's column names either way. Consumes spine_carrier.load. This seam feeds a MODEL, not a check: a cell the reader failed to map would not fail loudly, it would quietly narrow what the planner was told. That asymmetry is the reason the vocabulary has one home (repo-lock D-6) rather than a copy here.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-108.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-108)", "title": "IF-108", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-109": {"description": "reads the requirement spine through the CARRIER (IF-102) instead of csv.DictReader, so the SR rows behind the critique gate and the artifact recipe answers whichever of TOML/CSV is live and the rows arrive under today's column names either way. This seam feeds an LLM brief and a GATE at once: agent_loop reads SR Verification to decide whether a critique round is owed, and lifts SR/TC prose verbatim into the brief. A cell lost in translation would either skip a required round or narrow what the model was told - neither of which fails loudly.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-109.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-109)", "title": "IF-109", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-110": {"description": "reads the requirement spine through the CARRIER (IF-102) instead of csv.DictReader, so the LLR/TC rows the unified mint reads to place a new row answers whichever of TOML/CSV is live and the rows arrive under today's column names either way. The mint reads the spine to decide what already exists. It also WRITES Status back (the Modified->Verified flip), so this seam is the read half of a read-modify-write and the carrier must answer both consistently.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-110.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-110)", "title": "IF-110", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-111": {"description": "reads the requirement spine through the CARRIER (IF-102) instead of csv.DictReader, so the stakeholder-need rows the dashboard renders answers whichever of TOML/CSV is live and the rows arrive under today's column names either way. Reads the NEED tier specifically, through spine_carrier.folded_needs - the one home the edge-case fold now has. This module held one of the three copies that drifted and rendered a phantom SN-000 root in the icicle, which is why the seam is declared rather than left as an import.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-111.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-111)", "title": "IF-111", "type": "Interface"}, "IF-112": {"description": "reads the requirement spine through the CARRIER (IF-102): the stakeholder-need rows behind the README Must/Should coverage floor. Replaces a markdown table walk that discovered its `Priority` column by header text and tracked draft-ness by heading — both shapes the markdown carrier forced. The heading arm is the one that mattered: over a TOML file it finds no headings, so NO need reads as draft, and every draft need would be ADDED to a floor that gates the README.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "docs/okf/interfaces/IF-112.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/interfaces.csv (IF-112)", "title": "IF-112", "type": "Interface"}, "Interface": {"description": "107 Interface concept(s) — double-click (or focus and press Enter) to descend into them.", "fill": "#701a75", "href": "", "resource": "", "title": "", "type": "OKF type"}, "LLR-001": {"description": "Loads the four registries, computes the orphan set (SR without LLR/TC, LLR/TC without a parent, SN without an SR), and exits 1 under --strict.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-001.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-001)", "title": "SN->SR->LLR->TC join + orphan set", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-002": {"description": "Duplicate/malformed id detection per ID_PATTERNS, a per-row column-count check, and SR/LLR citation coherence (triangle_findings); the always-valid --strict-integrity floor.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-002.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-002)", "title": "Id + CSV-structure + citation integrity", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-003": {"description": "-000 example-row scan and required-field + closed-vocabulary validation over the real (non-placeholder) rows, plus the ratified-phase NUMERIC-ONLY rule (owner ruling 2026-08-01): once any spine row is phased (digit-parse arming), a non-Draft SR/LLR/TC whose Phase is not a full-cell bare integer is a --strict-schema finding (vacuous until armed; a prefixed v2/P1 now fails - the --phase/--ratify filters and the phase-drop join match the cell literally, so a prefix disarms them silently; phase_num stays digit-extract for grandfathering).", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-003.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-003)", "title": "Placeholder + schema checks", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-004": {"description": "Flags an AcceptanceCriteria comparative term with no predicate marker in the cell; warn-only, never joins the failure set.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-004.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-004)", "title": "Comparative-term advisory", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-005": {"description": "Integrity-checks the PB/REPO/PART/ASSET ids plus legacy MOD compatibility and resolves REPO/MOD DelegatedSRs against the SR set; -000 example rows ignored.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-005.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-005)", "title": "Off-spine integrity + back-links", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-006": {"description": "Builds the required-step plan per gate/tier and reports SKIP(missing) with a nonzero exit when a required tool is absent; the plan listing still names every step. The work-branch lane skip is LLR-141 (SR-133).", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-006.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-006)", "title": "Gate step plan + missing-tool guard", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-007": {"description": "Reads format/lint/test/tiers/coverage/arch-map from docs/stack.ini and resolves each step's command from that profile.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-007.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-007)", "title": "Stack profile loader", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-008": {"description": "Rejects a malformed profile, non-integer coverage threshold, or missing declared binary with a clear loud failure.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-008.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-008)", "title": "Profile validation", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-009": {"description": "Selects the file/skill set per stack+domain scope; omits Python-only artifacts and seeds files-mode arch-map for a non-Python stack.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-009.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-009)", "title": "Conditional profile seeding", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-010": {"description": "Writes the mapped kit files into --dest so the generated harness runs green out of the box.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-010.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-010)", "title": "Scaffold writer", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-011": {"description": "Skips an existing file unless --force.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-011.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-011)", "title": "Idempotent write + kit-version stamp", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-012": {"description": "Resolves intra-repo links, requires a PROJECT-VISION tag, and checks generated-doc freshness under --stale.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-012.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-012)", "title": "Link + vision-tag checker", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-013": {"description": "Validates an authored runtime-flow doc against its structural contract; a malformed flow fails.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-013.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-013)", "title": "Flow-doc contract checker", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-014": {"description": "Compares measured metrics to declared budgets and fails beyond tolerance at the row's block/warn level.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-014.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-014)", "title": "Budget comparator", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-015": {"description": "Resolves each PB row's Refs to a real SR/LLR/Module id; an empty or unresolvable Refs is a finding.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-015.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-015)", "title": "PB back-link resolution", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-016": {"description": "Walks the AST for stub bodies (pass-only/NotImplemented/ellipsis public functions) at the declared gate.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-016.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-016)", "title": "Stub marker detector", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-017": {"description": "Always-on scan of staged diff/message/outgoing range for PEM/token/key patterns, independent of the privacy toggle.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-017.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-017)", "title": "Secrets pattern scan", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-018": {"description": "Adds the PII/identity classes when docs/privacy-check is on, exempting configured author identities.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-018.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-018)", "title": "Two-axis privacy gating", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-019": {"description": "Runs trace --strict-integrity and the check_privacy secrets scan on staged content and blocks a failing commit.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-019.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-019)", "title": "Pre-commit floor", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-020": {"description": "Scans the outgoing commit range and blocks a push that would publish a secret or a private identity under privacy-check.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-020.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-020)", "title": "Pre-push outgoing scan", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-021": {"description": "Probes candidate interpreters by running one and skip-or-reports clearly when none works or a Store-alias exits nonzero.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-021.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-021)", "title": "Hook python probe", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-022": {"description": "Compares a vendored doc copy against its recorded upstream source and reports drift.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-022.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-022)", "title": "Vendored-doc drift check", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-023": {"description": "Regenerates the symbol map inside the BEGIN/END marker block and fails --check when the committed map has drifted.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-023.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-023)", "title": "Arch-map splice + drift check", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-024": {"description": "Parses the Permutations grammar (range/set/bool with @full|@pairwise|@boundaries) and emits the concrete case set.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-024.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-024)", "title": "Permutation expander", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-025": {"description": "Reads each SKILL.md applicability frontmatter and regenerates skills/INDEX.csv.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-025.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-025)", "title": "Skills index generator", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-026": {"description": "Runs the agent CLI headless: build_argv substitutes {model} into argv and, when a CmdTemplate carries no {prompt} placeholder (the default for both provider CLIs), routes the prompt to the child's STDIN (run_session writes it from a daemon thread then closes), while the no-wedge invariant holds — a fixed prompt plus EOF, never an interactive read. A codex session additionally captures that CLI's own --output-last-message file as the result (_codex_lastmsg_setup/_codex_lastmsg_read). stdin is otherwise closed so no prompt wedges the loop; the scope is the dispatcher and worker sessions, and the former status.md-resume read is retired rather than coexisting with them.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-026.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-026)", "title": "Headless session launch", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-027": {"description": "Refuses a non-git dir, a missing agent CLI, or a private author under privacy-check with a typed nonzero exit rather than hanging.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-027.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-027)", "title": "Preflight footing guard", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-028": {"description": "Ends each session in a typed code, guards a zero-commit HEAD, and reports an all-ERROR region as an unavailable agent, not a work stall.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-028.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-028)", "title": "Typed outcome + ERROR handling", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-029": {"description": "Takes an OS advisory lock the kernel releases on process death, so no stale-pid file wedges the next run.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-029.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-029)", "title": "Kernel advisory lock", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-030": {"description": "Refuses a second coordinator holding the same per-worktree lock, preventing a two-writer race.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-030.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-030)", "title": "Lock mutual exclusion", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-031": {"description": "The single first-non-comment-line reader the hooks, agent_loop.read_declared, and check_privacy all use so a declared policy value is read one way.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-031.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-031)", "title": "Shared declared-line parse", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-032": {"description": "The onboarding and dev-setup scaffold scripts that materialize and run to a green developer setup.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-032.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-032)", "title": "Onboard/dev-setup templates", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-033": {"description": "Emits docs/release-checklist.md, including a Performance-budgets section listing each warn-tier PB (id + allocation) read from the budgets registry.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-033.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-033)", "title": "Release checklist generator", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-034": {"description": "Loads the WI registry - the docs/work/ spec folder, or a legacy work-items.csv via dual-read (folder wins when it holds real specs; both-present is an integrity error) -; validates WI ids, predecessor resolution, and hard-edge acyclicity; respects the absent/placeholder/opt-out vacuity rules.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-034.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-034)", "title": "WI dependency-DAG validation", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-035": {"description": "Renders one self-contained root HTML file with completeness tiles, the spine hierarchy, and the WI roadmap DAG.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-035.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-035)", "title": "Offline project-state core", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-036": {"description": "Tokenizes each source file (stdlib tokenize; comments/NL/indent excluded, NEWLINE kept so windows cannot stitch unrelated statements), slides a --min-tokens window (default 30), groups candidate hits by line offset, and merges only step-1-overlapping token-window offsets into one finding per block, POSIX-normalizes the file pair, and fingerprints each block (a hash of its complete token signature, line-number-free). A finding is suppressed only when its fingerprint+pair is recorded in docs/dupes-allow (count-aware: N lines sanction N identical copies) or a legacy bare pair matches; --emit-census prints the lines.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-036.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-036)", "title": "Duplicate-block detector", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-037": {"description": "cmd_map parsed by parse_map (same KEY=value syntax; templates may not contain , or ; — the dispatcher wrapper covers those); session_template(phase) falls back to AGENT_CMD; every map entry preflighted (parse + executable); review-policy read via read_declared and printed in the banner; status_size_warning is pure and warn-only (limit<=0 disables).", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-037.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-037)", "title": "Per-phase cmd map + size tripwire", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-038": {"description": "Scans root *.md + docs/**/*.md; a backticked token is path-shaped when it carries a separator plus a known extension or top-level prefix (URLs/globs/placeholders excluded); GENERATED marker blocks and path-ok lines are skipped; sym:<module>.<name> is resolved against the module map parsed from architecture.md (module tail -> public symbols); warn-first, --strict exits 1.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-038.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-038)", "title": "Path + sym: reference checker", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-039": {"description": "emit() builds {relpath: content} for every real row (sorted, deterministic, no timestamps): frontmatter via JSON-quoted YAML scalars, bodies carrying the row prose plus up/down markdown links from locally built reverse maps, per-tier and root indexes, the UPSTREAM.md spec pin; on_disk() snapshots docs/okf for the byte-compare; write mode writes-if-changed and prunes; small loaders duplicated per the F5 rule (no new sibling import). Layer B2 adds a process-guides tier: _doc_title_and_summary derives each guide title+summary from the doc first heading/paragraph (inline links stripped so nothing dangles), emitted only after the vacuity gate so a fresh scaffold stays vacuous.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-039.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-039)", "title": "OKF bundle emitter", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-040": {"description": "Pure decide(tool_name, policy, override) -> allow|ask|deny|defer over SPAWN_TOOLS={Task,Agent}; main reads the PreToolUse JSON payload, reads docs/subagent-gate via the shared declared-line parse, applies the SUBAGENT_GATE=allow override, emits permissionDecision (deny -> exit 2), and appends each decision to docs/subagent-gate.log; any exception fails OPEN (allow) and logs, so a broken gate never wedges the agent's tools.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-040.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-040)", "title": "Subagent spawn gate", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-041": {"description": "Loads interfaces.csv; validates IF ids, SR back-links and Component tags, and emits the best-effort endpoint-module advisory.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-041.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-041)", "title": "IF-tier integrity", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-042": {"description": "Emits opt-out warn-first findings for missing endpoints/directions, uncited Active seams, and dangling Contracts citations without changing exit status.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-042.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-042)", "title": "Architecture-connectivity coverage warns", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-043": {"description": "gen_skills_index.check_agent_sync byte-compares each per-agent <agent>/skills/<name>/ file (.claude/.gemini/.agents) to the neutral project-trajectory/skills/<name>/ source; a copy that diverges is a --check-agents finding with a one-command fix, an orphan copy (no source) a non-failing warn, a source skill missing from a subset dir no finding. Vacuous without a source or per-agent dir; wired as the check.py skills-sync step + pre-commit floor (kit-only generator, so a scaffold no-ops). bootstrap.sync_agent_skills (--sync) force-refreshes only the <agent>/skills/<name>/ subtree from source.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-043.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-043)", "title": "Cross-agent skill fan-out drift check", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-044": {"description": "Parses pair rows and enable tokens; resolves versions; merges Env; selects by tier, family, cooldown, and order with legacy Provider compatibility.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-044.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-044)", "title": "Agent pair-row routing", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-045": {"description": "Schedules review-policy sessions in managed mode, constructs redacted prompt-map briefs, parses verdicts, logs selections, and preserves unmanaged behavior.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-045.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-045)", "title": "Managed review dispatch", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-046": {"description": "Computes advisory substance components and decay history; treats tripwires as gates and never rewards length.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-046.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-046)", "title": "Review substance scorer", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-047": {"description": "load_capabilities parses the docs/stack.ini [run] section (configparser, interpolation=None, case-preserving optionxform; declaration order kept; a name.desc row attaches to its command and an orphan or empty-value command is dropped) into an ordered (name, command, desc) list; --list prints name<TAB>desc per row; direct(name) launches by exact-match name (exit 2 on an unknown name) and launch() runs the shell line via subprocess shell=True, passing the child exit code straight through; interactive_menu prints the numbered menu, reads a pick from stdin, and returns EOF-safe without hanging; an absent or empty [run] prints guidance and exits 1. The run.* launchers are thin delegates forwarding their args to run_menu.py.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-047.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-047)", "title": "Run capability menu reader", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-048": {"description": "Detects Critique-scoped builds and assembles a fresh family-heterogeneous rubric/intent/artifact brief with no self-assessment.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-048.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-048)", "title": "Independent critique dispatch", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-049": {"description": "Derives component roots and uncontained modules; enforces TOP_VIEW_MAX with nesting, opt-out, and vacuity rules.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-049.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-049)", "title": "Bounded software top view", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-050": {"description": "Per-artifact gate rules (sr_gate/maturity_gate/sn_gate) + min-aggregation over in-scope artifacts + the cached docs/gate (basis line + --check rot guard, legacy value-only); the derived current phase (max over ratified rows via phase_num) rides the basis line and --print, the phase analogue of the derived gate. is_modified is recognized (F5-mirrored from trace.py, pinned by test_rule_sync) and counted across SR/LLR/TC onto the compared basis line as modified=N beside drafts=N - a surfacing count only, no gate arithmetic (a Modified SR reads G2 through the existing decomposed-unverified rung of sr_gate; maturity_gate stays Draft-only so a Modified child never caps).", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-050.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-050)", "title": "Derived-gate computation + hybrid cache", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-051": {"description": "Renders the Process tab as a fully self-contained conditional panel registered in build_html (the Knowledge-tab pattern: nav button + section id=process): (1) the artifact-lifecycle x gates panel derived from the live registries + docs/gate (current-gate highlight; per-tier counts from the spine), (2) the resume-loop panel (the managed agent_loop flow with its escalation edges), (3) the slices -> phase -> gates panel derived from the WI registry (docs/work/); links out to the process masters wherever a canonical home exists (bounded in-view restatement only where no single doc states the relationship); sorted inputs, no clocks; a data-less repo renders byte-identically; --check unchanged (the trajectory-map step).", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-051.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-051)", "title": "Process reference tab", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-052": {"description": "Builds phase/workstream/WI tiers by threshold, surfaces delivery Phase, and aggregates parent edges from deduplicated child edges.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-052.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-052)", "title": "Tiered When roadmap", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-055": {"description": "Adjudicated against the usability rubric (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md) by the SR-084/SR-085 critique loop: labels stay readable at default zoom with no clipped or overlapping text, and the reading experience the rubric's remaining anchors describe. Byte-deterministic; --check unchanged. This row keeps ONLY what a test cannot pin: the mechanized cores have left for child rows that name the tests owning them — T2 start-collapse (LLR-099/TC-102), T3 breadcrumb-restores-parent (LLR-100/TC-103, the whole anchor, no residue here) and T1 labelled entry (LLR-115/TC-120, the whole anchor, no residue here). Do NOT restate a child's claim here: an anchor lives in exactly one row.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-055.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-055)", "title": "Dashboard usability rendering", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-056": {"description": "Extends SR-050's process_panel with the project's two circular working loops as linked flow panels: (A) the intake loop - owner/agent intake -> triage into work items with spec detail -> the scheduler-derived ready frontier in the WI registry (docs/work/) -> build/review -> merge; (B) the human-decision loop - open-items population (incl. the gate-ratification table) -> human review and ruling -> the log's Decisions record -> merge - with the LLM_Agent entry node rendered once and shared by both loops. Each stage links to its canonical home (status.md, the WI registry (docs/work/), open-items.html, log.md); data-derived where a canonical source exists; a data-less repo renders byte-identically; --check unchanged (the trajectory-map step).", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-056.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-056)", "title": "Process tab intake + human-decision loop panels", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-057": {"description": "Polishes the tiered decomposition render: each tier column is sized no wider than its content within a declared bound (a per-tier max-width cap MAX_TIER_COL, not the current uniform column); one horizontal parent->child arrow is drawn per containment edge so containment is explicit rather than implied by nesting alone; and the hover/focus highlight persists on the last-hovered item until another takes it (a stateful highlight keyed to the last-hovered node id, killing the flash-on-exit). Self-contained, byte-deterministic; --check unchanged. Composes with SR-089's tiering without changing its >3 thresholds.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-057.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-057)", "title": "Decomposition render polish (columns, arrows, persistent hover)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-058": {"description": "Derives the dependency-ready frontier from the WI registry + dispatcher reservations (never prose), excludes blocked/deferred/reserved/protected-/exclusive-conflicting WIs with reason codes, and exposes ready --explain / --format json and simulate --jobs N.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-058.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-058)", "title": "WI-DAG frontier + deterministic traincar ordering", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-059": {"description": "Maps declared safety and policy inputs to one scheduling class and deterministic reason codes without side effects.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-059.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-059)", "title": "Pure safety classifier", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-060": {"description": "next-wi/run-phase are gone from every surface; the status snapshot is generated by gen_trajectory --status and re-derived by the trunk lane (trunk_step --regen) after each merge - never hand-copied, never written on a work branch. (The run-state generation half retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5.)", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-060.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-060)", "title": "next-wi/run-phase retirement + generated status/run-state", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-061": {"description": "Replaces internal --track assumptions with explicit --wi/--train/worktree assignment; assembles the worker prompt from AGENTS.md + WI row + SpecRef + predecessor context + train diff + rework finding (never status.md/next-wi); collision-safe per-assignment logs/review evidence naming the exact reviewed commit; the --track lane machinery is retired outright.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-061.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-061)", "title": "Explicit WI/train/worktree worker assignment", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-067": {"description": "Joins imports, component membership, and declared seams; reports uncovered cross-component edges with strict/plain and vacuity rules.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-067.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-067)", "title": "Cross-component import coverage", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-068": {"description": "Validates armed spec interface ids, Proposed rationale, and the intra-module escape with warn-first/strict severity.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-068.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-068)", "title": "Spec interface-section validation", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-069": {"description": "Parses rival plan tables; resolves clause/SR/IF coverage; validates dependencies; emits pairwise differences and distinct findings/malformed/absent-registry outcomes.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-069.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-069)", "title": "Rival-plan coverage comparison", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-070": {"description": "Implements typed round steps, spend and action caps, position-swapped agreement, persistence, and PAGE mapping.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-070.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-070)", "title": "Typed dual-plan round lifecycle", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-071": {"description": "Builds planner/critic/arbiter briefs only from SR/IF registries with strict template slots and override/fallback loading.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-071.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-071)", "title": "Allowlist-only dual-plan briefs", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-072": {"description": "Selects two fresh planner sessions across families where possible and applies the bounded nonresponse fallback with reason codes.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-072.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-072)", "title": "Dual-plan planner pair and fallback", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-073": {"description": "Converts coverage CLI results and report payload into clean, implicated-repair, repeated-failure, or malformed round records.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-073.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-073)", "title": "Coverage-step result adapter", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-074": {"description": "Allocates stable round/stage artifacts and appends registry-valid selected child WIs with mapped predecessors and preserved line endings.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-074.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-074)", "title": "Dual-plan artifact persistence and filer", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-075": {"description": "Finds done WI tokens only in hand-edited status.md, with plain/strict severity, and stands down for the generated marker, open/unknown ids, opt-out, and absent status.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-075.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-075)", "title": "Generated-status source-of-truth guard", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-076": {"description": "Runs planners, cross-critiques, bounded revisions and position-swapped arbiters as fresh sessions; selects only true-plan agreement.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-076.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-076)", "title": "Fresh-session dual-plan runner", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-077": {"description": "Enforces Deliverable iff done on every run and resolvable open-WI SpecRef under strict mode.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-077.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-077)", "title": "WI row coherence", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-078": {"description": "Renders software/component and OKF views only when their source bundles exist; omits each otherwise.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-078.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-078)", "title": "Conditional architecture and knowledge views", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-079": {"description": "Produces deterministic responsive HTML.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-079.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-079)", "title": "Deterministic responsive dashboard freshness", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-080": {"description": "Renders IF-labeled seams in dashboard and architecture views with deterministic table fallback when absent.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-080.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-080)", "title": "Declared-interface graph rendering", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-081": {"description": "Applies win-stay/lose-shift family swap, tier escalation, and ratification-level-keyed paging for shared failure, contradiction, and tripwires.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-081.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-081)", "title": "Fixed review escalation", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-082": {"description": "Loops CHANGES-REQUESTED through rework and fresh critique within AGENT_CRITIQUE_MAX, then pages via gate policy.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-082.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-082)", "title": "Bounded critique rework", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-083": {"description": "Accepts Critique as a verification value while retaining the LLR completeness requirement.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-083.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-083)", "title": "Critique verification vocabulary", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-084": {"description": "Warns when a Critique-scoped WI closes after CHANGES-REQUESTED with no TC, test, or rubric delta.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-084.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-084)", "title": "Critique closure ratchet", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-085": {"description": "Renders nested component membership and boundary-aggregated seams, falling back byte-identically to the flat view without containment.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-085.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-085)", "title": "Containerized How-SW rendering", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-086": {"description": "Tiers component containment to modules and bounds the visible top level by TOP_VIEW_MAX.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-086.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-086)", "title": "Tiered How-SW hierarchy", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-087": {"description": "Attaches IF seams to visible input/output ports and aggregates cross-container seams at boundaries.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-087.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-087)", "title": "Hierarchy seam ports", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-088": {"description": "Handles pointer and keyboard one-layer descent and breadcrumb restoration in self-contained views.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-088.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-088)", "title": "One-layer hierarchy navigation", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-089": {"description": "Cross-checks declarations against spine, critique, checkpoint, and dual-plan evidence; quarantines only the contradictory WI.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-089.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-089)", "title": "Structural safety cross-check", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-095": {"description": "Rejects dual rows on the direct worker path.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-095.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-095)", "title": "Dual-plan path isolation", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-096": {"description": "Commits SELECT or PAGE as one docs-only disposition and regenerates validation.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-096.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-096)", "title": "Serialized dual-plan disposition", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-097": {"description": "Emits R-F findings - done WI with non-empty SpecRef; live docs/specs file (README/-000 excluded, the spec_interface_findings idiom) with no open-WI citer - message-only, riding main's warn-plain/error-under-strict tier with no new branch.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-097.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-097)", "title": "Spec-lifecycle close-side findings", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-098": {"description": "Reads the docs/coverage-floors census (module + min percent; blank and # lines ignored) and coverage.py's JSON report (per-file percent_covered, path-normalized), fails on any module below its floor or absent from the report, SKIPs when the report is absent, is a no-op with an empty census, and fails loudly on a malformed floor line.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-098.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-098)", "title": "Per-module coverage-floor comparator", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-099": {"description": "The mechanized core of usability anchor T2 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md): a view whose scale EARNS tiering — a bundle spanning more than 3 OKF types, a module set above the component threshold (the SR-089 '>3' rule) — renders START-COLLAPSED as the shared type-tiered drill, one block per type or container, rather than a flat wall of nodes; at or below the threshold it stays flat. Covers the Knowledge, How-SW and landing What views; the What icicle start-collapses to one block per SN with descend-on-click, reusing _drill_layer_svg/_render_drill so the interaction idiom is single-sourced. SCOPE: T2's remaining 'reads legibly at default density' judgement stays perceptual under LLR-055/TC-055.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-099.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-099)", "title": "Default-density start-collapse (T2 core)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-100": {"description": "The mechanized core of usability anchor T3 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md): descending a container never replaces the view with no way back — the drill emits a declared breadcrumb nav (<nav class=\"crumbs\"> with a data-root-crumb root label) whose crumb click TRUNCATES the trail to that ancestor, restoring the parent view. SCOPE: the WHOLE anchor — the breadcrumb's presence AND its restore behaviour; T3 is retired as a critique anchor and no residue is left under LLR-055/TC-055.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-100.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-100)", "title": "Detail-in-context breadcrumb (T3 core)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-101": {"description": "The mechanized core of accessibility anchor A2 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-accessibility.md): no emitted <svg> declares the CHILDREN-PRESENTATIONAL role=img over a FOCUSABLE descendant, and any svg that does declare role=img carries its own accessible name (aria-label, or a first-direct-child <title> — per SVG-AAM a <title> nested in a <g> names the <g>, not the svg). Focusable means tabindex in any quoting OR a native SVG <a href>, which is tab-ordered without a tabindex. The role is chosen from the emitted BODY by _svg_role. SCOPE — and no wider: the container-role invariant as PROVEN by TC-104, over the artifact this repo ships plus the tiered fixture plus the predicate's own unit cases; it is NOT a proof over every reachable emitter configuration, so an emitter no rendered artifact exercises is unguarded. Name QUALITY belongs to LLR-108/TC-113.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-101.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-101)", "title": "Non-pruning SVG container roles (A2 core)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-102": {"description": "The mechanized core of uniformity anchor U5 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-uniformity.md): no hex value is reused across STATUS_FILL/TIER_FILL/OKF_TYPE_FILL/SW_NODE_FILL/PHASE_ACCENTS for an unrelated concept (the intentional TIER_FILL<->OKF_TYPE_FILL SN/SR/LLR/TC mirror is the one exception, asserted as a bijection rather than a set match), no PHASE_ACCENTS entry equals the CSS --accent token, and PHASE_ACCENTS clears a PAIRWISE (not merely adjacent) deltaE floor of 15. SCOPE: hex-identity and deltaE across these five declared palettes; the near-duplicate residue across vocabularies belongs to LLR-109/TC-114.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-102.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-102)", "title": "One-concept-per-colour de-collision (U5 core)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-103": {"description": "The mechanized core of uniformity anchor U3 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-uniformity.md): the hover/focus highlight ring resolves to ONE shared per-fill `--ring` custom property across every SVG emitter (drill/icicle/flat-DAG/knowledge) rather than a different hue per emitter, and the When tab's phase-accent key renders through the SAME `.legend`/`<i>` component every other legend in the document uses rather than a bespoke inline-chip idiom. SCOPE: highlight-token equality and legend-component equality, asserted structurally against the emitted CSS and markup. Spacing and exact visual weight belong to LLR-110/TC-115.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-103.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-103)", "title": "Uniform node/edge/legend/highlight styling (U3 core)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-104": {"description": "The mechanized core of uniformity anchor U1 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-uniformity.md): the Process tab's working-loops SVG no longer hardcodes 12px/9.5px/13px/13px on .stgt/.stgn/.hooplab/.hubname. .stgt/.stgn are the same per-node-label role as the shared --nlabel/--nsub tokens the icicle, dag and knowledge views already share, and reuse them directly; .hooplab/.hubname are a once-per-diagram headline label, a genuinely different role, so they take the one documented --nhead scale step. SCOPE: these four selectors resolve to a declared token, asserted structurally against the emitted CSS. Whether the sizes read as uniform belongs to LLR-111/TC-116.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-104.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-104)", "title": "Process-tab node-label type scale (U1 core)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-105": {"description": "The mechanized core of usability anchor T5 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md): EVERY control painted over a node's own fill clears the WCAG 3:1 UI-boundary floor against EVERY node fill declared in STATUS_FILL/TIER_FILL/OKF_TYPE_FILL/SW_NODE_FILL/PHASE_ACCENTS, in BOTH themes — computed per fill (white or near-black, whichever contrasts more), never a single fixed hue. Covers the descend/hover/focus ring AND the containment/descend arrow (.cedge shaft plus its marker head), which derives from the same per-fill --ring token; its marker head takes a per-ink marker because marker content is rendered from the defs tree and cannot see the referencing node's custom properties (a constraint recorded in _arrow_markers), and RING_INKS is the closed ink set that makes that emission total. SCOPE: a control's contrast against its host fill, arithmetic like the sibling A4 tests. Whether the affordance otherwise reads as inviting stays perceptual under LLR-055/TC-055.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-105.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-105)", "title": "Interactive-control contrast in both themes (T5 core)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-106": {"description": "The mechanized core of uniformity anchor U2 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-uniformity.md). Every concept's fill is LOOKED UP from a declared module-level palette, so the same concept cannot render two hexes — there is only one place a hex can come from. Concretely: every #rrggbb appearing in a rendered dashboard is either a member of a declared palette collection or a `--token:#hex` theme definition (surfaces, text and borders, which encode no concept). SCOPE: this proves single-sourcing of the VOCABULARY, not that a concept was assigned a well-chosen hue — collision and perceptual separation belong to LLR-102.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-106.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-106)", "title": "Single-sourced concept colour vocabulary (U2 core)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-107": {"description": "The mechanized core of uniformity anchor U4 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-uniformity.md). Two node roles exist and each is spelled ONE way in every emitter that renders it: an IDENTIFIED node (icicle .cell, flat-DAG .wi, knowledge .knode) advertises its registry id through `data-id`; a DETAIL-BEARING drill node (.block, in both the When and How-SW drills) advertises its detail-map key through `data-node`; and a node of either role is made focusable the same way, `tabindex=\"0\"` on the group. Neither role ever carries the other's attribute. SCOPE: ATTRIBUTE parity, not runtime behaviour parity.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-107.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-107)", "title": "One interaction idiom per node role (U4 core)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-108": {"description": "The QUALITY half of accessibility anchor A2, mechanizing what LLR-101 leaves perceptual. Three rules over every focusable element and role=img graphic: it HAS an accessible name; that name is never a bare registry id; and navigation LANDMARKS are distinct. SCOPE, narrower than a first measurement suggests: A2 governs INTERACTIVE elements and meaningful graphics — a <title> on a decorative edge path is a tooltip, not an accessible name. Uniqueness is asserted for landmarks ONLY: a descend control for the same container legitimately recurs across drill layers, one visible at a time, so those repeats are one control reached by different paths. Whether a well-formed, unique, descriptive name is the RIGHT description belongs to SR-054's reading-task critique.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-108.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-108)", "title": "Accessible-name quality, not merely presence (A2 residue)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-109": {"description": "Mechanizes what LLR-102 leaves perceptual: a PAIRWISE deltaE floor across every declared vocabulary, not only within PHASE_ACCENTS. Two floors, each a recorded judgement — 15 WITHIN a vocabulary (every member can sit beside every other in one legend) and 12 ACROSS (two vocabularies share no legend, so the floor is lower rather than absent). The declared tier<->okf mirror is exempt BY DESIGN and is asserted BYTE-EQUAL, so 'exempt' cannot drift into 'nearly equal'. SCOPE: perceptual SEPARATION of the declared palettes. Whether a given hue suits its concept is not asserted.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-109.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-109)", "title": "Pairwise deltaE floor across every vocabulary (U5 residue)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-110": {"description": "Mechanizes what LLR-103 leaves perceptual. Fourteen CSS tokens plus SVG_RX name one step per role for stroke-width, opacity and corner radius, near-duplicates merged into the role's step, and every emitted value must resolve to a declared token. stroke-opacity is checked under the opacity scale deliberately — it is the same scale applied to a stroke. SVG_RX is enforced against both the source literals and every render, because the rx presentation attribute cannot read a CSS custom property portably. SCOPE: token membership, asserted over paint surfaces (style blocks and style/fill/stroke attributes), never over prose that quotes CSS.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-110.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-110)", "title": "Declared weight/alpha/corner tokens (U3 residue)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-111": {"description": "Mechanizes what LLR-104 left perceptual. 'Do the sizes read as visually uniform' was undecidable because the scale was never written down: 18 raw font-size literals against 5 tokens, with .7/.75rem, .9/.95/.98rem, 1.05/1.1rem and 8.5/9px each being near-duplicate steps for ONE role 3-7% apart. Eleven steps are now declared in THREE documented families - node (px, because SVG geometry is fixed px and a rem resizes labels out of their boxes), page (rem), and one relative step (em, for text sized against its parent) - and every emitted font-size must resolve to one. Claiming a single scale across those units would be false, so the LLR states three. SCOPE: step membership over paint surfaces. Whether the chosen steps are well-proportioned is not asserted.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-111.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-111)", "title": "Declared type scale, no raw font-size literals (U1 residue)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-112": {"description": "The mechanized core of accessibility anchor A1 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-accessibility.md): every element the emitted page WIRES an interaction to is keyboard-focusable. The wired set is DERIVED from the document's own DOM-selecting calls and then classified — a control selector (its matches receive per-element click/focus/dblclick/keydown listeners) may match only elements carrying tabindex=0 or natively tab-ordered (a button; an SVG a-with-href, the LLR-101 predicate), and each non-control selector records why its matches are not focus stops. A newly wired selector fails the owning test until classified. The derivation reads EVERY DOM-selecting API (querySelector/querySelectorAll/closest/matches, any quote style) and fails loudly on a non-literal selector argument, which a static read cannot classify. A control-selector wiring loop that attaches click or dblclick must attach focus or keydown beside it, statically asserted. Order-integrity: document order IS emission order, no positive tabindex may reorder the walk, and tabindex=-1 is legal only on the roving tablist buttons. SCOPE — and no wider: focusability, order-integrity and activation PAIRING of the wired set over the shipped artifact plus the seven-fixture emitter sweep. Runtime behaviour equivalence is not driven, and whether the order FEELS sensible stays a reader-experience judgement. Runtime-created controls (the breadcrumb buttons) are native <button>s, keyboard-operable by construction and outside the static walk — a recorded limit.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-112.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-112)", "title": "Keyboard reachability of every wired control (A1 core)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-113": {"description": "The mechanized core of accessibility anchor A3: for every declared colour vocabulary (enumerated from the module constants, the U5/U2 closure, so a new vocabulary enters by existing), every member that actually PAINTS in a rendered document resolves to a legend swatch labelled in words in that same document, with CSS tokens resolved. Status members additionally pair a per-status glyph. The emitted JS detail-badge maps are SUBSTITUTED from TIER_FILL/STATUS_FILL rather than hand-copied, and three drift guards hold every copy equal to the constants: the emitted JS maps byte-match the Python palettes; the CSS status tokens match STATUS_FILL and are asserted theme-invariant; and the tier-legend bijection derives its expectation from the TIER_FILL dict, so a member dropped from the legend also fails. SCOPE and its narrowing: the worded cue must exist in the same document — that it sits within eyeshot of the painted element is layout this does not re-assert. The JS detail-panel badge stays out of the LEGEND rule because its background carries its concept as the badge's own visible text.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-113.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-113)", "title": "No information by colour alone (A3 core)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-114": {"description": "Accessibility anchor A4 bound. The WCAG 2.1 AA arithmetic is owned by six sibling tests — node fills vs label ink at 4.5:1, the emitted badge/boundary pairs, the no-opacity-discount rule, theme-token fills behind white text in BOTH themes, the hub-is-not-accent regression, and the per-fill focus ring at the 3:1 boundary floor — so this row adds the BINDING plus the gap none of them owned: the SET. Closed by reflection: every UPPERCASE module-level collection holding hex members must be one of the five declared vocabularies, and every member of the REFLECTED set clears 4.5:1 against its label ink (white, except the one declared queued dark-ink exception) and 3:1 for its computed focus ring. The walk is RECURSIVE (nested dicts, tuples, sets, bare strings), counts any 3/4/6/8-digit hex form as hex-bearing, and requires every member be CANONICAL 6-digit lowercase. The theme-token half is DERIVED from the emitted CSS: every fill:var(--x) token must carry a declared role, white-text-fill roles clear 4.5:1 under white in BOTH themes, container-fill roles keep --text readable in both. SCOPE — and no wider: contrast arithmetic over declared constants and emitted theme tokens (the ring boundary also binds under SR-054 T5 as LLR-105); a lowercase module-level palette would evade the reflection and is forbidden by the module's UPPERCASE-constant convention, a recorded reliance. Rendered-pixel measurement is not asserted.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-114.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-114)", "title": "Text-contrast floors, closed over the vocabulary set (A4)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-115": {"description": "The mechanized core of usability anchor T1 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md). Each of the three core reading tasks the rubric concretizes — find the project state, find the next work, find how the parts connect — is reachable within one tab switch of the landing view, its entry point a LABELLED nav control or a NAMED surface on the landing view, never an unlabelled region a reader must discover by clicking. THE DESIGN CONSTRAINT that makes the check honest: it is judged against the RENDERED artifact carrying real registry data, NEVER the nav skeleton. The guard resolves each task's entry point from the emitted document (task 1 -> the landing Definition-completeness/Execution cards, 0 switches; task 2 -> the landing Next-work card, 0 switches; task 3 -> the How-SW nav tab, 1 switch), asserts each is LABELLED and within one switch, and asserts the next-work surface is NON-EMPTY on the zero-active/all-queued fixture. SCOPE — PROVEN: a labelled entry point per task, reachable in one switch, and the next-work surface non-empty across the practice fixtures (zero-active, waiting-with-predecessor, drained). DELIBERATELY NOT: whether the reader subjectively knows what to do next; T1 leaves the critique entirely, with no residue under LLR-055/TC-055.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-115.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-115)", "title": "Task findability - labelled entry within one tab switch (T1 core)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-116": {"description": "The mechanized core of usability anchor T7 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md). Every emitted SVG (all three wrappers: _svg_wrap for dag/sw/know, the icicle panel, _drill_layer_svg) carries width:100% + max-width:<natural> + min-width:<natural x SHRINK_FLOOR> instead of a FIXED pixel width, so a diagram fills its container and scales down as the viewport narrows. SCOPE and its deliberate narrowing: a view whose natural width exceeds 390 / SHRINK_FLOOR still scrolls horizontally WITH its cue — the row's own fallback rule made mechanical. Closing that remainder needs a narrow-width re-layout (stacked columns), which is not this row.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-116.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-116)", "title": "Viewport-fit by scale-to-fit with a legibility floor (T7 core)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-117": {"description": "The mechanized core of usability anchor T6 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md). The measurable content is FAMILY PAIRING, not the absence of literals: a fixed status swatch carrying fixed white ink is theme-locked CORRECTLY, since neither side flips. A page inverts mid-view in exactly two ways and this row forbids both. (1) A SECOND THEME MECHANISM below :root — the document declares exactly one prefers-color-scheme block, targeting :root, and one color-scheme, also at :root. (2) A MIXED PAIR — a theme-varying ink on a theme-invariant surface, or the reverse. The two families are DERIVED from the emitted document (the dark block's override set IS the definition of theme-varying), never hand-listed, and the dark block may not override a token :root never declares. Applied to CSS rules declaring both a background and an ink, to every emitted SVG node pairing an inline rect fill with an inline text fill, and to CSS-declared SVG text fills paired against the family their host nodes actually paint in. SCOPE and its deliberate narrowings: --ring is excluded because it is stamped FROM the host fill (LLR-105 owns its floor); the JS-composed badge idiom pairs background and ink at runtime from the invariant vocabulary, where LLR-114's arithmetic owns it; and the page CSS has exactly ONE rule declaring a classifiable background AND ink today (body), so that half is prospective while the sweep's weight sits on the node and text-fill halves. Whether the chosen palettes are PLEASANT stays perceptual.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-117.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-117)", "title": "Theme-lock: one theme mechanism and no mixed-family surface/ink pair (T6 core)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-118": {"description": "The RENDERED half of the Modified/Draft attestation regime whose gate SR-049 derives. gen_open_items renders (a) every pending row of docs/requirements/open-items.csv as a decision brief and (b) every SR whose Status is Draft (ratification owed) or Modified (re-attest owed) with its whole chain's per-cell before/after. Two contracts the emitted file carries: the attestation-baseline STAMP (a --since render must be reproducible by the freshness gate, or --check compares against a different history) and the check-the-baseline empty state (an auto-derived baseline sitting AFTER a pre-regime amendment renders no cells, which must never read as nothing-changed). The machine-local advisory region is masked before comparing. SCOPE and its deliberate narrowings: this module RENDERS and owns no second opinion — the attestation model is trace.reattest_model and the pending pointers are gen_trajectory.pending_block, both imported, so a disagreement with `trace.py --ratify` means this view is the defect and the brief wins. NOT claimed: that the rendered page is legible or well-composed.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-118.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-118)", "title": "Generated owner decision surface (registry + attestation depth)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-119": {"description": "The mechanized floor of usability anchor T4 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md), widening the anchor's existing owner rather than adding a rubric anchor. The clause made objective: no character a block renders may fall outside that block's own rect, horizontally or vertically. The emitter FITS the sub-label to the column it will actually be drawn in — a second line when one will not hold it, then an ellipsis — with three text lines the ceiling, reusing the grid the id/name wrap idiom already proved fits row_h; a sub-label that already fits one line renders byte-identically to before. SCOPE — PROVEN: for every block of every drill layer in the emitted document, each label line's ink box lies inside its own node rect, measured from the emitted geometry with the emitter's declared per-character widths and the document's declared font sizes, entities counted as the single glyph they render. Second half: this emitter does not truncate the next-work title by a fixed budget — the card emits the whole clause and lets its own width do the fitting, the declared bound _NEXT_WORK_TITLE caps ONE item's HEIGHT rather than its text, and where it bites the remainder discloses through a NATIVE <details> cut at a word so head and remainder rejoin continuously when opened. DELIBERATELY NOT: whether an ellipsis a reader meets is ACTIONABLE — truncated-without-affordance stays T4's perceptual residue under LLR-055/TC-055, so T4 remains a live critique anchor; the full string keeps its homes in the block's title, aria-label and data-summary.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-119.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-119)", "title": "T4 label legibility, mechanized: ink inside its box, truncation with a reveal", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-120": {"description": "The mechanized floor of usability anchor T8 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md). T8 is two clauses and this row claims only the first: NO EDGE PASSES THROUGH AN UNRELATED NODE BOX. SCOPE — PROVEN: across the shipped dashboard plus one fixture per emitter, every emitted wire, sampled as the polyline a reader's eye follows, clears every node rect that is not its own source or target. MEASURED at the binding: 264 shipped wires, 0 through a box. DELIBERATELY NOT: crossing count, lane separation, or where a crossing falls — that residue stays under LLR-055/TC-055, which is why T8 remains a live critique anchor. Separately and NOT claimed here: at the router's own 3px clearance rather than the bare box, one wire does graze, so _detour_d exhausting its lane candidates and returning the least-bad fallback is a reachable state rather than a theoretical one; that wire's identity and the lane-separation work belong to the open work item.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-120.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-120)", "title": "No edge through an unrelated node box (T8 objective floor)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-121": {"description": "write_kit_version stamps docs/kit-version (SHA + date, -dirty on an uncommitted kit tree) so a re-sync is diffable.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-121.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-121)", "title": "Kit-version stamp", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-122": {"description": "dev-setup wires the pre-commit floor (core.hooksPath) from its baseline, so the floor is active from the setup the contributor actually runs.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-122.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-122)", "title": "dev-setup wires core.hooksPath", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-123": {"description": "Orders eligible traincars by (gate, Priority desc, downstream-dependent count desc, hard-path desc, first WI id).", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-123.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-123)", "title": "Deterministic traincar ordering", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-124": {"description": "docs/status.md's generated block is maintained by the trunk lane only: trunk_step --regen runs gen_trajectory --status after derive_gate (the gate is its input), and the status-map freshness gate byte-compares it on the trunk while work branches skip (SS5.2). (docs/run-state and its four-valued dispatcher outcome retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5.)", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-124.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-124)", "title": "Generated status + run-state", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-130": {"description": "Implements stale/missing --check with a git as-of stamp excluded from stamp-only comparison.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-130.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-130)", "title": "Dashboard staleness check", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-131": {"description": "Derives contradiction-safe single-WI scheduling from PlanMode and quarantines a contradictory SafetyClass.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-131.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-131)", "title": "Contradiction-safe dual-plan class", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-132": {"description": "Applies the declared level's continuation semantics to a PAGE at both the dispatcher disposition and the --dual-plan single-shot round: `page_action(human_held, keep_nondependent)` over the four cells the retired enum's three words could not span — a human-held tier pages, a loop-held one STALLs, and `None` normalizes to the strictest cell rather than to `bool(None)`'s most permissive one.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-132.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-132)", "title": "PAGE ratification-level mapping", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-133": {"description": "provenance_findings flags a WI id or a process-doc citation across the normative cells of all three spine registries, joining the --strict failure set via exit_code.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-133.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-133)", "title": "Spine stand-alone rule", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-134": {"description": "form_findings applies six rules — one obligation per requirement, no non-obligation modal, no actorless passive, no unfalsifiable term, no open-ended clause, no obligation keyword in an LLR Detail — skipping Draft rows, and joins the --strict failure set via exit_code.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-134.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-134)", "title": "Requirement form rules", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-135": {"description": "paraphrase_advisories compares a child cell's significant words (alphabetic runs of four characters or more, lowercased) against its parent's and reports when the share of the CHILD's words that also appear in the parent exceeds the declared threshold — the child is the denominator, so a long parent cannot dilute a short echoing child. Reported in its own advisory pipe, which never joins a failure set.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-135.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-135)", "title": "Paraphrase advisory", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-136": {"description": "Converts between the registry homes per the Phase 2a grammar (TOML +++ frontmatter, Deliverable in the body, status from the directory, order key only as a CSV-order migration artifact). --verify round-trips CSV->specs->CSV in a temp dir, exits nonzero on any cell mismatch, and reports byte identity with a reason. write_spec_file is the single spec writer (to_specs and plan_artifacts filing both go through it). Refuses unknown statuses by row, non-empty targets without --force, and any in-flight active/<branch>/ claim by name (status is the FIRST path component, the loaders' rule; conversion is a drained-stop operation per §3.2 - the Phase 4 claim protocol made the state reachable).", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-136.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-136)", "title": "Registry converter (CSV <-> spec folder)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-137": {"description": "Compiles docs/log.d/*.md into docs/log.md via plan_artifacts.append_log_summary in (git add-time, filename) order - all fragments validated first (name shape, ## heading, reserved section headings refused, an adding commit required - on HEAD, or on MERGE_HEAD during the integrator's merge composition), relative links rebased from docs/log.d/ to docs/, fragments deleted after append, all-or-nothing on any validation error. Never commits; the caller owns the commit. The artifact-regen half is LLR-142 (SR-134).", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-137.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-137)", "title": "Serial trunk step (log compile + regen)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-138": {"description": "pause_reason reads the ONE tracked home docs/work/pause via tracked_pause (TOML reason/since; malformation fails closed to the pinned loud placeholder). The legacy untracked lane/pause half retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5 - a stray local file is inert, pinned by test.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-138.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-138)", "title": "Tracked pause reader (dual-home)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-139": {"description": "Renders zero-or-one 'Paused since <since> - <reason>' bullet in the committed-tree-pure pending region from docs/work/pause - declared since verbatim, no clock, the since clause omitted when empty, fail-closed text on malformation pinned equal to the reader's placeholder.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-139.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-139)", "title": "Pause surfaced in the pending block", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-140": {"description": "claim: §2.3 steps 1+2 with the refusal ladder (tracked pause via agent_common.tracked_pause, dirty tree, existing branch, unsafe name, non-ordinary safety_class, off-frontier id) before a git mv + trunk commit + branch cut. finished_branches: a claimed branch whose tip holds no spec under active/<branch>/ (the closing move IS the signal). integrate_one: --no-ff --no-commit merge, trunk_step.py folded into the merge commit so it passes its own floor, check.py --jobs 0 at the declared tier on the composed tree read fail-closed (nonzero exit OR any SKIP line refuses), verdict gate per docs/review-policy using score_reviews.parse_verdict with the verdict's last branch commit required no older than the last non-review non-fragment commit, ff-only trunk advance, branch deleted on green, candidate parked on red. audit: git log --first-parent --no-merges over --since..HEAD; paths outside docs/work/, docs/log.d/, docs/log.md and the stack.ini [generated] set flag the commit.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-140.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-140)", "title": "Local integrator (claim + queue + audit)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-141": {"description": "On a claimed work branch the _TRUNK_FRESHNESS_STEPS set SKIPs with a §5.2 notice, exit code untouched; the plan listing still names every step. The claim signal answers in two stages - docs/work/active/<branch>/ on disk (the fast path), else the branch's own committed history (git log -1 -- docs/work/active/<branch>) - so the closing commit that archives the claim, and every commit after it, keep the skip. No git, a failing git, or an empty answer all read as the trunk lane: a false work-branch positive would switch the freshness gates off on the one branch that owns regenerating them.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-141.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-141)", "title": "Work-branch lane signal + freshness skip", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-142": {"description": "Regenerates arch-map, okf, derived-gate, dashboard, status block and open-items as subprocesses in dependency order (derived-gate before its two consumers), skipping absent artifact families with a notice and stopping loudly at the first failure. Never commits; the caller owns the commit.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-142.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-142)", "title": "Trunk-step artifact regen (dependency order)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-143": {"description": "The serial scheduling front end a plain agent_loop.py launch runs: each cycle re-derives the ready frontier (schedule.frontier), refuses on the tracked pause and on a dirty trunk at the cycle top, resumes a parked claimed branch else claims the next queued WI in build order (integrate.claim, branch name check-ref-format-validated), launches one worker session on the claimed branch's worktree (agent_loop.py --worktree --wi with the session dials forwarded), drains the serial merge queue (integrate.integrate) counting merges from finished_branches, and stops loudly on any composed refusal - stranded claims (active specs with no branch ref) refuse by name, NEEDS-HUMAN propagates as exit 7, a stall aborts, an empty frontier drains to exit 0. The session-config preflight applies only when work needs a worker. Never pushes.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-143.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-143)", "title": "Plain-launch drive loop (claim->build->integrate)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-144": {"description": "close_partial: commits the lane's work as-is, moves each claimed spec to the TERMINAL partial/ (nothing re-claims it, so nothing strands) and writes ONE IMMUTABLE per-close report under docs/handbacks/ naming the claimed outcome, the reason, the commit range and the keep/discard split. The report is the close EVENT's identity — immutable so it never moves, unique so a second close is a second document — which is what dissolved the starvation class five mutable-proxy dedup mechanisms died on. A second close is refused rather than overwriting the first, and any refusal restores every report the call wrote (schedule reads queued+blockref as blocked) and the driver cannot claim and return it forever. quarantine: the ruled red arm - reverts the product paths to the merge base and keeps the failing diff as a bar-inert .patch under docs/work/handback/, reading `git diff --name-status -z` as three-field RECORDS so a rename cannot revert the wrong path, checking every git return code, and restoring rather than reporting when a revert step fails. Both leave the branch finished, so integrate.branch_outcomes reads the outcome off the spec's DIRECTORY - complete/=merged, cancelled/=cancelled, any open folder=handback - and every lane ends in a merge.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-144.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-144)", "title": "Terminal lane outcomes (handback + quarantine)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-145": {"description": "move_spec moves a registry/spec file and keeps every markdown link in the repo true as ONE indivisible operation: the moved file's own relative targets are re-relativised against its new directory, every inbound link whose target resolves to the vacated path is redirected (link text untouched, #fragments carried, external/protocol-relative/bare-anchor targets left alone, root-relative targets excused only in the rebase half, line endings preserved via newline=''), and every rewritten file is staged with the move so the ritual lands as one changeset. integrate.claim and handback.close_partial call it for the claim and close moves; the CLI performs the worker's terminal close move and the dated docs/archive/specs/ archival. expected_relink is the pure oracle integrate._abandoned_claim compares byte-for-byte to excuse a crashed claim's own relink writes without widening its conviction to any .md edit.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-145.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-145)", "title": "Link-aware spec-move ritual (move + relink indivisible)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-146": {"description": "Scans root *.md + docs/**/*.md (check_doc_refs.doc_files - the same walk, so the two doc checks agree on the surface and generated trees stay skipped) plus docs/stack.ini, with docs/reviews/ records out of scope (a verdict quotes defective markers as evidence); each opt-in fig: marker is judged on its own attributes and must carry a non-empty, wordful cmd= and rev= pair (rev bare or quoted), or a non-empty derived= derivation; a marker is grammar prose that declares nothing only when every attribute value is wholly a placeholder token (angle-bracket-wrapped, or the bare ellipsis - so the convention text ships scaffold-clean); in a judged marker a placeholder-shaped value counts as absent, never as satisfying (an unfilled rev=<revision> names no revision), while a metacharacter inside a longer value is command text and the marker is judged on its attributes; findings name the missing half and quote the line; GENERATED marker blocks and fig-ok lines are exempt; warn-first, --strict exits 1. Presence only, never truth - re-derivation is rung 2, deliberately not built.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-146.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-146)", "title": "Declared-figure provenance presence check", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-147": {"description": "sn_gate reads the cited set sn_cited_ids builds from SR SN-Refs (an F5 duplicate of trace.py's parse, pinned equal by test_rule_sync like phase_num and LLR_EXEMPT): a RATIFIED SN cited by zero SRs caps the raw level at G0 - a ratified-but-unanswered need has not earned G1 - while a Draft SN stays exempt from the coverage rung and rides the existing draft rung (one fact, one finding; the itemized 'SN has no SR' listing remains trace.py's orphan rule at G2 strictness, reading the SAME cited set, so the gate input and the listing cannot contradict on one registry state). _raw_level rebuilds the cited set from the rows in scope, so the ex-draft counterfactual drops a Draft SR's citation with its row and never fabricates coverage. The compared basis line gains uncovered=N (ratified-and-uncited count) beside drafts=N/modified=N - additive for readers, but --check compares the line whole, so it is a cache-format change downstream repos regenerate through.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-147.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-147)", "title": "SN-coverage gate rung + uncovered basis count", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-148": {"description": "--next-phase prints max(Phase over non-draft spine rows) + 1 - the basis line's phase=N derivation exposed as an output mode (no second parse, docs/gate never written), bare so the intake mint helper and any agent can int() the answer when a confirmed scope change opens a new phase; a Draft row's phase is not yet scope and never bumps the answer; an unphased spine is the implicit foundation (1), so it prints 2. The boundary it serves is ruled (owner 2026-08-01): a phase increments on an adjudication-confirmed scope change or a ratified draft-SN batch, never on the raw derived-gate drop.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-148.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-148)", "title": "Derived next phase as an output mode", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-149": {"description": "The dispatcher's one scheduling decision (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A4/§A8), consuming schedule.kind_of - the §A1 classification's enforcement point. _kind_action is the ruled §A8 table verbatim per kind x human-held answer (`agent_common.human_holds`), and a human-held attestation/gate row SURFACES even under keep_nondependent — that dial answers whether OTHER lanes keep running, never whether the machine may ratify; _admission is the barrier as a pure decision: any exclusive-kind frontier row stops new admission (wait) until every lane is back, then ALL spine rows admit together as one exclusive batch (one branch, one agent_loop --wi 'A;B' worker, one re-attest window), attestation/gate rows surface under attended (drain, exit 0 naming the open-items cards) and admit as the queued batch at single-ratify's close; _lane_count resolves the lanes dial CLI > AGENT_LANES > stack.ini [agent-loop] > 1 (absent key = 1; the template seeds 2); gap_census is the empty-frontier ladder's rung-1 seam - unverified in-scope SRs, orphan rows and draft SNs from trace.analyze, handed to the intake mint helper (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A5.2), never minted in the lane.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-149.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-149)", "title": "§A8 admission: the kind x ratification-level table + the spine barrier", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-150": {"description": "One lane's mechanics extracted from the dispatcher (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A4.2): ensure the lane worktree (integrate.lane_worktree, the one home both the worker and the refresh agree on), build the worker argv with the plain launch's session dials forwarded explicitly, launch the worker subprocess (spawn_worker non-blocking for the lane table; run_worker its blocking form and the default worker seam), and run the §A2 station refresh as the lane's OWN subprocess (spawn_refresh - the invoker's integrate.py refresh CLI under the harness interpreter) so N lanes' bars overlap instead of queueing at the dispatcher (§A4.3). Reports outcomes, decides none: the tree signal (specs moved out of active/<branch>/) plus exit codes are the whole protocol - no state file, no back-channel.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-150.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-150)", "title": "Lane mechanics: worktree, worker subprocess, §A2 refresh subprocess", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-151": {"description": "The §A4.1 authority flip's integrator half (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A4.1): _claim_refusal's safety_class != ordinary arm is DELETED (admission is the dispatcher's decision) and the claim REQUIRES the dispatch lock (agent_common.dispatch_lock_path, out/agent-loop.lock - the lock a live dispatcher holds for its process lifetime), acquired at ONE site (_dispatch_lock, mirroring _slot's one-site discipline for the merge slot's own lock) with the ladder run before it so the clean-trunk rung never refuses over the lock's own file: a hand claim on an idle station works, a hand claim during live lanes is unrepresentable, and the dispatcher's in-process claim passes dispatch_lock_held=True. claim/_claim_refusal/_abandoned_claim/_claim_delta are batch-aware: a spine batch is ONE claim commit moving every batched spec (subject joins the ids; the single-WI subject stays byte-identical), and integrate() takes an optional branches= restriction so the dispatcher merges each lane's branch as its own refresh completes.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-151.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-151)", "title": "Dispatch-lock claim rung + the spine batch claim", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-152": {"description": "The `adjudication` kind (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A5.2) joins the declared SafetyClass vocabulary: exclusive on the concurrency axis, rank 1 on the order axis - the slot §A1 reserved, filled without renumbering the ruled table. The kind's lane runs NO product bar (integrate._lane_bar_directives + _refresh_bar: the refresh still merges trunk in, runs the trunk step and commits a verified Bar-Green attestation whose summary reads no-bar honestly) because its outputs are Status cells and the work registry, nothing a product bar can speak to; a mixed batch or unreadable frontmatter fails TOWARD the bar. The optional `bar = G1|G2|G3` frontmatter key rides the same directive read: bar declares verification strictness for this row's lane; it never affects scheduling (load_wis deliberately does not parse it) - the refresh passes the batch's strictest value to check.py --gate and refuses a malformed one loudly.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-152.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-152)", "title": "The adjudication kind: rank-1 slot + no-bar arm + the bar strictness key", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-153": {"description": "The R1/R3 invariant sealed (log.md Decisions 2026-08-01): a WI id is created only by a human trunk commit or this helper - lanes never mint. next_wi_id is max+1 over every spec FILENAME under docs/work/ (active/<branch>/ included). Three triggers, serial by construction: (a) the ratified/routed-cell diff on the merged commit via check_trajectory.staged_spine_amendments -> one adjudication row listing each changed row/cell/before-after (routed traced cells: SN-Refs, Verifies, and LLR SR-Refs per the ruled cell split); (b) a merged spec carrying ## Handback -> the disposition row (outcomes cancel / defer / re-queue with drafted follow-up / surface an open item; NEVER minted for an adjudication row - no recursion, and handback.hand_back refuses the act itself); (c) the dispatcher's gap census -> concrete gap-closure rows, deduped against every existing row. Drafts-not-mints: a merged adjudication row's ## Dispositions fenced-toml drafts mint at ITS merge, validated loudly. Tier signals are measurable (rows touched, gate delta, handback reason class). context_block renders the pure registry joins (cancelled precedent WITH REASONS first, pending OIs, the LLR/TC code map, CMP knowledge packs, IF seams, precedent reviews) - advisory-never-gating, three consumers. flip_verified enacts ruled decision 2: recommend-only under attended, flip under single-ratify/autonomous, unknown levels fail toward recommend. The mint commit mirrors the claim's bookkeeping shape; every derived title is deterministic, so the sweep CLI re-run is idempotent.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-153.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-153)", "title": "The unified trunk-side intake mint + the context block + the ratification-level arms", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-154": {"description": "integrate_one's post-merge arm is the intake's one honest hook point: the merge has landed, the slot is still held, trunk is serial - intake.intake_after_merge(root, pre-merge head, head, outcomes, branch) mints there as one bookkeeping commit (RULING-6: docs/work/ + the declared generated set), and a mint refusal stops the run loudly while the merge stands (recovery: fix trunk-side, then python intake.py sweep). The refresh's bar directives (_lane_bar_directives, read off the same trunk claim the slot reads) carry the adjudication no-bar arm and the bar strictness pin to _run_bar's --gate.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-154.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-154)", "title": "The merge slot's post-merge intake arm", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-155": {"description": "`process_config` parses `docs/process.toml` once; `declared_policy` reads a dial through it with the legacy file as fallback; `process_shape_findings` refuses every line shape the hooks' sh cannot follow; `config_conflicts` returns refusal STRINGS (never raises — `dispatch.run` sits in the tick loop's caller, where an exception would rewrite an exit-code contract) covering the double-declaration, the wrong type and the out-of-range value; `ratification_level` + `human_holds` are the ordinal and the ONE comparison every consumer makes.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-155.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-155)", "title": "The config layer: one reader, checked shape, one comparison", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-156": {"description": "`set_process_key` answers THREE states (`wrote`/`unchanged`/`missing`) and the write decides the delete; `add_if_missing` makes the write total. `gate-policy` gets its own arm because its single word expands to three dials, so it cannot be a one-key `LEGACY_CONFIG` row. `LEGACY_RATIFICATION` is F5-duplicated from `agent_common` (bootstrap imports no kit sibling — it must run standing on nothing but the kit directory it copies FROM) and pinned equal by `test_rule_sync`.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-156.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-156)", "title": "The legacy converter, and the one word that is not a rename", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-157": {"description": "`spine_stage` returns the LOWEST unfinished tier, where a MISSING artifact belongs to its own tier rather than to its parent (an SR with no LLR yet is stage 2, because what is being written is an LLR), `Modified` is the SR's own tier and is checked FIRST, and any other not-yet-Verified SR is checked LAST because a child in flight is the honest answer while it is in flight. `stage_to_gate` states the mapping to the harness gate once, deliberately lossy in one direction only. The value rides `docs/gate` as an APPENDED basis field.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-157.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-157)", "title": "The second axis: which tier is in process", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-158": {"description": "normative_text() joins a row's RATIFIED cells in header order with a unit separator, reusing spine_cell_class rather than a second list so the digest and the amend-without-flip warn can never disagree about what normative means; the id and Status are excluded because neither is content - the id is the key the digest is looked up BY, and folding Status in would make every flip look like an amendment and every amendment invisible behind its own flip. sn_normative_text() takes the RAW table line for a stakeholder need rather than a parsed projection, because the three need tables have different shapes and a projection would hash the garbling. current_digests() is the one reader that joins both halves. The WRITER and the co-mutation guard are not here yet: which cells hold the anchor depends on the registry carrier, ruled separately (OI-12).", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-158.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-158)", "title": "The digest that anchors an attestation", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-159": {"description": "`_judgement_first` is a STABLE PARTITION over the frontier, applied inside `_admission` before the surface/dispatch split so it can never move work past a human-held stop. `red_tc_census` rides the existing finding-string seam with a distinct prefix; `_red_tc_line` and `parse_red_tc` sit adjacent because the grammar is a contract between two modules, and `intake` routes on the parser rather than re-splitting prose.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-159.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-159)", "title": "Admission order and the red-TC census", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-160": {"description": "Three signals over OPEN rows only — Jaccard over title token sets at a deliberately high bar, shared `SR-Refs`, and a shared anchor-stripped `SpecRef` — each reported once per pair per signal with both ids, in sorted order. Printed on the warn-only line beside backlog staleness; never appended to `errors`.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-160.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-160)", "title": "The mechanical queue-overlap pre-filter", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-161": {"description": "`close_partial` writes the report FIRST (it is the event's identity), refuses when one already exists, and restores EVERY report this call wrote on any refusal — so a multi-spec lane cannot half-close. `intake._close_drafts` titles the disposition on the report PATH and nothing else, and says so LOUDLY when a terminal spec has no report rather than minting zero rows quietly.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-161.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-161)", "title": "The close ritual and the disposition it owes", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-162": {"description": "`load` reads a shipped template (or an operator override) and raises a named `PromptError`; `fill` refuses an unknown slot and an unfilled declared slot; `digest` normalizes line endings before hashing; `preflight` turns a missing template into a launch-time refusal. `gen_prompt_catalog.py` renders the catalogue with a `--check` freshness gate, and `agent_common.prompt_fingerprint` records the rendered result per session beside the template's own name.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-162.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-162)", "title": "Prompts as loaded files, catalogued and fingerprinted", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-163": {"description": "`split_cmd` recognises a JSON array ONLY when the value parses as a list, so no existing template can change meaning; a non-string token raises rather than being stringified. The prompt-transport decision reads the resulting TOKENS, so it is identical for both forms. Alongside it, `ADJUDICATE` joins `agent_route.WEIGHT_PHASES` and `agent_loop.DEFAULT_PHASE_TIER` with its own `route_intent` arm: cross-family like the reviewers, tier pinned by the row's measured estimate, and deliberately deaf to the implementer's escalation overrides.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-163.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-163)", "title": "Argv arrays, and the adjudicator as a routed phase", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-164": {"description": "`render` is PURE — it takes no arguments and reads only the shipped templates — so `--check` and the write path cannot disagree about what \"current\" means. `--check` compares on NORMALIZED line endings, for the same reason the digest is taken that way: a CRLF checkout is not a stale catalogue. The file is declared in `docs/stack.ini [generated]` as kind `promptcatalog`, so the trunk lane owns it and a work branch never commits it.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-164.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-164)", "title": "The generated prompt catalogue and its freshness gate", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-165": {"description": "convert() walks the four tiers and returns (findings, written_paths), so the caller decides whether a lossy conversion writes. rows_to_toml emits `[<table>.<ID>]` with the id PREFIX RETAINED and BARE - TOML allows `-` in a bare key - because ~6,400 hand-authored citations use the prefixed token and a numeric key buys no type change (TOML keys are always strings, and `001` and `1` are two legal DIFFERENT keys, which is a near-miss the prefixed form does not have). cell_to_value types by COLUMN, not by guessing: REF_COLS become arrays, INT_COLS become ints, an empty cell becomes an absent key, and everything else stays a string - this is a carrier change, not a schema change. toml_scalar emits multi-line basic strings for anything long or newline-bearing (tomllib is read-only by design, so the kit emits its own, as it already does in wi_convert.toml_string and bootstrap.set_process_key). sn_to_toml keeps the edge-case tier's own lifecycle/scenario/expected fields as THEMSELVES and writes kind= from the heading, retiring section-as-state. compare() re-reads what was emitted and diffs it against an {id: {key: text}} projection built from either carrier, so ONE comparison serves both instead of a second copy that drifts.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-165.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-165)", "title": "The spine carrier converter (markdown + CSV -> TOML)", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "LLR-166": {"description": "PURE like trace_text (text in, rows out; no git, no argv) except resolve()'s existence check, because the dual-home refusal is the one rule that must not be duplicated. rows_from_toml presents rows under TODAY'S COLUMN NAMES via SPINE_COLUMN - the exact inverse of migrate_carrier.KEY - so the carrier change is data plus a loader rather than a sixteen-module rename; SPINE_TABLE is keyed on the ID COLUMN, not on a path, because a path carries a carrier suffix and therefore moves. An absent key stays absent rather than becoming \"\", and a decode error returns None rather than {}. resolve() RAISES when both carriers exist rather than picking by precedence, and load() raises on a carrier that exists and will not parse. carriers() returns BOTH paths so an applicability test spans the cutover.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "docs/okf/low-level-requirements/LLR-166.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv (LLR-166)", "title": "The spine carrier's one reader, and the dual-home refusal", "type": "Low-Level Requirement"}, "Low-Level Requirement": {"description": "149 Low-Level Requirement concept(s) — double-click (or focus and press Enter) to descend into them.", "fill": "#64748b", "href": "", "resource": "", "title": "", "type": "OKF type"}, "Process Guide": {"description": "3 Process Guide concept(s) — double-click (or focus and press Enter) to descend into them.", "fill": "#9a3412", "href": "", "resource": "", "title": "", "type": "OKF type"}, "SN-001": {"description": "A team can drop the kit into a new or existing repo and get a working gated, requirement-traced process without hand-building the tooling.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-001.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-001)", "title": "A team can drop the kit into a new or existing repo and get a working gated, req", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-002": {"description": "The trace from need → requirement → design → test is mechanically verified, not manually asserted: every requirement links to a need and a test before a gate.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-002.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-002)", "title": "The trace from need → requirement → design → test is mechanically verified, not ", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-003": {"description": "The kit is stack-agnostic — a non-Python project uses it by re-pointing the harness at that stack's tools, with Python only as the reference.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-003.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-003)", "title": "The kit is stack-agnostic — a non-Python project uses it by re-pointing the harn", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-004": {"description": "Progress advances only through explicit approval gates (G1→G2→G3→…), and a gate passes only when its mechanical bar is met.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-004.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-004)", "title": "Progress advances only through explicit approval gates (G1→G2→G3→…), and a gate ", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-005": {"description": "AI agents and humans work from the same playbook, with the process enforced agent-neutrally (git hooks + CI), not by trusting any one agent.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-005.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-005)", "title": "AI agents and humans work from the same playbook, with the process enforced agen", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-006": {"description": "An agent can run unattended and resume from repo text alone; such a run never blocks on a prompt and fails clearly.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-006.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-006)", "title": "An agent can run unattended and resume from repo text alone; such a run never bl", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-007": {"description": "The kit's own changes stay traceable and tested — a change to a script is covered by a test exercised end-to-end against a real scaffold.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-007.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-007)", "title": "The kit's own changes stay traceable and tested — a change to a script is covere", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-008": {"description": "Gates are honest — a green never hides a skipped check, a stub, or an unmet criterion.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-008.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-008)", "title": "Gates are honest — a green never hides a skipped check, a stub, or an unmet crit", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-009": {"description": "A committed secret or private identity is caught before it publishes, in every repo, without extra setup.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-009.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-009)", "title": "A committed secret or private identity is caught before it publishes, in every r", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-010": {"description": "Documentation stays navigable and honest — links resolve, the vision is declared once, and generated views cannot silently rot.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-010.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-010)", "title": "Documentation stays navigable and honest — links resolve, the vision is declared", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-011": {"description": "The kit's scripts run on a clean Python 3.11+ with minimal, argued dependencies — stdlib by default, a non-stdlib dependency admitted only through a reviewed ledger row — on Windows and POSIX (and macOS).", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-011.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-011)", "title": "The kit's scripts run on a clean Python 3.11+ with minimal, argued dependencies ", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-012": {"description": "The process is right-sized, not ceremony for its own sake — small changes stay cheap, and heavy layers are opt-in.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-012.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-012)", "title": "The process is right-sized, not ceremony for its own sake — small changes stay c", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-013": {"description": "No Python 3 interpreter on PATH (or the Windows Store alias that resolves but exits nonzero)", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-013.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-013)", "title": "No Python 3 interpreter on PATH (or the Windows Store alias that resolves but ex", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-014": {"description": "A required harness tool is absent", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-014.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-014)", "title": "A required harness tool is absent", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-015": {"description": "The working directory is not a git repository", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-015.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-015)", "title": "The working directory is not a git repository", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-016": {"description": "An unattended run must never block on a prompt at launch or mid-run", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-016.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-016)", "title": "An unattended run must never block on a prompt at launch or mid-run", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-017": {"description": "The coordinator is killed / power-lost mid-session", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-017.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-017)", "title": "The coordinator is killed / power-lost mid-session", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-018": {"description": "A second coordinator is launched in the same checkout", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-018.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-018)", "title": "A second coordinator is launched in the same checkout", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-019": {"description": "A repo with no commits yet (HEAD absent)", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-019.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-019)", "title": "A repo with no commits yet (HEAD absent)", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-020": {"description": "The agent CLI / model errors before doing work (retired model, expired auth)", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-020.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-020)", "title": "The agent CLI / model errors before doing work (retired model, expired auth)", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-021": {"description": "A generated artifact (arch map, trace view) drifts from its source", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-021.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-021)", "title": "A generated artifact (arch map, trace view) drifts from its source", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-022": {"description": "A committed example row / placeholder is left in a registry at a gate", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-022.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-022)", "title": "A committed example row / placeholder is left in a registry at a gate", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-023": {"description": "A reviewer can see the project's progress and how its parts connect from one dashboard-like file.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-023.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-023)", "title": "A reviewer can see the project's progress and how its parts connect from one das", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-024": {"description": "Subjective/perceptual acceptance — a realistic-looking render, an artifact comparison with no crisp measurable interface — is adjudicated by an independent critical eye against a written rubric, never by the session that authored the artifact.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-024.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-024)", "title": "Subjective/perceptual acceptance — a realistic-looking render, an artifact compa", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-025": {"description": "A single command from the repo root (agent-resume) lets a configured LLM agent implement toward the vision — fully autonomously where enabled — with no human curating what comes next.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-025.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-025)", "title": "A single command from the repo root (agent-resume) lets a configured LLM agent i", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-026": {"description": "Several LLM families are configurable — selected per job and per capability level — and work that benefits from an independent second opinion is automatically routed to a *different* family wherever that is configured.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-026.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-026)", "title": "Several LLM families are configurable — selected per job and per capability leve", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-027": {"description": "Ready work fans out across bounded parallel lanes, while mutation of the integration branch stays serialized and gated.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-027.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-027)", "title": "Ready work fans out across bounded parallel lanes, while mutation of the integra", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-028": {"description": "Every policy dial has one home — a single hand-edited, machine-read file — and a repo that declares the same dial twice is REFUSED rather than resolved by precedence.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-028.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-028)", "title": "Every policy dial has one home — a single hand-edited, machine-read file — and a", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SN-029": {"description": "An autonomous run gets as far as it honestly can. Once triggered, the coordinator stops for a human judgement only when the declared ratification level reserves that tier for a human, when a round cannot converge on its own, or when requirement/test documentation is introduced or amended such that the gate drops below what automation is permitted to attest.", "fill": "#4338ca", "href": "docs/okf/stakeholder-needs/SN-029.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.md (SN-029)", "title": "An autonomous run gets as far as it honestly can. Once triggered, the coordinato", "type": "Stakeholder Need"}, "SR-001": {"description": "trace.py shall join the SN->SR->LLR->TC registries and, with --strict, exit nonzero if any orphan exists.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-001.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-001)", "title": "Trace join reports orphans", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-002": {"description": "trace.py shall fail --strict-integrity on a duplicated or malformed registry id, a data row whose column count differs from its header, or a TC that pairs an LLR with an SR it does not decompose.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-002.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-002)", "title": "Integrity floor for ids and CSV structure", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-003": {"description": "trace.py shall, under --no-placeholders, flag any leftover -000 example row, and under --strict-schema, require non-empty required fields and in-vocabulary Verification/Tier values.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-003.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-003)", "title": "Placeholder and schema checks", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-004": {"description": "trace.py shall warn (never fail) when an SR AcceptanceCriteria uses a comparative term with no pinning predicate in the cell.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-004.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-004)", "title": "Acceptance-criteria advisory", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-005": {"description": "trace.py shall integrity-check the optional off-spine registries (PB/REPO/PART/ASSET, plus legacy MOD compatibility) and validate their back-links without blocking a project that does not use them.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-005.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-005)", "title": "Off-spine registries stay traceable", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-006": {"description": "check.py shall run the active gate's required steps and fail the gate when a required tool is missing, reporting SKIP(missing) rather than silently passing.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-006.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-006)", "title": "Gate/tier harness enforces required steps", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-007": {"description": "check.py shall read the toolchain (format/lint/test commands, tiers, coverage, arch-map mode) from docs/stack.ini so a stack swap edits only that file.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-007.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-007)", "title": "Declared stack profile", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-008": {"description": "check.py shall fail loudly on a malformed stack.ini, a non-integer coverage threshold, or a missing declared binary, rather than proceeding on a bad profile.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-008.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-008)", "title": "Profile validation fails loudly", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-009": {"description": "bootstrap.py shall seed only the artifacts a chosen profile uses, omitting Python-only files for a non-Python stack.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-009.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-009)", "title": "Conditional scaffold profiles", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-010": {"description": "bootstrap.py shall produce a scaffold whose harness runs green immediately after generation.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-010.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-010)", "title": "Scaffold runs green out of the box", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-011": {"description": "bootstrap.py shall skip an existing file on a re-run unless --force is passed.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-011.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-011)", "title": "Idempotent re-runnable scaffold", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-012": {"description": "check_docs.py shall fail on a broken intra-repo link or a missing PROJECT-VISION tag, and check generated-doc freshness under --stale.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-012.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-012)", "title": "Doc navigability", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-013": {"description": "check_flows.py shall validate that the authored runtime-flow docs conform to their contract.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-013.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-013)", "title": "Runtime-flows check", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-014": {"description": "check_perf.py shall compare a run against declared budgets and fail when a metric regresses beyond its tolerance.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-014.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-014)", "title": "Perf comparator and budgets", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-015": {"description": "The performance-budgets registry (PB-###) shall keep every row's Refs resolvable to a real SR/LLR/Module.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-015.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-015)", "title": "Performance-budget back-links", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-016": {"description": "check_stubs.py shall detect stub/placeholder markers in source at its declared gate and fail when one is present.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-016.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-016)", "title": "No-stub detector", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-017": {"description": "check_privacy.py shall scan every commit's staged diff, message, and outgoing range for secret patterns regardless of the privacy toggle, unless docs/process.toml [policies] secrets_scan is explicitly false (the legacy docs/secrets-scan is still read through the migration window).", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-017.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-017)", "title": "Always-on secrets floor", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-018": {"description": "check_privacy.py shall add PII/identity classes when docs/privacy-check is on, exempting configured author identities.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-018.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-018)", "title": "Privacy gate two-axis", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-019": {"description": "The pre-commit hook shall run the integrity + secrets floor on every commit, agent-neutrally.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-019.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-019)", "title": "Pre-commit hook floor", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-020": {"description": "The pre-push hook shall scan the outgoing commit range and block a push that publishes a secret or (under privacy-check) a private identity.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-020.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-020)", "title": "Pre-push hook outgoing scan", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-021": {"description": "The git hooks shall locate a working python3 by running a candidate and skip-or-report clearly when none resolves or a Store-alias exits nonzero.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-021.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-021)", "title": "Hook python probe", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-022": {"description": "check_vendored.py shall detect a vendored copy of an upstream doc drifting from its source.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-022.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-022)", "title": "Vendored-doc drift", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-023": {"description": "gen_arch_map.py shall regenerate the symbol map inside its marker block and, under --check, fail when the committed map has drifted from source.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-023.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-023)", "title": "Arch-map generation", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-024": {"description": "gen_cases.py shall expand a Permutations spec into concrete dimensional cases.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-024.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-024)", "title": "Permutation case generation", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-025": {"description": "gen_skills_index.py shall regenerate skills/INDEX.csv from each SKILL.md applicability frontmatter.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-025.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-025)", "title": "Skills index + checked per-agent fan-out", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-026": {"description": "agent_loop.py shall resume headless with stdin closed, never blocking on a prompt. Resume authority (one path - the serial resume loop is retired): a worker resumes from its explicit claimed assignment plus the committed trailer evidence on its branch, and the integrator (SR-132) derives claim/queue state from trunk history alone; docs/status.md is a generated reference surface (SR-059), never a session input.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-026.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-026)", "title": "Coordinator resumes headless", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-027": {"description": "agent_loop.py shall refuse a broken footing in preflight — not a git repo, no agent CLI, or a private author under privacy-check — exiting a typed nonzero code rather than hanging.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-027.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-027)", "title": "Coordinator preflight", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-028": {"description": "agent_loop.py shall end each session in a typed outcome code, guard a zero-commit HEAD, and report an all-ERROR stall as an unavailable agent rather than a work stall.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-028.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-028)", "title": "Coordinator typed outcomes", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-029": {"description": "The per-worktree coordinator lock shall be a kernel advisory lock the OS releases on process death, leaving no stale-pid wedge.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-029.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-029)", "title": "Coordinator lock auto-release", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-030": {"description": "agent_loop.py shall refuse a second coordinator in the same checkout, preventing a two-writer race.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-030.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-030)", "title": "Second coordinator refused", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-031": {"description": "The policy readers shall share one parse so the hooks, agent_loop and check_privacy agree on every declared value. Since SN-028 that parse is docs/process.toml, read by two grammars — tomllib and the hooks' pure sh — whose agreement over the file's checked line shape is itself driven; the legacy one-word files stay readable through the migration window, and a dial declared in both homes is REFUSED rather than resolved by precedence.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-031.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-031)", "title": "Declared-policy readers agree", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-032": {"description": "The onboarding and dev-setup templates shall scaffold and run to a green developer setup.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-032.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-032)", "title": "Onboarding and dev-setup scaffold", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-033": {"description": "gen_release_checklist.py shall emit the release-gate checklist, including the warn-tier performance budgets that never fail a gate.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-033.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-033)", "title": "Release checklist generation", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-034": {"description": "Every kit script shall run on a clean Python 3.11+ using the standard library plus only those non-stdlib dependencies declared as a reviewed row in docs/dependencies.md.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-034.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-034)", "title": "Kit scripts run on stdlib plus ledger-declared dependencies", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-035": {"description": "The process and ID scheme shall be stack-agnostic.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-035.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-035)", "title": "Stack-agnostic across OSes", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-036": {"description": "A re-sync of an existing adoption shall integrate relevant kit updates without clobbering the adopter's filled-in files, per ADOPTING.md section 6 — take kit-owned scripts/hooks wholesale, regenerate generated docs, and preserve filled-in registries/config.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-036.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-036)", "title": "Deliberate re-sync integration", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-037": {"description": "SR-037 is superseded by SR-067;SR-068;SR-069; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-037.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-037)", "title": "Superseded: Work-item registry validation", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-038": {"description": "SR-038 is superseded by SR-070;SR-071;SR-072; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-038.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-038)", "title": "Superseded: Offline project-state view", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-039": {"description": "check_dupes.py shall report any window of at least a configurable minimum of consecutive significant source tokens appearing at more than one location, and exit nonzero unless the finding's block fingerprint plus file pair (or a legacy bare file pair) is recorded in the census.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-039.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-039)", "title": "Duplicate-code lint", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-040": {"description": "The unattended coordinator shall select the agent command template per in-process session phase (PLAN/BUILD/REVIEW-A/REVIEW-B/DESIGN-CHECK/CRITIQUE - the coordinator's own activity; the retired docs/run-phase file, deleted by SR-059, is not an input) via AGENT_CMD_MAP/--cmd-map, falling back to the single AGENT_CMD; surface the declared reviewer dial (docs/review-policy, default 1) at run start without enforcing it; and warn (never block) when a lane resume surface exceeds the declared size threshold.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-040.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-040)", "title": "Per-phase routing and review dial", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-041": {"description": "check_doc_refs.py shall flag a documentation reference to a repository path that does not exist and an asserted sym:<module>.<name> reference missing from the generated module-map inventory, warn-first (exit 0) with --strict gating; absent inventory skips the symbol tier cleanly.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-041.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-041)", "title": "Doc reference validation", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-042": {"description": "gen_okf.py shall export the spine registries AND the key process docs as a generated Open Knowledge Format bundle under docs/okf — one typed markdown concept per real row (graph as resolvable markdown links, per-tier indexes) plus a Process Guide concept per present process doc (a summary derived from that doc and a resource pointer to the unmodified source) — deterministic (no clocks), with --check failing on any stale, missing or extra bundle file; docs/okf-export off silences and placeholder-only registries are vacuous.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-042.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-042)", "title": "OKF knowledge-bundle export", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-043": {"description": "A PreToolUse subagent-spawn gate shall, during an unattended run, refuse or defer subagent spawns per docs/subagent-gate (off|ask|deny; absent=off), honor a launcher-set SUBAGENT_GATE=allow override the model cannot set, and fail open on any error so a broken gate never wedges the tools.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-043.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-043)", "title": "Subagent spawn gate", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-044": {"description": "SR-044 is superseded by SR-073;SR-074;SR-075;SR-076;SR-077;SR-078; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-044.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-044)", "title": "Superseded: Declared-interface connectivity", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-045": {"description": "SR-045 is superseded by SR-079;SR-080;SR-081;SR-082;SR-083; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-045.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-045)", "title": "Superseded: Heterogeneous implementer/reviewer scheduling", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-046": {"description": "The root run.cmd/run.sh/run.command launchers shall present every major capability an evaluator runs from a single declaration - one name = command line (plus an optional name.desc line) per capability in docs/stack.ini's [run] section - via a stdlib scripts/run_menu.py that reads it: no args gives a numbered interactive menu, run_menu.py <name> launches one directly with exit-code passthrough, and --list emits a stable name<TAB>desc machine listing (the agent surface); an absent or empty [run] section prints the no-launch-command-wired-yet guidance and exits 1; the launch command is declared once (the duplicated RUN_CMD is retired) and works on Windows and POSIX.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-046.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-046)", "title": "Run capability menu", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-047": {"description": "SR-047 is superseded by SR-084;SR-085;SR-086; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-047.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-047)", "title": "Superseded: Subjective-quality critique loop", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-048": {"description": "SR-048 is superseded by SR-087;SR-088; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-048.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-048)", "title": "Superseded: How-SW top view bounded and containerized", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-049": {"description": "derive_gate.py shall compute the active gate from the spine artifact states (SN section-state + SR/LLR/TC Status maturity) and cache it to docs/gate rather than accept a hand-set value, so a gate advances only when the mechanical states do. Three Status values are recognized case-insensitively: Draft (G0 + decomposition exemptions), Verified (G3), and Modified — a post-attestation amendment owing a re-attest, deriving G2 through the existing decomposed-unverified rung with no arithmetic of its own; the cached basis line carries modified=N beside drafts=N so the pending state never hides.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-049.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-049)", "title": "Derived gate from artifact states", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-050": {"description": "gen_trajectory.py shall render a Process reference tab in PROJECT_STATE.html beside the existing views, presenting how the project is built as three linked panels: (1) artifact lifecycle x gates - Vision -> SN (G1) -> SR (G1->G2) -> LLR + architecture (G2) -> TC (G2->G3) -> code+tests (G3), each stage linked to its process-doc section and annotated with this repo live tier counts; (2) the resume loop - the managed agent_loop flow (read status -> PLAN -> BUILD -> REVIEW -> INTEGRATE -> commit -> hook/gate -> repeat) with its escalation edges; (3) slices -> phase -> gates - a per-WI slice ends at the commit bar, a phase closes at the gate bar, CI runs the same bar every push. Data-derived where a canonical source exists (docs/gate, the spine registries, the WI registry (docs/work/)); self-contained and byte-deterministic; a data-less repo renders byte-identically; --check freshness unchanged.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-050.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-050)", "title": "Process reference view", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-051": {"description": "SR-051 is superseded by SR-089;SR-090;SR-091;SR-092; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-051.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-051)", "title": "Superseded: Tiered drill-down views", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-052": {"description": "PROJECT_STATE.html shall be operable and readable without a pointer or full color perception: every interactive element (tabs, expandable blocks, SVG nodes with detail panels) is keyboard-reachable and carries an accessible name (title/aria/visible text); no information is encoded by color alone (status/phase/type encodings carry a text or shape cue); text keeps readable contrast against its fill.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-052.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-052)", "title": "Dashboard accessibility (rubric-adjudicated)", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-053": {"description": "PROJECT_STATE.html shall read as one system across its tabs and views: one type scale and spacing rhythm; one status/phase/type color vocabulary applied consistently wherever the same concept renders; uniform node, edge, legend, and detail-panel styling across the SVG emitters; the same interaction idiom (expand, hover, detail) wherever the same structure appears.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-053.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-053)", "title": "Dashboard UI uniformity (rubric-adjudicated)", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-054": {"description": "PROJECT_STATE.html shall support its core reading tasks with low friction: a first-time reviewer finds the project state, the next work, and how the parts connect, each within one tab switch; views default to a legible density (start-collapsed per the greater-than-3 rule) and reveal detail without losing context; labels stay readable at default zoom with no clipped or overlapping text.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-054.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-054)", "title": "Dashboard usability (rubric-adjudicated)", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-055": {"description": "The Process tab shall additionally render the project's two circular working loops as linked flow panels: (A) the intake loop - owner/agent intake -> triage into work items with spec detail -> the scheduler-derived ready frontier in the WI registry (docs/work/) -> build/review -> merge; and (B) the human-decision loop - open-items population (including the gate-ratification table) -> human review and ruling -> the log's Decisions record -> merge - with the LLM_Agent entry point rendered once and shared by both loops; each stage links to its canonical home (status.md, the WI registry (docs/work/), open-items.html, log.md); the panels stay data-derived where a canonical source exists, self-contained, byte-deterministic; a data-less repo renders byte-identically and --check freshness is unchanged.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-055.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-055)", "title": "Ingest and human-decision process loops", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-056": {"description": "The tiered decomposition views shall render right-sized columns (a tier column no wider than its content requires within a declared bound); one horizontal parent-to-child arrow per containment edge, making containment explicit rather than implied by nesting alone; and a hover/focus highlight that persists on the last-hovered item until another takes it (no flash-on-exit); self-contained, byte-deterministic, --check freshness unchanged.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-056.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-056)", "title": "Decomposition render polish", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-057": {"description": "A stdlib schedule.py library/CLI shall derive the dependency-ready frontier from the tracked WI registry plus the active claims - never from prose - excluding blocked (queued + blockref), deferred, claimed, protected-conflicting and exclusive-conflicting WIs, and exposing ready --explain, ready --format json and simulate --jobs N.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-057.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-057)", "title": "WI-DAG frontier scheduling", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-058": {"description": "SR-058 is superseded by SR-093;SR-094;SR-095; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-058.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-058)", "title": "Superseded: Deterministic safety classification", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-059": {"description": "The migration shall delete docs/next-wi and docs/run-phase outright (fresh scaffold and migrated repo) and remove every live dependency on them; docs/status.md's derived snapshot is GENERATED (gen_trajectory --status) and freshness-gated, never hand-copied. (The generated docs/run-state surface this row once paired with retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5 - the stop banner and exit codes carry the outcome.)", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-059.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-059)", "title": "Retire next-wi; generated status surface", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-060": {"description": "The session engine shall run explicit per-worker claimed assignments (--wi, on the branch integrate.py claim cut; the session tag defaults to the branch name): a worker prompt is assembled from AGENTS.md, the WI row, its SpecRef, predecessor context, the current branch diff, and any rework finding (never docs/status.md or docs/next-wi); worker branches never edit root status, other branches' claims, the root log, or generated artifacts, and the result channel is committed trailer evidence (WI: / Blocked-WI: + BlockRef:).", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-060.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-060)", "title": "Explicit worker assignment mode", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-061": {"description": "SR-061 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-061.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-061)", "title": "Superseded: Parallel-by-default dispatcher", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-062": {"description": "SR-062 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-062.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-062)", "title": "Superseded: Change-train continuation", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-063": {"description": "SR-063 is superseded by SR-096;SR-097;SR-098; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-063.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-063)", "title": "Superseded: Atomic serialized integration", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-064": {"description": "SR-064 is superseded by SR-099;SR-100;SR-101; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-064.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-064)", "title": "Superseded: Crash safety and git-as-authority recovery", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-065": {"description": "SR-065 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-065.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-065)", "title": "Superseded: Parallel-execution telemetry and downstream migration", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-066": {"description": "SR-066 is superseded by SR-102;SR-103;SR-104;SR-105;SR-106;SR-107;SR-108; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-066.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-066)", "title": "Superseded: Dual-plan decomposition round", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-067": {"description": "check_trajectory.py shall validate WI id syntax, predecessor resolution, and acyclic hard dependencies; an absent or placeholder-only registry is vacuous and docs/trajectory-check: off opts out.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-067.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-067)", "title": "Work-item dependency validation", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-068": {"description": "check_trajectory.py shall require Deliverable to be non-empty exactly when Status is done on every run and, under --strict, require each open WI SpecRef to resolve in-repo.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-068.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-068)", "title": "Work-item row coherence", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-069": {"description": "check_trajectory.py shall flag a done WI token in hand-edited status.md, warning plain and failing --strict, while standing down for the generated marker, open or unknown ids, opt-out, and absent status.md.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-069.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-069)", "title": "Generated status source-of-truth guard", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-070": {"description": "gen_trajectory.py shall generate one self-contained root HTML file containing completeness tiles, the SN-to-SR-to-LLR-to-TC hierarchy, and the WI roadmap DAG.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/system-requirements/SR-070.md", "resource": "docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv (SR-070)", "title": "Offline project-state core", "type": "System Requirement"}, "SR-071": {"description": "gen_trajectory.py shall render software/component and OKF knowledge views only when their source bundles exist, omitting each view when its source is absent.", "fill": "#0e7490", "href": "docs/okf/