   <script>
-    const archDetails = {"SN-001": {"tier": "sn", "title": "A team can drop the kit into a new or existing repo and get a working gated, requirement-traced process without hand-building the tooling.", "body": "A team can drop the kit into a new or existing repo and get a working gated, requirement-traced process without hand-building the tooling.", "meta": "Why: It is the kit's reason to exist — a *reusable starting point*. · Acceptance: bootstrap.py --dest <repo> produces a scaffold whose harness runs green out of the box; a re-sync onto an existing repo never clobbers the repo's own files.", "status": ""}, "SN-002": {"tier": "sn", "title": "The trace from need → requirement → design → test is mechanically verified, not manually asserted: every requirement links to a need and a test before a gate.", "body": "The trace from need → requirement → design → test is mechanically verified, not manually asserted: every requirement links to a need and a test before a gate.", "meta": "Why: *Requirement-traced … trust what ships*; a hand-maintained trace rots. · Acceptance: trace.py --strict reports zero orphans across the joined SN→SR→LLR→TC spine; a malformed/duplicate id fails at any stage.", "status": ""}, "SN-003": {"tier": "sn", "title": "The kit is stack-agnostic — a non-Python project uses it by re-pointing the harness at that stack's tools, with Python only as the reference.", "body": "The kit is stack-agnostic — a non-Python project uses it by re-pointing the harness at that stack's tools, with Python only as the reference.", "meta": "Why: *Stack-agnostic with Python-first reference scripts.* · Acceptance: The toolchain is declared once in docs/stack.ini; a stack swap edits that file, not a kit script; a non-Python bootstrap profile omits Python-only artifacts.", "status": ""}, "SN-004": {"tier": "sn", "title": "Progress advances only through explicit approval gates (G1→G2→G3→…), and a gate passes only when its mechanical bar is met.", "body": "Progress advances only through explicit approval gates (G1→G2→G3→…), and a gate passes only when its mechanical bar is met.", "meta": "Why: *Explicit approval gates so you can trust what ships.* · Acceptance: check.py --gate GN enforces that gate's required steps; a missing required tool fails, never silently skips.", "status": ""}, "SN-005": {"tier": "sn", "title": "AI agents and humans work from the same playbook, with the process enforced agent-neutrally (git hooks + CI), not by trusting any one agent.", "body": "AI agents and humans work from the same playbook, with the process enforced agent-neutrally (git hooks + CI), not by trusting any one agent.", "meta": "Why: *AI agents and humans working from the same playbook*; enforcement can't depend on which agent showed up. · Acceptance: The enforcement floor is git + CI running the *same* harness a human runs; per-agent configs only mirror it, never replace it.", "status": ""}, "SN-006": {"tier": "sn", "title": "An agent can run unattended and resume from repo text alone; such a run never blocks on a prompt and fails clearly.", "body": "An agent can run unattended and resume from repo text alone; such a run never blocks on a prompt and fails clearly.", "meta": "Why: Agents develop here; a walk-away run must be safe and resumable. · Acceptance: agent_loop.py resumes from docs/status.md, exits a typed code at each end state, and a preflight refuses a broken footing (no agent CLI, not a git repo, private author under privacy-check) rather than hanging.", "status": ""}, "SN-007": {"tier": "sn", "title": "The kit's own changes stay traceable and tested — a change to a script is covered by a test exercised end-to-end against a real scaffold.", "body": "The kit's own changes stay traceable and tested — a change to a script is covered by a test exercised end-to-end against a real scaffold.", "meta": "Why: The kit is a foundation many repos inherit; a regression here propagates downstream. (This need is what Thread 47 makes first-class.) · Acceptance: The suite bootstraps a temp scaffold and runs every script; pytest -q green is required before each change lands.", "status": ""}, "SN-008": {"tier": "sn", "title": "Gates are honest — a green never hides a skipped check, a stub, or an unmet criterion.", "body": "Gates are honest — a green never hides a skipped check, a stub, or an unmet criterion.", "meta": "Why: *Trust what ships*; a false green is worse than a red. · Acceptance: check.py fails (not skips) on a missing required tool — the explicitly-requested --lenient local mode is the one sanctioned degrade to SKIP (SR-006), never a CI/gate default; the no-stub detector and the privacy/secrets floor run at their declared gate.", "status": ""}, "SN-009": {"tier": "sn", "title": "A committed secret or private identity is caught before it publishes, in every repo, without extra setup.", "body": "A committed secret or private identity is caught before it publishes, in every repo, without extra setup.", "meta": "Why: Trust + safety; a leaked key or PII is near-irreversible once pushed. · Acceptance: check_privacy.py's always-on secrets floor scans every commit's staged diff, message, and outgoing range; the privacy gate adds PII/identity classes when the privacy_check dial is on.", "status": ""}, "SN-010": {"tier": "sn", "title": "Documentation stays navigable and honest — links resolve, the vision is declared once, and generated views cannot silently rot.", "body": "Documentation stays navigable and honest — links resolve, the vision is declared once, and generated views cannot silently rot.", "meta": "Why: *Readable and correct over the long run.* · Acceptance: check_docs.py fails on a broken intra-repo link or a missing PROJECT-VISION tag; every generated artifact carries a --check freshness contract.", "status": ""}, "SN-011": {"tier": "sn", "title": "The kit's scripts run on a clean Python 3.11+ with minimal, argued dependencies — stdlib by default, a non-stdlib dependency admitted only through a reviewed ledger row — on Windows and POSIX (and macOS).", "body": "The kit's scripts run on a clean Python 3.11+ with minimal, argued dependencies — stdlib by default, a non-stdlib dependency admitted only through a reviewed ledger row — on Windows and POSIX (and macOS).", "meta": "Why: Portability — the kit must not impose an *unargued* dependency stack on a downstream repo to run its checks. But a blanket \"no dependencies\" rule is itself a design constraint: it can force a worse hand-rolled alternative than a well-chosen tool, so the bar is argument, not abstinence (owner RULING-3, 2026-07-28). · Acceptance: Every non-stdlib import in the kit's scripts is declared as a row in docs/dependencies.md naming what it replaces, why hand-rolling is worse, and the ruling that admitted it — tests/test_dependency_ledger.py fails the suite on an undeclared import; checks an adopter runs (shipped tier) stay stdlib-*preferred*, since a dependency there forces every adopter to install it; the CI matrix is green on Linux + Windows + macOS.", "status": ""}, "SN-012": {"tier": "sn", "title": "The process is right-sized, not ceremony for its own sake — small changes stay cheap, and heavy layers are opt-in.", "body": "The process is right-sized, not ceremony for its own sake — small changes stay cheap, and heavy layers are opt-in.", "meta": "Why: An over-heavy process is abandoned; adoption depends on proportionality. · Acceptance: Opt-in layers (perf, guardrails, unattended, parallel tracks, OKF export) cost a repo that doesn't use them nothing; the proportionality doctrine governs LLR/TC granularity.", "status": ""}, "SN-013": {"tier": "sn", "title": "No Python 3 interpreter on PATH (or the Windows Store alias that resolves but exits nonzero)", "body": "No Python 3 interpreter on PATH (or the Windows Store alias that resolves but exits nonzero)", "meta": "Why: Provision · Acceptance: The git hooks / coordinator probe by running a candidate and skip-or-report clearly; they never crash cryptically.", "status": ""}, "SN-014": {"tier": "sn", "title": "A required harness tool is absent", "body": "A required harness tool is absent", "meta": "Why: Provision · Acceptance: check.py reports SKIP(missing) and fails the gate — a missing tool is never a silent pass (SN-008).", "status": ""}, "SN-015": {"tier": "sn", "title": "The working directory is not a git repository", "body": "The working directory is not a git repository", "meta": "Why: Startup · Acceptance: The coordinator preflight reports \"not a git repo\" and exits nonzero; it never hangs.", "status": ""}, "SN-016": {"tier": "sn", "title": "An unattended run must never block on a prompt at launch or mid-run", "body": "An unattended run must never block on a prompt at launch or mid-run", "meta": "Why: Startup→Runtime · Acceptance: agent_loop.py runs headless (stdin closed); a rate limit backs off, a stall aborts to protect the budget — the loop is never wedged by a prompt.", "status": ""}, "SN-017": {"tier": "sn", "title": "The coordinator is killed / power-lost mid-session", "body": "The coordinator is killed / power-lost mid-session", "meta": "Why: Runtime · Acceptance: The per-worktree lock is a kernel advisory lock the OS releases on death, so the next run is not wedged (no stale-pid file). *(tests/test_agent_loop.py::test_lock_auto_released_when_holder_dies)*", "status": ""}, "SN-018": {"tier": "sn", "title": "A second coordinator is launched in the same checkout", "body": "A second coordinator is launched in the same checkout", "meta": "Why: Runtime · Acceptance: It is refused rather than risking a two-writer race. *(tests/test_agent_loop.py::test_lock_excludes_a_second_process)*", "status": ""}, "SN-019": {"tier": "sn", "title": "A repo with no commits yet (HEAD absent)", "body": "A repo with no commits yet (HEAD absent)", "meta": "Why: Runtime · Acceptance: The coordinator's rev-parse guard does not crash the loop. *(tests/test_agent_loop.py::test_zero_commit_repo_is_guarded)*", "status": ""}, "SN-020": {"tier": "sn", "title": "The agent CLI / model errors before doing work (retired model, expired auth)", "body": "The agent CLI / model errors before doing work (retired model, expired auth)", "meta": "Why: Runtime · Acceptance: The session is logged ERROR and an all-ERROR stall is reported as an unavailable agent, not a work stall. *(tests/test_agent_loop.py — error region)*", "status": ""}, "SN-021": {"tier": "sn", "title": "A generated artifact (arch map, trace view) drifts from its source", "body": "A generated artifact (arch map, trace view) drifts from its source", "meta": "Why: Runtime · Acceptance: Its --check fails at the gate — a stale generated doc is a red, not a silent rot (SN-010).", "status": ""}, "SN-022": {"tier": "sn", "title": "A committed example row / placeholder is left in a registry at a gate", "body": "A committed example row / placeholder is left in a registry at a gate", "meta": "Why: Runtime · Acceptance: --no-placeholders flags a leftover -000 row from G2 on; a fresh scaffold stays green until it claims a gate.", "status": ""}, "SN-023": {"tier": "sn", "title": "A reviewer can see the project's progress and how its parts connect from one dashboard-like file.", "body": "A reviewer can see the project's progress and how its parts connect from one dashboard-like file.", "meta": "Why: *Readable and correct over the long run*; the relationships between the parts (which module talks to which, over what contract) are as much of the project's truth as its requirement tree, and scattering them hides architectural drift. · Acceptance: The root PROJECT_STATE.html renders both the roadmap/decomposition and the declared interface graph; every module in the arch-map inventory is a declared IF-### interface endpoint or an explicit source/sink, checked mechanically (warn-first).", "status": ""}, "SN-024": {"tier": "sn", "title": "Subjective/perceptual acceptance — a realistic-looking render, an artifact comparison with no crisp measurable interface — is adjudicated by an independent critical eye against a written rubric, never by the session that authored the artifact.", "body": "Subjective/perceptual acceptance — a realistic-looking render, an artifact comparison with no crisp measurable interface — is adjudicated by an independent critical eye against a written rubric, never by the session that authored the artifact.", "meta": "Why: *Trust what ships*; an implementer session cannot judge its own output, and a lax test case lets awkward work ship because nothing gave it the critical eye — another agent wearing a different hat must say *where and why* it isn't good enough and drive rework. · Acceptance: A Verification=Critique requirement is judged by a fresh, family-heterogeneous critique session against a docs/rubrics/ rubric derived from the SN/SR intent (not the possibly-lax TC); the verdict cites numbered rubric anchor ids, bounded iteration drives rework, and the loop escalates to the human on budget exhaustion.", "status": ""}, "SN-025": {"tier": "sn", "title": "A single command from the repo root (agent-resume) lets a configured LLM agent implement toward the vision — fully autonomously where enabled — with no human curating what comes next.", "body": "A single command from the repo root (agent-resume) lets a configured LLM agent implement toward the vision — fully autonomously where enabled — with no human curating what comes next.", "meta": "Why: Walk-away autonomy is the whole promise of the unattended layer: if a human must hand-maintain a next-step pointer, the loop is attended in practice and the pointer rots against the registry it paraphrases. Extends SN-006 (a *resumable* unattended run) into a *self-directing* one. · Acceptance: A plain launch derives what to do next from the tracked WI DAG plus Git — never from prose or a hand-maintained pointer (the docs/next-wi file this need made unnecessary, retired by WI-180) and never from predefined tracks; the ready frontier is ordered deterministically, so two readers of the same registry dispatch the same work; the status surface a human reads is generated, never hand-copied.", "status": ""}, "SN-026": {"tier": "sn", "title": "Several LLM families are configurable — selected per job and per capability level — and work that benefits from an independent second opinion is automatically routed to a *different* family wherever that is configured.", "body": "Several LLM families are configurable — selected per job and per capability level — and work that benefits from an independent second opinion is automatically routed to a *different* family wherever that is configured.", "meta": "Why: One family is a single point of both failure and bias: an outage, a retired model or an expired auth stalls the whole loop, and a reviewer drawn from the implementer's own family shares its blind spots — so a same-family review reads like corroboration while adding little. Declaring the families and levels in a registry, rather than hard-coding one vendor, is also what keeps the kit vendor-neutral as models turn over. · Acceptance: docs/agents.csv declares (family × model × tier) pair-rows and docs/agents-enabled is the consent surface whose presence turns managed selection on; the coordinator resolves a row per in-process phase and tier, prefers a cross-family draw for the reviewer, critic and planner-pair sessions, degrades to the *documented* same-family mode when only one family is routable rather than silently skipping the second opinion, and logs every selection before launch — never a silent model swap.", "status": ""}, "SN-027": {"tier": "sn", "title": "Ready work fans out across bounded parallel lanes, while mutation of the integration branch stays serialized and gated.", "body": "Ready work fans out across bounded parallel lanes, while mutation of the integration branch stays serialized and gated.", "meta": "Why: Throughput: the WI DAG already encodes what may proceed, so a frontier that advances one item at a time idles for no reason. But parallel *integration* is precisely how a reviewed process ships an unreviewed tree — so the fan-out must narrow back to a single serialized, gated seam before anything lands. · Acceptance: A launch with independent ready WIs runs up to the configured worker ceiling (default 2) concurrently in isolated worktrees; every finished branch lands through one serial, fail-closed integrator that runs the declared bar on the composed tree; --jobs 1 preserves the serial semantic; a declared pause stops claiming and drains what is already in flight; a crash at any lifecycle boundary recovers from Git history alone, without double-assignment or half-integrated authoritative state. Spec of record: docs/archive/specs/parallel-wi-dispatch.2026-07-20.md + docs/concurrency-restructure.md.", "status": ""}, "SN-028": {"tier": "sn", "title": "Every policy dial has one home — a single hand-edited, machine-read file — and a repo that declares the same dial twice is REFUSED rather than resolved by precedence.", "body": "Every policy dial has one home — a single hand-edited, machine-read file — and a repo that declares the same dial twice is REFUSED rather than resolved by precedence.", "meta": "Why: The dials had accreted into ~10 one-word files under docs/, an idiom that was never ruled: it was held in place by the git hooks' pure-sh parse (a Python-less box must still fail closed on a declared privacy gate) and by a 3.8 floor with no tomllib. Both reasons are spent. Scattering costs an owner one file per question and a reader five ad-hoc parsers; worse, two sources for one dial is exactly the state where two readers disagree about the same policy, and a precedence rule makes that disagreement invisible instead of loud. · Acceptance: docs/process.toml holds every process dial under bare [section] headers, one key = value per line; the SHAPE is checked rather than conventional, because two grammars read the file (tomllib and the hooks' sh) and every shape only one of them understands is a silent flip of a security gate; the two readings are pinned equal over a table of adversarial files; a legacy one-word file still present alongside its key is a REFUSAL naming both, and bootstrap.py --migrate-config (run by bootstrap and by the documented re-sync) converts and deletes the legacy files so an adopter never meets that refusal un-aided; a wrong-typed or out-of-range dial is refused, never defaulted.", "status": ""}, "SN-029": {"tier": "sn", "title": "An autonomous run gets as far as it honestly can. Once triggered, the coordinator stops for a human judgement only when the declared ratification level reserves that tier for a human, when a round cannot converge on its own, or when requirement/test documentation is introduced or amended such that the gate drops below what automation is permitted to attest.", "body": "An autonomous run gets as far as it honestly can. Once triggered, the coordinator stops for a human judgement only when the declared ratification level reserves that tier for a human, when a round cannot converge on its own, or when requirement/test documentation is introduced or amended such that the gate drops below what automation is permitted to attest.", "meta": "Why: The unattended layer only pays for itself if a walk-away run actually walks away — so what stops it must be exactly the set of judgements the owner reserved, and nothing else. The three-value gate-authority enum (attended / single-ratify / autonomous) could not express \"TCs are human-held but LLRs are not\", which is the distinction an owner actually wants to dial; four independent tables each re-interpreted the same word, each with its own fail-safe direction. And \"has this been ratified?\" was answered by walking git for the newest commit where a row read Verified — sound only while every amendment flips its row in the same commit, which is precisely the sanctioned path the amendment detector deliberately ignores. So the blessed way to amend an attested requirement was invisible to every consumer, and a stakeholder need had no anchor at all (an SN has no Status cell). · Acceptance: A cumulative 0-4 level names the highest tier a human still ratifies, compared against a separately derived SPINE STAGE (which tier is in process) with a declared mapping to the harness gate; every failure direction — an unreadable stage, an out-of-range level, a wrong-typed dial — resolves toward MORE human involvement, because the failure that matters is a machine ratifying something a human meant to hold; each acceptance is anchored ON THE ACCEPTED ARTIFACT'S OWN ROW — the commit whose tree carries the accepted text, plus a digest of that row's normative cells — never in a second registry keyed on the same artifact; and text that has moved away from what was accepted surfaces regardless of any Status movement, which is the one signal the sanctioned amend-and-flip path would otherwise hide — so an AMENDED requirement drops the derived stage exactly as a newly introduced one does.", "status": ""}, "SR-001": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Trace join reports orphans", "body": "trace.py shall join the SN->SR->LLR->TC registries and, with --strict, exit nonzero if any orphan exists.", "meta": "Acceptance: --strict exits 0 on a fully linked chain and 1 when any SR lacks an LLR/TC, any LLR/TC lacks a parent, or any SN lacks an SR; the orphan list names each at-fault id.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-002": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Integrity floor for ids and CSV structure", "body": "trace.py shall fail --strict-integrity on a duplicated or malformed registry id, a data row whose column count differs from its header, or a TC that pairs an LLR with an SR it does not decompose.", "meta": "Acceptance: --strict-integrity exits 1 on a duplicate/malformed SR/LLR/TC/PB/REPO/MOD/PART/ASSET/CMP/IF id, a mis-columned row, or an incoherent SR/LLR pairing in one TC, and 0 otherwise; orphans do not affect it. MOD is the legacy compatibility id for current REPO rows.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-003": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Placeholder and schema checks", "body": "trace.py shall, under --no-placeholders, flag any leftover -000 example row, and under --strict-schema, require non-empty required fields and in-vocabulary Verification/Tier values.", "meta": "Acceptance: --no-placeholders reports every -000 row across SR/LLR/TC and the SN doc; --strict-schema reports an empty required field or an out-of-vocabulary Verification/Tier value.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-004": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Acceptance-criteria advisory", "body": "trace.py shall warn (never fail) when an SR AcceptanceCriteria uses a comparative term with no pinning predicate in the cell.", "meta": "Acceptance: A comparative claim with no pinning predicate marker emits a stdout WARNING and a report line; the exit code is unchanged.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-005": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Off-spine registries stay traceable", "body": "trace.py shall integrity-check the optional off-spine registries (PB/REPO/PART/ASSET, plus legacy MOD compatibility) and validate their back-links without blocking a project that does not use them.", "meta": "Acceptance: A PB row whose Refs name no real SR/LLR/Module, or a REPO row (including a legacy MOD row) delegating an unknown SR, is a finding; a -000 example row in any of them is ignored.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-006": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Gate/tier harness enforces required steps", "body": "check.py shall run the active gate's required steps and fail the gate when a required tool is missing, reporting SKIP(missing) rather than silently passing.", "meta": "Acceptance: check.py --gate GN runs that gate's steps; a required-but-absent tool fails the gate with guidance (never a silent pass), and --lenient degrades it to SKIP; --tier selects the declared step subset.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-007": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Declared stack profile", "body": "check.py shall read the toolchain (format/lint/test commands, tiers, coverage, arch-map mode) from docs/stack.ini so a stack swap edits only that file.", "meta": "Acceptance: check.py resolves each step's command from stack.ini; changing the profile changes the commands run, with no script edit.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-008": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Profile validation fails loudly", "body": "check.py shall fail loudly on a malformed stack.ini, a non-integer coverage threshold, or a missing declared binary, rather than proceeding on a bad profile.", "meta": "Acceptance: A malformed profile, non-integer coverage, or missing binary produces a clear nonzero failure at the relevant step.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-009": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Conditional scaffold profiles", "body": "bootstrap.py shall seed only the artifacts a chosen profile uses, omitting Python-only files for a non-Python stack.", "meta": "Acceptance: A non-Python profile omits Python-only artifacts and seeds files-mode arch-map; a profile that selects no opt-in layers seeds none of them.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-010": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Scaffold runs green out of the box", "body": "bootstrap.py shall produce a scaffold whose harness runs green immediately after generation.", "meta": "Acceptance: bootstrap.py --dest produces a scaffold on which the harness passes; the meta-suite runs every script against that scaffold.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-011": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Idempotent re-runnable scaffold", "body": "bootstrap.py shall skip an existing file on a re-run unless --force is passed.", "meta": "Acceptance: A re-run leaves every existing file (kit- or project-owned) byte-unchanged without --force; --force overwrites.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-012": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Doc navigability", "body": "check_docs.py shall fail on a broken intra-repo link or a missing PROJECT-VISION tag, and check generated-doc freshness under --stale.", "meta": "Acceptance: check_docs.py --stale fails on a dead intra-repo link or absent PROJECT-VISION tag and passes a clean doc set.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-013": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Runtime-flows check", "body": "check_flows.py shall validate that the authored runtime-flow docs conform to their contract.", "meta": "Acceptance: check_flows.py passes a conformant flow doc and fails a malformed one.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-014": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Perf comparator and budgets", "body": "check_perf.py shall compare a run against declared budgets and fail when a metric regresses beyond its tolerance.", "meta": "Acceptance: A metric within tolerance passes; one beyond its budget's tolerance fails at the declared block/warn level.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-015": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Performance-budget back-links", "body": "The performance-budgets registry (PB-###) shall keep every row's Refs resolvable to a real SR/LLR/Module.", "meta": "Acceptance: A PB row with an unresolvable or empty Refs is a finding; a resolvable one passes.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-016": {"tier": "sr", "title": "No-stub detector", "body": "check_stubs.py shall detect stub/placeholder markers in source at its declared gate and fail when one is present.", "meta": "Acceptance: A stub/NotImplemented/pass-only public function at the declared gate fails; clean source passes.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-017": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Always-on secrets floor", "body": "check_privacy.py shall scan every commit's staged diff, message, and outgoing range for secret patterns regardless of the privacy toggle, unless docs/process.toml [policies] secrets_scan is explicitly false (the legacy docs/secrets-scan is still read through the migration window).", "meta": "Acceptance: A staged PEM header, token, or key pattern is flagged and blocks; a clean diff passes; the floor runs with privacy-check off, and is disabled only by an explicit secrets_scan = false.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-018": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Privacy gate two-axis", "body": "check_privacy.py shall add PII/identity classes when docs/privacy-check is on, exempting configured author identities.", "meta": "Acceptance: With privacy-check on, an identity/PII class is flagged unless the author is in EXEMPT_EMAILS; with it off, only the secrets floor runs.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-019": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Pre-commit hook floor", "body": "The pre-commit hook shall run the integrity + secrets floor on every commit, agent-neutrally.", "meta": "Acceptance: The hook runs trace --strict-integrity and the secrets scan on staged content and blocks a commit that fails either.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-020": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Pre-push hook outgoing scan", "body": "The pre-push hook shall scan the outgoing commit range and block a push that publishes a secret or (under privacy-check) a private identity.", "meta": "Acceptance: The hook scans the push range and blocks when a secret (always) or an identity/PII class (privacy-check on) appears.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-021": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Hook python probe", "body": "The git hooks shall locate a working python3 by running a candidate and skip-or-report clearly when none resolves or a Store-alias exits nonzero.", "meta": "Acceptance: With no working python3 the hook reports clearly and does not crash; with one, it runs the checks.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-022": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Vendored-doc drift", "body": "check_vendored.py shall detect a vendored copy of an upstream doc drifting from its source.", "meta": "Acceptance: A drifted vendored copy is a finding; an in-sync copy passes.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-023": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Arch-map generation", "body": "gen_arch_map.py shall regenerate the symbol map inside its marker block and, under --check, fail when the committed map has drifted from source.", "meta": "Acceptance: --check fails when the map is stale and passes when current; regeneration rewrites only the marker block.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-024": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Permutation case generation", "body": "gen_cases.py shall expand a Permutations spec into concrete dimensional cases.", "meta": "Acceptance: A spec grammar (range/set/bool with @full|@pairwise|@boundaries) yields the expected case set.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-025": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Skills index + checked per-agent fan-out", "body": "gen_skills_index.py shall regenerate skills/INDEX.csv from each SKILL.md applicability frontmatter.", "meta": "Acceptance: The regenerated INDEX.csv reflects the current SKILL.md frontmatter, and a drifted index is detectable.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-026": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Coordinator resumes headless", "body": "agent_loop.py shall resume headless with stdin closed, never blocking on a prompt. Resume authority (one path - the serial resume loop is retired): a worker resumes from its explicit claimed assignment plus the committed trailer evidence on its branch, and the integrator (SR-132) derives claim/queue state from trunk history alone; docs/status.md is a generated reference surface (SR-059), never a session input.", "meta": "Acceptance: A worker reconciles from its assignment + committed evidence and the integrator from trunk history; every mode runs with stdin closed and a prompt cannot wedge it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-027": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Coordinator preflight", "body": "agent_loop.py shall refuse a broken footing in preflight — not a git repo, no agent CLI, or a private author under privacy-check — exiting a typed nonzero code rather than hanging.", "meta": "Acceptance: Outside a git repo, with no agent CLI, or with a private author under privacy-check, preflight exits a typed nonzero code.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-028": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Coordinator typed outcomes", "body": "agent_loop.py shall end each session in a typed outcome code, guard a zero-commit HEAD, and report an all-ERROR stall as an unavailable agent rather than a work stall.", "meta": "Acceptance: A repo with no commits does not crash the loop; an agent-CLI error is logged ERROR; an all-ERROR region is reported as an unavailable agent.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-029": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Coordinator lock auto-release", "body": "The per-worktree coordinator lock shall be a kernel advisory lock the OS releases on process death, leaving no stale-pid wedge.", "meta": "Acceptance: After the lock holder dies, the next run acquires the lock; no stale-pid file blocks it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-030": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Second coordinator refused", "body": "agent_loop.py shall refuse a second coordinator in the same checkout, preventing a two-writer race.", "meta": "Acceptance: A second coordinator on a held lock is refused; only one writer runs per checkout.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-031": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Declared-policy readers agree", "body": "The policy readers shall share one parse so the hooks, agent_loop and check_privacy agree on every declared value. Since SN-028 that parse is docs/process.toml, read by two grammars — tomllib and the hooks' pure sh — whose agreement over the file's checked line shape is itself driven; the legacy one-word files stay readable through the migration window, and a dial declared in both homes is REFUSED rather than resolved by precedence.", "meta": "Acceptance: Every enforcer reads the same value for the same dial; the hooks' sh and tomllib return the same answer over the adversarial file-shape table (including a trailing-comment decoy, which once made the privacy gate fail OPEN); a legacy file present alongside its key is a refusal naming both.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-032": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Onboarding and dev-setup scaffold", "body": "The onboarding and dev-setup templates shall scaffold and run to a green developer setup.", "meta": "Acceptance: The scaffolded onboard/dev-setup scripts run to a green setup.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-033": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Release checklist generation", "body": "gen_release_checklist.py shall emit the release-gate checklist, including the warn-tier performance budgets that never fail a gate.", "meta": "Acceptance: Running the generator emits the checklist content: the perf-budget section lists each warn-tier PB with its id and allocation.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-034": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Kit scripts run on stdlib plus ledger-declared dependencies", "body": "Every kit script shall run on a clean Python 3.11+ using the standard library plus only those non-stdlib dependencies declared as a reviewed row in docs/dependencies.md.", "meta": "Acceptance: An AST scan of scripts/*.py resolves every top-level import to stdlib, a local sibling, or a Kind=python row in docs/dependencies.md; an undeclared non-stdlib import fails the suite; every declared row names what it replaces, why hand-rolling is worse, and the ruling that admitted it; a shipped-tier row (one an adopter is forced to install) exists only with an owner ruling recorded in the ledger, stdlib remaining preferred there.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-035": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Stack-agnostic across OSes", "body": "The process and ID scheme shall be stack-agnostic.", "meta": "Acceptance: The ID scheme is language-neutral.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-036": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Deliberate re-sync integration", "body": "A re-sync of an existing adoption shall integrate relevant kit updates without clobbering the adopter's filled-in files, per ADOPTING.md section 6 — take kit-owned scripts/hooks wholesale, regenerate generated docs, and preserve filled-in registries/config.", "meta": "Acceptance: Following ADOPTING.md section 6 against the docs/kit-version diff, kit-owned files are taken wholesale, generated docs regenerated, and filled-in files preserved.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-037": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Work-item registry validation", "body": "SR-037 is superseded by SR-067;SR-068;SR-069; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "meta": "Acceptance: Every listed target resolves; the supersession graph is acyclic; no LLR decomposes this row; TC-099 inspects the frozen migration map.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-038": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Offline project-state view", "body": "SR-038 is superseded by SR-070;SR-071;SR-072; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "meta": "Acceptance: Every listed target resolves; the supersession graph is acyclic; no LLR decomposes this row; TC-099 inspects the frozen migration map.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-039": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Duplicate-code lint", "body": "check_dupes.py shall report any window of at least a configurable minimum of consecutive significant source tokens appearing at more than one location, and exit nonzero unless the finding's block fingerprint plus file pair (or a legacy bare file pair) is recorded in the census.", "meta": "Acceptance: A copy-pasted helper across two files exits 1 naming both file:line locations; clean source exits 0; raising --min-tokens above the block's length passes; a docs/dupes-allow fingerprint+pair line suppresses that block while a new distinct block in the same pair still fails; --emit-census regenerates the lines.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-040": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Per-phase routing and review dial", "body": "The unattended coordinator shall select the agent command template per in-process session phase (PLAN/BUILD/REVIEW-A/REVIEW-B/DESIGN-CHECK/CRITIQUE - the coordinator's own activity; the retired docs/run-phase file, deleted by SR-059, is not an input) via AGENT_CMD_MAP/--cmd-map, falling back to the single AGENT_CMD; surface the declared reviewer dial (docs/review-policy, default 1) at run start without enforcing it; and warn (never block) when a lane resume surface exceeds the declared size threshold.", "meta": "Acceptance: A mapped session phase invokes its own command template and the default template does not fire; a broken map entry fails preflight before iteration 1; the banner surfaces the reviewer dial from docs/process.toml [policies] review_rounds; an oversized status.md warns on stderr while the run proceeds; AGENT_STATUS_WARN_BYTES=0 silences.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-041": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Doc reference validation", "body": "check_doc_refs.py shall flag a documentation reference to a repository path that does not exist and an asserted sym:<module>.<name> reference missing from the generated module-map inventory, warn-first (exit 0) with --strict gating; absent inventory skips the symbol tier cleanly.", "meta": "Acceptance: A backticked repo path that does not exist warns and gates only under --strict; URLs, globs, placeholders, generated blocks and path-ok lines are ignored; a dangling sym: reference is flagged against the module map; with no inventory the symbol tier skips and the path tier still runs.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-042": {"tier": "sr", "title": "OKF knowledge-bundle export", "body": "gen_okf.py shall export the spine registries AND the key process docs as a generated Open Knowledge Format bundle under docs/okf — one typed markdown concept per real row (graph as resolvable markdown links, per-tier indexes) plus a Process Guide concept per present process doc (a summary derived from that doc and a resource pointer to the unmodified source) — deterministic (no clocks), with --check failing on any stale, missing or extra bundle file; docs/okf-export off silences and placeholder-only registries are vacuous.", "meta": "Acceptance: One frontmatter-typed file per real SN/SR/LLR/TC row whose graph links resolve; a Process Guide concept for each present process doc carrying a summary derived from the doc (not hand-authored) with the source left unmodified; regeneration is byte-identical; --check trips on a registry OR doc edit, a missing bundle and a hand-added file; the write mode prunes extras; off and placeholder-only registries pass silently, and process guides emit only alongside a real spine.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-043": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Subagent spawn gate", "body": "A PreToolUse subagent-spawn gate shall, during an unattended run, refuse or defer subagent spawns per docs/subagent-gate (off|ask|deny; absent=off), honor a launcher-set SUBAGENT_GATE=allow override the model cannot set, and fail open on any error so a broken gate never wedges the tools.", "meta": "Acceptance: With docs/subagent-gate: deny a Task/Agent spawn is refused (permissionDecision=deny, exit 2); ask defers to approval; off/absent and SUBAGENT_GATE=allow allow it; a non-spawn tool is not gated; a malformed payload fails open (allow) and is logged.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-044": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Declared-interface connectivity", "body": "SR-044 is superseded by SR-073;SR-074;SR-075;SR-076;SR-077;SR-078; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "meta": "Acceptance: Every listed target resolves; the supersession graph is acyclic; no LLR decomposes this row; TC-099 inspects the frozen migration map.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-045": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Heterogeneous implementer/reviewer scheduling", "body": "SR-045 is superseded by SR-079;SR-080;SR-081;SR-082;SR-083; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "meta": "Acceptance: Every listed target resolves; the supersession graph is acyclic; no LLR decomposes this row; TC-099 inspects the frozen migration map.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-046": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Run capability menu", "body": "The root run.cmd/run.sh/run.command launchers shall present every major capability an evaluator runs from a single declaration - one name = command line (plus an optional name.desc line) per capability in docs/stack.ini's [run] section - via a stdlib scripts/run_menu.py that reads it: no args gives a numbered interactive menu, run_menu.py <name> launches one directly with exit-code passthrough, and --list emits a stable name<TAB>desc machine listing (the agent surface); an absent or empty [run] section prints the no-launch-command-wired-yet guidance and exits 1; the launch command is declared once (the duplicated RUN_CMD is retired) and works on Windows and POSIX.", "meta": "Acceptance: run_menu.py --list prints one name<TAB>desc line per declared capability in declaration order; run_menu.py <name> runs that capability's command and returns its exit code; the no-arg menu launches a stdin-selected capability non-interactively and does not hang on closed stdin; an absent/empty [run] section prints guidance and exits 1; a name.desc row renders in the menu and list; the run.* launchers delegate to run_menu.py with no RUN_CMD; the scaffold ships run_menu.py plus the delegate launchers.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-047": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Subjective-quality critique loop", "body": "SR-047 is superseded by SR-084;SR-085;SR-086; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "meta": "Acceptance: Every listed target resolves; the supersession graph is acyclic; no LLR decomposes this row; TC-099 inspects the frozen migration map.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-048": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: How-SW top view bounded and containerized", "body": "SR-048 is superseded by SR-087;SR-088; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "meta": "Acceptance: Every listed target resolves; the supersession graph is acyclic; no LLR decomposes this row; TC-099 inspects the frozen migration map.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-049": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Derived gate from artifact states", "body": "derive_gate.py shall compute the active gate from the spine artifact states (SN section-state + SR/LLR/TC Status maturity) and cache it to docs/gate rather than accept a hand-set value, so a gate advances only when the mechanical states do. Three Status values are recognized case-insensitively: Draft (G0 + decomposition exemptions), Verified (G3), and Modified — a post-attestation amendment owing a re-attest, deriving G2 through the existing decomposed-unverified rung with no arithmetic of its own; the cached basis line carries modified=N beside drafts=N so the pending state never hides.", "meta": "Acceptance: derive_gate.py --print on the meta computes the per-phase gates from the current states (a Modified SR's phase reads G2 until re-attested); --check exits nonzero when a state change is not reflected in the cached docs/gate basis; the compared line includes the modified=N count.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-050": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Process reference view", "body": "gen_trajectory.py shall render a Process reference tab in PROJECT_STATE.html beside the existing views, presenting how the project is built as three linked panels: (1) artifact lifecycle x gates - Vision -> SN (G1) -> SR (G1->G2) -> LLR + architecture (G2) -> TC (G2->G3) -> code+tests (G3), each stage linked to its process-doc section and annotated with this repo live tier counts; (2) the resume loop - the managed agent_loop flow (read status -> PLAN -> BUILD -> REVIEW -> INTEGRATE -> commit -> hook/gate -> repeat) with its escalation edges; (3) slices -> phase -> gates - a per-WI slice ends at the commit bar, a phase closes at the gate bar, CI runs the same bar every push. Data-derived where a canonical source exists (docs/gate, the spine registries, the WI registry (docs/work/)); self-contained and byte-deterministic; a data-less repo renders byte-identically; --check freshness unchanged.", "meta": "Acceptance: The Process tab renders the three panels; the current-gate highlight reflects docs/gate and the tier counts match the registries; process-doc links resolve; a bundle-/data-less repo renders byte-identically; generation is byte-deterministic and --check trips on stale output.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-051": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Tiered drill-down views", "body": "SR-051 is superseded by SR-089;SR-090;SR-091;SR-092; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "meta": "Acceptance: Every listed target resolves; the supersession graph is acyclic; no LLR decomposes this row; TC-099 inspects the frozen migration map.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-052": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Dashboard accessibility (rubric-adjudicated)", "body": "PROJECT_STATE.html shall be operable and readable without a pointer or full color perception: every interactive element (tabs, expandable blocks, SVG nodes with detail panels) is keyboard-reachable and carries an accessible name (title/aria/visible text); no information is encoded by color alone (status/phase/type encodings carry a text or shape cue); text keeps readable contrast against its fill.", "meta": "Acceptance: A fresh family-heterogeneous CRITIQUE session judging the generated dashboard against docs/rubrics/dashboard-accessibility.md (authored at [v3]-[g2] from this SR's intent) returns APPROVE citing numbered anchors; keyboard reachability, accessible names, color-independent encoding, and contrast are each a rubric anchor.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-053": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Dashboard UI uniformity (rubric-adjudicated)", "body": "PROJECT_STATE.html shall read as one system across its tabs and views: one type scale and spacing rhythm; one status/phase/type color vocabulary applied consistently wherever the same concept renders; uniform node, edge, legend, and detail-panel styling across the SVG emitters; the same interaction idiom (expand, hover, detail) wherever the same structure appears.", "meta": "Acceptance: A fresh family-heterogeneous CRITIQUE session judging the generated dashboard against docs/rubrics/dashboard-uniformity.md (authored at [v3]-[g2]) returns APPROVE citing numbered anchors; type scale, color vocabulary, per-emitter node/edge/legend styling, and interaction-idiom consistency are each a rubric anchor.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-054": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Dashboard usability (rubric-adjudicated)", "body": "PROJECT_STATE.html shall support its core reading tasks with low friction: a first-time reviewer finds the project state, the next work, and how the parts connect, each within one tab switch; views default to a legible density (start-collapsed per the greater-than-3 rule) and reveal detail without losing context; labels stay readable at default zoom with no clipped or overlapping text.", "meta": "Acceptance: A fresh family-heterogeneous CRITIQUE session judging the generated dashboard against docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md (authored at [v3]-[g2]) returns APPROVE citing numbered anchors. The RUBRIC is the acceptance instrument and the single home for which anchors are live: an anchor whose core has been bound to a test is retired there, and a verdict must not cite a retired anchor. This row therefore does not enumerate the live set - it would be a third copy of it, and the copy that goes stale. A clause of the requirement above that a test now holds is verified through the LLR/TC chain, which the registry already records, not by a verdict.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-055": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Ingest and human-decision process loops", "body": "The Process tab shall additionally render the project's two circular working loops as linked flow panels: (A) the intake loop - owner/agent intake -> triage into work items with spec detail -> the scheduler-derived ready frontier in the WI registry (docs/work/) -> build/review -> merge; and (B) the human-decision loop - open-items population (including the gate-ratification table) -> human review and ruling -> the log's Decisions record -> merge - with the LLM_Agent entry point rendered once and shared by both loops; each stage links to its canonical home (status.md, the WI registry (docs/work/), open-items.html, log.md); the panels stay data-derived where a canonical source exists, self-contained, byte-deterministic; a data-less repo renders byte-identically and --check freshness is unchanged.", "meta": "Acceptance: The Process tab renders both loop panels with the shared LLM_Agent entry point appearing once; every stage's link resolves to its canonical doc; the gate-ratification stage appears in loop B; a data-less repo renders byte-identically; generation is byte-deterministic and --check trips on stale output.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-056": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Decomposition render polish", "body": "The tiered decomposition views shall render right-sized columns (a tier column no wider than its content requires within a declared bound); one horizontal parent-to-child arrow per containment edge, making containment explicit rather than implied by nesting alone; and a hover/focus highlight that persists on the last-hovered item until another takes it (no flash-on-exit); self-contained, byte-deterministic, --check freshness unchanged.", "meta": "Acceptance: Each containment edge renders exactly one parent-to-child arrow; column widths honor the declared bound; the persistent-highlight contract is present and keyed to the last-hovered node; a data-less repo renders byte-identically; deterministic; --check trips on stale output.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-057": {"tier": "sr", "title": "WI-DAG frontier scheduling", "body": "A stdlib schedule.py library/CLI shall derive the dependency-ready frontier from the tracked WI registry plus the active claims - never from prose - excluding blocked (queued + blockref), deferred, claimed, protected-conflicting and exclusive-conflicting WIs, and exposing ready --explain, ready --format json and simulate --jobs N.", "meta": "Acceptance: On a fixture registry the ready set contains exactly the WIs whose hard predecessors are done, and excludes each blocked/deferred/claimed/exclusive-conflicting WI with a stated reason code.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-058": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Deterministic safety classification", "body": "SR-058 is superseded by SR-093;SR-094;SR-095; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "meta": "Acceptance: Every listed target resolves; the supersession graph is acyclic; no LLR decomposes this row; TC-099 inspects the frozen migration map.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-059": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Retire next-wi; generated status surface", "body": "The migration shall delete docs/next-wi and docs/run-phase outright (fresh scaffold and migrated repo) and remove every live dependency on them; docs/status.md's derived snapshot is GENERATED (gen_trajectory --status) and freshness-gated, never hand-copied. (The generated docs/run-state surface this row once paired with retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5 - the stop banner and exit codes carry the outcome.)", "meta": "Acceptance: A fresh scaffold ships no next-wi/run-phase and is parallel-by-default; no live instruction or executable surface reads, writes, validates, generates or links next-wi; a build-out lane's phase resolves from its {phase}-{gate} branch with run-phase absent.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-060": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Explicit worker assignment mode", "body": "The session engine shall run explicit per-worker claimed assignments (--wi, on the branch integrate.py claim cut; the session tag defaults to the branch name): a worker prompt is assembled from AGENTS.md, the WI row, its SpecRef, predecessor context, the current branch diff, and any rework finding (never docs/status.md or docs/next-wi); worker branches never edit root status, other branches' claims, the root log, or generated artifacts, and the result channel is committed trailer evidence (WI: / Blocked-WI: + BlockRef:).", "meta": "Acceptance: A worker runs from an explicit claimed assignment with no lane files; two concurrent workers write non-colliding logs/review evidence; a worker branch never mutates root coordination truth.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-061": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Parallel-by-default dispatcher", "body": "SR-061 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "meta": "Acceptance: The SupersededBy link resolves to SR-132; no LLR cites this row; TC-133 cites it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-062": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Change-train continuation", "body": "SR-062 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "meta": "Acceptance: The SupersededBy link resolves to SR-132; no LLR cites this row; TC-133 cites it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-063": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Atomic serialized integration", "body": "SR-063 is superseded by SR-096;SR-097;SR-098; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "meta": "Acceptance: Every listed target resolves; the supersession graph is acyclic; no LLR decomposes this row; TC-099 inspects the frozen migration map.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-064": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Crash safety and git-as-authority recovery", "body": "SR-064 is superseded by SR-099;SR-100;SR-101; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "meta": "Acceptance: Every listed target resolves; the supersession graph is acyclic; no LLR decomposes this row; TC-099 inspects the frozen migration map.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-065": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Parallel-execution telemetry and downstream migration", "body": "SR-065 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "meta": "Acceptance: The SupersededBy link resolves to SR-132; no LLR cites this row; TC-133 cites it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-066": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Dual-plan decomposition round", "body": "SR-066 is superseded by SR-102;SR-103;SR-104;SR-105;SR-106;SR-107;SR-108; active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement rows.", "meta": "Acceptance: Every listed target resolves; the supersession graph is acyclic; no LLR decomposes this row; TC-099 inspects the frozen migration map.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-067": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Work-item dependency validation", "body": "check_trajectory.py shall validate WI id syntax, predecessor resolution, and acyclic hard dependencies; an absent or placeholder-only registry is vacuous and docs/trajectory-check: off opts out.", "meta": "Acceptance: Valid rows pass; malformed ids, unknown predecessors, and cycles name the offender and fail; absent, placeholder-only, and opted-out registries pass.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-068": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Work-item row coherence", "body": "check_trajectory.py shall require Deliverable to be non-empty exactly when Status is done on every run and, under --strict, require each open WI SpecRef to resolve in-repo.", "meta": "Acceptance: Either Deliverable/status mismatch fails every run; an empty or dangling open-WI SpecRef fails --strict; coherent rows pass.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-069": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Generated status source-of-truth guard", "body": "check_trajectory.py shall flag a done WI token in hand-edited status.md, warning plain and failing --strict, while standing down for the generated marker, open or unknown ids, opt-out, and absent status.md.", "meta": "Acceptance: The hand-edited done-token case warns/fails by mode; generated status, open/unknown ids, opt-out, and absent status remain silent.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-070": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Offline project-state core", "body": "gen_trajectory.py shall generate one self-contained root HTML file containing completeness tiles, the SN-to-SR-to-LLR-to-TC hierarchy, and the WI roadmap DAG.", "meta": "Acceptance: The root file renders all three registry-derived views without external fetches and remains usable offline.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-071": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Conditional dashboard architecture and knowledge views", "body": "gen_trajectory.py shall render software/component and OKF knowledge views only when their source bundles exist, omitting each view when its source is absent.", "meta": "Acceptance: Each source-backed view renders when present; removing its source omits it, and a source-less repo remains byte-stable.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-072": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Deterministic responsive dashboard freshness", "body": "gen_trajectory.py shall produce byte-deterministic responsive output.", "meta": "Acceptance: Repeated generation is byte-identical, and mobile CSS is present.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-073": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Interface-registry integrity", "body": "trace.py shall integrity-check IF ids, SR back-links, Component references, and emit the best-effort ThisProject-to-LLR-Module advisory.", "meta": "Acceptance: Malformed or duplicate ids and unresolved SR/Component links are findings; unmatched ThisProject emits only an advisory; an absent registry is vacuous.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-074": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Architecture-connectivity coverage advisory", "body": "check_trajectory.py shall emit opt-out, warn-first coverage for undeclared endpoints or directions, uncited Active seams, and dangling Contracts citations.", "meta": "Acceptance: Each uncovered condition warns without changing exit status; source/sink notes and docs/interfaces-check: off suppress their declared cases; a small or absent inventory is vacuous.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-075": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Declared-interface rendering", "body": "gen_trajectory.py and gen_arch_map.py shall render declared IF seams in the dashboard and architecture map, retaining the table fallback when seams are absent.", "meta": "Acceptance: Declared seams produce labeled graph edges in both views; no seams preserves the fallback; generation remains deterministic.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-076": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Cross-component import coverage", "body": "check_trajectory.py shall report an import edge crossing component boundaries when no declared interface covers it, with the declared vacuity, opt-out, and strictness behavior.", "meta": "Acceptance: An uncovered mapped cross-component edge warns plain and fails strict; a covering seam, intra-component or unmapped edge, absent imports, and opt-out stay silent.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-077": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Spec interface-section validation", "body": "check_trajectory.py shall validate an armed spec's Interfaces section for resolvable ids, Proposed rationales, and the intra-module escape.", "meta": "Acceptance: Unknown ids and rationale-less Proposed seams warn plain and fail strict; resolvable citations, justified Proposed seams, intra-module declarations, and unarmed specs pass.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-078": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Rival-plan coverage comparison", "body": "plan_coverage.py shall compare rival plans' clause, SR, and interface coverage while distinguishing findings, malformed inputs, and absent-registry notes.", "meta": "Acceptance: Commensurable plans emit per-plan and pairwise coverage; bad refs or plan graphs exit 1; malformed inputs exit 2; absent registries produce notes without failure.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-079": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Agent pair-row routing", "body": "agent_route.py shall parse and select the pair-row registry with enable-list resolution, version ordering, Env merge, cooldown, family preference/fallback, and tier-up-never-down.", "meta": "Acceptance: Selection obeys registry order and all routing constraints, resolves version-less tokens deterministically, merges Env, preserves legacy Provider compatibility, and reports no route honestly.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-080": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Managed review-session scheduling", "body": "The unattended coordinator shall schedule review-policy sessions with redacted prompt-map briefs, parse verdicts, and log selections while preserving unmanaged behavior without an enable-list.", "meta": "Acceptance: Policies 0/1/2 schedule zero/one/two reviewers; prompt-map preflight and redaction hold; every selection is logged; unmanaged mode retains legacy behavior.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-081": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Review substance scoring", "body": "score_reviews.py shall score review substance into advisory decay history while treating severity hygiene and anti-gaming tripwires as non-scored gates and never rewarding length.", "meta": "Acceptance: Component scores, corroboration, decay, and every tripwire match their fixtures; length adds no score; verdict and scoreboard round trips are deterministic.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-082": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Fixed review escalation", "body": "The coordinator shall apply fixed win-stay/lose-shift escalation and page for shared failure, contradictions and tripwires per the declared ratification level, keyed on whether the tier in process is still the human's to ratify and on the separate drain dial the retired three-value enum bundled into the same word.", "meta": "Acceptance: Two failed gates swap family, a failed swap raises tier, and shared top-tier failure, contradictions, or tripwires page according to gate policy.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-083": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Dual-plan planner-pair selection", "body": "agent_route.py shall select two fresh planner hats across families where possible and use the bounded runtime-nonresponse fallback.", "meta": "Acceptance: Two-family pools diversify; one-family and single-row pools create fresh sessions with reason codes; nonresponse excludes the dead family; no responder is explicit.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-084": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Independent subjective-quality critique dispatch", "body": "The coordinator shall trigger a fresh family-heterogeneous Critique session only for Critique-scoped builds and construct its brief from rubric, intent, and artifact recipe without self-assessment.", "meta": "Acceptance: Critique-scoped builds schedule the session; other builds do not; the prompt contains rubric/intent/recipe and excludes build transcript and self-assessment.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-085": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Bounded critique rework loop", "body": "The coordinator shall iterate BUILD-to-CRITIQUE rework within AGENT_CRITIQUE_MAX and page through gate policy when the cap is exhausted.", "meta": "Acceptance: CHANGES-REQUESTED reworks and re-critiques; approval stops; the configured cap pages through the declared policy.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-086": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Critique verification and closure ratchet", "body": "trace.py shall accept Critique in Verification without exempting its LLR.", "meta": "Acceptance: Critique is accepted while unknown methods fail, and its LLR remains required.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-087": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Bounded software top view", "body": "check_trajectory.py shall derive the top-level module/component view and enforce TOP_VIEW_MAX with nesting, opt-out, and small or absent inventory vacuity.", "meta": "Acceptance: An oversized uncontained view warns/fails strict; valid containment clears it; nested components count at roots; opt-out and small/absent inventories are vacuous.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-088": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Containerized How-SW rendering", "body": "gen_trajectory.py shall render How-SW membership in component containers with boundary-aggregated seams and retain the flat fallback without containment.", "meta": "Acceptance: Contained modules render under components with deduplicated crossing edges; nested membership is preserved; no containment remains byte-identical and flat.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-089": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Tiered When roadmap view", "body": "gen_trajectory.py shall tier the When view phase-to-workstream-to-WI by count, expose delivery phase, and aggregate parent edges from child edges.", "meta": "Acceptance: Tiers collapse only above threshold, leaf phases are visible, and each parent edge equals the deduplicated union of crossing child edges.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-090": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Tiered How-SW hierarchy", "body": "gen_trajectory.py shall tier How-SW from components to modules whenever containment exists and bound the top level by TOP_VIEW_MAX.", "meta": "Acceptance: Contained inventories render component tiers that drill to modules and respect the shared top-view cap; uncontained inventories retain the flat view.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-091": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Interface-wired hierarchy ports", "body": "gen_trajectory.py shall attach IF seams to visible block input/output ports and aggregate cross-container seams to container boundaries.", "meta": "Acceptance: At every visible tier seams terminate at the correct ports; crossing seams aggregate at their container boundary without duplication.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-092": {"tier": "sr", "title": "One-layer hierarchy navigation", "body": "gen_trajectory.py shall descend one hierarchy layer by pointer or keyboard and restore parent views through a breadcrumb.", "meta": "Acceptance: Double-click and Enter/Space descend focused containers exactly one layer; breadcrumb actions restore prior levels; interactions remain self-contained.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-093": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Pure safety classification", "body": "schedule.py shall purely classify every declared safety and policy input into one scheduling class plus deterministic reason codes.", "meta": "Acceptance: Every declared value and policy input maps deterministically; missing, unknown, or contradictory declarations fail closed only for the affected WI; all consumers agree.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-094": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Structural safety cross-check", "body": "schedule.py shall cross-check SafetyClass declarations against structural evidence and fail an invalid, missing, or contradictory WI closed without stopping disjoint classified work.", "meta": "Acceptance: Spine, critique, checkpoint, and dual-plan structure override dishonest declarations; the affected WI is quarantined with reasons while disjoint valid work remains schedulable.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-095": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Safety-aware traincar packing", "body": "SR-095 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "meta": "Acceptance: The SupersededBy link resolves to SR-132; no LLR cites this row; TC-133 cites it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-096": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Atomic CAS integration", "body": "SR-096 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "meta": "Acceptance: The SupersededBy link resolves to SR-132; no LLR cites this row; TC-133 cites it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-097": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Serialized blocked disposition", "body": "SR-097 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "meta": "Acceptance: The SupersededBy link resolves to SR-132; no LLR cites this row; TC-133 cites it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-098": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Durable publication intent", "body": "SR-098 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "meta": "Acceptance: The SupersededBy link resolves to SR-132; no LLR cites this row; TC-133 cites it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-099": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Git evidence enumeration", "body": "SR-099 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "meta": "Acceptance: The SupersededBy link resolves to SR-132; no LLR cites this row; TC-133 cites it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-100": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Ownership-state reconstruction", "body": "SR-100 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "meta": "Acceptance: The SupersededBy link resolves to SR-132; no LLR cites this row; TC-133 cites it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-101": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Lifecycle-boundary recovery", "body": "SR-101 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "meta": "Acceptance: The SupersededBy link resolves to SR-132; no LLR cites this row; TC-133 cites it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-102": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Typed dual-plan state machine", "body": "plan_round.py shall enforce the typed dual-plan state machine, spend ledger, one-repair and revision caps, position-swap agreement, and PAGE outcomes.", "meta": "Acceptance: Happy path selects within budget; illegal repeats raise caps; repair and revision rules hold; arbiter instability or exhaustion pages; state survives JSON round trip.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-103": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Allowlist-only dual-plan briefs", "body": "plan_briefs.py shall assemble planner, critic, and arbiter briefs only from SR/IF registries and strict prompt-template filling.", "meta": "Acceptance: Briefs contain allowlisted registry material, exclude status/log/self-assessment data, reject unknown or unfilled slots, and honor override then kit fallback.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-104": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Dual-plan coverage-step adapter", "body": "plan_coverage_step.py shall adapt mechanical coverage results into clean, implicated-repair, repeated-failure, and malformed round outcomes.", "meta": "Acceptance: Exit 0 advances, first findings implicate and repair once, repeated findings page, malformed is distinct and fails closed, and report payload is captured.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-105": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Stable dual-plan artifacts and WI filing", "body": "plan_artifacts.py shall persist stable round artifacts and file selected queued child WIs with mapped predecessors and registry-valid fields.", "meta": "Acceptance: Round ids and stage names are stable; selected rows receive sequential ids, mapped dependencies, empty Deliverables and valid specs/tiers; line endings and prior log content survive.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-106": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Fresh-session dual-plan execution", "body": "plan_runner.py shall run two planners, cross-critiques, bounded revisions, and position-swapped arbiters as fresh sessions and select only on true-plan agreement.", "meta": "Acceptance: The complete round runs unattended with fresh session identities; runtime fallback is bounded; content agreement selects; position bias, missing rubric, or no route pages honestly.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-107": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Dual-plan direct-path isolation", "body": "The worker shall refuse a dual row on the direct path.", "meta": "Acceptance: Direct workers reject dual rows and --dual-plan rejects non-dual rows.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-108": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Dual-plan round disposition", "body": "The --dual-plan round shall commit SELECT as a docs-only disposition (children filed as queued specs, validated atomically with their generated views) and surface PAGE per gate policy, never silently.", "meta": "Acceptance: SELECT files children that pass the registry validator; a PAGE preserves the round's evidence on disk.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-109": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Spec-lifecycle close-side coherence", "body": "check_trajectory.py shall flag a done WI whose SpecRef is non-empty and a live docs/specs file (scaffold README/-000 exemplars excluded) cited by no open WI, warning on every run and failing under --strict.", "meta": "Acceptance: A done row with a SpecRef and an uncited live spec each produce an R-F WARN with exit 0 on a plain run and exit 1 under --strict; a shared doc cited by any open (incl. deferred/blocked) WI stays clean; docs/specs README and -000 exemplars never flag.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-110": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Per-module coverage floors", "body": "check_coverage.py shall fail when a module listed in the coverage-floor census is below its declared per-module line-coverage percentage in the pytest-cov JSON report, or is absent from that report, so the global --cov-fail-under floor cannot hide a thin high-risk module; an absent report skips and an empty census is a no-op.", "meta": "Acceptance: A module below its floor exits 1 naming it; a declared module absent from the report exits 1; all floors holding exits 0; no coverage report (e.g. the smoke tier) SKIPs; an absent or empty census is a clean no-op; a malformed floor line fails loudly.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-111": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Kit-version stamp", "body": "bootstrap.py shall stamp docs/kit-version with the kit commit SHA and date, marked -dirty on an uncommitted kit tree.", "meta": "Acceptance: docs/kit-version records the kit SHA and date; a scaffold from an uncommitted kit tree is marked -dirty.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-112": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Checked per-agent skill fan-out", "body": "The per-agent skill copies (.claude/.gemini/.agents) shall be a checked, generated fan-out of the one neutral skills/ source.", "meta": "Acceptance: A per-agent copy that diverges byte-for-byte from source is detectable and refreshed by one command.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-113": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Dev-setup wires the process floor", "body": "dev-setup shall wire the pre-commit process floor (core.hooksPath) from its baseline.", "meta": "Acceptance: After dev-setup the pre-commit floor is active without a separate setup step.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-114": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Kit scripts run across the supported OSes", "body": "The kit scripts shall run on Python 3.11+ across Linux, Windows and macOS.", "meta": "Acceptance: The CI matrix spans Linux, Windows and macOS on Python 3.11 and latest (3.x) and is green; macOS runs on current Python only — the 3.11 floor is a platform-agnostic language/stdlib guarantee already exercised on Linux and Windows, while macOS's job is OS-specific behavior (fcntl.flock, paths, git worktrees) that does not vary by Python version, so the macOS+3.11 cell is excluded as a redundant-coverage call, not a runner-availability workaround (3.11 has arm64 macOS builds).", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-115": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Deterministic ready ordering", "body": "schedule.py shall order the eligible ready frontier deterministically by gate class, then Priority descending, then transitive downstream-dependent count descending, then remaining hard-path length descending, then WI id.", "meta": "Acceptance: Ordering is identical across --explain and --format json; Priority outranks downstream-dependent count within one gate class; simulate --jobs 1 matches the serial order.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-116": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Generated status surface", "body": "The trunk lane shall maintain docs/status.md's generated snapshot as a freshness-gated reference surface never written on a worker branch (trunk_step --regen; concurrency-restructure SS5.2). (The docs/run-state half of this row retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5.)", "meta": "Acceptance: The status snapshot regenerates on the trunk after a merge and fails its freshness gate when stale; work branches skip the gate and never commit the artifact.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-117": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Atomic traincar reservation and lane leasing", "body": "SR-117 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "meta": "Acceptance: The SupersededBy link resolves to SR-132; no LLR cites this row; TC-133 cites it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-118": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Traincar build and review shape", "body": "SR-118 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "meta": "Acceptance: The SupersededBy link resolves to SR-132; no LLR cites this row; TC-133 cites it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-119": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Release on early train end", "body": "SR-119 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "meta": "Acceptance: The SupersededBy link resolves to SR-132; no LLR cites this row; TC-133 cites it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-120": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Blocked-constituent disposition", "body": "SR-120 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "meta": "Acceptance: The SupersededBy link resolves to SR-132; no LLR cites this row; TC-133 cites it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-121": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Superseded: Gated downstream migration to the parallel default", "body": "SR-121 is superseded by SR-132 (concurrency-restructure Phase 5, 2026-07-29): the bespoke dispatcher/reservation machinery this specified was deleted outright — git plus the serial local integrator is the integration seam (claims are serial trunk commits, the composed tree is the candidate by construction, recovery is plain git history). Active requirements, implementation links, and decomposition evidence shall cite the replacement row.", "meta": "Acceptance: The SupersededBy link resolves to SR-132; no LLR cites this row; TC-133 cites it.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-122": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Dashboard freshness check", "body": "gen_trajectory.py --check shall fail on stale or missing output, the visible git-derived as-of stamp being excluded from stamp-only comparisons.", "meta": "Acceptance: Stale or missing output fails --check, and as-of behavior is stable in and outside Git.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-123": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Critique closure ratchet", "body": "check_trajectory.py shall warn when a Critique WI closes after CHANGES-REQUESTED without validation evidence changing.", "meta": "Acceptance: The lax-closure case warns, while an approval or a TC, test or rubric delta stays silent.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-124": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Contradiction-safe dual-plan dispatcher class", "body": "schedule.py shall derive a contradiction-safe single-WI scheduling class from PlanMode.", "meta": "Acceptance: PlanMode derives a single-WI class, and a contradictory SafetyClass quarantines the row.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-125": {"tier": "sr", "title": "PAGE maps through gate policy", "body": "The --dual-plan single-shot round shall map a PAGE through the declared ratification level, hard-gating a human only while the tier in process is still HUMAN-HELD and otherwise reaching the pause-free attention (STALL) end state.", "meta": "Acceptance: A PAGE preserves evidence and follows the declared level (a human-held tier -> NEEDS-HUMAN exit; a loop-held tier -> STALL exit, no human gate); an unreadable level or stage resolves to human-held, so the failure direction is the gate, never past it.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-126": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Spine stand-alone rule", "body": "trace.py shall fail under --strict when any spine row's normative text cites a work item or a process doc.", "meta": "Acceptance: A WI id or a process-doc citation in an SR Title/Requirement/Rationale/AcceptanceCriteria, an LLR Title/Detail/Rationale, or a TC Method/Expected/Parameters exits 1 under --strict and names the registry, row and cell; a script name, artifact path, rubric or sibling spine id does not; pointer columns are out of scope.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-127": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Requirement form: one testable obligation", "body": "trace.py shall fail under --strict when a non-Draft requirement repeats its obligation keyword, uses a different modal in normative text, states an obligation with no actor, or uses an unfalsifiable or open-ended term.", "meta": "Acceptance: A second `shall`, a non-`shall` modal, an actorless `shall be <participle>`, an unfalsifiable term, an open-ended escape clause, or a `shall` in an LLR Detail exits 1 under --strict; a Draft row, a zero-`shall` row, `must` as a project's own obligation keyword, and a multi-clause AcceptanceCriteria do not.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-128": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Paraphrase advisory", "body": "trace.py shall warn (never fail) when an SR Rationale re-words its own Requirement, or an LLR Detail re-words its parent SR Requirement, past a declared word-overlap threshold.", "meta": "Acceptance: An SR Rationale overlapping its own Requirement, or an LLR Detail overlapping its parent SR Requirement, past the declared threshold emits a stdout WARNING and a report line; the exit code is unchanged.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-129": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Registry representation migration", "body": "The kit shall provide a mechanical converter between the work-item registry's two homes - the docs/work/ spec folder (status = directory, TOML frontmatter, Deliverable in the body) and the legacy docs/requirements/work-items.csv - refusing any status outside the known vocabulary, any materialization into a non-empty target, and any conversion while an in-flight active/<branch>/ claim exists (conversion is a drained-stop operation), with the round-trip proven over the live registry before an authority flip.", "meta": "Acceptance: wi_convert --verify over a populated registry exits 0 reporting cell-exact equality; an unknown status refuses naming the row; folder->CSV->folder is parse-equal including a hand-filed spec with no order key; an in-flight claim refuses conversion by name.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-130": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Serial trunk step compiles log fragments and regenerates authority", "body": "A serial trunk step shall compile docs/log.d/ work-branch log fragments into docs/log.md in merge order derived from git history - validating every fragment before any write, rebasing relative links to the log's home, deleting compiled fragments, failing loudly at the first error and never committing.", "meta": "Acceptance: Two committed fragments compile in git-add order with links rebased docs/-relative and the fragments deleted; an uncommitted or malformed fragment blocks the whole batch with nothing appended; a fragment-free run is a clean no-op.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-131": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Tracked pause drains claiming to a merged stop", "body": "A tracked docs/work/pause file (TOML: reason, since) shall pause claiming - everything in flight finishes and integrates - read via pause_reason as the ONE pause home (the legacy untracked docs/pause half retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5), failing closed on malformation.", "meta": "Acceptance: A tracked pause file pauses the coordinator via pause_reason under the unchanged None/''/reason contract; a malformed file pauses with the pinned loud placeholder; a stray legacy docs/pause file is inert.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-132": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Local integrator: serial fail-closed merge queue", "body": "The local integrator shall claim work via a serial trunk commit (queued spec to docs/work/active/<branch>/, branch cut from that commit, refused while docs/work/pause is present), merge each finished claimed branch --no-ff onto a candidate worktree with the trunk step folded into the merge commit, run the declared bar on the composed tree - refusing on a missing or empty check declaration and on any SKIP in the bar's own report - require the policy-dialed verdict artifacts with git-derived freshness, fast-forward the trunk only on green, and stop loudly on red.", "meta": "Acceptance: A claim moves the spec and cuts the branch in one serial trunk step and is refused when paused, dirty, name-unsafe, not-ordinary or not-ready; a finished branch is detected from its tree alone; an absent or stale or CHANGES-REQUESTED verdict refuses the merge; a missing stack.ini or empty [product] test refuses; a red bar parks the candidate and stops the queue; the RULING-6 window audit flags a non-merge product commit.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-133": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Work-branch lane skip for freshness steps", "body": "On a claimed work branch (one whose history holds a docs/work/active/<branch>/ claim) check.py's generated-artifact freshness steps shall SKIP with a stated trunk-lane reason - generated artifacts are trunk-only - while every non-freshness step still runs.", "meta": "Acceptance: on a claimed work branch the declared freshness steps SKIP with a §5.2 notice and exit code untouched while skills-sync, trajectory and registry-integrity still run; the closing commit and its successors keep the skip (the signal derives from branch history, not the working tree); the trunk lane never skips them.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-134": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Trunk step regenerates the declared artifacts in dependency order", "body": "The serial trunk step shall regenerate the declared generated artifacts in dependency order - the derived gate before its consumers - failing loudly at the first error and never committing.", "meta": "Acceptance: --regen skips absent artifact families with a notice and stops loudly at the first failing generator; derive_gate runs before the dashboard/status/open-items regens.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-135": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Pending surface renders the declared pause verbatim", "body": "The generated pending surface shall render a declared pause's since and reason verbatim - no clock - clearing when the pause file is deleted.", "meta": "Acceptance: the pending block renders Paused since <date> - <reason> with the declared date verbatim (no clock), omits the stamp when since is empty, renders the pinned fail-closed placeholder on malformation, and clears when the file is deleted.", "status": "Modified"}, "SR-136": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Declared-figure provenance", "body": "check_figures.py shall flag a declared driven figure - a line opting in under the fig: marker convention - that does not carry both the command that produced it and the revision it was driven at, or a derivation naming the declared figures it is computed from, warn-first (exit 0) with --strict gating; presence only, never truth, and an unmarked figure is out of scope by design.", "meta": "Acceptance: A fig: marker missing cmd= or rev= (or carrying only empty or wordless values) warns naming the missing half and gates only under --strict, each marker on a line judged on its own attributes; cmd= plus rev= (bare or quoted), or a non-empty derived=, passes; unmarked figure prose, placeholder-grammar examples, fig-ok lines, GENERATED blocks and docs/reviews/ records are ignored; docs/stack.ini is scanned beside the root and docs markdown surfaces.", "status": "Verified"}, "SR-137": {"tier": "sr", "title": "One policy home, with a checked shape", "body": "The kit shall read every process policy dial from a single `docs/process.toml`, and shall REFUSE — never resolve by precedence — a repo in which any dial is declared both there and in its legacy one-word file. The file's line shape shall be a checked contract: one `key = value` per line under a bare `[section]` header, no dotted keys, no inline tables, no multi-line strings.", "meta": "Acceptance: A dial declared in both homes produces a refusal naming the key and both files, at every guarded entry point (the dispatcher's pre-claim preflight, intake's adjudication arm, the integrator's verdict gate); a dotted key, inline table, multi-line string or hook-read key under the wrong section is refused by the shape check AND still reads ON in both readers; the hooks' sh and `tomllib` return the same answer over the adversarial file table; a wrong-typed or out-of-range dial is refused rather than defaulted.", "status": "Draft"}, "SR-138": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Legacy config converts automatically and totally", "body": "`bootstrap.py --migrate-config` shall fold every legacy one-word policy file into `docs/process.toml` and delete it, and shall NEVER delete a file whose value it did not manage to write. Both bootstrap and the documented re-sync shall run it.", "meta": "Acceptance: A repo carrying legacy files converts in one pass and reports what moved; a second pass is a no-op; a legacy file whose value cannot be written is LEFT IN PLACE with its value intact and named in the report; the retired gate-authority word expands to all three dials it meant rather than to a single key.", "status": "Draft"}, "SR-139": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Ratification as an ordinal over a derived spine stage", "body": "The kit shall express human ratification authority as a cumulative integer 0-4 naming the highest spine tier a human still ratifies, compared against a separately derived SPINE STAGE (0=SN..4=nothing in process) with a declared, auditable mapping to the harness gate. Every unreadable or out-of-range input shall resolve toward MORE human involvement.", "meta": "Acceptance: Level N holds exactly the tiers the shipped template documents (cumulative, strictly-less-than the stage); stage 4 is held by no level, the end-of-run hold being its own dial; an unreadable stage, an absent dial, and a wrong-typed dial all read as human-held; an out-of-range level falls back to the conservative end AND is refused loudly; a legacy gate-policy word reads as all three dials it meant.", "status": "Draft"}, "SR-140": {"tier": "sr", "title": "The attestation anchor lives on the artifact's own row", "body": "The kit shall record each acceptance ON THE ACCEPTED ARTIFACT'S OWN ROW - the commit whose tree carries the text that was accepted, and a digest of that row's NORMATIVE cells - never in a second registry keyed on the same artifact; shall report text that has moved away from its recorded anchor regardless of any Status movement; and shall treat an anchor written in the same commit as the text it anchors as an error.", "meta": "Acceptance: An artifact whose normative text has moved since its recorded anchor is reported whether or not its Status moved; an anchor that cannot be recomputed, or that names an artifact which is not a current row, is a finding rather than silence; a commit that writes an anchor cell while also changing that row's normative cells is refused, because a re-stamp in the same breath as an amendment hides the amendment; and the same rules hold for a stakeholder need, which has no Status cell for the amendment machinery to watch.", "status": "Draft"}, "SR-141": {"tier": "sr", "title": "The loop's priority order is stated and pinned", "body": "Admission shall prefer `adjudication` rows over all other ready work, as a stable partition applied at admission rather than by renumbering the ruled rank table, and shall not thereby admit anything past a human-held stop.", "meta": "Acceptance: An adjudication row admits ahead of a ready spine batch; the ruled rank table is unchanged, so `--explain` still reports it; the partition is stable (non-adjudication rows keep their relative order); a human-held attestation still drains and surfaces with the adjudication row waiting behind the stop.", "status": "Draft"}, "SR-142": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Red test cases under a closed implementation are named and judged", "body": "The idle census shall name, as its own class, a test case that is not green while every requirement it verifies is cited by a work item recorded `done`, and shall route it to an ADJUDICATION row whose tier is estimated from measured breadth rather than defaulted.", "meta": "Acceptance: A not-green TC under a `done` citer is named; `Verified`, `Draft` and `Modified` are exempt (the last belongs to the amendment adjudication, and two judgement rows for one event is a double-count); an open, `partial` or `cancelled` citer makes nothing red; a TC verifying a mix of claimed and unclaimed targets is not red; the census line's grammar has exactly one reader.", "status": "Draft"}, "SR-143": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Queue overlap is made visible, never blocked", "body": "The validator shall report, warn-only and never in the exit code, pairs of OPEN work items that overlap by near-identical title, shared requirement citation, or a shared spec of record.", "meta": "Acceptance: Each overlapping OPEN pair is named once per signal with both ids, in deterministic order; a closed row never conflicts; the title bar is high enough that ordinary unrelated work is silent and a shared stopword is not a signal; the findings never join the exit code, not even under --strict.", "status": "Draft"}, "SR-144": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Every lane close is a terminal state with an immutable record", "body": "A lane that cannot finish shall close into a TERMINAL `partial/` state with one immutable per-close report naming the claimed outcome, the reason, the commit range and the keep/discard split, and shall merge like any other branch. A report declaring neither a split nor an explicit deferral shall be refused, and a second close of the same event shall be refused rather than overwriting the first.", "meta": "Acceptance: A partial close is clean under the real validator (the terminal Deliverable rule is exempt, because an early close leaves the spec's definition byte-identical by design); a terminal spec with no report is LOUD rather than silently un-disposed; a second close is refused and leaves no residue in the lane; a multi-spec close that refuses restores every report it wrote.", "status": "Draft"}, "SR-145": {"tier": "sr", "title": "A lane's outcome is a claim; the adjudicator overrules by minting", "body": "Every `partial` and `cancelled` close shall earn one disposition row, keyed on the close's own immutable report path; a `complete` close shall be spot-checked at a declared rate rather than gated. Continuing partial work shall mean DRAFTING A SUCCESSOR carrying explicit lineage, never re-opening a terminal row.", "meta": "Acceptance: A partial or cancelled close mints exactly one disposition; a re-sweep mints none; a genuinely second close is a second report and so a second row; a disposition row never mints a disposition for itself (refused at both ends); a successor's lineage survives the id change.", "status": "Draft"}, "SR-146": {"tier": "sr", "title": "Prompts are reviewable files with a per-session audit trail", "body": "Every prompt the loop launches shall be a shipped file with strictly filled slots, listed by digest in a freshness-gated generated catalogue, with each session recording which template it used and the fingerprint of what that rendered to.", "meta": "Acceptance: An unknown or unfilled slot is a refusal; a missing shipped template is a named preflight refusal rather than a first-session crash; the catalogue lists every shipped prompt with its digest and `--check` fails when stale; a line-ending difference is not a digest change; an operator override is named in the session record rather than only in the launch flags.", "status": "Draft"}, "SR-147": {"tier": "sr", "title": "One machine-parseable carrier for the requirement spine", "body": "The kit shall carry the SN->SR->LLR->TC spine in ONE machine-parseable carrier rather than two (stakeholder needs as markdown prose tables, the other three tiers as CSV), and shall provide a mechanical converter that proves the conversion cell-for-cell over the live registries BEFORE the authority flip - refusing, with a nonzero exit that names the row and the field, any conversion in which a cell does not survive the round-trip.", "meta": "Acceptance: migrate_carrier.py --check over the live registries exits 0 reporting the round-trip clean for every row of every tier; a cell that is dropped, truncated, re-typed or re-ordered is REFUSED with exit 1 and a finding naming the registry, the row id and the field; and the emitted text re-parses with tomllib.", "status": "Draft"}, "LLR-001": {"tier": "llr", "title": "SN->SR->LLR->TC join + orphan set", "body": "Loads the four registries, computes the orphan set (SR without LLR/TC, LLR/TC without a parent, SN without an SR), and exits 1 under --strict.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/trace.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-002": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Id + CSV-structure + citation integrity", "body": "Duplicate/malformed id detection per ID_PATTERNS, a per-row column-count check, and SR/LLR citation coherence (triangle_findings); the always-valid --strict-integrity floor.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/trace.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-003": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Placeholder + schema checks", "body": "-000 example-row scan and required-field + closed-vocabulary validation over the real (non-placeholder) rows, plus the ratified-phase NUMERIC-ONLY rule (owner ruling 2026-08-01): once any spine row is phased (digit-parse arming), a non-Draft SR/LLR/TC whose Phase is not a full-cell bare integer is a --strict-schema finding (vacuous until armed; a prefixed v2/P1 now fails - the --phase/--ratify filters and the phase-drop join match the cell literally, so a prefix disarms them silently; phase_num stays digit-extract for grandfathering).", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/trace.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-004": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Comparative-term advisory", "body": "Flags an AcceptanceCriteria comparative term with no predicate marker in the cell; warn-only, never joins the failure set.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/trace_text.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-005": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Off-spine integrity + back-links", "body": "Integrity-checks the PB/REPO/PART/ASSET ids plus legacy MOD compatibility and resolves REPO/MOD DelegatedSRs against the SR set; -000 example rows ignored.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/trace.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-006": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Gate step plan + missing-tool guard", "body": "Builds the required-step plan per gate/tier and reports SKIP(missing) with a nonzero exit when a required tool is absent; the plan listing still names every step. The work-branch lane skip is LLR-141 (SR-133).", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-007": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Stack profile loader", "body": "Reads format/lint/test/tiers/coverage/arch-map from docs/stack.ini and resolves each step's command from that profile.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-008": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Profile validation", "body": "Rejects a malformed profile, non-integer coverage threshold, or missing declared binary with a clear loud failure.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-009": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Conditional profile seeding", "body": "Selects the file/skill set per stack+domain scope; omits Python-only artifacts and seeds files-mode arch-map for a non-Python stack.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/bootstrap.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-010": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Scaffold writer", "body": "Writes the mapped kit files into --dest so the generated harness runs green out of the box.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/bootstrap.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-011": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Idempotent write + kit-version stamp", "body": "Skips an existing file unless --force.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/bootstrap.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-012": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Link + vision-tag checker", "body": "Resolves intra-repo links, requires a PROJECT-VISION tag, and checks generated-doc freshness under --stale.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_docs.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-013": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Flow-doc contract checker", "body": "Validates an authored runtime-flow doc against its structural contract; a malformed flow fails.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_flows.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-014": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Budget comparator", "body": "Compares measured metrics to declared budgets and fails beyond tolerance at the row's block/warn level.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_perf.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-015": {"tier": "llr", "title": "PB back-link resolution", "body": "Resolves each PB row's Refs to a real SR/LLR/Module id; an empty or unresolvable Refs is a finding.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/trace.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-016": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Stub marker detector", "body": "Walks the AST for stub bodies (pass-only/NotImplemented/ellipsis public functions) at the declared gate.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_stubs.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-017": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Secrets pattern scan", "body": "Always-on scan of staged diff/message/outgoing range for PEM/token/key patterns, independent of the privacy toggle.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_privacy.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-018": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Two-axis privacy gating", "body": "Adds the PII/identity classes when docs/privacy-check is on, exempting configured author identities.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_privacy.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-019": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Pre-commit floor", "body": "Runs trace --strict-integrity and the check_privacy secrets scan on staged content and blocks a failing commit.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/hooks/pre-commit", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-020": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Pre-push outgoing scan", "body": "Scans the outgoing commit range and blocks a push that would publish a secret or a private identity under privacy-check.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/hooks/pre-push", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-021": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Hook python probe", "body": "Probes candidate interpreters by running one and skip-or-reports clearly when none works or a Store-alias exits nonzero.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/hooks/pre-push", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-022": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Vendored-doc drift check", "body": "Compares a vendored doc copy against its recorded upstream source and reports drift.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_vendored.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-023": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Arch-map splice + drift check", "body": "Regenerates the symbol map inside the BEGIN/END marker block and fails --check when the committed map has drifted.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_arch_map.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-024": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Permutation expander", "body": "Parses the Permutations grammar (range/set/bool with @full|@pairwise|@boundaries) and emits the concrete case set.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_cases.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-025": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Skills index generator", "body": "Reads each SKILL.md applicability frontmatter and regenerates skills/INDEX.csv.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_skills_index.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-026": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Headless session launch", "body": "Runs the agent CLI headless: build_argv substitutes {model} into argv and, when a CmdTemplate carries no {prompt} placeholder (the default for both provider CLIs), routes the prompt to the child's STDIN (run_session writes it from a daemon thread then closes), while the no-wedge invariant holds — a fixed prompt plus EOF, never an interactive read. A codex session additionally captures that CLI's own --output-last-message file as the result (_codex_lastmsg_setup/_codex_lastmsg_read). stdin is otherwise closed so no prompt wedges the loop; the scope is the dispatcher and worker sessions, and the former status.md-resume read is retired rather than coexisting with them.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_session.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-027": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Preflight footing guard", "body": "Refuses a non-git dir, a missing agent CLI, or a private author under privacy-check with a typed nonzero exit rather than hanging.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_common.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-028": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Typed outcome + ERROR handling", "body": "Ends each session in a typed code, guards a zero-commit HEAD, and reports an all-ERROR region as an unavailable agent, not a work stall.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_loop.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-029": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Kernel advisory lock", "body": "Takes an OS advisory lock the kernel releases on process death, so no stale-pid file wedges the next run.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_common.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-030": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Lock mutual exclusion", "body": "Refuses a second coordinator holding the same per-worktree lock, preventing a two-writer race.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_common.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-031": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Shared declared-line parse", "body": "The single first-non-comment-line reader the hooks, agent_loop.read_declared, and check_privacy all use so a declared policy value is read one way.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_privacy.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-032": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Onboard/dev-setup templates", "body": "The onboarding and dev-setup scaffold scripts that materialize and run to a green developer setup.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/onboard.template.sh", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-033": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Release checklist generator", "body": "Emits docs/release-checklist.md, including a Performance-budgets section listing each warn-tier PB (id + allocation) read from the budgets registry.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_release_checklist.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-034": {"tier": "llr", "title": "WI dependency-DAG validation", "body": "Loads the WI registry - the docs/work/ spec folder, or a legacy work-items.csv via dual-read (folder wins when it holds real specs; both-present is an integrity error) -; validates WI ids, predecessor resolution, and hard-edge acyclicity; respects the absent/placeholder/opt-out vacuity rules.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-035": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Offline project-state core", "body": "Renders one self-contained root HTML file with completeness tiles, the spine hierarchy, and the WI roadmap DAG.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-036": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Duplicate-block detector", "body": "Tokenizes each source file (stdlib tokenize; comments/NL/indent excluded, NEWLINE kept so windows cannot stitch unrelated statements), slides a --min-tokens window (default 30), groups candidate hits by line offset, and merges only step-1-overlapping token-window offsets into one finding per block, POSIX-normalizes the file pair, and fingerprints each block (a hash of its complete token signature, line-number-free). A finding is suppressed only when its fingerprint+pair is recorded in docs/dupes-allow (count-aware: N lines sanction N identical copies) or a legacy bare pair matches; --emit-census prints the lines.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_dupes.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-037": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Per-phase cmd map + size tripwire", "body": "cmd_map parsed by parse_map (same KEY=value syntax; templates may not contain , or ; — the dispatcher wrapper covers those); session_template(phase) falls back to AGENT_CMD; every map entry preflighted (parse + executable); review-policy read via read_declared and printed in the banner; status_size_warning is pure and warn-only (limit<=0 disables).", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_loop.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-038": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Path + sym: reference checker", "body": "Scans root *.md + docs/**/*.md; a backticked token is path-shaped when it carries a separator plus a known extension or top-level prefix (URLs/globs/placeholders excluded); GENERATED marker blocks and path-ok lines are skipped; sym:<module>.<name> is resolved against the module map parsed from architecture.md (module tail -> public symbols); warn-first, --strict exits 1.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_doc_refs.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-039": {"tier": "llr", "title": "OKF bundle emitter", "body": "emit() builds {relpath: content} for every real row (sorted, deterministic, no timestamps): frontmatter via JSON-quoted YAML scalars, bodies carrying the row prose plus up/down markdown links from locally built reverse maps, per-tier and root indexes, the UPSTREAM.md spec pin; on_disk() snapshots docs/okf for the byte-compare; write mode writes-if-changed and prunes; small loaders duplicated per the F5 rule (no new sibling import). Layer B2 adds a process-guides tier: _doc_title_and_summary derives each guide title+summary from the doc first heading/paragraph (inline links stripped so nothing dangles), emitted only after the vacuity gate so a fresh scaffold stays vacuous.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_okf.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-040": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Subagent spawn gate", "body": "Pure decide(tool_name, policy, override) -> allow|ask|deny|defer over SPAWN_TOOLS={Task,Agent}; main reads the PreToolUse JSON payload, reads docs/subagent-gate via the shared declared-line parse, applies the SUBAGENT_GATE=allow override, emits permissionDecision (deny -> exit 2), and appends each decision to docs/subagent-gate.log; any exception fails OPEN (allow) and logs, so a broken gate never wedges the agent's tools.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/subagent_gate.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-041": {"tier": "llr", "title": "IF-tier integrity", "body": "Loads interfaces.csv; validates IF ids, SR back-links and Component tags, and emits the best-effort endpoint-module advisory.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/trace.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-042": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Architecture-connectivity coverage warns", "body": "Emits opt-out warn-first findings for missing endpoints/directions, uncited Active seams, and dangling Contracts citations without changing exit status.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-043": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Cross-agent skill fan-out drift check", "body": "gen_skills_index.check_agent_sync byte-compares each per-agent <agent>/skills/<name>/ file (.claude/.gemini/.agents) to the neutral project-trajectory/skills/<name>/ source; a copy that diverges is a --check-agents finding with a one-command fix, an orphan copy (no source) a non-failing warn, a source skill missing from a subset dir no finding. Vacuous without a source or per-agent dir; wired as the check.py skills-sync step + pre-commit floor (kit-only generator, so a scaffold no-ops). bootstrap.sync_agent_skills (--sync) force-refreshes only the <agent>/skills/<name>/ subtree from source.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_skills_index.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-044": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Agent pair-row routing", "body": "Parses pair rows and enable tokens; resolves versions; merges Env; selects by tier, family, cooldown, and order with legacy Provider compatibility.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_route.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-045": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Managed review dispatch", "body": "Schedules review-policy sessions in managed mode, constructs redacted prompt-map briefs, parses verdicts, logs selections, and preserves unmanaged behavior.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_loop.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-046": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Review substance scorer", "body": "Computes advisory substance components and decay history; treats tripwires as gates and never rewards length.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/score_reviews.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-047": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Run capability menu reader", "body": "load_capabilities parses the docs/stack.ini [run] section (configparser, interpolation=None, case-preserving optionxform; declaration order kept; a name.desc row attaches to its command and an orphan or empty-value command is dropped) into an ordered (name, command, desc) list; --list prints name<TAB>desc per row; direct(name) launches by exact-match name (exit 2 on an unknown name) and launch() runs the shell line via subprocess shell=True, passing the child exit code straight through; interactive_menu prints the numbered menu, reads a pick from stdin, and returns EOF-safe without hanging; an absent or empty [run] prints guidance and exits 1. The run.* launchers are thin delegates forwarding their args to run_menu.py.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/run_menu.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-048": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Independent critique dispatch", "body": "Detects Critique-scoped builds and assembles a fresh family-heterogeneous rubric/intent/artifact brief with no self-assessment.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_loop.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-049": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Bounded software top view", "body": "Derives component roots and uncontained modules; enforces TOP_VIEW_MAX with nesting, opt-out, and vacuity rules.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-050": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Derived-gate computation + hybrid cache", "body": "Per-artifact gate rules (sr_gate/maturity_gate/sn_gate) + min-aggregation over in-scope artifacts + the cached docs/gate (basis line + --check rot guard, legacy value-only); the derived current phase (max over ratified rows via phase_num) rides the basis line and --print, the phase analogue of the derived gate. is_modified is recognized (F5-mirrored from trace.py, pinned by test_rule_sync) and counted across SR/LLR/TC onto the compared basis line as modified=N beside drafts=N - a surfacing count only, no gate arithmetic (a Modified SR reads G2 through the existing decomposed-unverified rung of sr_gate; maturity_gate stays Draft-only so a Modified child never caps).", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/derive_gate.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-051": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Process reference tab", "body": "Renders the Process tab as a fully self-contained conditional panel registered in build_html (the Knowledge-tab pattern: nav button + section id=process): (1) the artifact-lifecycle x gates panel derived from the live registries + docs/gate (current-gate highlight; per-tier counts from the spine), (2) the resume-loop panel (the managed agent_loop flow with its escalation edges), (3) the slices -> phase -> gates panel derived from the WI registry (docs/work/); links out to the process masters wherever a canonical home exists (bounded in-view restatement only where no single doc states the relationship); sorted inputs, no clocks; a data-less repo renders byte-identically; --check unchanged (the trajectory-map step).", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_panels.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-052": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Tiered When roadmap", "body": "Builds phase/workstream/WI tiers by threshold, surfaces delivery Phase, and aggregates parent edges from deduplicated child edges.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_views.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-055": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Dashboard usability rendering", "body": "Adjudicated against the usability rubric (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md) by the SR-084/SR-085 critique loop: labels stay readable at default zoom with no clipped or overlapping text, and the reading experience the rubric's remaining anchors describe. Byte-deterministic; --check unchanged. This row keeps ONLY what a test cannot pin: the mechanized cores have left for child rows that name the tests owning them — T2 start-collapse (LLR-099/TC-102), T3 breadcrumb-restores-parent (LLR-100/TC-103, the whole anchor, no residue here) and T1 labelled entry (LLR-115/TC-120, the whole anchor, no residue here). Do NOT restate a child's claim here: an anchor lives in exactly one row.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-056": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Process tab intake + human-decision loop panels", "body": "Extends SR-050's process_panel with the project's two circular working loops as linked flow panels: (A) the intake loop - owner/agent intake -> triage into work items with spec detail -> the scheduler-derived ready frontier in the WI registry (docs/work/) -> build/review -> merge; (B) the human-decision loop - open-items population (incl. the gate-ratification table) -> human review and ruling -> the log's Decisions record -> merge - with the LLM_Agent entry node rendered once and shared by both loops. Each stage links to its canonical home (status.md, the WI registry (docs/work/), open-items.html, log.md); data-derived where a canonical source exists; a data-less repo renders byte-identically; --check unchanged (the trajectory-map step).", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_panels.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-057": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Decomposition render polish (columns, arrows, persistent hover)", "body": "Polishes the tiered decomposition render: each tier column is sized no wider than its content within a declared bound (a per-tier max-width cap MAX_TIER_COL, not the current uniform column); one horizontal parent->child arrow is drawn per containment edge so containment is explicit rather than implied by nesting alone; and the hover/focus highlight persists on the last-hovered item until another takes it (a stateful highlight keyed to the last-hovered node id, killing the flash-on-exit). Self-contained, byte-deterministic; --check unchanged. Composes with SR-089's tiering without changing its >3 thresholds.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_views.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-058": {"tier": "llr", "title": "WI-DAG frontier + deterministic traincar ordering", "body": "Derives the dependency-ready frontier from the WI registry + dispatcher reservations (never prose), excludes blocked/deferred/reserved/protected-/exclusive-conflicting WIs with reason codes, and exposes ready --explain / --format json and simulate --jobs N.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/schedule.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-059": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Pure safety classifier", "body": "Maps declared safety and policy inputs to one scheduling class and deterministic reason codes without side effects.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/schedule.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-060": {"tier": "llr", "title": "next-wi/run-phase retirement + generated status/run-state", "body": "next-wi/run-phase are gone from every surface; the status snapshot is generated by gen_trajectory --status and re-derived by the trunk lane (trunk_step --regen) after each merge - never hand-copied, never written on a work branch. (The run-state generation half retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5.)", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/trunk_step.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-061": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Explicit WI/train/worktree worker assignment", "body": "Replaces internal --track assumptions with explicit --wi/--train/worktree assignment; assembles the worker prompt from AGENTS.md + WI row + SpecRef + predecessor context + train diff + rework finding (never status.md/next-wi); collision-safe per-assignment logs/review evidence naming the exact reviewed commit; the --track lane machinery is retired outright.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_loop.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-067": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Cross-component import coverage", "body": "Joins imports, component membership, and declared seams; reports uncovered cross-component edges with strict/plain and vacuity rules.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-068": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Spec interface-section validation", "body": "Validates armed spec interface ids, Proposed rationale, and the intra-module escape with warn-first/strict severity.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-069": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Rival-plan coverage comparison", "body": "Parses rival plan tables; resolves clause/SR/IF coverage; validates dependencies; emits pairwise differences and distinct findings/malformed/absent-registry outcomes.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/plan_coverage.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-070": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Typed dual-plan round lifecycle", "body": "Implements typed round steps, spend and action caps, position-swapped agreement, persistence, and PAGE mapping.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/plan_round.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-071": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Allowlist-only dual-plan briefs", "body": "Builds planner/critic/arbiter briefs only from SR/IF registries with strict template slots and override/fallback loading.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/plan_briefs.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-072": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Dual-plan planner pair and fallback", "body": "Selects two fresh planner sessions across families where possible and applies the bounded nonresponse fallback with reason codes.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_route.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-073": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Coverage-step result adapter", "body": "Converts coverage CLI results and report payload into clean, implicated-repair, repeated-failure, or malformed round records.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/plan_coverage_step.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-074": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Dual-plan artifact persistence and filer", "body": "Allocates stable round/stage artifacts and appends registry-valid selected child WIs with mapped predecessors and preserved line endings.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/plan_artifacts.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-075": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Generated-status source-of-truth guard", "body": "Finds done WI tokens only in hand-edited status.md, with plain/strict severity, and stands down for the generated marker, open/unknown ids, opt-out, and absent status.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-076": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Fresh-session dual-plan runner", "body": "Runs planners, cross-critiques, bounded revisions and position-swapped arbiters as fresh sessions; selects only true-plan agreement.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/plan_runner.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-077": {"tier": "llr", "title": "WI row coherence", "body": "Enforces Deliverable iff done on every run and resolvable open-WI SpecRef under strict mode.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-078": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Conditional architecture and knowledge views", "body": "Renders software/component and OKF views only when their source bundles exist; omits each otherwise.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_parse.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-079": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Deterministic responsive dashboard freshness", "body": "Produces deterministic responsive HTML.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-080": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Declared-interface graph rendering", "body": "Renders IF-labeled seams in dashboard and architecture views with deterministic table fallback when absent.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py;project-trajectory/scripts/gen_arch_map.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-081": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Fixed review escalation", "body": "Applies win-stay/lose-shift family swap, tier escalation, and ratification-level-keyed paging for shared failure, contradiction, and tripwires.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_route.py", "status": "Modified"}, "LLR-082": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Bounded critique rework", "body": "Loops CHANGES-REQUESTED through rework and fresh critique within AGENT_CRITIQUE_MAX, then pages via gate policy.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_loop.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-083": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Critique verification vocabulary", "body": "Accepts Critique as a verification value while retaining the LLR completeness requirement.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/trace.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-084": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Critique closure ratchet", "body": "Warns when a Critique-scoped WI closes after CHANGES-REQUESTED with no TC, test, or rubric delta.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-085": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Containerized How-SW rendering", "body": "Renders nested component membership and boundary-aggregated seams, falling back byte-identically to the flat view without containment.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_views.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-086": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Tiered How-SW hierarchy", "body": "Tiers component containment to modules and bounds the visible top level by TOP_VIEW_MAX.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_views.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-087": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Hierarchy seam ports", "body": "Attaches IF seams to visible input/output ports and aggregates cross-container seams at boundaries.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-088": {"tier": "llr", "title": "One-layer hierarchy navigation", "body": "Handles pointer and keyboard one-layer descent and breadcrumb restoration in self-contained views.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-089": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Structural safety cross-check", "body": "Cross-checks declarations against spine, critique, checkpoint, and dual-plan evidence; quarantines only the contradictory WI.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/schedule.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-095": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Dual-plan path isolation", "body": "Rejects dual rows on the direct worker path.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_loop.py;project-trajectory/scripts/schedule.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-096": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Serialized dual-plan disposition", "body": "Commits SELECT or PAGE as one docs-only disposition and regenerates validation.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_loop.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-097": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Spec-lifecycle close-side findings", "body": "Emits R-F findings - done WI with non-empty SpecRef; live docs/specs file (README/-000 excluded, the spec_interface_findings idiom) with no open-WI citer - message-only, riding main's warn-plain/error-under-strict tier with no new branch.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-098": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Per-module coverage-floor comparator", "body": "Reads the docs/coverage-floors census (module + min percent; blank and # lines ignored) and coverage.py's JSON report (per-file percent_covered, path-normalized), fails on any module below its floor or absent from the report, SKIPs when the report is absent, is a no-op with an empty census, and fails loudly on a malformed floor line.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_coverage.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-099": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Default-density start-collapse (T2 core)", "body": "The mechanized core of usability anchor T2 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md): a view whose scale EARNS tiering — a bundle spanning more than 3 OKF types, a module set above the component threshold (the SR-089 '>3' rule) — renders START-COLLAPSED as the shared type-tiered drill, one block per type or container, rather than a flat wall of nodes; at or below the threshold it stays flat. Covers the Knowledge, How-SW and landing What views; the What icicle start-collapses to one block per SN with descend-on-click, reusing _drill_layer_svg/_render_drill so the interaction idiom is single-sourced. SCOPE: T2's remaining 'reads legibly at default density' judgement stays perceptual under LLR-055/TC-055.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-100": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Detail-in-context breadcrumb (T3 core)", "body": "The mechanized core of usability anchor T3 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md): descending a container never replaces the view with no way back — the drill emits a declared breadcrumb nav (<nav class=\"crumbs\"> with a data-root-crumb root label) whose crumb click TRUNCATES the trail to that ancestor, restoring the parent view. SCOPE: the WHOLE anchor — the breadcrumb's presence AND its restore behaviour; T3 is retired as a critique anchor and no residue is left under LLR-055/TC-055.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-101": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Non-pruning SVG container roles (A2 core)", "body": "The mechanized core of accessibility anchor A2 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-accessibility.md): no emitted <svg> declares the CHILDREN-PRESENTATIONAL role=img over a FOCUSABLE descendant, and any svg that does declare role=img carries its own accessible name (aria-label, or a first-direct-child <title> — per SVG-AAM a <title> nested in a <g> names the <g>, not the svg). Focusable means tabindex in any quoting OR a native SVG <a href>, which is tab-ordered without a tabindex. The role is chosen from the emitted BODY by _svg_role. SCOPE — and no wider: the container-role invariant as PROVEN by TC-104, over the artifact this repo ships plus the tiered fixture plus the predicate's own unit cases; it is NOT a proof over every reachable emitter configuration, so an emitter no rendered artifact exercises is unguarded. Name QUALITY belongs to LLR-108/TC-113.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-102": {"tier": "llr", "title": "One-concept-per-colour de-collision (U5 core)", "body": "The mechanized core of uniformity anchor U5 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-uniformity.md): no hex value is reused across STATUS_FILL/TIER_FILL/OKF_TYPE_FILL/SW_NODE_FILL/PHASE_ACCENTS for an unrelated concept (the intentional TIER_FILL<->OKF_TYPE_FILL SN/SR/LLR/TC mirror is the one exception, asserted as a bijection rather than a set match), no PHASE_ACCENTS entry equals the CSS --accent token, and PHASE_ACCENTS clears a PAIRWISE (not merely adjacent) deltaE floor of 15. SCOPE: hex-identity and deltaE across these five declared palettes; the near-duplicate residue across vocabularies belongs to LLR-109/TC-114.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-103": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Uniform node/edge/legend/highlight styling (U3 core)", "body": "The mechanized core of uniformity anchor U3 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-uniformity.md): the hover/focus highlight ring resolves to ONE shared per-fill `--ring` custom property across every SVG emitter (drill/icicle/flat-DAG/knowledge) rather than a different hue per emitter, and the When tab's phase-accent key renders through the SAME `.legend`/`<i>` component every other legend in the document uses rather than a bespoke inline-chip idiom. SCOPE: highlight-token equality and legend-component equality, asserted structurally against the emitted CSS and markup. Spacing and exact visual weight belong to LLR-110/TC-115.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-104": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Process-tab node-label type scale (U1 core)", "body": "The mechanized core of uniformity anchor U1 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-uniformity.md): the Process tab's working-loops SVG no longer hardcodes 12px/9.5px/13px/13px on .stgt/.stgn/.hooplab/.hubname. .stgt/.stgn are the same per-node-label role as the shared --nlabel/--nsub tokens the icicle, dag and knowledge views already share, and reuse them directly; .hooplab/.hubname are a once-per-diagram headline label, a genuinely different role, so they take the one documented --nhead scale step. SCOPE: these four selectors resolve to a declared token, asserted structurally against the emitted CSS. Whether the sizes read as uniform belongs to LLR-111/TC-116.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-105": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Interactive-control contrast in both themes (T5 core)", "body": "The mechanized core of usability anchor T5 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md): EVERY control painted over a node's own fill clears the WCAG 3:1 UI-boundary floor against EVERY node fill declared in STATUS_FILL/TIER_FILL/OKF_TYPE_FILL/SW_NODE_FILL/PHASE_ACCENTS, in BOTH themes — computed per fill (white or near-black, whichever contrasts more), never a single fixed hue. Covers the descend/hover/focus ring AND the containment/descend arrow (.cedge shaft plus its marker head), which derives from the same per-fill --ring token; its marker head takes a per-ink marker because marker content is rendered from the defs tree and cannot see the referencing node's custom properties (a constraint recorded in _arrow_markers), and RING_INKS is the closed ink set that makes that emission total. SCOPE: a control's contrast against its host fill, arithmetic like the sibling A4 tests. Whether the affordance otherwise reads as inviting stays perceptual under LLR-055/TC-055.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-106": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Single-sourced concept colour vocabulary (U2 core)", "body": "The mechanized core of uniformity anchor U2 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-uniformity.md). Every concept's fill is LOOKED UP from a declared module-level palette, so the same concept cannot render two hexes — there is only one place a hex can come from. Concretely: every #rrggbb appearing in a rendered dashboard is either a member of a declared palette collection or a `--token:#hex` theme definition (surfaces, text and borders, which encode no concept). SCOPE: this proves single-sourcing of the VOCABULARY, not that a concept was assigned a well-chosen hue — collision and perceptual separation belong to LLR-102.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-107": {"tier": "llr", "title": "One interaction idiom per node role (U4 core)", "body": "The mechanized core of uniformity anchor U4 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-uniformity.md). Two node roles exist and each is spelled ONE way in every emitter that renders it: an IDENTIFIED node (icicle .cell, flat-DAG .wi, knowledge .knode) advertises its registry id through `data-id`; a DETAIL-BEARING drill node (.block, in both the When and How-SW drills) advertises its detail-map key through `data-node`; and a node of either role is made focusable the same way, `tabindex=\"0\"` on the group. Neither role ever carries the other's attribute. SCOPE: ATTRIBUTE parity, not runtime behaviour parity.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-108": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Accessible-name quality, not merely presence (A2 residue)", "body": "The QUALITY half of accessibility anchor A2, mechanizing what LLR-101 leaves perceptual. Three rules over every focusable element and role=img graphic: it HAS an accessible name; that name is never a bare registry id; and navigation LANDMARKS are distinct. SCOPE, narrower than a first measurement suggests: A2 governs INTERACTIVE elements and meaningful graphics — a <title> on a decorative edge path is a tooltip, not an accessible name. Uniqueness is asserted for landmarks ONLY: a descend control for the same container legitimately recurs across drill layers, one visible at a time, so those repeats are one control reached by different paths. Whether a well-formed, unique, descriptive name is the RIGHT description belongs to SR-054's reading-task critique.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-109": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Pairwise deltaE floor across every vocabulary (U5 residue)", "body": "Mechanizes what LLR-102 leaves perceptual: a PAIRWISE deltaE floor across every declared vocabulary, not only within PHASE_ACCENTS. Two floors, each a recorded judgement — 15 WITHIN a vocabulary (every member can sit beside every other in one legend) and 12 ACROSS (two vocabularies share no legend, so the floor is lower rather than absent). The declared tier<->okf mirror is exempt BY DESIGN and is asserted BYTE-EQUAL, so 'exempt' cannot drift into 'nearly equal'. SCOPE: perceptual SEPARATION of the declared palettes. Whether a given hue suits its concept is not asserted.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-110": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Declared weight/alpha/corner tokens (U3 residue)", "body": "Mechanizes what LLR-103 leaves perceptual. Fourteen CSS tokens plus SVG_RX name one step per role for stroke-width, opacity and corner radius, near-duplicates merged into the role's step, and every emitted value must resolve to a declared token. stroke-opacity is checked under the opacity scale deliberately — it is the same scale applied to a stroke. SVG_RX is enforced against both the source literals and every render, because the rx presentation attribute cannot read a CSS custom property portably. SCOPE: token membership, asserted over paint surfaces (style blocks and style/fill/stroke attributes), never over prose that quotes CSS.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-111": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Declared type scale, no raw font-size literals (U1 residue)", "body": "Mechanizes what LLR-104 left perceptual. 'Do the sizes read as visually uniform' was undecidable because the scale was never written down: 18 raw font-size literals against 5 tokens, with .7/.75rem, .9/.95/.98rem, 1.05/1.1rem and 8.5/9px each being near-duplicate steps for ONE role 3-7% apart. Eleven steps are now declared in THREE documented families - node (px, because SVG geometry is fixed px and a rem resizes labels out of their boxes), page (rem), and one relative step (em, for text sized against its parent) - and every emitted font-size must resolve to one. Claiming a single scale across those units would be false, so the LLR states three. SCOPE: step membership over paint surfaces. Whether the chosen steps are well-proportioned is not asserted.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-112": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Keyboard reachability of every wired control (A1 core)", "body": "The mechanized core of accessibility anchor A1 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-accessibility.md): every element the emitted page WIRES an interaction to is keyboard-focusable. The wired set is DERIVED from the document's own DOM-selecting calls and then classified — a control selector (its matches receive per-element click/focus/dblclick/keydown listeners) may match only elements carrying tabindex=0 or natively tab-ordered (a button; an SVG a-with-href, the LLR-101 predicate), and each non-control selector records why its matches are not focus stops. A newly wired selector fails the owning test until classified. The derivation reads EVERY DOM-selecting API (querySelector/querySelectorAll/closest/matches, any quote style) and fails loudly on a non-literal selector argument, which a static read cannot classify. A control-selector wiring loop that attaches click or dblclick must attach focus or keydown beside it, statically asserted. Order-integrity: document order IS emission order, no positive tabindex may reorder the walk, and tabindex=-1 is legal only on the roving tablist buttons. SCOPE — and no wider: focusability, order-integrity and activation PAIRING of the wired set over the shipped artifact plus the seven-fixture emitter sweep. Runtime behaviour equivalence is not driven, and whether the order FEELS sensible stays a reader-experience judgement. Runtime-created controls (the breadcrumb buttons) are native <button>s, keyboard-operable by construction and outside the static walk — a recorded limit.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-113": {"tier": "llr", "title": "No information by colour alone (A3 core)", "body": "The mechanized core of accessibility anchor A3: for every declared colour vocabulary (enumerated from the module constants, the U5/U2 closure, so a new vocabulary enters by existing), every member that actually PAINTS in a rendered document resolves to a legend swatch labelled in words in that same document, with CSS tokens resolved. Status members additionally pair a per-status glyph. The emitted JS detail-badge maps are SUBSTITUTED from TIER_FILL/STATUS_FILL rather than hand-copied, and three drift guards hold every copy equal to the constants: the emitted JS maps byte-match the Python palettes; the CSS status tokens match STATUS_FILL and are asserted theme-invariant; and the tier-legend bijection derives its expectation from the TIER_FILL dict, so a member dropped from the legend also fails. SCOPE and its narrowing: the worded cue must exist in the same document — that it sits within eyeshot of the painted element is layout this does not re-assert. The JS detail-panel badge stays out of the LEGEND rule because its background carries its concept as the badge's own visible text.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-114": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Text-contrast floors, closed over the vocabulary set (A4)", "body": "Accessibility anchor A4 bound. The WCAG 2.1 AA arithmetic is owned by six sibling tests — node fills vs label ink at 4.5:1, the emitted badge/boundary pairs, the no-opacity-discount rule, theme-token fills behind white text in BOTH themes, the hub-is-not-accent regression, and the per-fill focus ring at the 3:1 boundary floor — so this row adds the BINDING plus the gap none of them owned: the SET. Closed by reflection: every UPPERCASE module-level collection holding hex members must be one of the five declared vocabularies, and every member of the REFLECTED set clears 4.5:1 against its label ink (white, except the one declared queued dark-ink exception) and 3:1 for its computed focus ring. The walk is RECURSIVE (nested dicts, tuples, sets, bare strings), counts any 3/4/6/8-digit hex form as hex-bearing, and requires every member be CANONICAL 6-digit lowercase. The theme-token half is DERIVED from the emitted CSS: every fill:var(--x) token must carry a declared role, white-text-fill roles clear 4.5:1 under white in BOTH themes, container-fill roles keep --text readable in both. SCOPE — and no wider: contrast arithmetic over declared constants and emitted theme tokens (the ring boundary also binds under SR-054 T5 as LLR-105); a lowercase module-level palette would evade the reflection and is forbidden by the module's UPPERCASE-constant convention, a recorded reliance. Rendered-pixel measurement is not asserted.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-115": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Task findability - labelled entry within one tab switch (T1 core)", "body": "The mechanized core of usability anchor T1 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md). Each of the three core reading tasks the rubric concretizes — find the project state, find the next work, find how the parts connect — is reachable within one tab switch of the landing view, its entry point a LABELLED nav control or a NAMED surface on the landing view, never an unlabelled region a reader must discover by clicking. THE DESIGN CONSTRAINT that makes the check honest: it is judged against the RENDERED artifact carrying real registry data, NEVER the nav skeleton. The guard resolves each task's entry point from the emitted document (task 1 -> the landing Definition-completeness/Execution cards, 0 switches; task 2 -> the landing Next-work card, 0 switches; task 3 -> the How-SW nav tab, 1 switch), asserts each is LABELLED and within one switch, and asserts the next-work surface is NON-EMPTY on the zero-active/all-queued fixture. SCOPE — PROVEN: a labelled entry point per task, reachable in one switch, and the next-work surface non-empty across the practice fixtures (zero-active, waiting-with-predecessor, drained). DELIBERATELY NOT: whether the reader subjectively knows what to do next; T1 leaves the critique entirely, with no residue under LLR-055/TC-055.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-116": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Viewport-fit by scale-to-fit with a legibility floor (T7 core)", "body": "The mechanized core of usability anchor T7 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md). Every emitted SVG (all three wrappers: _svg_wrap for dag/sw/know, the icicle panel, _drill_layer_svg) carries width:100% + max-width:<natural> + min-width:<natural x SHRINK_FLOOR> instead of a FIXED pixel width, so a diagram fills its container and scales down as the viewport narrows. SCOPE and its deliberate narrowing: a view whose natural width exceeds 390 / SHRINK_FLOOR still scrolls horizontally WITH its cue — the row's own fallback rule made mechanical. Closing that remainder needs a narrow-width re-layout (stacked columns), which is not this row.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_render.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-117": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Theme-lock: one theme mechanism and no mixed-family surface/ink pair (T6 core)", "body": "The mechanized core of usability anchor T6 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md). The measurable content is FAMILY PAIRING, not the absence of literals: a fixed status swatch carrying fixed white ink is theme-locked CORRECTLY, since neither side flips. A page inverts mid-view in exactly two ways and this row forbids both. (1) A SECOND THEME MECHANISM below :root — the document declares exactly one prefers-color-scheme block, targeting :root, and one color-scheme, also at :root. (2) A MIXED PAIR — a theme-varying ink on a theme-invariant surface, or the reverse. The two families are DERIVED from the emitted document (the dark block's override set IS the definition of theme-varying), never hand-listed, and the dark block may not override a token :root never declares. Applied to CSS rules declaring both a background and an ink, to every emitted SVG node pairing an inline rect fill with an inline text fill, and to CSS-declared SVG text fills paired against the family their host nodes actually paint in. SCOPE and its deliberate narrowings: --ring is excluded because it is stamped FROM the host fill (LLR-105 owns its floor); the JS-composed badge idiom pairs background and ink at runtime from the invariant vocabulary, where LLR-114's arithmetic owns it; and the page CSS has exactly ONE rule declaring a classifiable background AND ink today (body), so that half is prospective while the sweep's weight sits on the node and text-fill halves. Whether the chosen palettes are PLEASANT stays perceptual.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-118": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Generated owner decision surface (registry + attestation depth)", "body": "The RENDERED half of the Modified/Draft attestation regime whose gate SR-049 derives. gen_open_items renders (a) every pending row of docs/requirements/open-items.csv as a decision brief and (b) every SR whose Status is Draft (ratification owed) or Modified (re-attest owed) with its whole chain's per-cell before/after. Two contracts the emitted file carries: the attestation-baseline STAMP (a --since render must be reproducible by the freshness gate, or --check compares against a different history) and the check-the-baseline empty state (an auto-derived baseline sitting AFTER a pre-regime amendment renders no cells, which must never read as nothing-changed). The machine-local advisory region is masked before comparing. SCOPE and its deliberate narrowings: this module RENDERS and owns no second opinion — the attestation model is trace.reattest_model and the pending pointers are gen_trajectory.pending_block, both imported, so a disagreement with `trace.py --ratify` means this view is the defect and the brief wins. NOT claimed: that the rendered page is legible or well-composed.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_open_items.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-119": {"tier": "llr", "title": "T4 label legibility, mechanized: ink inside its box, truncation with a reveal", "body": "The mechanized floor of usability anchor T4 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md), widening the anchor's existing owner rather than adding a rubric anchor. The clause made objective: no character a block renders may fall outside that block's own rect, horizontally or vertically. The emitter FITS the sub-label to the column it will actually be drawn in — a second line when one will not hold it, then an ellipsis — with three text lines the ceiling, reusing the grid the id/name wrap idiom already proved fits row_h; a sub-label that already fits one line renders byte-identically to before. SCOPE — PROVEN: for every block of every drill layer in the emitted document, each label line's ink box lies inside its own node rect, measured from the emitted geometry with the emitter's declared per-character widths and the document's declared font sizes, entities counted as the single glyph they render. Second half: this emitter does not truncate the next-work title by a fixed budget — the card emits the whole clause and lets its own width do the fitting, the declared bound _NEXT_WORK_TITLE caps ONE item's HEIGHT rather than its text, and where it bites the remainder discloses through a NATIVE <details> cut at a word so head and remainder rejoin continuously when opened. DELIBERATELY NOT: whether an ellipsis a reader meets is ACTIONABLE — truncated-without-affordance stays T4's perceptual residue under LLR-055/TC-055, so T4 remains a live critique anchor; the full string keeps its homes in the block's title, aria-label and data-summary.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-120": {"tier": "llr", "title": "No edge through an unrelated node box (T8 objective floor)", "body": "The mechanized floor of usability anchor T8 (docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md). T8 is two clauses and this row claims only the first: NO EDGE PASSES THROUGH AN UNRELATED NODE BOX. SCOPE — PROVEN: across the shipped dashboard plus one fixture per emitter, every emitted wire, sampled as the polyline a reader's eye follows, clears every node rect that is not its own source or target. MEASURED at the binding: 264 shipped wires, 0 through a box. DELIBERATELY NOT: crossing count, lane separation, or where a crossing falls — that residue stays under LLR-055/TC-055, which is why T8 remains a live critique anchor. Separately and NOT claimed here: at the router's own 3px clearance rather than the bare box, one wire does graze, so _detour_d exhausting its lane candidates and returning the least-bad fallback is a reachable state rather than a theoretical one; that wire's identity and the lane-separation work belong to the open work item.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_graph.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-121": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Kit-version stamp", "body": "write_kit_version stamps docs/kit-version (SHA + date, -dirty on an uncommitted kit tree) so a re-sync is diffable.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/bootstrap.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-122": {"tier": "llr", "title": "dev-setup wires core.hooksPath", "body": "dev-setup wires the pre-commit floor (core.hooksPath) from its baseline, so the floor is active from the setup the contributor actually runs.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/dev-setup.template.sh", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-123": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Deterministic traincar ordering", "body": "Orders eligible traincars by (gate, Priority desc, downstream-dependent count desc, hard-path desc, first WI id).", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/schedule.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-124": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Generated status + run-state", "body": "docs/status.md's generated block is maintained by the trunk lane only: trunk_step --regen runs gen_trajectory --status after derive_gate (the gate is its input), and the status-map freshness gate byte-compares it on the trunk while work branches skip (SS5.2). (docs/run-state and its four-valued dispatcher outcome retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5.)", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/trunk_step.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-130": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Dashboard staleness check", "body": "Implements stale/missing --check with a git as-of stamp excluded from stamp-only comparison.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-131": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Contradiction-safe dual-plan class", "body": "Derives contradiction-safe single-WI scheduling from PlanMode and quarantines a contradictory SafetyClass.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/schedule.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-132": {"tier": "llr", "title": "PAGE ratification-level mapping", "body": "Applies the declared level's continuation semantics to a PAGE at both the dispatcher disposition and the --dual-plan single-shot round: `page_action(human_held, keep_nondependent)` over the four cells the retired enum's three words could not span — a human-held tier pages, a loop-held one STALLs, and `None` normalizes to the strictest cell rather than to `bool(None)`'s most permissive one.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_loop.py", "status": "Modified"}, "LLR-133": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Spine stand-alone rule", "body": "provenance_findings flags a WI id or a process-doc citation across the normative cells of all three spine registries, joining the --strict failure set via exit_code.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/trace_text.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-134": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Requirement form rules", "body": "form_findings applies six rules — one obligation per requirement, no non-obligation modal, no actorless passive, no unfalsifiable term, no open-ended clause, no obligation keyword in an LLR Detail — skipping Draft rows, and joins the --strict failure set via exit_code.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/trace_text.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-135": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Paraphrase advisory", "body": "paraphrase_advisories compares a child cell's significant words (alphabetic runs of four characters or more, lowercased) against its parent's and reports when the share of the CHILD's words that also appear in the parent exceeds the declared threshold — the child is the denominator, so a long parent cannot dilute a short echoing child. Reported in its own advisory pipe, which never joins a failure set.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/trace_text.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-136": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Registry converter (CSV <-> spec folder)", "body": "Converts between the registry homes per the Phase 2a grammar (TOML +++ frontmatter, Deliverable in the body, status from the directory, order key only as a CSV-order migration artifact). --verify round-trips CSV->specs->CSV in a temp dir, exits nonzero on any cell mismatch, and reports byte identity with a reason. write_spec_file is the single spec writer (to_specs and plan_artifacts filing both go through it). Refuses unknown statuses by row, non-empty targets without --force, and any in-flight active/<branch>/ claim by name (status is the FIRST path component, the loaders' rule; conversion is a drained-stop operation per §3.2 - the Phase 4 claim protocol made the state reachable).", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/wi_convert.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-137": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Serial trunk step (log compile + regen)", "body": "Compiles docs/log.d/*.md into docs/log.md via plan_artifacts.append_log_summary in (git add-time, filename) order - all fragments validated first (name shape, ## heading, reserved section headings refused, an adding commit required - on HEAD, or on MERGE_HEAD during the integrator's merge composition), relative links rebased from docs/log.d/ to docs/, fragments deleted after append, all-or-nothing on any validation error. Never commits; the caller owns the commit. The artifact-regen half is LLR-142 (SR-134).", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/trunk_step.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-138": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Tracked pause reader (dual-home)", "body": "pause_reason reads the ONE tracked home docs/work/pause via tracked_pause (TOML reason/since; malformation fails closed to the pinned loud placeholder). The legacy untracked lane/pause half retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5 - a stray local file is inert, pinned by test.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_common.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-139": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Pause surfaced in the pending block", "body": "Renders zero-or-one 'Paused since <since> - <reason>' bullet in the committed-tree-pure pending region from docs/work/pause - declared since verbatim, no clock, the since clause omitted when empty, fail-closed text on malformation pinned equal to the reader's placeholder.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/traj_status.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-140": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Local integrator (claim + queue + audit)", "body": "claim: §2.3 steps 1+2 with the refusal ladder (tracked pause via agent_common.tracked_pause, dirty tree, existing branch, unsafe name, non-ordinary safety_class, off-frontier id) before a git mv + trunk commit + branch cut. finished_branches: a claimed branch whose tip holds no spec under active/<branch>/ (the closing move IS the signal). integrate_one: --no-ff --no-commit merge, trunk_step.py folded into the merge commit so it passes its own floor, check.py --jobs 0 at the declared tier on the composed tree read fail-closed (nonzero exit OR any SKIP line refuses), verdict gate per docs/review-policy using score_reviews.parse_verdict with the verdict's last branch commit required no older than the last non-review non-fragment commit, ff-only trunk advance, branch deleted on green, candidate parked on red. audit: git log --first-parent --no-merges over --since..HEAD; paths outside docs/work/, docs/log.d/, docs/log.md and the stack.ini [generated] set flag the commit.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/integrate.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-141": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Work-branch lane signal + freshness skip", "body": "On a claimed work branch the _TRUNK_FRESHNESS_STEPS set SKIPs with a §5.2 notice, exit code untouched; the plan listing still names every step. The claim signal answers in two stages - docs/work/active/<branch>/ on disk (the fast path), else the branch's own committed history (git log -1 -- docs/work/active/<branch>) - so the closing commit that archives the claim, and every commit after it, keep the skip. No git, a failing git, or an empty answer all read as the trunk lane: a false work-branch positive would switch the freshness gates off on the one branch that owns regenerating them.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-142": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Trunk-step artifact regen (dependency order)", "body": "Regenerates arch-map, okf, derived-gate, dashboard, status block and open-items as subprocesses in dependency order (derived-gate before its two consumers), skipping absent artifact families with a notice and stopping loudly at the first failure. Never commits; the caller owns the commit.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/trunk_step.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-143": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Plain-launch drive loop (claim->build->integrate)", "body": "The serial scheduling front end a plain agent_loop.py launch runs: each cycle re-derives the ready frontier (schedule.frontier), refuses on the tracked pause and on a dirty trunk at the cycle top, resumes a parked claimed branch else claims the next queued WI in build order (integrate.claim, branch name check-ref-format-validated), launches one worker session on the claimed branch's worktree (agent_loop.py --worktree --wi with the session dials forwarded), drains the serial merge queue (integrate.integrate) counting merges from finished_branches, and stops loudly on any composed refusal - stranded claims (active specs with no branch ref) refuse by name, NEEDS-HUMAN propagates as exit 7, a stall aborts, an empty frontier drains to exit 0. The session-config preflight applies only when work needs a worker. Never pushes.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/drive.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-144": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Terminal lane outcomes (handback + quarantine)", "body": "close_partial: commits the lane's work as-is, moves each claimed spec to the TERMINAL partial/ (nothing re-claims it, so nothing strands) and writes ONE IMMUTABLE per-close report under docs/handbacks/ naming the claimed outcome, the reason, the commit range and the keep/discard split. The report is the close EVENT's identity — immutable so it never moves, unique so a second close is a second document — which is what dissolved the starvation class five mutable-proxy dedup mechanisms died on. A second close is refused rather than overwriting the first, and any refusal restores every report the call wrote (schedule reads queued+blockref as blocked) and the driver cannot claim and return it forever. quarantine: the ruled red arm - reverts the product paths to the merge base and keeps the failing diff as a bar-inert .patch under docs/work/handback/, reading `git diff --name-status -z` as three-field RECORDS so a rename cannot revert the wrong path, checking every git return code, and restoring rather than reporting when a revert step fails. Both leave the branch finished, so integrate.branch_outcomes reads the outcome off the spec's DIRECTORY - complete/=merged, cancelled/=cancelled, any open folder=handback - and every lane ends in a merge.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/handback.py", "status": "Modified"}, "LLR-145": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Link-aware spec-move ritual (move + relink indivisible)", "body": "move_spec moves a registry/spec file and keeps every markdown link in the repo true as ONE indivisible operation: the moved file's own relative targets are re-relativised against its new directory, every inbound link whose target resolves to the vacated path is redirected (link text untouched, #fragments carried, external/protocol-relative/bare-anchor targets left alone, root-relative targets excused only in the rebase half, line endings preserved via newline=''), and every rewritten file is staged with the move so the ritual lands as one changeset. integrate.claim and handback.close_partial call it for the claim and close moves; the CLI performs the worker's terminal close move and the dated docs/archive/specs/ archival. expected_relink is the pure oracle integrate._abandoned_claim compares byte-for-byte to excuse a crashed claim's own relink writes without widening its conviction to any .md edit.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/spec_move.py", "status": "Modified"}, "LLR-146": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Declared-figure provenance presence check", "body": "Scans root *.md + docs/**/*.md (check_doc_refs.doc_files - the same walk, so the two doc checks agree on the surface and generated trees stay skipped) plus docs/stack.ini, with docs/reviews/ records out of scope (a verdict quotes defective markers as evidence); each opt-in fig: marker is judged on its own attributes and must carry a non-empty, wordful cmd= and rev= pair (rev bare or quoted), or a non-empty derived= derivation; a marker is grammar prose that declares nothing only when every attribute value is wholly a placeholder token (angle-bracket-wrapped, or the bare ellipsis - so the convention text ships scaffold-clean); in a judged marker a placeholder-shaped value counts as absent, never as satisfying (an unfilled rev=<revision> names no revision), while a metacharacter inside a longer value is command text and the marker is judged on its attributes; findings name the missing half and quote the line; GENERATED marker blocks and fig-ok lines are exempt; warn-first, --strict exits 1. Presence only, never truth - re-derivation is rung 2, deliberately not built.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_figures.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-147": {"tier": "llr", "title": "SN-coverage gate rung + uncovered basis count", "body": "sn_gate reads the cited set sn_cited_ids builds from SR SN-Refs (an F5 duplicate of trace.py's parse, pinned equal by test_rule_sync like phase_num and LLR_EXEMPT): a RATIFIED SN cited by zero SRs caps the raw level at G0 - a ratified-but-unanswered need has not earned G1 - while a Draft SN stays exempt from the coverage rung and rides the existing draft rung (one fact, one finding; the itemized 'SN has no SR' listing remains trace.py's orphan rule at G2 strictness, reading the SAME cited set, so the gate input and the listing cannot contradict on one registry state). _raw_level rebuilds the cited set from the rows in scope, so the ex-draft counterfactual drops a Draft SR's citation with its row and never fabricates coverage. The compared basis line gains uncovered=N (ratified-and-uncited count) beside drafts=N/modified=N - additive for readers, but --check compares the line whole, so it is a cache-format change downstream repos regenerate through.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/derive_gate.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-148": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Derived next phase as an output mode", "body": "--next-phase prints max(Phase over non-draft spine rows) + 1 - the basis line's phase=N derivation exposed as an output mode (no second parse, docs/gate never written), bare so the intake mint helper and any agent can int() the answer when a confirmed scope change opens a new phase; a Draft row's phase is not yet scope and never bumps the answer; an unphased spine is the implicit foundation (1), so it prints 2. The boundary it serves is ruled (owner 2026-08-01): a phase increments on an adjudication-confirmed scope change or a ratified draft-SN batch, never on the raw derived-gate drop.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/derive_gate.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-149": {"tier": "llr", "title": "§A8 admission: the kind x ratification-level table + the spine barrier", "body": "The dispatcher's one scheduling decision (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A4/§A8), consuming schedule.kind_of - the §A1 classification's enforcement point. _kind_action is the ruled §A8 table verbatim per kind x human-held answer (`agent_common.human_holds`), and a human-held attestation/gate row SURFACES even under keep_nondependent — that dial answers whether OTHER lanes keep running, never whether the machine may ratify; _admission is the barrier as a pure decision: any exclusive-kind frontier row stops new admission (wait) until every lane is back, then ALL spine rows admit together as one exclusive batch (one branch, one agent_loop --wi 'A;B' worker, one re-attest window), attestation/gate rows surface under attended (drain, exit 0 naming the open-items cards) and admit as the queued batch at single-ratify's close; _lane_count resolves the lanes dial CLI > AGENT_LANES > stack.ini [agent-loop] > 1 (absent key = 1; the template seeds 2); gap_census is the empty-frontier ladder's rung-1 seam - unverified in-scope SRs, orphan rows and draft SNs from trace.analyze, handed to the intake mint helper (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A5.2), never minted in the lane.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/dispatch.py", "status": "Modified"}, "LLR-150": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Lane mechanics: worktree, worker subprocess, §A2 refresh subprocess", "body": "One lane's mechanics extracted from the dispatcher (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A4.2): ensure the lane worktree (integrate.lane_worktree, the one home both the worker and the refresh agree on), build the worker argv with the plain launch's session dials forwarded explicitly, launch the worker subprocess (spawn_worker non-blocking for the lane table; run_worker its blocking form and the default worker seam), and run the §A2 station refresh as the lane's OWN subprocess (spawn_refresh - the invoker's integrate.py refresh CLI under the harness interpreter) so N lanes' bars overlap instead of queueing at the dispatcher (§A4.3). Reports outcomes, decides none: the tree signal (specs moved out of active/<branch>/) plus exit codes are the whole protocol - no state file, no back-channel.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/lane.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-151": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Dispatch-lock claim rung + the spine batch claim", "body": "The §A4.1 authority flip's integrator half (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A4.1): _claim_refusal's safety_class != ordinary arm is DELETED (admission is the dispatcher's decision) and the claim REQUIRES the dispatch lock (agent_common.dispatch_lock_path, out/agent-loop.lock - the lock a live dispatcher holds for its process lifetime), acquired at ONE site (_dispatch_lock, mirroring _slot's one-site discipline for the merge slot's own lock) with the ladder run before it so the clean-trunk rung never refuses over the lock's own file: a hand claim on an idle station works, a hand claim during live lanes is unrepresentable, and the dispatcher's in-process claim passes dispatch_lock_held=True. claim/_claim_refusal/_abandoned_claim/_claim_delta are batch-aware: a spine batch is ONE claim commit moving every batched spec (subject joins the ids; the single-WI subject stays byte-identical), and integrate() takes an optional branches= restriction so the dispatcher merges each lane's branch as its own refresh completes.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/integrate.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-152": {"tier": "llr", "title": "The adjudication kind: rank-1 slot + no-bar arm + the bar strictness key", "body": "The `adjudication` kind (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A5.2) joins the declared SafetyClass vocabulary: exclusive on the concurrency axis, rank 1 on the order axis - the slot §A1 reserved, filled without renumbering the ruled table. The kind's lane runs NO product bar (integrate._lane_bar_directives + _refresh_bar: the refresh still merges trunk in, runs the trunk step and commits a verified Bar-Green attestation whose summary reads no-bar honestly) because its outputs are Status cells and the work registry, nothing a product bar can speak to; a mixed batch or unreadable frontmatter fails TOWARD the bar. The optional `bar = G1|G2|G3` frontmatter key rides the same directive read: bar declares verification strictness for this row's lane; it never affects scheduling (load_wis deliberately does not parse it) - the refresh passes the batch's strictest value to check.py --gate and refuses a malformed one loudly.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/schedule.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-153": {"tier": "llr", "title": "The unified trunk-side intake mint + the context block + the ratification-level arms", "body": "The R1/R3 invariant sealed (log.md Decisions 2026-08-01): a WI id is created only by a human trunk commit or this helper - lanes never mint. next_wi_id is max+1 over every spec FILENAME under docs/work/ (active/<branch>/ included). Three triggers, serial by construction: (a) the ratified/routed-cell diff on the merged commit via check_trajectory.staged_spine_amendments -> one adjudication row listing each changed row/cell/before-after (routed traced cells: SN-Refs, Verifies, and LLR SR-Refs per the ruled cell split); (b) a merged spec carrying ## Handback -> the disposition row (outcomes cancel / defer / re-queue with drafted follow-up / surface an open item; NEVER minted for an adjudication row - no recursion, and handback.hand_back refuses the act itself); (c) the dispatcher's gap census -> concrete gap-closure rows, deduped against every existing row. Drafts-not-mints: a merged adjudication row's ## Dispositions fenced-toml drafts mint at ITS merge, validated loudly. Tier signals are measurable (rows touched, gate delta, handback reason class). context_block renders the pure registry joins (cancelled precedent WITH REASONS first, pending OIs, the LLR/TC code map, CMP knowledge packs, IF seams, precedent reviews) - advisory-never-gating, three consumers. flip_verified enacts ruled decision 2: recommend-only under attended, flip under single-ratify/autonomous, unknown levels fail toward recommend. The mint commit mirrors the claim's bookkeeping shape; every derived title is deterministic, so the sweep CLI re-run is idempotent.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/intake.py", "status": "Modified"}, "LLR-154": {"tier": "llr", "title": "The merge slot's post-merge intake arm", "body": "integrate_one's post-merge arm is the intake's one honest hook point: the merge has landed, the slot is still held, trunk is serial - intake.intake_after_merge(root, pre-merge head, head, outcomes, branch) mints there as one bookkeeping commit (RULING-6: docs/work/ + the declared generated set), and a mint refusal stops the run loudly while the merge stands (recovery: fix trunk-side, then python intake.py sweep). The refresh's bar directives (_lane_bar_directives, read off the same trunk claim the slot reads) carry the adjudication no-bar arm and the bar strictness pin to _run_bar's --gate.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/integrate.py", "status": "Verified"}, "LLR-155": {"tier": "llr", "title": "The config layer: one reader, checked shape, one comparison", "body": "`process_config` parses `docs/process.toml` once; `declared_policy` reads a dial through it with the legacy file as fallback; `process_shape_findings` refuses every line shape the hooks' sh cannot follow; `config_conflicts` returns refusal STRINGS (never raises — `dispatch.run` sits in the tick loop's caller, where an exception would rewrite an exit-code contract) covering the double-declaration, the wrong type and the out-of-range value; `ratification_level` + `human_holds` are the ordinal and the ONE comparison every consumer makes.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_common.py", "status": "Draft"}, "LLR-156": {"tier": "llr", "title": "The legacy converter, and the one word that is not a rename", "body": "`set_process_key` answers THREE states (`wrote`/`unchanged`/`missing`) and the write decides the delete; `add_if_missing` makes the write total. `gate-policy` gets its own arm because its single word expands to three dials, so it cannot be a one-key `LEGACY_CONFIG` row. `LEGACY_RATIFICATION` is F5-duplicated from `agent_common` (bootstrap imports no kit sibling — it must run standing on nothing but the kit directory it copies FROM) and pinned equal by `test_rule_sync`.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/bootstrap.py", "status": "Draft"}, "LLR-157": {"tier": "llr", "title": "The second axis: which tier is in process", "body": "`spine_stage` returns the LOWEST unfinished tier, where a MISSING artifact belongs to its own tier rather than to its parent (an SR with no LLR yet is stage 2, because what is being written is an LLR), `Modified` is the SR's own tier and is checked FIRST, and any other not-yet-Verified SR is checked LAST because a child in flight is the honest answer while it is in flight. `stage_to_gate` states the mapping to the harness gate once, deliberately lossy in one direction only. The value rides `docs/gate` as an APPENDED basis field.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/derive_gate.py", "status": "Draft"}, "LLR-158": {"tier": "llr", "title": "The digest that anchors an attestation", "body": "normative_text() joins a row's RATIFIED cells in header order with a unit separator, reusing spine_cell_class rather than a second list so the digest and the amend-without-flip warn can never disagree about what normative means; the id and Status are excluded because neither is content - the id is the key the digest is looked up BY, and folding Status in would make every flip look like an amendment and every amendment invisible behind its own flip. sn_normative_text() takes the RAW table line for a stakeholder need rather than a parsed projection, because the three need tables have different shapes and a projection would hash the garbling. current_digests() is the one reader that joins both halves. The WRITER and the co-mutation guard are not here yet: which cells hold the anchor depends on the registry carrier, ruled separately (OI-12).", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py", "status": "Draft"}, "LLR-159": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Admission order and the red-TC census", "body": "`_judgement_first` is a STABLE PARTITION over the frontier, applied inside `_admission` before the surface/dispatch split so it can never move work past a human-held stop. `red_tc_census` rides the existing finding-string seam with a distinct prefix; `_red_tc_line` and `parse_red_tc` sit adjacent because the grammar is a contract between two modules, and `intake` routes on the parser rather than re-splitting prose.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/dispatch.py", "status": "Draft"}, "LLR-160": {"tier": "llr", "title": "The mechanical queue-overlap pre-filter", "body": "Three signals over OPEN rows only — Jaccard over title token sets at a deliberately high bar, shared `SR-Refs`, and a shared anchor-stripped `SpecRef` — each reported once per pair per signal with both ids, in sorted order. Printed on the warn-only line beside backlog staleness; never appended to `errors`.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py", "status": "Draft"}, "LLR-161": {"tier": "llr", "title": "The close ritual and the disposition it owes", "body": "`close_partial` writes the report FIRST (it is the event's identity), refuses when one already exists, and restores EVERY report this call wrote on any refusal — so a multi-spec lane cannot half-close. `intake._close_drafts` titles the disposition on the report PATH and nothing else, and says so LOUDLY when a terminal spec has no report rather than minting zero rows quietly.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/handback.py", "status": "Draft"}, "LLR-162": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Prompts as loaded files, catalogued and fingerprinted", "body": "`load` reads a shipped template (or an operator override) and raises a named `PromptError`; `fill` refuses an unknown slot and an unfilled declared slot; `digest` normalizes line endings before hashing; `preflight` turns a missing template into a launch-time refusal. `gen_prompt_catalog.py` renders the catalogue with a `--check` freshness gate, and `agent_common.prompt_fingerprint` records the rendered result per session beside the template's own name.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/prompts.py", "status": "Draft"}, "LLR-163": {"tier": "llr", "title": "Argv arrays, and the adjudicator as a routed phase", "body": "`split_cmd` recognises a JSON array ONLY when the value parses as a list, so no existing template can change meaning; a non-string token raises rather than being stringified. The prompt-transport decision reads the resulting TOKENS, so it is identical for both forms. Alongside it, `ADJUDICATE` joins `agent_route.WEIGHT_PHASES` and `agent_loop.DEFAULT_PHASE_TIER` with its own `route_intent` arm: cross-family like the reviewers, tier pinned by the row's measured estimate, and deliberately deaf to the implementer's escalation overrides.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/agent_session.py", "status": "Draft"}, "LLR-164": {"tier": "llr", "title": "The generated prompt catalogue and its freshness gate", "body": "`render` is PURE — it takes no arguments and reads only the shipped templates — so `--check` and the write path cannot disagree about what \"current\" means. `--check` compares on NORMALIZED line endings, for the same reason the digest is taken that way: a CRLF checkout is not a stale catalogue. The file is declared in `docs/stack.ini [generated]` as kind `promptcatalog`, so the trunk lane owns it and a work branch never commits it.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/gen_prompt_catalog.py", "status": "Draft"}, "LLR-165": {"tier": "llr", "title": "The spine carrier converter (markdown + CSV -> TOML)", "body": "convert() walks the four tiers and returns (findings, written_paths), so the caller decides whether a lossy conversion writes. rows_to_toml emits `[<table>.<ID>]` with the id PREFIX RETAINED and BARE - TOML allows `-` in a bare key - because ~6,400 hand-authored citations use the prefixed token and a numeric key buys no type change (TOML keys are always strings, and `001` and `1` are two legal DIFFERENT keys, which is a near-miss the prefixed form does not have). cell_to_value types by COLUMN, not by guessing: REF_COLS become arrays, INT_COLS become ints, an empty cell becomes an absent key, and everything else stays a string - this is a carrier change, not a schema change. toml_scalar emits multi-line basic strings for anything long or newline-bearing (tomllib is read-only by design, so the kit emits its own, as it already does in wi_convert.toml_string and bootstrap.set_process_key). sn_to_toml keeps the edge-case tier's own lifecycle/scenario/expected fields as THEMSELVES and writes kind= from the heading, retiring section-as-state. compare() re-reads what was emitted and diffs it against an {id: {key: text}} projection built from either carrier, so ONE comparison serves both instead of a second copy that drifts.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/migrate_carrier.py", "status": "Draft"}, "LLR-166": {"tier": "llr", "title": "The spine carrier's one reader, and the dual-home refusal", "body": "PURE like trace_text (text in, rows out; no git, no argv) except resolve()'s existence check, because the dual-home refusal is the one rule that must not be duplicated. rows_from_toml presents rows under TODAY'S COLUMN NAMES via SPINE_COLUMN - the exact inverse of migrate_carrier.KEY - so the carrier change is data plus a loader rather than a sixteen-module rename; SPINE_TABLE is keyed on the ID COLUMN, not on a path, because a path carries a carrier suffix and therefore moves. An absent key stays absent rather than becoming \"\", and a decode error returns None rather than {}. resolve() RAISES when both carriers exist rather than picking by precedence, and load() raises on a carrier that exists and will not parse. carriers() returns BOTH paths so an applicability test spans the cutover.", "meta": "Module: project-trajectory/scripts/spine_carrier.py", "status": "Draft"}, "TC-001": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-001;LLR-001", "body": "Satisfies SR-001 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the trace suite; a linked chain is orphan-free and an injected orphan fails --strict.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-002": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-002;LLR-002", "body": "Satisfies SR-002 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the registry-checks suite; duplicate/malformed ids and mis-columned rows fail --strict-integrity.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-003": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-003;LLR-003", "body": "Satisfies SR-003 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the registry-checks suite; leftover -000 rows, empty/out-of-vocab fields, and (once the spine is phased) a ratified row whose Phase is not a full-cell bare integer (blank, prefixed like P1/v2, or unparseable) are flagged.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-004": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-004;LLR-004", "body": "Satisfies SR-004 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the acceptance-criteria advisory suite; an unpinned comparative warns without changing the exit code.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-005": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-005;LLR-005", "body": "Satisfies SR-005 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the off-spine registry suites; PB and current REPO/legacy MOD back-link findings fire and -000 rows are ignored.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-006": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-006;LLR-006", "body": "Satisfies SR-006 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the check-harness suite; gate steps run and a missing required tool fails with SKIP(missing).", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-007": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-007;LLR-007", "body": "Satisfies SR-007 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the stack-profile suite; commands and tiers/coverage/arch-map resolve from stack.ini.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-008": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-008;LLR-008", "body": "Satisfies SR-008 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the stack-profile suite; malformed/non-integer/missing-binary profiles fail loudly.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-009": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-009;LLR-009", "body": "Satisfies SR-009 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the profile suite; a non-Python profile omits Python-only files and seeds files-mode arch-map.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-010": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-010;LLR-010", "body": "Satisfies SR-010 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the bootstrap suite; a fresh scaffold's harness runs green.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-011": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-011;LLR-011;SR-111;LLR-121", "body": "Satisfies SR-011 and SR-111 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the bootstrap suite; a re-run leaves existing files unchanged, --force overwrites, and docs/kit-version is stamped.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-012": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-012;LLR-012", "body": "Satisfies SR-012 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the check-docs suite; a broken link or missing vision tag fails --stale.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-013": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-013;LLR-013", "body": "Satisfies SR-013 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the check-flows suite; a conformant flow passes and a malformed one fails.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-014": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-014;LLR-014", "body": "Satisfies SR-014 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the check-perf suite; a within-tolerance metric passes and a regression fails.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-015": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-015;LLR-015", "body": "Satisfies SR-015 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the perf-budgets suite; an unresolvable or empty PB Refs is a finding.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-016": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-016;LLR-016", "body": "Satisfies SR-016 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the check-stubs suite; a stub at the declared gate fails and clean source passes.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-017": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-017;LLR-017", "body": "Satisfies SR-017 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the check-privacy suite; a staged secret is blocked with privacy-check off.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-018": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-018;LLR-018", "body": "Satisfies SR-018 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the check-privacy suite; PII/identity classes fire only with privacy-check on and honor EXEMPT_EMAILS.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-019": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-019;LLR-019", "body": "Satisfies SR-019 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the pre-commit-hook suite; a failing integrity/secrets check blocks the commit.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-020": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-020;LLR-020", "body": "Satisfies SR-020 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the pre-push-hook suite; a secret/identity in the push range blocks the push.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-021": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-021;LLR-021", "body": "Satisfies SR-021 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the hook suites' python-probe cases; a missing/aliased python3 reports clearly without crashing.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-022": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-022;LLR-022", "body": "Satisfies SR-022 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the check-vendored suite; a drifted vendored copy is a finding.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-023": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-023;LLR-023", "body": "Satisfies SR-023 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the gen-arch-map suite; --check fails on a stale map and regeneration rewrites only the marker block.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-024": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-024;LLR-024", "body": "Satisfies SR-024 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the gen-cases suite; the spec grammar expands to the expected case set.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-025": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-025;LLR-025", "body": "Satisfies SR-025 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the skills-index suite; INDEX.csv regenerates from the SKILL.md frontmatter.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-026": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-026;LLR-026", "body": "Satisfies SR-026 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the agent-loop suite; the dispatcher and its workers resume headless (registry + reservations + explicit assignments) without blocking on any prompt; a no-{prompt} CmdTemplate delivers its prompt on stdin (build_argv/run_session), a large prompt surviving untruncated.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-027": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-027;LLR-027", "body": "Satisfies SR-027 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the agent-loop suite; preflight exits a typed code on a non-git dir / missing CLI / private author.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-028": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-028;LLR-028", "body": "Satisfies SR-028 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the agent-loop suite; a zero-commit repo is guarded and an all-ERROR region reads as an unavailable agent.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-029": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-029;LLR-029", "body": "Satisfies SR-029 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the agent-loop suite's lock tests (ported from the retired tracks suite): a hard-exited holder's kernel lock is OS-released and the next run acquires it cleanly.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-030": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-030;LLR-030", "body": "Satisfies SR-030 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the agent-loop suite's lock tests (ported from the retired tracks suite): a second coordinator on a held lock is refused cross-process; a contention errno refuses; an unlockable filesystem degrades warn-only.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-031": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-031;LLR-031", "body": "Satisfies SR-031 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the process-config and policy suites: every enforcer reads one value per dial from docs/process.toml, the hooks' sh and tomllib agree over the adversarial shape table, and a mixed config is refused.", "status": "Modified"}, "TC-032": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-032;LLR-032;SR-113;LLR-122", "body": "Satisfies SR-032 and SR-113 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the onboard/dev-setup suite; the scaffolded scripts run to a green setup and dev-setup wires the pre-commit floor (core.hooksPath).", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-033": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-033;LLR-033", "body": "Satisfies SR-033 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run gen_release_checklist.py over a warn-tier PB budget; assert the generated checklist lists the budget id and its allocation.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-034": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-034", "body": "Satisfies SR-034 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the dependency-ledger suite; an AST scan asserts every kit script's top-level imports resolve to stdlib, a local sibling, or a Kind=python row in docs/dependencies.md, and the scanner is driven over a fixture tree with a genuinely undeclared import to prove it catches one.", "status": "Modified"}, "TC-035": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-035;SR-114", "body": "Satisfies SR-035 and SR-114 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Analyze the CI matrix result across Linux/Windows/macOS x Python 3.11/latest.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-036": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-036", "body": "Satisfies SR-036 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Inspect a re-sync done per ADOPTING.md section 6 against the docs/kit-version diff — kit-owned taken wholesale, generated docs regenerated, filled-in files preserved. Process doc + downstream-resync skill; no automated test.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-037": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-067;LLR-034", "body": "Valid graphs pass; malformed ids, unknown predecessors and cycles fail with named offenders; vacuous cases pass.", "meta": "Method: Run WI id, predecessor, cycle, placeholder, absent-registry, and opt-out validation cases.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-038": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-070;LLR-035", "body": "One self-contained root HTML file contains all core views and no external fetches.", "meta": "Method: Generate the core offline dashboard and inspect completeness, spine hierarchy, and roadmap output.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-039": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-039;LLR-036", "body": "Satisfies SR-039 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the check-dupes suite; a seeded copy-pasted helper fails naming both file:line locations (one merged finding), clean source passes, the threshold is tunable, in-block repetition does not self-report, a fingerprint+pair census line suppresses that block while a NEW distinct block in the same listed pair still fails, a same-line separate-block interior edit defeats a stale census, --emit-census round-trips to green, a legacy bare pair still coarsely suppresses, and unallowed() is count-aware.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-040": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-040;LLR-037", "body": "Satisfies SR-040 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the agent-loop suite; a REVIEW-B-mapped phase invokes the second fake CLI and not the default, a broken map entry exits preflight naming the phase, the banner carries review-policy, and a bloated status.md warns without blocking.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-041": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-041;LLR-038", "body": "Satisfies SR-041 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the doc-refs suite; a dangling path warns then gates under --strict, non-path backticks/URLs/globs pass, a path-ok line is exempt, a dangling or unknown-module sym: is flagged, and the symbol tier skips cleanly without an inventory.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-042": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-042;LLR-039", "body": "Satisfies SR-042 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the gen-okf suite; typed linked concepts generate, Process Guide concepts emit for present docs with derived summaries and unmodified sources (and none on a vacuous scaffold), regeneration is byte-stable, --check trips on registry/doc edits/extras and recovers after regen, extras are pruned, placeholder-only is vacuous, off silences, and the okf step is wired at G3.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-043": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-043;LLR-040", "body": "Satisfies SR-043 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the subagent-gate suite; a Task/Agent spawn under deny is refused (permissionDecision=deny, exit 2), under ask defers, off/absent and the SUBAGENT_GATE=allow override allow it, a non-spawn tool is not gated, and a malformed payload fails open (allow) with a log line.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-044": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-073;LLR-041", "body": "Integrity and unresolved links fail as declared; endpoint mismatch is advisory; absent registry is vacuous.", "meta": "Method: Run IF id, SR back-link, Component membership, and endpoint-advisory trace cases.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-046": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-079;LLR-044", "body": "Every routing rule is deterministic and legacy Provider registries remain compatible.", "meta": "Method: Run pair-row parsing, resolution, Env, cooldown, family preference/fallback, and tier-selection cases.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-045": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-112;LLR-043", "body": "Satisfies SR-112 AcceptanceCriteria (checked per-agent fan-out)", "meta": "Method: Run the skills-sync suite: a hand-edited per-agent copy fails gen_skills_index --check-agents, bootstrap --sync restores byte-identity, a repo with no per-agent dir (and a scaffold with no neutral source) is vacuous, bootstrap --agents codex populates .agents/skills from source, and --sync never touches a non-skills file (a sentinel survives).", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-047": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-046;LLR-047", "body": "Satisfies SR-046 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run the run-menu suite: --list prints name<TAB>desc for each declared capability in declaration order (an empty desc renders as a trailing tab); run_menu.py <name> runs the command and returns its exit code (a nonzero passes through); the no-arg menu launches a capability from piped stdin and returns without hanging on closed stdin; an unknown name exits 2 and an absent or empty [run] section prints guidance and exits 1; a name.desc row renders in the menu; and the scaffold ships scripts/run_menu.py plus run.* launchers that delegate to it with no RUN_CMD.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-048": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-084;LLR-048", "body": "Only Critique-scoped builds schedule a fresh heterogeneous critic and its prompt excludes self-assessment.", "meta": "Method: Run Critique scope detection and redacted rubric/intent/artifact brief cases.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-049": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-087;LLR-049", "body": "Oversized uncontained views warn/fail strict; valid containment clears; nested, small, absent, and opted-out behavior matches the rule.", "meta": "Method: Run TOP_VIEW_MAX, containment, nesting, opt-out, and vacuity cases.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-050": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-049;LLR-050", "body": "derive_gate computes the correct gate per artifact state (draft drop, undecomposed G1, decomposed-unverified G2 - including a Modified SR, whose phase reads G2 with children counted-but-never-capping - and verified G3) and --check catches drift; the compared basis line carries modified=N beside drafts=N; --print shows the derived current phase (the meta reads phase=4)", "meta": "Method: derive the gate from fixture states across every per-artifact rule and guard the cache; --print reports the derived current phase", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-051": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-050;LLR-051", "body": "Process tab present with data-derived joins correct; links resolve; data-less render byte-identical; deterministic; --check trips.", "meta": "Method: Run the dashboard suite; the Process tab renders its three panels from the live registries + docs/gate (the current-gate highlight matches docs/gate and the tier counts match the spine), process-doc link-outs resolve, a bundle-/data-less repo renders byte-identically (no Process data joins), and generation stays byte-deterministic with --check tripping stale output.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-052": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-089;LLR-052", "body": "Tier thresholds, phase display, and deduplicated parent edges match the declared hierarchy.", "meta": "Method: Run When-view phase/workstream thresholds, delivery-phase labels, and parent-edge aggregation.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-055": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-054;LLR-055", "body": "APPROVE citing numbered anchors. The rubric is the single home of the live-vs-retired anchor set (its header states the live set; each retired anchor carries its retirement and binding in place): a verdict cites only anchors the rubric lists live and never a retired one, and a clause a test now holds is verified through the LLR/TC chain the registry records, not by a verdict. (The live/retired enumeration formerly copied here was the third home of that fact and is deliberately not restated.)", "meta": "Method: A fresh, family-heterogeneous CRITIQUE session (SR-084/SR-085 loop) adjudicates the generated PROJECT_STATE.html against docs/rubrics/dashboard-usability.md, receiving the rubric + SN/SR intent + the rendered PNG matrix from scripts/dashboard-shots/shoot.mjs (widths x themes x tabs) as the artifact recipe and never the implementer's self-assessment; VERDICT machine line cites numbered anchors.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-056": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-055;LLR-056;IF-093;IF-094", "body": "Both loops are explicit closed cycles (data-cycle=closed on each hoop disc); the LLM_Agent hub renders exactly once as the shared central junction of both hoops; every loop edge is directional (one floparrow marker each), 6 for the 5-stage intake loop + 5 for the 4-stage decision loop = 11; loop A stages = [intake -> triage-to-WI -> scheduler-derived ready frontier -> build/review -> merge]; loop B stages = [open-items population incl. gate-ratification -> human review/ruling -> log Decisions -> merge], each card keyed to its loop (data-node a-*/b-*); all stage links resolve; data-less render byte-identical; deterministic; --check trips.", "meta": "Method: Run the dashboard suite; the Process tab renders both working loops (intake loop A + human-decision loop B) as two intersecting hoops drawn in one self-contained SVG, each a directed closed cycle wired by arrow-headed edges and meeting at the single shared LLM_Agent hub, every stage's link resolving to its canonical doc, the gate-ratification stage present in loop B, a data-less repo rendering byte-identically, and generation byte-deterministic with --check tripping stale output.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-057": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-056;LLR-057", "body": "Exactly one parent->child arrow per containment edge; each tier column width <= the declared bound MAX_TIER_COL (a named constant = the widest member label + a fixed padding - a declared value, not an adjective); the persistent-highlight attribute is present and keyed to the last-hovered node id (no flash-on-exit); deterministic; --check trips.", "meta": "Method: Run the dashboard suite; the tiered decomposition views render one horizontal parent->child arrow per containment edge, each tier column no wider than the declared bound, and a persistent last-hovered highlight contract keyed to the last-hovered node; byte-deterministic with --check tripping stale output.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-058": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-057;LLR-058;SR-115;LLR-123", "body": "Satisfies SR-057 and SR-115 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run schedule.py against a fixture registry; the ready frontier, the exclusions with reason codes, and the deterministic traincar order match across ready --explain, ready --format json, simulate, and the dispatcher; a soft edge never blocks and Priority outranks downstream-dependent count within a gate class.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-059": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-093;LLR-059", "body": "Each input yields one deterministic class/reason set and all consumers agree.", "meta": "Method: Drive the pure classifier over every declared safety and policy input and compare consumer outputs.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-060": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-059;LLR-060;SR-116;LLR-124", "body": "Satisfies SR-059 and SR-116 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Drive trunk_step --regen and the status-map freshness gate on fixture repos; assert the snapshot regenerates on the trunk, fails fresh when stale, and no next-wi/run-phase surface exists.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-061": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-060;LLR-061", "body": "Satisfies SR-060 AcceptanceCriteria", "meta": "Method: Run two concurrent workers from explicit --wi/--train/worktree assignments; assert no lane-local status/next/run-state file, non-colliding logs/review evidence naming the exact reviewed commit, no root-truth mutation, and --track refused as an unknown flag (retired).", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-067": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-076;LLR-067", "body": "Uncovered cross-component edges warn/fail strict; covered and vacuous cases stay silent.", "meta": "Method: Run the cross-component import coverage matrix.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-076": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-106;LLR-076", "body": "True-plan agreement selects and files; runtime nonresponse falls back once; position bias, missing rubric, or no route pages.", "meta": "Method: Run the full fresh-session dual-plan round including fallback and position-swapped arbitration.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-075": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-069;LLR-075", "body": "Hand-edited done ids warn/fail by mode; generated/open/unknown/vacuous cases stay silent.", "meta": "Method: Run the hand-edited/generated status source-of-truth matrix.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-074": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-105;LLR-074", "body": "Artifacts and queued rows are stable and registry-valid with prior content preserved.", "meta": "Method: Build a fixture round and verify allocation, stable stage writes, selected-WI filing, dependency mapping, validation, line endings, and log append.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-073": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-104;LLR-073", "body": "Each CLI result becomes the correct typed round outcome and captured report payload.", "meta": "Method: Drive the coverage adapter through clean, first-finding repair, repeated finding, malformed, and implicated-plan cases.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-072": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-083;LLR-072", "body": "Fresh-session selection and every PAIR reason/fallback match the declared branch.", "meta": "Method: Drive planner_pair and planner_fallback across diverse, degraded, cooled, nonresponsive, and unavailable pools.", "status": "Verified"}, "TC-071": {"tier": "tc", "title": "verifies SR-103;LLR-071", "body": "Only SR/IF content appears; sentinels do not;