 <section class="band"><p class="eyebrow">1 · Pending decisions</p><article class="card" id="OI-13"><h3><span class="rid">OI-13</span><span>What does Status MEAN - align the vocabulary across all six registries, including the interfaces pair</span></h3><div class="field"><span class="k">One line</span><span class="v">rule what <code>Status</code> means across the six registries that carry one - the word names six different vocabularies today, one of them (<code>interfaces.csv</code>) undeclared, self-overlapping, read by nothing mechanical, and fed verbatim to LLM planning briefs; recommendation is to reserve <code>Status</code> for ratifiable-artifact maturity and rename the rest, executed WITH OI-12 because both rewrite registry definitions</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">What is being decided</span><span class="v">Raised by the owner 2026-08-09 while ruling on whether a stakeholder need should gain a <code>Status</code> cell: -we need to align on all the registry definitions. I am afraid this will impact the interface definitions as well.- Verified, and it does. SIX CARRIERS TODAY, only one of them closed: (1) SR/LLR/TC <code>Status</code> - OPEN vocabulary with three magic values (<code>Draft</code>, <code>Verified</code>, <code>Modified</code>) matched by exact equality, case-insensitive, everything else legal and mechanically inert; (2) SN - NO column at all, maturity is section-as-state, a heading whose text CONTAINS the word draft, so the SN rule is a substring match where the row rule is an equality; (3) WI - directory-as-state, seven directories mapping to eight in-memory names (<code>complete/</code> becomes <code>done</code>) with <code>blocked</code> DERIVED and never stored - the only CLOSED vocabulary in the repo, because an unknown directory is a loader refusal; (4) IF <code>Status</code> - {Stable, Active, Draft}; (5) OI <code>Status</code> - only <code>pending</code> carries meaning; (6) CMP <code>State</code>. THE INTERFACES REGISTRY IS THE WORST OF THE SIX AND IS A DEFECT RATHER THAN A DESIGN CHOICE: <code>PROCESS.md</code> section 8 names the IF row&#x27;s fields as -direction, counterpart, contract, the SR-Refs that realize/rely on it, version, and stability- and <code>Status</code> IS NOT AMONG THEM, yet it ships in the template and carries a value on all 95 live rows (Stable 87, Active 4, Draft 4); it OVERLAPS its own neighbour, since <code>Stability</code> is {Stable, Experimental} and the word Stable appears in both columns of the same row meaning different things; and their enforcement is inverted from what a reader would assume - <code>Stability</code>, the DECLARED field, is read by exactly one consumer (<code>gen_release_checklist</code>), while <code>Status</code>, the UNDECLARED one, is read by nothing mechanical at all and its only consumer is <code>plan_briefs.IF_SURFACE_COLUMNS</code>, which hands it verbatim into the dual-plan LLM briefs. An undeclared, unvalidated, self-overlapping column is being presented to a model as fact.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Blast radius</span><span class="v">REGISTRY DEFINITIONS AND TEMPLATES, which is why this is coupled to OI-12 rather than independent of it. Any answer touches <code>docs/requirements/interfaces.csv</code> plus its shipped template, <code>PROCESS.md</code> section 8&#x27;s field list, and - if the SN half is included - <code>stakeholder-needs.md</code>, its template, and the section-as-state readers in <code>trace.py</code> and <code>derive_gate.py</code> (F5 twins pinned by <code>tests/test_rule_sync.py</code>). A RENAME additionally touches every reader of the renamed column and every adopting repo, which is the same blast radius OI-12 carries, for the same files. NOTE THE SHARP EDGE ON THE SN HALF: giving an SN a <code>Status</code> cell while the section-as-state rule still stands is TWO HOMES FOR ONE DIAL, precisely the state SN-028 just shipped a REFUSAL for - so the cell and the retirement of section-as-state must land in ONE commit, never both live at once. What this does NOT touch: the gate arithmetic. <code>derive_gate</code> reads the three magic spine values and the SN section-state; nothing here proposes changing what they MEAN, only what the word is allowed to name elsewhere. Also independent of the carrier question in one direction only - the vocabulary survives any carrier, but executing a rename twice (once on CSV, once after a TOML conversion) is the same double-labour trap OI-12&#x27;s own sequencing note records.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Options</span><span class="v">(a) DECLARE, DO NOT UNIFY - leave each registry its own vocabulary and write down what each one means, adding a schema check where the vocabulary is meant to be closed. FOR: smallest possible change; the vocabularies genuinely describe different things, and a requirement&#x27;s maturity is not an interface&#x27;s stability, so forcing one word onto both would be a false unification; it fixes the actual defect (undeclared, unvalidated) without a rename. AGAINST: it leaves one word naming six things, which is the condition that produced the <code>interfaces.csv</code> overlap in the first place, and leaves a reader (or an agent) to infer from context which vocabulary a <code>Status</code> cell is speaking. (b) RESERVE THE WORD - <code>Status</code> means maturity-of-a-ratifiable-artifact and nothing else (the spine tiers, plus SN if that half is taken); everything else is RENAMED to what it actually is - the IF column collapses into <code>Stability</code> or becomes <code>Lifecycle</code>, OI becomes <code>Decision</code> state, CMP stays <code>State</code>. FOR: one word, one meaning, which is the kit&#x27;s own single-source doctrine applied to a vocabulary rather than to a file; it makes the six-way ambiguity unrepresentable instead of documented; and it removes the undeclared IF column rather than blessing it. AGAINST: a rename across registries, templates, readers and every adopting repo - a kit version bump with a migration, and one that is pure churn if the value it carries is never read. (c) SN HALF ONLY - add the SN <code>Status</code> cell (with section-as-state retired in the same commit) and leave the other five alone. FOR: it unblocks D-2 and puts the five Draft needs onto the owner surface, which is the concrete thing anyone is waiting for. AGAINST: it makes the ambiguity WORSE by adding a sixth <code>Status</code> while the interfaces defect stands, and it is the option most likely to be described later as -we knew and did nothing-.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Recommendation</span><span class="v">(b) as the destination, and EXECUTE IT WITH OI-12, not before or after. The coupling is the whole point: both items rewrite registry definitions, both touch every template, and both force a downstream migration - so doing them separately pays that cost twice, which is the same double-labour objection that reordered OI-12&#x27;s own plan. If OI-12 rules TOML, the vocabulary lands as typed keys in the new carrier and the rename is absorbed by a migration that was happening anyway. TWO THINGS SHOULD NOT WAIT FOR EITHER RULING, because they are defects rather than design questions. FIRST, the <code>interfaces.csv</code> <code>Status</code> column: it is undeclared in <code>PROCESS.md</code> section 8, overlaps <code>Stability</code> in the same row, is read by nothing mechanical, and is fed verbatim to LLM planning briefs - that last fact is what makes it urgent rather than untidy, because a model is being handed an unvalidated field as though it were a contract. Either declare it with a distinct meaning or delete it and keep the field section 8 actually names. SECOND, the SN half is genuinely blocking D-2 and the owner surface; if the sitting wants the five Draft needs visible before this is ruled, take (c) KNOWINGLY, record it here as an accepted interim, and let (b) absorb it - what must not happen is (c) arriving by default and being mistaken for a decision.</span></div></article><article class="card" id="OI-14"><h3><span class="rid">OI-14</span><span>What is an IF row&#x27;s Contract cell FOR - declare the interface registry&#x27;s content contract</span></h3><div class="field"><span class="k">One line</span><span class="v">rule what the <code>Contract</code> cell must contain, may contain, and must not - measured over all 95 live rows it carries design narrative and history rather than a pinned specification (1 percent requirement voice, 13 percent a callable signature, 27 percent a WI id), and nothing validates any of it because <code>--strict-schema</code> covers SR/LLR/TC only; recommendation is DECLARE now and SPLIT gradually, never a 95-row rewrite</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">What is being decided</span><span class="v">Raised by the owner 2026-08-09 while reviewing the registries: -the prose in IF appear to be requirements, when it is intended to be interface definitions.- MEASURED OVER ALL 95 LIVE ROWS, and the reading holds with one correction. They are NOT requirements in the shall-statement sense - exactly one row uses shall or must - and section 8&#x27;s backing rule holds mechanically, since ZERO rows have an empty <code>SR-Refs</code>, so every seam does hang off a real requirement. What the cells actually carry is DESIGN NARRATIVE: what a module does across the seam, which WI split it from what, and which defect the shape was chosen to avoid. 27 percent name a <code>WI-###</code>, 14 percent cite another registry id, 11 percent carry a rationale connective, 9 percent narrate a past incident, and only 13 percent name a callable signature. Median 260 characters, mean 323, max 968, with 21 rows over 500; in the five longest cells the history and incident sentences outnumber everything else. THE CONTENT IS NOT MISJUDGED, IT IS UNFILED - and the reason is structural rather than careless: the kit is stack-agnostic so a type signature cannot be demanded (a seam may be a file format, a CLI, or an external actor), and the IF row has NO <code>Rationale</code> and NO <code>Detail</code> column, so the why has nowhere else in the row to go. Someone had a true and useful thing to say and one cell to say it in.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Blast radius</span><span class="v">THE WHOLE IF REGISTRY HAS NO SCHEMA TIER. <code>trace.py</code>&#x27;s <code>--strict-schema</code> covers SR, LLR and TC only, so on an IF row nothing is required, nothing is enum-checked, and nothing bounds a cell&#x27;s content - <code>Contract</code> is unconstrained, <code>Status</code> is undeclared (OI-13), and <code>Stability</code> is validated nowhere despite being the field <code>PROCESS.md</code> section 8 actually names. Ruling this therefore means adding a schema tier, not just writing prose. THREE CONSUMERS FEEL IT. (1) <code>plan_briefs.IF_SURFACE_COLUMNS</code> feeds <code>Contract</code> VERBATIM into the dual-plan LLM planning briefs, so history sentences spend brief budget without constraining behaviour and narrative sits mixed with normative statement in a prompt - the sharpest practical cost, and the one that argues for a split rather than a declaration alone. (2) A consuming row -pins the version- per section 8, but nothing in the cell marks which sentence is the pinned obligation, so a <code>Version</code> bump is measured against an unstated baseline. (3) 27 percent of cells name a <code>WI-###</code>, duplicating <code>log.md</code>; a WI id ages, and a cancelled row&#x27;s id sitting in a <code>Contract</code> cell still reads as authority. AND ONE COUPLING A RULER SHOULD KNOW BEFORE DECIDING: how many IF rows this repo needs is not a free choice - <code>check_trajectory.cross_component_findings</code> (WI-064) makes an import edge between two components a finding unless a covering IF row exists, so the CMP partition DETERMINES the IF population. Ruling IF&#x27;s content contract while the component model is unsettled rules half a question; the two should at least be read together.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Options</span><span class="v">(a) DECLARE, LEAVE THE ROWS - write down in <code>PROCESS.md</code> section 8 and the shipped template what a <code>Contract</code> cell must contain, and give IF a schema tier (required fields, the closed <code>Stability</code> vocabulary). FOR: cheapest by far; it fixes the actual defect, which is that nothing SAYS what the cell is for, without touching 95 cells of prose that a maintainer wrote for a reason; new and amended rows land correct immediately. AGAINST: the 95 existing cells stay mixed, so the LLM-brief dilution and the unstated-pin problem both survive the ruling; a declaration nothing enforces retroactively is the weakest form of a rule. (b) SPLIT THE CELL - <code>Contract</code> keeps the binding specification; a new <code>Rationale</code> column takes the why; history is removed from the cells and lives in <code>log.md</code>, where it already does. FOR: it puts each kind of content in a home that exists for it, gives the LLM brief a cell worth quoting verbatim, and makes -what am I pinning- answerable. AGAINST: it is a schema change on a shipped registry (template, every adopter, <code>IF_SURFACE_COLUMNS</code>, the arch-graph render), and done as a SWEEP it is 95 prose rewrites with a real chance of quietly losing load-bearing maintainer knowledge - the <code>WI-218</code> facts are useful, just misfiled. (c) RULE IT FREE-FORM PROSE - accept that <code>Contract</code> is a description, say so in section 8, and stop implying a specification: stop feeding it to planning briefs as authority, and drop the -pins the version- language it cannot support. FOR: honest; it ends a claim the registry has never met, which is the one thing that is definitely wrong today. AGAINST: it gives up the seam registry&#x27;s whole point - an interface nobody can pin is a comment - and it would be the first place this kit answers -we imply a check nobody performs- by lowering the claim rather than building the check.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Recommendation</span><span class="v">(a) AS THE IMMEDIATE ACT, (b) AS THE DIRECTION, executed PER ROW AS ROWS ARE TOUCHED rather than as a sweep. Declaring costs almost nothing and fixes the root defect - the cell has never had a stated purpose, so no one has been writing it wrongly, only differently. Add the schema tier in the same pass, because <code>Stability</code> being enum-checked and <code>Contract</code> being required are the two rules the registry can enforce today without any prose migration. Then let the split arrive with normal work: a row whose seam is edited gets its history moved to the log and its why moved to the new column, so the corpus converges without a 95-row commit nobody can review carefully. REJECT (c): the fix for -we imply a check nobody performs- is to build the check, and this kit has ruled that way before. TWO THINGS THAT DO NOT WAIT. First, <code>IF_SURFACE_COLUMNS</code> hands <code>Contract</code> to LLM planning briefs today - whatever is ruled here, that consumption should be stated in the declaration rather than discovered, because it is the one place a mixed cell has a behavioural cost rather than an aesthetic one. Second, read this WITH the component question: <code>cross_component_findings</code> makes the CMP partition decide how many IF rows must exist, so a ruling on IF content that assumes the current 95 rows are the right 95 is resting on an unruled component model.</span></div></article></section>
