ostic.</ins></div><div class="cellname">Rationale <span class="pill">88% of the words changed</span></div><div class="diff"><span class="eq">Realizes SN-003 </span><del>and SN-011 </del><span class="eq">— </span><del>portability</del><ins>a method that presumes one language</ins><span class="eq"> is </span><del>analyzed</del><ins>re-derived</ins><span class="eq"> </span><del>via</del><ins>by every adopter on a different stack, and</ins><span class="eq"> the </span><del>CI</del><ins>re-derivation</ins><span class="eq"> </span><del>matrix,</del><ins>is</ins><span class="eq"> </span><del>not</del><ins>where</ins><span class="eq"> </span><del>a</del><ins>the</ins><span class="eq"> </span><del>single</del><ins>traceability</ins><span class="eq"> </span><del>unit</del><ins>guarantees</ins><span class="eq"> </span><del>test.</del><ins>get dropped. Shipping per-stack variants was rejected: it multiplies the surface that must stay consistent.</ins></div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria <span class="pill">0% of the words changed</span></div><div class="diff"><span class="eq">The</span><del> CI matrix spans Linux, Windows and macOS on Python 3.11 and latest (3.x) and is green; macOS runs on current Python (3.x) only — the 3.11 floor is a platform-agnostic language/stdlib guarantee already exercised on Linux and Windows, while macOS&#x27;s job is OS-specific behavior (fcntl.flock, paths, git worktrees) that does not vary by Python version, so the macOS+3.11 cell is excluded as a redundant-coverage call (M-27), not a runner-availability workaround (3.11 has arm64 macOS builds); the</del><span class="eq"> ID scheme is language-neutral.</span></div><div class="ctx"><div class="ctxhead">the rest of this SR — unchanged since the baseline</div><div class="field"><span class="k">SR-ID</span><span class="v">SR-035</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Title</span><span class="v">Stack-agnostic across OSes</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Priority</span><s