des self-assessment.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Automated</span><span class="v">Yes</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Evidence</span><span class="v">tests/test_agent_loop_critique.py::test_critique_scheduled_when_critique_sr_in_scope; tests/test_agent_loop_critique.py::test_no_critique_when_no_critique_sr; tests/test_agent_loop_critique.py::test_critique_prompt_is_redacted_and_carries_rubric_and_intent</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Status</span><span class="v">Verified</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Phase</span><span class="v">1</span></div></div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-093-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-093</span><span>Pure safety classification</span><span class="pill">re-attest owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">baseline a5052a913 (2026-07-25) — from --since</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-093</span><span class="pill">changed</span></div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs <span class="pill">100% of the words changed</span></div><div class="diff"><del>SN-025;SN-008</del><ins>SN-027; SN-008</ins></div><div class="cellname">Rationale <span class="pill">8% of the words changed</span></div><div class="diff"><span class="eq">Realizes </span><del>SN-025;SN-008</del><ins>SN-027;SN-008</ins><span class="eq"> by isolating one independently reviewable obligation formerly carried by SR-058.</span></div><div class="ctx"><div class="ctxhead">the rest of this SR — unchanged since the baseline</div><div class="field"><span class="k">SR-ID</span><span class="v">SR-093</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Title</span><span class="v">Pure safety classification</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Requirement</span><span class="v">schedule.py shall purely classify every declared safety and policy input into one scheduling class plus deterministic reason codes.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">AcceptanceCriteria</span><span class="v">Every declared value and policy input maps deterministically; missing, unknown, or contradictory declarations fail closed only for the affected WI; all consumers agree.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Priority</span><span class="v">M</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Verification</span><span class="v">Test</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Status</span><span class="v">Modified</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Phase</span><span class="v">4</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Area</span><span class="v">Parallel dispatch</span></div></div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-059</span><span class="pill">changed</span></div><div class="cellname">TestRefs <span class="pill">50% of the words changed</span></div><div class="diff"><del>(see</del><ins>(see;</ins><span class="eq"> TC-059)</span></div><div class="ctx"><div class="ctxhead">the rest of this LLR — unchanged since the baseline</div><div class="field"><span class="k">LLR-ID</span><span class="v">LLR-059</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">SR-Refs</span><span class="v">SR-093</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Title</span><span class="v">Pure safety classifier</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Module</span><span class="v">project-trajectory/scripts/schedule.py</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">CodeSymbol</span><span class="v">classify/safety_class</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Detail</span><span class="v">Maps declared safety and policy inputs to one scheduling class and deterministic reason codes without side effects.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Status</span><span class="v">Verified</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Component</span><span class="v">CMP-004</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Phase</span><span class="v">4</span></div></div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-149</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-149</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-093</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">§A8 admission: the kind x ratification-level table + the spine barrier</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/dispatch.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">_kind_action/_admission/_lane_count/gap_census</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">The dispatcher&#x27;s one scheduling decision (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A4/§A8), consuming schedule.kind_of - the §A1 classification&#x27;s enforcement point. _kind_action is the ruled §A8 table verbatim per kind x human-held answer (`agent_common.human_holds`), and a human-held attestation/gate row SURFACES even under keep_nondependent — that dial answers whether OTHER lanes keep running, never whether the machine may ratify; _admission is the barrier as a pure decision: any exclusive-kind frontier row stops new admission (wait) until every lane is back, then ALL spine rows admit together as one exclusive batch (one branch, one agent_loop --wi &#x27;A;B&#x27; worker, one re-attest window), attestation/gate rows surface under attended (drain, exit 0 naming the open-items cards) and admit as the queued batch at single-ratify&#x27;s close; _lane_count resolves the lanes dial CLI &gt; AGENT_LANES &gt; stack.ini [agent-loop] &gt; 1 (absent key = 1; the template seeds 2); gap_census is the empty-frontier ladder&#x27;s rung-1 seam - unverified in-scope SRs, orphan rows and draft SNs from trace.analyze, handed to the intake mint helper (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A5.2), never minted in the lane.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">The §A4.1 authority flip: admission replaced the claim rung&#x27;s deleted safety_class refusal, so the policy must live at ONE decision point and be driven as a pure function.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC-143)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-152</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-152</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-093</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">The adjudication kind: rank-1 slot + no-bar arm + the bar strictness key</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/schedule.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">SAFETY_CLASSES/_KIND_CONCURRENCY/_KIND_RANK</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">The `adjudication` kind (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A5.2) joins the declared SafetyClass vocabulary: exclusive on the concurrency axis, rank 1 on the order axis - the slot §A1 reserved, filled without renumbering the ruled table. The kind&#x27;s lane runs NO product bar (integrate._lane_bar_directives + _refresh_bar: the refresh still merges trunk in, runs the trunk step and commits a verified Bar-Green attestation whose summary reads no-bar honestly) because its outputs are Status cells and the work registry, nothing a product bar can speak to; a mixed batch or unreadable frontmatter fails TOWARD the bar. The optional `bar = G1|G2|G3` frontmatter key rides the same directive read: bar declares verification strictness for this row&#x27;s lane; it never affects scheduling (load_wis deliberately does not parse it) - the refresh passes the batch&#x27;s strictest value to check.py --gate and refuses a malformed one loudly.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Narrowing the amendment detector to ratified cells without the kind would move mis-fires from spurious window to MISSED window; the two are a pair (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A5.2).</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC-146)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-059</span><span class="pill">changed</span></div><div class="cellname">Verifies <span class="pill">100% of the words changed</span></div><div class="diff"><del>SR-093;LLR-059</del><ins>SR-093; LLR-059</ins></div><div class="ctx"><div class="ctxhead">the rest of this TC — unchanged since the baseline</div><div class="field"><span class="k">TC-ID</span><span class="v">TC-059</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Level</span><span class="v">Unit</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Method</span><span class="v">Drive the pure classifier over every declared safety and policy input and compare consumer outputs.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Tier</span><span class="v">Full</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Expected</span><span class="v">Each input yields one deterministic class/reason set and all consumers agree.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Automated</span><span class="v">Yes</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Evidence</span><span class="v">tests/test_schedule.py::test_classify_every_declared_value_is_deterministic; tests/test_schedule.py::test_missing_and_unknown_safetyclass_fail_closed_only_for_that_wi; tests/test_schedule.py::test_cli_ready_json_matches_library</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Status</span><span class="v">Verified</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Phase</span><span class="v">4</span></div></div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-143</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-143</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-093; LLR-149</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Unit</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the dispatch admission suite: an exclusive row + a busy lane answers wait (never a parallel claim slipped past the barrier) while the same frontier without one keeps filling lanes; lanes back -&gt; every spine row admits as ONE batch ahead of ordinary work; the §A8 table verbatim per kind x level (attended surfaces attestation/gate rows, single-ratify keeps non-dependent work and admits the queued ratification batch only at the close, autonomous dispatches them exclusive); the lanes ladder resolves CLI &gt; AGENT_LANES &gt; stack.ini &gt; 1 with absent/malformed/sub-1 falling to serial; gap_census names unverified SRs, orphan rows and draft SNs mechanically and answers [] on a registry-less repo; kind_of agrees with classify at every quarantine.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-093 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_dispatch_admission.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-146</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-146</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-093; LLR-152</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Unit</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the schedule + integrate suites&#x27; adjudication cases: the kind classifies exclusive at rank 1 (the reserved slot, pinned by name); an adjudication-only lane&#x27;s refresh runs NO bar yet still commits a verified Bar-Green attestation whose summary says no-bar, while a mixed batch bars as usual (fail toward the bar); bar = G2 reaches check.py as --gate G2 (asserted off the recording stub&#x27;s own argv), an undeclared bar passes no --gate, and a malformed value refuses the refresh naming it.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-093 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_schedule.py; tests/test_integrate.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-094-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-094</span><span>Structural safety cross-check</span><span class="pill">re-attest owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">baseline a5052a913 (2026-07-25) — from --since</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-094</span><span class="pill">changed</span></div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs <span class="pill">100% of the words changed</span></div><div class="diff"><del>SN-025;SN-008</del><ins>SN-027; SN-008</ins></div><div class="cellname">Rationale <span class="pill">8% of the words changed</span></div><div class="diff"><span class="eq">Realizes </span><del>SN-025;SN-008</del><ins>SN-027;SN-008</ins><span class="eq"> by isolating one independently reviewable obligation formerly carried by SR-058.</span></div><div class="ctx"><div class="ctxhead">the rest of this SR — unchanged since the baseline</div><div class="field"><span class="k">SR-ID</span><span class="v">SR-094</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Title</span><span class="v">Structural safety cross-check</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Requirement</span><span class="v">schedule.py shall cross-check SafetyClass declarations against structural evidence and fail an invalid, missing, or contradictory WI closed without stopping disjoint classified work.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">AcceptanceCriteria</span><span class="v">Spine, critique, checkpoint, and dual-plan structure override dishonest declarations; the affected WI is quarantined with reasons while disjoint valid work remains schedulable.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Priority</span><span class="v">M</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Verification</span><span class="v">Test</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Status</span><span class="v">Modified</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Phase</span><span class="v">4</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Area</span><span class="v">Parallel dispatch</span></div></div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-089</span><span class="pill">changed</span></div><div class="cellname">TestRefs <span class="pill">50% of the words changed</span></div><div class="diff"><del>(see</del><ins>(see;</ins><span class="eq"> TC-091)</span></div><div class="ctx"><div class="ctxhead">the rest of this LLR — unchanged since the baseline</div><div class="field"><span class="k">LLR-ID</span><span class="v">LLR-089</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">SR-Refs</span><span class="v">SR-094</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Title</span><span class="v">Structural safety cross-check</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Module</span><span class="v">project-trajectory/scripts/schedule.py</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">CodeSymbol</span><span class="v">structural_safety/classify</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Detail</span><span class="v">Cross-checks declarations against spine, critique, checkpoint, and dual-plan evidence; quarantines only the contradictory WI.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Status</span><span class="v">Verified</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Component</span><span class="v">CMP-004</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Phase</span><span class="v">4</span></div></div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-091</span><span class="pill">changed</span></div><div class="cellname">Verifies <span class="pill">100% of the words changed</span></div><div class="diff"><del>SR-094;LLR-089</del><ins>SR-094; LLR-089</ins></div><div class="cellname">Evidence <span class="pill">50% of the words changed</span></div><div class="diff"><span class="eq">tests/test_schedule.py::test_missing_and_unknown_safetyclass_fail_closed_only_for_that_wi; tests/test_schedule.py::test_ordinary_declaration_with_structural_spine_fails_closed; </span><del>tests/test_schedule.py::test_critique_and_checkpoint_force_single_wi_traincar;</del><ins>tests/test_schedule.py::test_critique_is_parallel_and_ranks_ahead_of_ordinary;</ins><span class="eq"> </span><del>tests/test_schedule.py::test_planmode_dual_contradicting_safetyclass_quarantines; tests/test_agent_loop_dispatch.py::test_unclassified_wi_fails_closed_without_stopping_others</del><ins>tests/test_schedule.py::test_planmode_dual_contradicting_safetyclass_quarantines</ins></div><div class="ctx"><div class="ctxhead">the rest of this TC — unchanged since the baseline</div><div class="field"><span class="k">TC-ID</span><span class="v">TC-091</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Level</span><span class="v">Unit</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Method</span><span class="v">Classify missing, unknown, structurally contradictory, critique, checkpoint, and dual-plan WIs beside disjoint valid work.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Tier</span><span class="v">Full</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Expected</span><span class="v">Only invalid rows fail closed with reason codes and disjoint valid rows remain schedulable.</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Automated</span><span class="v">Yes</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Status</span><span class="v">Verified</span></div><div class="field"><span class="k">Phase</span><span class="v">4</span></div></div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-114-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-114</span><span>Kit scripts run across the supported OSes</span><span class="pill">re-attest owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">baseline a5052a913 (2026-07-25) — from --since</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-114</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-114</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Kit scripts run across the supported OSes</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-003; SN-011</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">The kit scripts shall run on Python 3.11+ across Linux, Windows and macOS.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Realizes SN-011 — a script that works only where its author ran it turns portability into a defect the adopter finds, and the path, line-ending and locking differences that break it are invisible on a single platform. Testing one platform and reading the others by inspection was rejected: every one of those differences has been a real failure here.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">The CI matrix spans Linux, Windows and macOS on Python 3.11 and latest (3.x) and is green; macOS runs on current Python only — the 3.11 floor is a platform-agnostic language/stdlib guarantee already exercised on Linux and Windows, while macOS&#x27;s job is OS-specific behavior (fcntl.flock, paths, git worktrees) that does not vary by Python version, so the macOS+3.11 cell is excluded as a redundant-coverage call, not a runner-availability workaround (3.11 has arm64 macOS builds).</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">M</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Analysis</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">1</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Portability</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-035</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-035</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-035; SR-114</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Analysis</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Analyze the CI matrix result across Linux/Windows/macOS x Python 3.11/latest.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-035 and SR-114 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">.github/workflows/test.yml</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">1</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-115-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-115</span><span>Deterministic ready ordering</span><span class="pill">re-attest owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">baseline a5052a913 (2026-07-25) — from --since</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-115</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-115</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Deterministic ready ordering</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-025; SN-006</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">schedule.py shall order the eligible ready frontier deterministically by gate class, then Priority descending, then transitive downstream-dependent count descending, then remaining hard-path length descending, then WI id.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Realizes SN-025 — a frontier that is correct but unordered lets two readers of the same registry dispatch different work, so a rerun stops being reproducible and a scheduling bug cannot be replayed. Ordering by WI id alone was rejected: it ignores the critical path, so the chain everything waits on gets picked last.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">Ordering is identical across --explain and --format json; Priority outranks downstream-dependent count within one gate class; simulate --jobs 1 matches the serial order.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">M</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Parallel dispatch</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-123</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-123</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-115</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Deterministic traincar ordering</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/schedule.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">frontier/evaluate</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">Orders eligible traincars by (gate, Priority desc, downstream-dependent count desc, hard-path desc, first WI id).</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Split from the frontier row: deriving WHICH traincars are eligible and deciding WHICH runs first fail independently, and only the second one makes a run replayable.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-058</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-058</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-057; LLR-058; SR-115; LLR-123</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run schedule.py against a fixture registry; the ready frontier, the exclusions with reason codes, and the deterministic traincar order match across ready --explain, ready --format json, simulate, and the dispatcher; a soft edge never blocks and Priority outranks downstream-dependent count within a gate class.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-057 and SR-115 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_schedule.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-116-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-116</span><span>Generated status surface</span><span class="pill">re-attest owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">baseline a5052a913 (2026-07-25) — from --since</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-116</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-116</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Generated status surface</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-025; SN-010</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">The trunk lane shall maintain docs/status.md&#x27;s generated snapshot as a freshness-gated reference surface never written on a worker branch (trunk_step --regen; concurrency-restructure SS5.2). (The docs/run-state half of this row retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5.)</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Realizes SN-010 — an agent-authored status surface drifts from the state it reports and is trusted anyway, and a worker branch writing it makes the snapshot describe one lane instead of the project. Letting each worker append its own status was rejected: it produces a file that conflicts on every integration and reports no coherent whole.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">The status snapshot regenerates on the trunk after a merge and fails its freshness gate when stale; work branches skip the gate and never commit the artifact.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">M</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Parallel dispatch</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-124</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-124</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-116</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Generated status + run-state</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/trunk_step.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">REGEN_STEPS/regen</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">docs/status.md&#x27;s generated block is maintained by the trunk lane only: trunk_step --regen runs gen_trajectory --status after derive_gate (the gate is its input), and the status-map freshness gate byte-compares it on the trunk while work branches skip (SS5.2). (docs/run-state and its four-valued dispatcher outcome retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5.)</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Split from the retirement row: removing the old pointer and generating the surfaces that replace it are separable, and the second is what keeps the replacement from rotting the same way.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-060</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-060</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-059; LLR-060; SR-116; LLR-124</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Drive trunk_step --regen and the status-map freshness gate on fixture repos; assert the snapshot regenerates on the trunk, fails fresh when stale, and no next-wi/run-phase surface exists.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-059 and SR-116 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_trunk_step.py; tests/test_gen_trajectory.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-125-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-125</span><span>PAGE maps through gate policy</span><span class="pill">re-attest owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">baseline a5052a913 (2026-07-25) — from --since</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-125</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-125</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">PAGE maps through gate policy</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-024; SN-006</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">The --dual-plan single-shot round shall map a PAGE through the declared ratification level, hard-gating a human only while the tier in process is still HUMAN-HELD and otherwise reaching the pause-free attention (STALL) end state.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Realizes SN-016 — a PAGE that always stops for a human makes the unattended loop attended in practice, while one that never stops discards the judgement the round asked for. Two separate mappings for the dispatcher and the single-shot round were rejected: they drift, and the drift shows up as a loop that hangs on one path only.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">A PAGE preserves evidence and follows the declared level (a human-held tier -&gt; NEEDS-HUMAN exit; a loop-held tier -&gt; STALL exit, no human gate); an unreadable level or stage resolves to human-held, so the failure direction is the gate, never past it.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">S</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Dual-plan decomposition</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-132</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-132</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-125</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">PAGE ratification-level mapping</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/agent_loop.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">main</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">Applies the declared level&#x27;s continuation semantics to a PAGE at both the dispatcher disposition and the --dual-plan single-shot round: `page_action(human_held, keep_nondependent)` over the four cells the retired enum&#x27;s three words could not span — a human-held tier pages, a loop-held one STALLs, and `None` normalizes to the strictest cell rather than to `bool(None)`&#x27;s most permissive one.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Split from the disposition row: committing the outcome and deciding whether it stops a human are independent, and the second is shared with a second entry point that would otherwise drift.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-098</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-098</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-108; LLR-096; SR-125; LLR-132</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run dispatcher SELECT, human-held PAGE, loop-held PAGE continuation, regeneration failure, and salvage cases, plus the dual-plan PAGE on a human-held tier, on a loop-held tier, and with keep_nondependent, at both the dispatcher and the --dual-plan single-shot entry.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-108 and SR-125 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_dual_plan_round.py::test_full_round_unattended_selects_and_files; tests/test_dual_plan_round.py::test_arbiter_disagreement_pages; tests/test_dual_plan_round.py::test_arbiter_disagreement_on_a_loop_held_tier_stalls_not_pages; tests/test_dual_plan_round.py::test_arbiter_disagreement_with_keep_nondependent_stalls_not_pages</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-130-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-130</span><span>Serial trunk step compiles log fragments and regenerates authority</span><span class="pill">re-attest owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">baseline a5052a913 (2026-07-25) — from --since</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-130</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-130</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Serial trunk step compiles log fragments and regenerates authority</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-027; SN-008</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">A serial trunk step shall compile docs/log.d/ work-branch log fragments into docs/log.md in merge order derived from git history - validating every fragment before any write, rebasing relative links to the log&#x27;s home, deleting compiled fragments, failing loudly at the first error and never committing.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">The §5.1/§5.2 shared-surface rules (concurrency-restructure): no work branch writes log.md, so hand-merged logs become unrepresentable; the one deliberately serial actor stays tiny, idempotent and fail-loud (§5.5). The artifact-regen half is SR-134&#x27;s obligation (one row, one obligation).</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">Two committed fragments compile in git-add order with links rebased docs/-relative and the fragments deleted; an uncommitted or malformed fragment blocks the whole batch with nothing appended; a fragment-free run is a clean no-op.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">M</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Trajectory</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-137</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-137</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-130</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Serial trunk step (log compile + regen)</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/trunk_step.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">compile_log/ordered_fragments/rebase_links</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">Compiles docs/log.d/*.md into docs/log.md via plan_artifacts.append_log_summary in (git add-time, filename) order - all fragments validated first (name shape, ## heading, reserved section headings refused, an adding commit required - on HEAD, or on MERGE_HEAD during the integrator&#x27;s merge composition), relative links rebased from docs/log.d/ to docs/, fragments deleted after append, all-or-nothing on any validation error. Never commits; the caller owns the commit. The artifact-regen half is LLR-142 (SR-134).</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">One serial actor per §5.5.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC-130)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-130</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-130</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-130; LLR-137</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Unit</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the trunk-step suite: committed fragments compile in git order with links rebased and fragments deleted; an uncommitted, heading-less or reserved-heading fragment blocks the whole batch; a fragment-free run is a clean idempotent no-op.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-130 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_trunk_step.py::test_fragments_compile_in_git_order_and_are_deleted</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-131-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-131</span><span>Tracked pause drains claiming to a merged stop</span><span class="pill">re-attest owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">baseline a5052a913 (2026-07-25) — from --since</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-131</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-131</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Tracked pause drains claiming to a merged stop</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-027; SN-016</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">A tracked docs/work/pause file (TOML: reason, since) shall pause claiming - everything in flight finishes and integrates - read via pause_reason as the ONE pause home (the legacy untracked docs/pause half retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5), failing closed on malformation.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">§5.6: pause means stop claiming and drain to a merged stop - never a strand. The tracked form survives clones, its reason is diffable history, and unpausing is an auditable deletion commit; a broken pause file must still pause, loudly. The pending-surface render is SR-135&#x27;s obligation (one row, one obligation).</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">A tracked pause file pauses the coordinator via pause_reason under the unchanged None/&#x27;&#x27;/reason contract; a malformed file pauses with the pinned loud placeholder; a stray legacy docs/pause file is inert.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">M</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Trajectory</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-138</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-138</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-131</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Tracked pause reader (dual-home)</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/agent_common.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">tracked_pause/pause_reason</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">pause_reason reads the ONE tracked home docs/work/pause via tracked_pause (TOML reason/since; malformation fails closed to the pinned loud placeholder). The legacy untracked lane/pause half retired with the dispatcher at Phase 5 - a stray local file is inert, pinned by test.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">A broken pause file must still pause; two homes during the migration window, one meaning.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC-131)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-131</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-131</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-131; LLR-138</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Unit</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the pause suites: the tracked home pauses via pause_reason with the unchanged return contract; a stray legacy file is inert; malformation pauses fail-closed with the pinned loud placeholder.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-131 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_agent_loop_policy.py::test_pause_reason_reads_the_tracked_home; tests/test_agent_loop_policy.py::test_legacy_pause_file_no_longer_pauses</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-132-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-132</span><span>Local integrator: serial fail-closed merge queue</span><span class="pill">re-attest owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">baseline a5052a913 (2026-07-25) — from --since</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-132</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-132</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Local integrator: serial fail-closed merge queue</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-027; SN-008</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">The local integrator shall claim work via a serial trunk commit (queued spec to docs/work/active/&lt;branch&gt;/, branch cut from that commit, refused while docs/work/pause is present), merge each finished claimed branch --no-ff onto a candidate worktree with the trunk step folded into the merge commit, run the declared bar on the composed tree - refusing on a missing or empty check declaration and on any SKIP in the bar&#x27;s own report - require the policy-dialed verdict artifacts with git-derived freshness, fast-forward the trunk only on green, and stop loudly on red.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">The §1.2 seam&#x27;s default backend: serial =&gt; composed-tree gating by construction; fail-closed against the dispatcher&#x27;s recorded fail-open (a skipped bar merged 8 trains with zero gate-verified). Verdict freshness is git-derived because the WI-scoped §5.4 naming dropped the sha7 filename binding - without it a stale APPROVE clears the gate.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">A claim moves the spec and cuts the branch in one serial trunk step and is refused when paused, dirty, name-unsafe, not-ordinary or not-ready; a finished branch is detected from its tree alone; an absent or stale or CHANGES-REQUESTED verdict refuses the merge; a missing stack.ini or empty [product] test refuses; a red bar parks the candidate and stops the queue; the RULING-6 window audit flags a non-merge product commit.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">M</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Trajectory</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-140</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-140</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-132</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Local integrator (claim + queue + audit)</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/integrate.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">claim/finished_branches/_verdict_gate/integrate_one/audit</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">claim: §2.3 steps 1+2 with the refusal ladder (tracked pause via agent_common.tracked_pause, dirty tree, existing branch, unsafe name, non-ordinary safety_class, off-frontier id) before a git mv + trunk commit + branch cut. finished_branches: a claimed branch whose tip holds no spec under active/&lt;branch&gt;/ (the closing move IS the signal). integrate_one: --no-ff --no-commit merge, trunk_step.py folded into the merge commit so it passes its own floor, check.py --jobs 0 at the declared tier on the composed tree read fail-closed (nonzero exit OR any SKIP line refuses), verdict gate per docs/review-policy using score_reviews.parse_verdict with the verdict&#x27;s last branch commit required no older than the last non-review non-fragment commit, ff-only trunk advance, branch deleted on green, candidate parked on red. audit: git log --first-parent --no-merges over --since..HEAD; paths outside docs/work/, docs/log.d/, docs/log.md and the stack.ini [generated] set flag the commit.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">One serial actor, one page; every refusal names its reason; the queue stops at the first red rather than composing past it.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC-132)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-144</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-144</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-132</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Terminal lane outcomes (handback + quarantine)</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/handback.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">close_partial/quarantine/report_path/render_report/_restore/diff_records</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">close_partial: commits the lane&#x27;s work as-is, moves each claimed spec to the TERMINAL partial/ (nothing re-claims it, so nothing strands) and writes ONE IMMUTABLE per-close report under docs/handbacks/ naming the claimed outcome, the reason, the commit range and the keep/discard split. The report is the close EVENT&#x27;s identity — immutable so it never moves, unique so a second close is a second document — which is what dissolved the starvation class five mutable-proxy dedup mechanisms died on. A second close is refused rather than overwriting the first, and any refusal restores every report the call wrote (schedule reads queued+blockref as blocked) and the driver cannot claim and return it forever. quarantine: the ruled red arm - reverts the product paths to the merge base and keeps the failing diff as a bar-inert .patch under docs/work/handback/, reading `git diff --name-status -z` as three-field RECORDS so a rename cannot revert the wrong path, checking every git return code, and restoring rather than reporting when a revert step fails. Both leave the branch finished, so integrate.branch_outcomes reads the outcome off the spec&#x27;s DIRECTORY - complete/=merged, cancelled/=cancelled, any open folder=handback - and every lane ends in a merge.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Making the outcome the directory leaves no fourth answer and no state file that could hold one; partial work lands in trunk where a future WI can find it instead of on a branch nobody will look at.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC-138)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-145</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-145</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-132</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Link-aware spec-move ritual (move + relink indivisible)</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/spec_move.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">move_spec/expected_relink/archive_dest</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">move_spec moves a registry/spec file and keeps every markdown link in the repo true as ONE indivisible operation: the moved file&#x27;s own relative targets are re-relativised against its new directory, every inbound link whose target resolves to the vacated path is redirected (link text untouched, #fragments carried, external/protocol-relative/bare-anchor targets left alone, root-relative targets excused only in the rebase half, line endings preserved via newline=&#x27;&#x27;), and every rewritten file is staged with the move so the ritual lands as one changeset. integrate.claim and handback.close_partial call it for the claim and close moves; the CLI performs the worker&#x27;s terminal close move and the dated docs/archive/specs/ archival. expected_relink is the pure oracle integrate._abandoned_claim compares byte-for-byte to excuse a crashed claim&#x27;s own relink writes without widening its conviction to any .md edit.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">A bare move strands both link directions and the break surfaces late, on the composed tree (measured: one probe archival took the broken-link count from 4 to 8 - 3 outbound, 1 inbound). Making move+relink one indivisible operation is a constraint that leaves the two-thirds state unrepresentable, rather than a check that detects it after the parallel work is done.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC-139)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-150</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-150</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-132</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Lane mechanics: worktree, worker subprocess, §A2 refresh subprocess</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/lane.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">ensure_worktree/worker_argv/spawn_worker/run_worker/spawn_refresh</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">One lane&#x27;s mechanics extracted from the dispatcher (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A4.2): ensure the lane worktree (integrate.lane_worktree, the one home both the worker and the refresh agree on), build the worker argv with the plain launch&#x27;s session dials forwarded explicitly, launch the worker subprocess (spawn_worker non-blocking for the lane table; run_worker its blocking form and the default worker seam), and run the §A2 station refresh as the lane&#x27;s OWN subprocess (spawn_refresh - the invoker&#x27;s integrate.py refresh CLI under the harness interpreter) so N lanes&#x27; bars overlap instead of queueing at the dispatcher (§A4.3). Reports outcomes, decides none: the tree signal (specs moved out of active/&lt;branch&gt;/) plus exit codes are the whole protocol - no state file, no back-channel.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">The §A4.2 split keeps the deciding half (dispatch.py) apart from the running half, so at lanes=1 the dispatcher degenerates to the serial loop and the concurrency is a table size, not a second code path.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC-144)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-151</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-151</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-132</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Dispatch-lock claim rung + the spine batch claim</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/integrate.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">_dispatch_lock/claim/_claim_refusal/_abandoned_claim</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">The §A4.1 authority flip&#x27;s integrator half (docs/concurrency-v2.md §A4.1): _claim_refusal&#x27;s safety_class != ordinary arm is DELETED (admission is the dispatcher&#x27;s decision) and the claim REQUIRES the dispatch lock (agent_common.dispatch_lock_path, out/agent-loop.lock - the lock a live dispatcher holds for its process lifetime), acquired at ONE site (_dispatch_lock, mirroring _slot&#x27;s one-site discipline for the merge slot&#x27;s own lock) with the ladder run before it so the clean-trunk rung never refuses over the lock&#x27;s own file: a hand claim on an idle station works, a hand claim during live lanes is unrepresentable, and the dispatcher&#x27;s in-process claim passes dispatch_lock_held=True. claim/_claim_refusal/_abandoned_claim/_claim_delta are batch-aware: a spine batch is ONE claim commit moving every batched spec (subject joins the ids; the single-WI subject stays byte-identical), and integrate() takes an optional branches= restriction so the dispatcher merges each lane&#x27;s branch as its own refresh completes.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">A constraint instead of a check: the state the old refusal guarded against (a claim written beside live lanes) is now unrepresentable, and the batch makes N spine changes cost one re-attest window (§A4).</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC-145)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-153</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-153</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-132</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">The unified trunk-side intake mint + the context block + the ratification-level arms</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/intake.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">intake_after_merge/mint_gap_rows/parse_dispositions/next_wi_id/context_block/flip_verified</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">The R1/R3 invariant sealed (log.md Decisions 2026-08-01): a WI id is created only by a human trunk commit or this helper - lanes never mint. next_wi_id is max+1 over every spec FILENAME under docs/work/ (active/&lt;branch&gt;/ included). Three triggers, serial by construction: (a) the ratified/routed-cell diff on the merged commit via check_trajectory.staged_spine_amendments -&gt; one adjudication row listing each changed row/cell/before-after (routed traced cells: SN-Refs, Verifies, and LLR SR-Refs per the ruled cell split); (b) a merged spec carrying ## Handback -&gt; the disposition row (outcomes cancel / defer / re-queue with drafted follow-up / surface an open item; NEVER minted for an adjudication row - no recursion, and handback.hand_back refuses the act itself); (c) the dispatcher&#x27;s gap census -&gt; concrete gap-closure rows, deduped against every existing row. Drafts-not-mints: a merged adjudication row&#x27;s ## Dispositions fenced-toml drafts mint at ITS merge, validated loudly. Tier signals are measurable (rows touched, gate delta, handback reason class). context_block renders the pure registry joins (cancelled precedent WITH REASONS first, pending OIs, the LLR/TC code map, CMP knowledge packs, IF seams, precedent reviews) - advisory-never-gating, three consumers. flip_verified enacts ruled decision 2: recommend-only under attended, flip under single-ratify/autonomous, unknown levels fail toward recommend. The mint commit mirrors the claim&#x27;s bookkeeping shape; every derived title is deterministic, so the sweep CLI re-run is idempotent.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">A detected event must FORCE a row into the registry with nobody watching, and an id two lanes could pick at once must stay unrepresentable (R1).</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC-147)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-154</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-154</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-132</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">The merge slot&#x27;s post-merge intake arm</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/integrate.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">integrate_one/_lane_bar_directives/_refresh_bar</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">integrate_one&#x27;s post-merge arm is the intake&#x27;s one honest hook point: the merge has landed, the slot is still held, trunk is serial - intake.intake_after_merge(root, pre-merge head, head, outcomes, branch) mints there as one bookkeeping commit (RULING-6: docs/work/ + the declared generated set), and a mint refusal stops the run loudly while the merge stands (recovery: fix trunk-side, then python intake.py sweep). The refresh&#x27;s bar directives (_lane_bar_directives, read off the same trunk claim the slot reads) carry the adjudication no-bar arm and the bar strictness pin to _run_bar&#x27;s --gate.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Minting anywhere else re-opens the two-lanes-one-id collision the trunk-only ruling ended; the slot is the only place trunk moves under a held lock.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC-148)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-132</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-132</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-132; LLR-140</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Unit</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the integrator suite: each claim refusal fires by name and the green claim moves the spec and cuts the branch; finished-branch detection keys on the tree; the verdict gate refuses absent, CHANGES-REQUESTED and stale-APPROVE verdicts and passes a fresh APPROVE; a missing or empty bar declaration refuses; the window audit flags a non-merge product commit and passes a bookkeeping-only window; one end-to-end claim-&gt;work-&gt;close-&gt;integrate run fast-forwards the trunk to a --no-ff merge commit.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-132 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_integrate.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-138</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-138</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-132; LLR-144</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Unit</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the handback suite: a partial close moves each claimed spec to the terminal partial/ and writes its immutable per-close report under docs/handbacks/, leaves the branch finished, is CLEAN under the real trajectory check (R-A exempts partial — the spec&#x27;s definition is deliberately byte-identical), refuses a second close without residue, and is LOUD when a terminal spec has no report; an already-declared blockref and an existing Deliverable survive; a branch trunk holds no claim for is refused. The quarantine arm reverts the product paths and keeps the failing diff as a bar-inert .patch, reverts a RENAME correctly (the --name-status -z stream read as records, not pairs - mutation-proven), leaves the handback bookkeeping alone, refuses when the red is not the lane&#x27;s own code, and restores rather than reports when a revert step fails.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-132 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_handback.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-139</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-139</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-132; LLR-145</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Unit</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the spec_move suite: an archival move redirects inbound links from every depth (text untouched, fragments carried, external URLs and bare anchors left alone), rebases the moved spec&#x27;s own links one directory deeper, preserves CRLF on both sides, and stages a git mv when the tree is a repo; the REAL check_docs passes over the archived fixture while the MUTATION TWIN - the same move without the rebase - fails it, so the guard reproduces the defect it fixes; a move with rewritten text (the handback shape) still runs the whole ritual; a missing source and an existing destination refuse; the CLI archives to the dated destination and refuses nonzero. The wired call sites are driven in their own suites: integrate.claim runs the ritual inside the one claim commit and a crashed relinked claim still re-cuts while a non-relink .md edit convicts (tests/test_integrate.py); the handback return runs it in reverse (tests/test_handback.py).</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-132 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_spec_move.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-144</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-144</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-132; LLR-150</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the dispatch loop suite on real git repos with the real bar: a spine batch claims as one commit and its worker receives both ids together, ordinary work admitted only after the window closes; a NEEDS-HUMAN worker hands back, the lane&#x27;s refresh runs as its own subprocess and the branch merges; a red refresh quarantines a merges-nothing branch once and stops the run for a done-asserting one; an attended gate row drains and exits 0 with the ratifications banner; the empty-frontier ladder hands the gap census to the intake mint (rows minted in one bookkeeping commit, driven by the same run, deduped on the next idle pass) and counts rung-2 cards off the shared pending read; pause/dirty-trunk/stall/crash-resume keep their serial-loop contracts at lanes=1.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-132 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_dispatch.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-145</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-145</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-132; LLR-151</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Unit</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the integrator claim suite: a spine claim succeeds on an idle station (the deleted safety-class arm, red-then-green); a hand claim while another holder has the dispatch lock refuses naming the lock and leaves the queue untouched, while dispatch_lock_held=True proceeds; a spine batch claims as ONE bookkeeping commit whose subject joins the ids and whose specs all land in active/&lt;branch&gt;/; every pre-existing single-WI claim contract (abandoned-claim re-cut, relink oracle, minted-id rung, RULING-6 audit) holds unchanged.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-132 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_integrate.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-147</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-147</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-132; LLR-153; IF-091</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the intake suite against real git repos, red-then-green per trigger (trigger (b) keys on the close&#x27;s immutable REPORT PATH, so a re-sweep dedupes exactly and a genuinely second close is a second row): a ratified SR amendment mints ONE adjudication row (id max+1, blockref empty, the before/after listing in ## Context) and an LLR SR-Refs re-point mints while a Module-only move stays silent (the ruled routing); a merged handback mints the disposition row (NEEDS-HUMAN reason routes strong; a handed-back adjudication row mints NOTHING and its own handback attempt is refused structurally); a merged adjudication row&#x27;s ## Dispositions drafts mint at its merge with planmode=dual passing through kind-derived, and a malformed block, an unknown key, a second adjudication kind or a bad bar value refuse with nothing minted; the census mints gap rows once and dedupes on re-run; every re-run is idempotent by exact-title dedup; the context block renders every join in failure-cost order and answers the empty string on a bare repo; both gate-policy arms enact (attended recommends and leaves the registries byte-identical; single-ratify and autonomous flip only the named Status cells, cell-exact elsewhere, idempotent).</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-132 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_intake.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-148</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-148</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-132; LLR-154; IF-090</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the integrator + dispatcher suites&#x27; intake-arm cases: a merged branch that amended a ratified SR cell yields the adjudication row as its own mint: commit immediately after the merge commit on trunk (in-slot, serial); the dispatcher&#x27;s empty-frontier rung 1 mints gap rows and KEEPS DRIVING them in the same run, then exits honestly when every gap already carries a row; a census-mint refusal stops the run loudly; the handback loop tests drive the disposition row the same run mints.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-132 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_integrate.py; tests/test_dispatch.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-158</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-158</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-132; IF-101</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Unit</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the id-watermark suite. It drives trace.py&#x27;s CLI so the rules are asserted on the EXIT CODE, not just as a function call - the wiring into --strict-integrity is the guarantee, and a suite that only calls watermark_findings() survived deleting it. Covers the mint seam IF-101 declares: next_wi_id counts from the mark, so a deleted id is not re-issued, and the reader&#x27;s refusal on an absent mark is NOT caught.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Smoke</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">A live id above its mark, a missing space, a malformed line, an absent file, a lowered mark and a hand-raised mark each fail loud; headroom left by a deleted row stays legal; the mint returns the next unused id after a deletion and refuses without a mark.</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_id_watermark.py::test_the_rules_are_actually_WIRED_INTO_the_integrity_floor; tests/test_id_watermark.py::test_the_mint_does_not_reissue_a_deleted_id</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-133-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-133</span><span>Work-branch lane skip for freshness steps</span><span class="pill">re-attest owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">baseline a5052a913 (2026-07-25) — from --since</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-133</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-133</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Work-branch lane skip for freshness steps</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-027; SN-008</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">On a claimed work branch (one whose history holds a docs/work/active/&lt;branch&gt;/ claim) check.py&#x27;s generated-artifact freshness steps shall SKIP with a stated trunk-lane reason - generated artifacts are trunk-only - while every non-freshness step still runs.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">The skip is not a weakening: a work branch cannot commit generated artifacts at all (concurrency-restructure §5.2), so demanding their freshness there would force the very conflict the rule deletes; the skip is reported, never silent, and any signal ambiguity resolves toward the full trunk bar. Decomposed from SR-006 so each row states one obligation.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">on a claimed work branch the declared freshness steps SKIP with a §5.2 notice and exit code untouched while skills-sync, trajectory and registry-integrity still run; the closing commit and its successors keep the skip (the signal derives from branch history, not the working tree); the trunk lane never skips them.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">M</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Gate harness</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-141</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-141</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-133</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Work-branch lane signal + freshness skip</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/check.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">_claimed_work_branch/steps</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">On a claimed work branch the _TRUNK_FRESHNESS_STEPS set SKIPs with a §5.2 notice, exit code untouched; the plan listing still names every step. The claim signal answers in two stages - docs/work/active/&lt;branch&gt;/ on disk (the fast path), else the branch&#x27;s own committed history (git log -1 -- docs/work/active/&lt;branch&gt;) - so the closing commit that archives the claim, and every commit after it, keep the skip. No git, a failing git, or an empty answer all read as the trunk lane: a false work-branch positive would switch the freshness gates off on the one branch that owns regenerating them.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">A false work-branch positive would relax the trunk&#x27;s freshness bar, so every ambiguity resolves toward the trunk lane.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC-134)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-001</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-134</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-134</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-133; LLR-141</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the check-harness lane suite: a claimed work branch skips the declared freshness steps with the §5.2 notice and untouched exit code while non-freshness steps still run; the closing commit and its successors keep the skip (history-derived signal); the trunk lane never skips.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-133 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_check_lane.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-134-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-134</span><span>Trunk step regenerates the declared artifacts in dependency order</span><span class="pill">re-attest owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">baseline a5052a913 (2026-07-25) — from --since</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-134</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-134</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Trunk step regenerates the declared artifacts in dependency order</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-027; SN-008</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">The serial trunk step shall regenerate the declared generated artifacts in dependency order - the derived gate before its consumers - failing loudly at the first error and never committing.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Stale-artifact merge conflicts become unrepresentable when only the serial trunk actor regenerates (§5.2/§5.5); dependency order because docs/gate is an input to the dashboard and status views, not an output. Decomposed from SR-130 so each row states one obligation.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">--regen skips absent artifact families with a notice and stops loudly at the first failing generator; derive_gate runs before the dashboard/status/open-items regens.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">M</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Trajectory</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-142</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-142</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-134</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Trunk-step artifact regen (dependency order)</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/trunk_step.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">regen</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">Regenerates arch-map, okf, derived-gate, dashboard, status block and open-items as subprocesses in dependency order (derived-gate before its two consumers), skipping absent artifact families with a notice and stopping loudly at the first failure. Never commits; the caller owns the commit.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">derive_gate runs before the dashboard/status regens because docs/gate is their input, not their output.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC-135)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-135</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-135</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-134; LLR-142</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Unit</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the trunk-step regen suite: --regen skips absent artifact families with a notice and stops loudly at the first failing generator, regenerating in dependency order.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-134 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_trunk_step.py::test_regen_skips_absent_artifact_families; tests/test_trunk_step.py::test_regen_fails_loudly_on_a_broken_generator</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-135-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-135</span><span>Pending surface renders the declared pause verbatim</span><span class="pill">re-attest owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">baseline a5052a913 (2026-07-25) — from --since</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-135</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-135</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Pending surface renders the declared pause verbatim</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-027; SN-016</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">The generated pending surface shall render a declared pause&#x27;s since and reason verbatim - no clock - clearing when the pause file is deleted.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">An open pause is a visible accruing cost, never a forgotten one (the stale-reason lesson). Decomposed from SR-131 so each row states one obligation.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">the pending block renders Paused since &lt;date&gt; - &lt;reason&gt; with the declared date verbatim (no clock), omits the stamp when since is empty, renders the pinned fail-closed placeholder on malformation, and clears when the file is deleted.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">M</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Modified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Trajectory</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-139</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-139</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-135</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Pause surfaced in the pending block</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/traj_status.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">_pause_pending/pending_block</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">Renders zero-or-one &#x27;Paused since &lt;since&gt; - &lt;reason&gt;&#x27; bullet in the committed-tree-pure pending region from docs/work/pause - declared since verbatim, no clock, the since clause omitted when empty, fail-closed text on malformation pinned equal to the reader&#x27;s placeholder.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">An open pause is a visible accruing cost, never a forgotten one.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">(see; TC-136)</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-002</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-136</span><span class="pill">ADDED since baseline</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-136</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-135; LLR-139</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Unit</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the pending-surface pause suite: the block renders since/reason verbatim (no clock), omits the stamp when since is empty, matches the reader&#x27;s fail-closed placeholder on malformation, clears on deletion, and is deterministic.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-135 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_gen_trajectory_pending.py::test_tracked_pause_projects_reason_and_declared_since; tests/test_gen_trajectory_pending.py::test_pause_malformed_text_matches_the_coordinator_reader</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Verified</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">4</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-137-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-137</span><span>One policy home, with a checked shape</span><span class="pill ratify">ratification owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">no baseline — awaiting its FIRST ratification — no attested baseline exists</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-137</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-137</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">One policy home, with a checked shape</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-028</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">The kit shall read every process policy dial from a single `docs/process.toml`, and shall REFUSE — never resolve by precedence — a repo in which any dial is declared both there and in its legacy one-word file. The file&#x27;s line shape shall be a checked contract: one `key = value` per line under a bare `[section]` header, no dotted keys, no inline tables, no multi-line strings.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Two grammars read this file — `tomllib` in Python and the three git hooks in pure sh, so a Python-less box still fails closed on a declared privacy gate (M-42). TOML is far more expressive than a `grep -E` can follow, so every shape only one reader understands is a silent flip of a security gate; the first cut shipped five such shapes, three of them fail-OPEN. Narrowing the shape and CHECKING the narrowing is what makes one file safe for two readers.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">A dial declared in both homes produces a refusal naming the key and both files, at every guarded entry point (the dispatcher&#x27;s pre-claim preflight, intake&#x27;s adjudication arm, the integrator&#x27;s verdict gate); a dotted key, inline table, multi-line string or hook-read key under the wrong section is refused by the shape check AND still reads ON in both readers; the hooks&#x27; sh and `tomllib` return the same answer over the adversarial file table; a wrong-typed or out-of-range dial is refused rather than defaulted.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">S</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Process</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-155</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-155</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-137; SR-139</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">The config layer: one reader, checked shape, one comparison</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/agent_common.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">process_config/process_shape_findings/config_conflicts/ratification_level/human_holds</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">`process_config` parses `docs/process.toml` once; `declared_policy` reads a dial through it with the legacy file as fallback; `process_shape_findings` refuses every line shape the hooks&#x27; sh cannot follow; `config_conflicts` returns refusal STRINGS (never raises — `dispatch.run` sits in the tick loop&#x27;s caller, where an exception would rewrite an exit-code contract) covering the double-declaration, the wrong type and the out-of-range value; `ratification_level` + `human_holds` are the ordinal and the ONE comparison every consumer makes.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Five entry points read policy without passing through `agent_loop.main`, so the refusal has to live where the value is read. The comparison is stated once for the same reason the shape is checked: the failure modes here are silent, and a rule restated at four call sites is four chances to state it differently.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">TC-150; TC-151</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-002</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-150</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-150</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-137; LLR-155</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the process-config suite: it sources the SHIPPED hook&#x27;s own sh functions and drives them against `tomllib` over a table of adversarial file shapes (trailing comments, dotted keys, inline tables, uppercase TRUE, BOM, CRLF, wrong section), asserts the two agree on every shape and that an unparseable file reads ON in both, and drives the mixed-config refusal and the wrong-typed-dial refusal.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-137 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_process_config.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-151</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-151</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-139; LLR-155; LLR-157</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Unit</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the ratification-level suite: the full level x stage matrix against the ladder the shipped template documents, every malformed input (negative, out-of-range, string, float, bool, absent, unparseable), the legacy-word translation, and the spine-stage corners (draft need, empty spine, uncited need, Modified SR, missing child, LLR-exempt SR).</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Smoke</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-139 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_ratification_level.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-138-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-138</span><span>Legacy config converts automatically and totally</span><span class="pill ratify">ratification owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">no baseline — awaiting its FIRST ratification — no attested baseline exists</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-138</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-138</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Legacy config converts automatically and totally</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-028</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">`bootstrap.py --migrate-config` shall fold every legacy one-word policy file into `docs/process.toml` and delete it, and shall NEVER delete a file whose value it did not manage to write. Both bootstrap and the documented re-sync shall run it.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">The hard mixed-config refusal in SR-137 is only safe to ship if a downstream adopter never meets it un-aided. And the converter&#x27;s own failure mode is the sharp one: an earlier cut deleted a legacy file on the strength of a write that never happened and reported success, destroying a declared `privacy_check = true` under a green `migrated:` line.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">A repo carrying legacy files converts in one pass and reports what moved; a second pass is a no-op; a legacy file whose value cannot be written is LEFT IN PLACE with its value intact and named in the report; the retired gate-authority word expands to all three dials it meant rather than to a single key.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">S</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Process</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-156</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-156</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-138</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">The legacy converter, and the one word that is not a rename</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/bootstrap.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">migrate_legacy_config/_migrate_gate_policy/set_process_key/LEGACY_RATIFICATION</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">`set_process_key` answers THREE states (`wrote`/`unchanged`/`missing`) and the write decides the delete; `add_if_missing` makes the write total. `gate-policy` gets its own arm because its single word expands to three dials, so it cannot be a one-key `LEGACY_CONFIG` row. `LEGACY_RATIFICATION` is F5-duplicated from `agent_common` (bootstrap imports no kit sibling — it must run standing on nothing but the kit directory it copies FROM) and pinned equal by `test_rule_sync`.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">This is duplicated POLICY, not plumbing: a migrator and a reader disagreeing about what `single-ratify` meant would let a repo scaffold with one posture and run with another — which is the shadowing defect the ordinal was introduced to remove, returning through the migration path.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">TC-152</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-002</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-152</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-152</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-138; LLR-156</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the migration cases in the process-config suite: a full conversion deletes every legacy file and lands all three ratification dials; a second pass is a no-op; a hand-trimmed process.toml leaves the legacy file in place with its value intact; the F5 translation tables are pinned equal and the retired enum key is absent from the shipped template.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-138 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_process_config.py;tests/test_rule_sync.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-139-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-139</span><span>Ratification as an ordinal over a derived spine stage</span><span class="pill ratify">ratification owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">no baseline — awaiting its FIRST ratification — no attested baseline exists</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-139</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-139</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Ratification as an ordinal over a derived spine stage</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-029</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">The kit shall express human ratification authority as a cumulative integer 0-4 naming the highest spine tier a human still ratifies, compared against a separately derived SPINE STAGE (0=SN..4=nothing in process) with a declared, auditable mapping to the harness gate. Every unreadable or out-of-range input shall resolve toward MORE human involvement.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">The retired three-value enum could not express &quot;TCs are human-held but LLRs are not&quot;, and four tables each re-interpreted the same word with its own fail-safe direction. The failure that matters is a machine ratifying something a human meant to hold, so every degrade has to point the same way — including the ones that look like kindness: clamping an out-of-range level toward 0 reads as &quot;nothing is human-held&quot; and disarms every hold in the repo.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">Level N holds exactly the tiers the shipped template documents (cumulative, strictly-less-than the stage); stage 4 is held by no level, the end-of-run hold being its own dial; an unreadable stage, an absent dial, and a wrong-typed dial all read as human-held; an out-of-range level falls back to the conservative end AND is refused loudly; a legacy gate-policy word reads as all three dials it meant.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">S</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Process</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-155</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-155</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-137; SR-139</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">The config layer: one reader, checked shape, one comparison</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/agent_common.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">process_config/process_shape_findings/config_conflicts/ratification_level/human_holds</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">`process_config` parses `docs/process.toml` once; `declared_policy` reads a dial through it with the legacy file as fallback; `process_shape_findings` refuses every line shape the hooks&#x27; sh cannot follow; `config_conflicts` returns refusal STRINGS (never raises — `dispatch.run` sits in the tick loop&#x27;s caller, where an exception would rewrite an exit-code contract) covering the double-declaration, the wrong type and the out-of-range value; `ratification_level` + `human_holds` are the ordinal and the ONE comparison every consumer makes.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Five entry points read policy without passing through `agent_loop.main`, so the refusal has to live where the value is read. The comparison is stated once for the same reason the shape is checked: the failure modes here are silent, and a rule restated at four call sites is four chances to state it differently.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">TC-150; TC-151</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-002</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-157</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-157</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-139</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">The second axis: which tier is in process</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/derive_gate.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">spine_stage/stage_to_gate/_decomposed_sr_ids</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">`spine_stage` returns the LOWEST unfinished tier, where a MISSING artifact belongs to its own tier rather than to its parent (an SR with no LLR yet is stage 2, because what is being written is an LLR), `Modified` is the SR&#x27;s own tier and is checked FIRST, and any other not-yet-Verified SR is checked LAST because a child in flight is the honest answer while it is in flight. `stage_to_gate` states the mapping to the harness gate once, deliberately lossy in one direction only. The value rides `docs/gate` as an APPENDED basis field.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Reading a missing child as the parent&#x27;s tier made stages 2 and 3 unreachable during exactly the period they describe, leaving the axis unable to express the distinction it exists for. Appending rather than inserting the basis field is required: `check.py`&#x27;s basis regex depends on the existing field order.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">TC-151</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-002</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-150</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-150</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-137; LLR-155</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the process-config suite: it sources the SHIPPED hook&#x27;s own sh functions and drives them against `tomllib` over a table of adversarial file shapes (trailing comments, dotted keys, inline tables, uppercase TRUE, BOM, CRLF, wrong section), asserts the two agree on every shape and that an unparseable file reads ON in both, and drives the mixed-config refusal and the wrong-typed-dial refusal.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-137 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_process_config.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-151</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-151</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-139; LLR-155; LLR-157</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Unit</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the ratification-level suite: the full level x stage matrix against the ladder the shipped template documents, every malformed input (negative, out-of-range, string, float, bool, absent, unparseable), the legacy-word translation, and the spine-stage corners (draft need, empty spine, uncited need, Modified SR, missing child, LLR-exempt SR).</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Smoke</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-139 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_ratification_level.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-140-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-140</span><span>The attestation anchor lives on the artifact&#x27;s own row</span><span class="pill ratify">ratification owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">no baseline — awaiting its FIRST ratification — no attested baseline exists</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-140</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-140</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">The attestation anchor lives on the artifact&#x27;s own row</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-029</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">The kit shall record each acceptance ON THE ACCEPTED ARTIFACT&#x27;S OWN ROW - the commit whose tree carries the text that was accepted, and a digest of that row&#x27;s NORMATIVE cells - never in a second registry keyed on the same artifact; shall report text that has moved away from its recorded anchor regardless of any Status movement; and shall treat an anchor written in the same commit as the text it anchors as an error.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Attestation STATE is already the row&#x27;s own Status - derive_gate computes the gate from those cells and reads no other source - so a separate ledger was a second home for a fact the row already carried, and three of its seven columns duplicated cells outright (Decision=ratified|meaning IS Status; superseded IS SupersededBy). What the ledger got right is the ANCHOR ITSELF: &#x27;has this been ratified&#x27; was answered by walking git for the newest commit where a row read Verified, which is sound only while every amendment flips its row in the same commit - precisely the sanctioned path the staged-amendment guard deliberately ignores. Two cells are kept rather than one because they fail in DIFFERENT directions: the commit is what a human can review and what the before/after brief diffs against, while the digest is what survives a squash-merge, a rebase or a shallow clone, any of which makes a recorded sha unresolvable and would otherwise silence drift detection repo-wide at once. Owner ruling 2026-08-09, docs/repo-lock.md D-1.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">An artifact whose normative text has moved since its recorded anchor is reported whether or not its Status moved; an anchor that cannot be recomputed, or that names an artifact which is not a current row, is a finding rather than silence; a commit that writes an anchor cell while also changing that row&#x27;s normative cells is refused, because a re-stamp in the same breath as an amendment hides the amendment; and the same rules hold for a stakeholder need, which has no Status cell for the amendment machinery to watch.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">S</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Process</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-158</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-158</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-140</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">The digest that anchors an attestation</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">normative_text/sn_normative_text/digest/current_digests</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">normative_text() joins a row&#x27;s RATIFIED cells in header order with a unit separator, reusing spine_cell_class rather than a second list so the digest and the amend-without-flip warn can never disagree about what normative means; the id and Status are excluded because neither is content - the id is the key the digest is looked up BY, and folding Status in would make every flip look like an amendment and every amendment invisible behind its own flip. sn_normative_text() takes the RAW table line for a stakeholder need rather than a parsed projection, because the three need tables have different shapes and a projection would hash the garbling. current_digests() is the one reader that joins both halves. The WRITER and the co-mutation guard are not here yet: which cells hold the anchor depends on the registry carrier, ruled separately (OI-12).</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Integrity is hard because a ledger that does not parse is not an anchor at all and every reader silently falls back to the git-history derivation it replaces. Removal was the gap: it silenced all three rungs at once, so the strongest form of rewriting was the one form the guard could not see.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">TC-153</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-001</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-153</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-153</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-140; LLR-158</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the attestation-digest suite: it DRIVES the premise rather than asserting it - the amendment seam is shown blind to a sanctioned amend+flip over a real two-commit git repo, which is why an anchor recorded at acceptance time is owed at all - then pins what the digest is taken over (a traced cell and the Status marker must not move it; a ratified cell must), that a need&#x27;s digest is its raw table line and survives re-indentation, and that current_digests covers every real spine row and every real need while excluding -000 rows and ids that appear only in prose.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Smoke</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-140 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_attestation_digest.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-141-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-141</span><span>The loop&#x27;s priority order is stated and pinned</span><span class="pill ratify">ratification owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">no baseline — awaiting its FIRST ratification — no attested baseline exists</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-141</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-141</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">The loop&#x27;s priority order is stated and pinned</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-025</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">Admission shall prefer `adjudication` rows over all other ready work, as a stable partition applied at admission rather than by renumbering the ruled rank table, and shall not thereby admit anything past a human-held stop.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">A ratification is the one act this loop cannot cheaply take back — the ledger is append-only by design — so a spine batch that re-attests requirements must not run ahead of an unjudged claim that may overturn its premise. Renumbering the ruled rank table would migrate every downstream repo&#x27;s ordering for a rung that belongs to admission, so the two orders are kept distinct and the disagreement is stated.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">An adjudication row admits ahead of a ready spine batch; the ruled rank table is unchanged, so `--explain` still reports it; the partition is stable (non-adjudication rows keep their relative order); a human-held attestation still drains and surfaces with the adjudication row waiting behind the stop.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">S</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Process</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-159</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-159</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-141; SR-142</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Admission order and the red-TC census</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/dispatch.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">_judgement_first/_admission/red_tc_census/parse_red_tc/_implemented_ids</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">`_judgement_first` is a STABLE PARTITION over the frontier, applied inside `_admission` before the surface/dispatch split so it can never move work past a human-held stop. `red_tc_census` rides the existing finding-string seam with a distinct prefix; `_red_tc_line` and `parse_red_tc` sit adjacent because the grammar is a contract between two modules, and `intake` routes on the parser rather than re-splitting prose.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">The alternative to the partition — renumbering `schedule._KIND_RANK` — would change ordering for every downstream repo for a rung that belongs to admission. The alternative to the parser is prose carrying control flow, which is the exact defect the typed `suggested_tier` field replaced.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">TC-154</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-154</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-154</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-141; SR-142; LLR-159</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the loop-order suite: an adjudication row admits ahead of a ready spine batch while the ruled rank table is unchanged; the partition is stable and cannot move work past a human-held stop; a red TC is named only under a `done` citer, with the three exempt statuses and the mixed-target case driven; the census grammar round-trips and matches nothing else.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Smoke</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-141 and SR-142 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_loop_order.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-142-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-142</span><span>Red test cases under a closed implementation are named and judged</span><span class="pill ratify">ratification owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">no baseline — awaiting its FIRST ratification — no attested baseline exists</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-142</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-142</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Red test cases under a closed implementation are named and judged</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-025</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">The idle census shall name, as its own class, a test case that is not green while every requirement it verifies is cited by a work item recorded `done`, and shall route it to an ADJUDICATION row whose tier is estimated from measured breadth rather than defaulted.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">The pair is a contradiction the registries state plainly and nothing was reading: the work is closed and the evidence for it is red. It is not one situation but three — a stale TC, an optimistic close, or a moved requirement — and choosing between them is a judgement, which is what the adjudication kind is for. The statuses that are NOT red are stated as exemptions because Status is an open vocabulary: failing toward naming a gap beats failing toward missing one.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">A not-green TC under a `done` citer is named; `Verified`, `Draft` and `Modified` are exempt (the last belongs to the amendment adjudication, and two judgement rows for one event is a double-count); an open, `partial` or `cancelled` citer makes nothing red; a TC verifying a mix of claimed and unclaimed targets is not red; the census line&#x27;s grammar has exactly one reader.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">S</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Process</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-159</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-159</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-141; SR-142</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Admission order and the red-TC census</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/dispatch.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">_judgement_first/_admission/red_tc_census/parse_red_tc/_implemented_ids</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">`_judgement_first` is a STABLE PARTITION over the frontier, applied inside `_admission` before the surface/dispatch split so it can never move work past a human-held stop. `red_tc_census` rides the existing finding-string seam with a distinct prefix; `_red_tc_line` and `parse_red_tc` sit adjacent because the grammar is a contract between two modules, and `intake` routes on the parser rather than re-splitting prose.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">The alternative to the partition — renumbering `schedule._KIND_RANK` — would change ordering for every downstream repo for a rung that belongs to admission. The alternative to the parser is prose carrying control flow, which is the exact defect the typed `suggested_tier` field replaced.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">TC-154</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-004</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-154</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-154</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-141; SR-142; LLR-159</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the loop-order suite: an adjudication row admits ahead of a ready spine batch while the ruled rank table is unchanged; the partition is stable and cannot move work past a human-held stop; a red TC is named only under a `done` citer, with the three exempt statuses and the mixed-target case driven; the census grammar round-trips and matches nothing else.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Smoke</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-141 and SR-142 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_loop_order.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-143-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-143</span><span>Queue overlap is made visible, never blocked</span><span class="pill ratify">ratification owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">no baseline — awaiting its FIRST ratification — no attested baseline exists</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-143</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-143</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Queue overlap is made visible, never blocked</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-025</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">The validator shall report, warn-only and never in the exit code, pairs of OPEN work items that overlap by near-identical title, shared requirement citation, or a shared spec of record.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">A queue that silently grows two rows for one job is the failure; a checker that cannot tell a legitimate split from a duplicate must not block on the difference. Overlap is frequently correct — two rows may answer one requirement from different sides — so the whole contribution of the mechanical rung is visibility, and the judgement half belongs to an adjudicator with the scope in front of it.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">Each overlapping OPEN pair is named once per signal with both ids, in deterministic order; a closed row never conflicts; the title bar is high enough that ordinary unrelated work is silent and a shared stopword is not a signal; the findings never join the exit code, not even under --strict.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">S</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Process</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-160</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-160</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-143</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">The mechanical queue-overlap pre-filter</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/check_trajectory.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">queue_conflict_findings/_title_tokens</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">Three signals over OPEN rows only — Jaccard over title token sets at a deliberately high bar, shared `SR-Refs`, and a shared anchor-stripped `SpecRef` — each reported once per pair per signal with both ids, in sorted order. Printed on the warn-only line beside backlog staleness; never appended to `errors`.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">A low similarity bar turns a useful warn into noise that gets ignored, which is worse than not having the warn: this rung catches the same job minted twice, not two rows in the same area.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">TC-155</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-001</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-155</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-155</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-143; LLR-160</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Unit</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the queue-conflict cases: each of the three signals fires with both ids in deterministic order, a closed row never conflicts, unrelated open rows and a shared stopword are silent, and the findings are wired on the warn-only line rather than into the exit code.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Smoke</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-143 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_loop_order.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-144-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-144</span><span>Every lane close is a terminal state with an immutable record</span><span class="pill ratify">ratification owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">no baseline — awaiting its FIRST ratification — no attested baseline exists</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-144</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-144</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Every lane close is a terminal state with an immutable record</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-027</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">A lane that cannot finish shall close into a TERMINAL `partial/` state with one immutable per-close report naming the claimed outcome, the reason, the commit range and the keep/discard split, and shall merge like any other branch. A report declaring neither a split nor an explicit deferral shall be refused, and a second close of the same event shall be refused rather than overwriting the first.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Five successive dedup mechanisms leaked because each reconstructed the return event from a MUTABLE proxy; a document that never moves dissolves the class instead of mitigating it. The refusals are what make &quot;immutable&quot; a property rather than a claim. The split is a field because its absence merged rejected code onto trunk on 2026-08-03: silence about it is different from saying &quot;I could not judge&quot;, and only silence is unactionable.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">A partial close is clean under the real validator (the terminal Deliverable rule is exempt, because an early close leaves the spec&#x27;s definition byte-identical by design); a terminal spec with no report is LOUD rather than silently un-disposed; a second close is refused and leaves no residue in the lane; a multi-spec close that refuses restores every report it wrote.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">S</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Process</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-161</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-161</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-144; SR-145</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">The close ritual and the disposition it owes</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/handback.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">close_partial/render_report/report_path/_restore/_split_lines</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">`close_partial` writes the report FIRST (it is the event&#x27;s identity), refuses when one already exists, and restores EVERY report this call wrote on any refusal — so a multi-spec lane cannot half-close. `intake._close_drafts` titles the disposition on the report PATH and nothing else, and says so LOUDLY when a terminal spec has no report rather than minting zero rows quietly.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">The path is the only token in the event&#x27;s identity that cannot move. An earlier cut folded the report&#x27;s `claimed_outcome` in as well, which re-read a MUTABLE field on every sweep, so one edit minted a second disposition for the same close — the starvation class returning through a new proxy.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">TC-156</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-003</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-156</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-156</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-144; SR-145; LLR-161</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the handback suite over real git worktrees: a partial close is clean under the REAL trajectory check (not a stub), a terminal spec with no report is loud, a second close is refused and leaves no residue, and the disposition is keyed on the report path so a re-sweep mints nothing.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-144 and SR-145 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_handback.py;tests/test_intake.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-145-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-145</span><span>A lane&#x27;s outcome is a claim; the adjudicator overrules by minting</span><span class="pill ratify">ratification owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">no baseline — awaiting its FIRST ratification — no attested baseline exists</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-145</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-145</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">A lane&#x27;s outcome is a claim; the adjudicator overrules by minting</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-027</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">Every `partial` and `cancelled` close shall earn one disposition row, keyed on the close&#x27;s own immutable report path; a `complete` close shall be spot-checked at a declared rate rather than gated. Continuing partial work shall mean DRAFTING A SUCCESSOR carrying explicit lineage, never re-opening a terminal row.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">The lane is entitled to assert what happened; what it MEANS is the adjudicator&#x27;s, and making that authoritative by minting keeps history honest — the original move is never reversed in place. Keying the disposition on the report PATH rather than on any field of it is what makes re-sweep idempotence correct: an earlier cut keyed on the report&#x27;s mutable outcome field and re-minted on every edit. Adjudicating every green close would rebuild the verdict gate under a new name, over work two gates already judged.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">A partial or cancelled close mints exactly one disposition; a re-sweep mints none; a genuinely second close is a second report and so a second row; a disposition row never mints a disposition for itself (refused at both ends); a successor&#x27;s lineage survives the id change.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">S</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Process</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-161</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-161</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-144; SR-145</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">The close ritual and the disposition it owes</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/handback.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">close_partial/render_report/report_path/_restore/_split_lines</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">`close_partial` writes the report FIRST (it is the event&#x27;s identity), refuses when one already exists, and restores EVERY report this call wrote on any refusal — so a multi-spec lane cannot half-close. `intake._close_drafts` titles the disposition on the report PATH and nothing else, and says so LOUDLY when a terminal spec has no report rather than minting zero rows quietly.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">The path is the only token in the event&#x27;s identity that cannot move. An earlier cut folded the report&#x27;s `claimed_outcome` in as well, which re-read a MUTABLE field on every sweep, so one edit minted a second disposition for the same close — the starvation class returning through a new proxy.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">TC-156</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-003</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-156</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-156</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-144; SR-145; LLR-161</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the handback suite over real git worktrees: a partial close is clean under the REAL trajectory check (not a stub), a terminal spec with no report is loud, a second close is refused and leaves no residue, and the disposition is keyed on the report path so a re-sweep mints nothing.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Full</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-144 and SR-145 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_handback.py;tests/test_intake.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-146-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-146</span><span>Prompts are reviewable files with a per-session audit trail</span><span class="pill ratify">ratification owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">no baseline — awaiting its FIRST ratification — no attested baseline exists</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-146</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-146</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Prompts are reviewable files with a per-session audit trail</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-005</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">Every prompt the loop launches shall be a shipped file with strictly filled slots, listed by digest in a freshness-gated generated catalogue, with each session recording which template it used and the fingerprint of what that rendered to.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Prose steers the sessions this loop launches and had been reviewable only by reading Python source, which makes the process trusted rather than inspectable. The audit trail is the part that makes the move useful rather than cosmetic: without a per-session digest, &quot;which instruction did this session see&quot; stays an inference. (The argv-vs-shell-string transport decision is design, and lives one tier down in LLR-163.)</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">An unknown or unfilled slot is a refusal; a missing shipped template is a named preflight refusal rather than a first-session crash; the catalogue lists every shipped prompt with its digest and `--check` fails when stale; a line-ending difference is not a digest change; an operator override is named in the session record rather than only in the launch flags.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">S</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Process</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-162</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-162</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-146</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Prompts as loaded files, catalogued and fingerprinted</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/prompts.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">load/fill/strict_check/digest/catalog_rows/preflight</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">`load` reads a shipped template (or an operator override) and raises a named `PromptError`; `fill` refuses an unknown slot and an unfilled declared slot; `digest` normalizes line endings before hashing; `preflight` turns a missing template into a launch-time refusal. `gen_prompt_catalog.py` renders the catalogue with a `--check` freshness gate, and `agent_common.prompt_fingerprint` records the rendered result per session beside the template&#x27;s own name.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">Normalizing before hashing is not cosmetic: the same template checked out CRLF on Windows and LF on POSIX is the same prompt, and a telemetry field that said otherwise would report a model change on every clone.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">TC-157</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-002</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-163</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-163</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-146</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">Argv arrays, and the adjudicator as a routed phase</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/agent_session.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">split_cmd/build_argv</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">`split_cmd` recognises a JSON array ONLY when the value parses as a list, so no existing template can change meaning; a non-string token raises rather than being stringified. The prompt-transport decision reads the resulting TOKENS, so it is identical for both forms. Alongside it, `ADJUDICATE` joins `agent_route.WEIGHT_PHASES` and `agent_loop.DEFAULT_PHASE_TIER` with its own `route_intent` arm: cross-family like the reviewers, tier pinned by the row&#x27;s measured estimate, and deliberately deaf to the implementer&#x27;s escalation overrides.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">A silent coercion in a registry is a launch that does not match its declaration. And an adjudicator inheriting the implementer&#x27;s escalation would silently re-tier a judgement because a different row&#x27;s build went badly.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">TC-157</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-002</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-164</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-164</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-146</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">The generated prompt catalogue and its freshness gate</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/gen_prompt_catalog.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">render/main</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">`render` is PURE — it takes no arguments and reads only the shipped templates — so `--check` and the write path cannot disagree about what &quot;current&quot; means. `--check` compares on NORMALIZED line endings, for the same reason the digest is taken that way: a CRLF checkout is not a stale catalogue. The file is declared in `docs/stack.ini [generated]` as kind `promptcatalog`, so the trunk lane owns it and a work branch never commits it.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">A generated artifact nobody re-generates is a document that lies, and a lying catalogue is worse than none: it invites the operator to conclude the wrong thing about the session they are debugging. Same contract `gen_arch_map --check` and `gen_skills_index --check` hold.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">TC-157</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-002</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-157</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-157</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-146; LLR-162; LLR-163</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the prompts and routing suites: strict slot filling and its refusals, the preflight on a missing template, the catalogue&#x27;s freshness gate, the digest&#x27;s line-ending normalization, the argv-array form with its fall-through and refusal cases, and the ADJUDICATE phase&#x27;s tier, cross-family rule and escalation deafness.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Smoke</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-146 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_prompts.py;tests/test_routing_and_prompts.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div></article><article class="card" id="SR-147-attest"><h3><span class="rid">SR-147</span><span>One machine-parseable carrier for the requirement spine</span><span class="pill ratify">ratification owed</span></h3><p class="baseline">no baseline — awaiting its FIRST ratification — no attested baseline exists</p><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">SR SR-147</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">SR-ID</div><div class="diff">SR-147</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">One machine-parseable carrier for the requirement spine</div><div class="cellname">SN-Refs</div><div class="diff">SN-002; SN-012</div><div class="cellname">Requirement</div><div class="diff">The kit shall carry the SN-&gt;SR-&gt;LLR-&gt;TC spine in ONE machine-parseable carrier rather than two (stakeholder needs as markdown prose tables, the other three tiers as CSV), and shall provide a mechanical converter that proves the conversion cell-for-cell over the live registries BEFORE the authority flip - refusing, with a nonzero exit that names the row and the field, any conversion in which a cell does not survive the round-trip.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">The two-carrier split has no recorded rationale and costs on both sides. Reading 32 need rows takes ~166 code lines across 14 functions in 8 modules - six bespoke scanners, two of them F5 twins pinned by nothing but a docstring, which have already drifted once and rendered a phantom root in the dashboard - while reading all 436 SR+LLR+TC rows takes csv.DictReader. CSV also cannot represent the cells that actually exist: five hold a literal pipe, 552 hold commas, the longest is 1,553 characters, and an embedded newline has no representation at all. A structured carrier turns three integrity rules into properties of the parse - a duplicate id becomes a decode error, a ref list becomes a typed array (retiring the split-on-whitespace rule that read `and` as an orphan), and an empty cell becomes an absent key, so `unset` and `set to empty` stop being the same value. The converter is the migration&#x27;s evidence, not its convenience: SR-129&#x27;s 140-cell lesson is that an unproven representation change is exactly where a registry silently loses cells.</div><div class="cellname">AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="diff">migrate_carrier.py --check over the live registries exits 0 reporting the round-trip clean for every row of every tier; a cell that is dropped, truncated, re-typed or re-ordered is REFUSED with exit 1 and a finding naming the registry, the row id and the field; and the emitted text re-parses with tomllib.</div><div class="cellname">Priority</div><div class="diff">C</div><div class="cellname">Verification</div><div class="diff">Test</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div><div class="cellname">Area</div><div class="diff">Trajectory</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-165</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-165</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-147</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">The spine carrier converter (markdown + CSV -&gt; TOML)</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/migrate_carrier.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">convert/rows_to_toml/sn_to_toml/compare/toml_scalar</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">convert() walks the four tiers and returns (findings, written_paths), so the caller decides whether a lossy conversion writes. rows_to_toml emits `[&lt;table&gt;.&lt;ID&gt;]` with the id PREFIX RETAINED and BARE - TOML allows `-` in a bare key - because ~6,400 hand-authored citations use the prefixed token and a numeric key buys no type change (TOML keys are always strings, and `001` and `1` are two legal DIFFERENT keys, which is a near-miss the prefixed form does not have). cell_to_value types by COLUMN, not by guessing: REF_COLS become arrays, INT_COLS become ints, an empty cell becomes an absent key, and everything else stays a string - this is a carrier change, not a schema change. toml_scalar emits multi-line basic strings for anything long or newline-bearing (tomllib is read-only by design, so the kit emits its own, as it already does in wi_convert.toml_string and bootstrap.set_process_key). sn_to_toml keeps the edge-case tier&#x27;s own lifecycle/scenario/expected fields as THEMSELVES and writes kind= from the heading, retiring section-as-state. compare() re-reads what was emitted and diffs it against an {id: {key: text}} projection built from either carrier, so ONE comparison serves both instead of a second copy that drifts.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">The round-trip check is the requirement, not a convenience - and a check that cannot fail would be the false green SN-008 forbids, which is why the loss detector is driven against real corruption classes rather than only against a clean run. Ref cells compare on the TOKEN LIST because the conversion re-joins them with the canonical separator and the original spacing was never part of the meaning; comparing raw text there would report a loss that is not one. The edge-case fold that traj_parse._sn_fields performs is a PRESENTATION rule the markdown table forced, so it stays in the exporters - baking it into the carrier would make the exporter&#x27;s reading the only reading there is.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">TC-159</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-005</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">LLR LLR-166</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">LLR-ID</div><div class="diff">LLR-166</div><div class="cellname">SR-Refs</div><div class="diff">SR-147</div><div class="cellname">Title</div><div class="diff">The spine carrier&#x27;s one reader, and the dual-home refusal</div><div class="cellname">Module</div><div class="diff">project-trajectory/scripts/spine_carrier.py</div><div class="cellname">CodeSymbol</div><div class="diff">SPINE_TABLE/SPINE_COLUMN/rows_from_toml/rows_from_text/resolve/load</div><div class="cellname">Detail</div><div class="diff">PURE like trace_text (text in, rows out; no git, no argv) except resolve()&#x27;s existence check, because the dual-home refusal is the one rule that must not be duplicated. rows_from_toml presents rows under TODAY&#x27;S COLUMN NAMES via SPINE_COLUMN - the exact inverse of migrate_carrier.KEY - so the carrier change is data plus a loader rather than a sixteen-module rename; SPINE_TABLE is keyed on the ID COLUMN, not on a path, because a path carries a carrier suffix and therefore moves. An absent key stays absent rather than becoming &quot;&quot;, and a decode error returns None rather than {}. resolve() RAISES when both carriers exist rather than picking by precedence, and load() raises on a carrier that exists and will not parse. carriers() returns BOTH paths so an applicability test spans the cutover.</div><div class="cellname">Rationale</div><div class="diff">None-vs-{} and absent-vs-empty are the same defect twice: {} says the registry had no rows, which for a baseline read means re-bless everything with no diff and for a gate means nothing to check, so a carrier that cannot be read must be ABSENT rather than empty. The dual-home REFUSAL follows the rule bootstrap --migrate-config already applies to the policy dials: a precedence rule lets a half-finished migration keep working while quietly reading the stale half, so the conversion is never finished and nobody finds out. ONE HOME for the vocabulary is an owner ruling (2026-08-10, repo-lock D-6) amending F5: duplicating a vocabulary fails silently - the copy that has not learned a column returns a row missing that cell, which every consumer reads as &quot;the cell is empty&quot;.</div><div class="cellname">TestRefs</div><div class="diff">TC-160</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Component</div><div class="diff">CMP-001</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-159</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-159</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-147; LLR-165</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Run the carrier-converter suite: it converts the live spine, re-parses the emitted TOML with tomllib and asserts the round-trip is cell-for-cell identical to the source for every row of every tier, then DRIVES the loss detector against corruption classes (a dropped row, a dropped field, a truncated value, a re-typed value, a mangled ref list) and asserts each is REFUSED with a finding naming the row and field, so the clean result is evidence rather than a check that cannot fail.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Smoke</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-147 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_migrate_carrier.py</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div><div class="row"><div class="row-head"><span class="rid">TC TC-160</span><span class="pill">current content</span></div><div class="cellname">TC-ID</div><div class="diff">TC-160</div><div class="cellname">Verifies</div><div class="diff">SR-147; LLR-166</div><div class="cellname">Level</div><div class="diff">Integration</div><div class="cellname">Method</div><div class="diff">Drive the carrier reader over a REAL repo whose spine is cut over from CSV to TOML in one commit, and assert the four properties that fail open if wrong: the pre-migration revision still reads through the carrier it used then; the post-migration revision reads through the new one presenting the same column names; a lossless cutover is SILENT to the amendment guard while text smuggled into that same commit is NAMED with its row and cell; and a carrier that does not parse - including the duplicate id the carrier turns into a decode error - is reported absent rather than empty. Then pin the vocabulary against drift: the reader is the exact inverse of the writer&#x27;s KEY, and every column of every LIVE spine header is driven through the pair so the agreement cannot be vacuous.</div><div class="cellname">Tier</div><div class="diff">Smoke</div><div class="cellname">Expected</div><div class="diff">Satisfies SR-147 AcceptanceCriteria</div><div class="cellname">Automated</div><div class="diff">Yes</div><div class="cellname">Evidence</div><div class="diff">tests/test_trajectory_staged.py::test_baseline_read_survives_the_carrier_cutover</div><div class="cellname">Status</div><div class="diff">Draft</div><div class="cellname">Phase</div><div class="diff">5</div></div></article></section>
